#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[Preface]
<preface>
For more than sixty years, computer systems were engineered under a single, unspoken covenant: computation lives behind glass.

Inside the digital sandbox, software is blessed with the superpower of idempotency. If a thread encounters an unhandled null pointer, the operating system reclaims its virtual memory. If a network packet drops in transit, the transport layer transparently retries. If a database transaction encounters a write conflict, it rolls back to state $S_0$ as though the operation never began. In the digital world, errors are harmlessly contained behind glowing screens:

$ upright("Software Exception") & arrow.r^(quad upright("try/catch") quad) upright("HTTP 500 Retry")\
 & quad \( upright("Physical World State ") W_t upright(" Unchanged") \) $

The moment software is wired to a motor inverter, that sixty-year covenant vanishes.

A floating-point tensor written to a memory-mapped timer register becomes physical torque. Gate drivers pulse, switching amperes of phase current through copper stator windings. Magnetic flux accelerates kilograms of steel, aluminum, and glass through three-dimensional space, converting electrical energy into kinetic momentum ($upright(bold(p)) = m upright(bold(v))$) and irreversible Joule heat ($I^2 R$).

By the fundamental conservation laws of classical mechanics and thermodynamics, physical actions cannot be rewound:

$ upright("Physical Actuation ") A_t & arrow.r.double.long upright("Matter &Momentum Mutate ") \( W_t arrow.r W_(t + 1) \)\
 & arrow.r.double.long upright("Altered Sensory Stream ") O_(t + 1) $

Because physical actions permanently alter the state of the world, every subsequent sensor observation is an endogenous consequence of the system's own prior decisions. You cannot #NormalTok("ctrl+z"); kinetic momentum, and you cannot catch a dropped workpiece with a software exception handler. The machine must live with the physical consequences of its choices.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[The Digital Agent Illusion versus Physical Reality]
<the-digital-agent-illusion-versus-physical-reality>
In recent years, artificial intelligence has popularized the concept of an #emph[agent]. In modern software engineering, an agent is commonly conceptualized as a large language model operating in an interactive loop: it receives a text prompt, reflects across scratchpad tokens, executes a tool or API call, and observes the response before taking its next turn.

That formulation is powerful, but it carries a dangerous hidden assumption: the assumption that time pauses while the model thinks.

A digital software agent operates in a turn-based, synthetic timeline. If a cloud foundation model requires three seconds to deliberate, query a vector database, and generate tokens, the external software environment waits idly. When that same paradigm is connected to physical actuators, the illusion collapses.

The physical universe does not pause while a neural network deliberates. While a high-capacity vision-language model evaluates self-attention over millions of parameters, a moving manipulator continues traveling along its kinematic vector ($d = v dot.op Delta t$), winding temperatures rise, and sensor observations grow stale. In Physical AI, time is not an arbitrary turn counter; it is an unyielding dynamical state variable.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[The Intellectual Collision: Two Crippled Paradigms]
<the-intellectual-collision-two-crippled-paradigms>
Physical AI Systems exists because two foundational engineering cultures have reached their historical limits:

The first paradigm is the #emph[Closed-World Trap of Classical Robotics and Automation]. For decades, robotics and control engineering achieved astonishing precision within tightly controlled factory cages. By relying on hand-crafted rigid-body kinematics, pre-computed CAD models, and static finite-state machines, classical control provided deterministic Lyapunov stability and sub-millimeter repeatability. Yet the moment a machine is deployed into an unstructured, human-centric environment---where lighting shifts, object geometries vary, and friction is unpredictable---the rigid CAD assumptions fracture, and the system fails.

The second paradigm is the #emph[Digital Sandbox Trap of Modern Machine Learning]. Modern deep learning brought high-capacity foundation models capable of breathtaking open-world semantic understanding. Yet machine learning practitioners routinely operate under the belief that high benchmark accuracy on a static test dataset or high episode reward in a domain-randomized physics simulator equates to real-world reliability. They deploy stochastic neural networks directly to motor drivers, ignoring worst-case tail latencies ($P_99$), unified memory bus contention, and the physical reality that an operating system kernel panic will leave motor phase coils latched in a high-power state.

Physical AI Systems is the grand synthesis of these two worlds. It does not treat machine learning as a replacement for physical laws, nor does it dismiss learned foundation models out of fear of non-determinism. Instead, it introduces an explicit architectural privilege boundary: caging the open-world intelligence of foundation models within the deterministic, real-time mathematical safety invariants of cyber-physical systems.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[The Durable Systems Question]
<the-durable-systems-question>
This textbook is neither a guide to training larger foundation models on cloud clusters nor a classical robotics survey deriving pages of coordinate transformations. The single question governing every architectural pattern, mathematical contract, and systems interface across these chapters is:

#quote(block: true)[
#emph["What must the surrounding system know, measure, enforce, preserve, and prove before an unverified learned proposal may produce a physical consequence?"]
]

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[The Systems Stance and Its Boundaries]
<the-systems-stance-and-its-boundaries>
Being candid about our perspective means being equally clear about what this book is, what it is not, and the first-principles viewpoint we bring to the table:

- #strong[What this book is not:] It is not an exhaustive treatise on classical mechanical kinematics, Special Euclidean Lie algebra ($S E \( 3 \)$ screw theory), or nonlinear Lyapunov control proofs (readers seeking classical robotics mechanics should consult Craig, Spong, Lynch & Park, and Slotine). Nor is it a disembodied deep learning theory survey focused on loss landscapes and gradient optimization behind glass (for which Goodfellow, Bishop, and Murphy are standard).
- #strong[What this book is:] The missing systems architecture bridge. We focus squarely on the multi-rate software runtimes, heterogeneous silicon placement, memory bus contention, hardware-isolated privilege boundaries, empirical tail-latency metrology, and zero-heap safety contracts required to host high-capacity learned models on physical machines without risking hardware destruction.
- #strong[Our Equation Philosophy:] We pursue first principles. Equations throughout this book are intentionally canonical, simple, and clean. Rather than performative control theory notation, every mathematical relation in this text is a back-of-the-napkin SI invariant---translating computational delay into unguided physical travel, bus saturation into tail-latency blowouts, and motor current into thermal accumulation.

By making these boundaries explicit, our aim is to move the systems needle forward---giving computer scientists, machine learning practitioners, and embedded roboticists the shared architectural foundation needed to build dependable Physical AI systems.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[The Evolutionary Arc of Machine Learning Systems]
<the-evolutionary-arc-of-machine-learning-systems>
Physical AI represents the fourth major phase in the multi-decade evolution of machine learning systems:

- #strong[The Cloud Era (2012--2020):] Systems engineering concentrated on distributed training clusters, parameter servers, and low-latency serving engines for stateless perception and language models @krizhevsky2012imagenet@vaswani2017attention. Computation remained entirely advisory software within digital sandboxes.
- #strong[The TinyML Era (2018--2023):] The community tackled a fundamental silicon barrier, compressing neural networks to fit microcontrollers with kilobytes of SRAM and milliwatts of power. This era established INT8 quantization and static memory arenas, yet deployments remained largely open-loop sensory triggers that did not close an autonomous physical control loop.
- #strong[The Foundation Model Era (2023--2026):] Large multimodal foundation models introduced open-world scene understanding and semantic reasoning. However, these models operate at slow deliberative cadences ($0.5 upright(" to ") 2 upright(" Hz")$), draw significant power, exhibit heavy tail latency ($P_99$), and lack intrinsic awareness of physical inertia or microsecond safety bounds.
- #strong[The Physical AI Systems Era (2026+):] Learned models leave the digital sandbox to exercise delegated physical authority over matter and energy under hard real-time constraints. This era unifies high-level semantic reasoning, spatial perception, multi-rate planning, and bare-metal safety vetoes ($1000 upright(" Hz")$) on heterogeneous silicon.

