#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[The Proposal and Permission (Dual-Brain) Core]
<the-proposal-and-permission-dual-brain-core>
To reconcile stochastic artificial intelligence with deterministic physical safety, this book centers around an architectural foundation: the Proposal and Permission Privilege Split.

The Host Brain (Linux MPU or neural accelerator) operates as an unprivileged proposal engine. It ingests raw sensor streams, tracks spatial belief, queries multimodal vision-language models, and decodes smooth trajectory action chunks, emitting time-bounded intent leases ($cal(L)_(upright("intent"))$).

The Reflex Brain (real-time microcontroller) operates as the privileged permission authority. Executing bare-metal C or FreeRTOS at $1000 upright(" Hz")$ with strictly zero dynamic heap allocation, it continuously projects candidate proposals onto forward-invariant safe sets ($h \( x \) gt.eq 0$), evaluates dynamic stopping clearance ($d_(upright("stop")) lt.eq d_(upright("clear"))$), and commands actuator power stages.

If the Host Brain panics, encounters unified memory bus contention, or hallucinates an unsafe trajectory, the Reflex Brain intercepts the violation within one millisecond, overriding the proposal and transitioning the machine into a safe physical state.

