#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[The Pedagogical Journey: The Engineering Dossier]
<the-pedagogical-journey-the-engineering-dossier>
This book is organized around a concrete, semester-long engineering progression. As you work through each chapter and companion hardware laboratory on the desk-bench Physical AI Kit (Arduino UNO Q), you do not simply complete disconnected exercises. You construct an eleven-artifact #emph[Engineering Dossier]:

- #strong[Part I (Foundations):] You formulate the physical laws that bound intelligent action: tracing the Causal Boundary in the Loop Charter, measuring worst-case tail latencies and stopping distances in the Requirements Ledger, and defining the multi-rate execution hierarchy in the Workflow Charter.
- #strong[Part II (The Embodied Lifecycle):] You construct the five sensory-motor organs of the physical agent: designing zero-copy camera ingestion contracts, tracking spatial coordinate frame trees, bounding high-level VLM intent leases, synthesizing jerk-continuous action chunks, and implementing 1 kHz microcontroller safety enforcers.
- #strong[Part III (Integration and Release):] You place these components across heterogeneous silicon, configure memory bus Quality-of-Service priorities to prevent frame-buffer starvation, design bumpless human-machine authority handoffs, and assemble a formal Claim-Argument-Evidence safety case.

The curriculum culminates in #strong[?\@sec-assurance] and #ref(<sec-frontier>, supplement: [Chapter]): the #emph[Whole-System Bench Defense] and oral capstone release gate. In this capstone milestone, your integrated physical machine operates under live, unannounced, cross-layer fault injection---surviving camera dropouts, clock skews, memory bus saturation, and operating system kernel panics---to justify an evidence-backed release verdict before a red-team review board.

Welcome to Physical AI Systems. Let us bridge the digital mind and the physical machine.

#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[About the Author]
<about-the-author>
Vijay Janapa Reddi is the Gordon McKay Professor of Electrical Engineering and Computer Engineering at Harvard University and a Visiting Professor at ETH Zurich in the Integrated Systems Laboratory (IIS), D-ITET. He is a founding member and Vice President of #link("https://mlcommons.org/")[MLCommons] and the author of the open-source reference text #link("https://mlsysbook.ai")[#emph[Machine Learning Systems]].

His research sits at the intersection of computer architecture, machine learning systems, and edge robotics, focusing on how computing hardware and learned software co-design to interact with the physical world. Over the past decade, his work has concentrated on democratizing applied systems through open-source education and reproducible benchmarks, connecting first-principles silicon constraints directly to accessible, bench-verified learning.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Why I Am Writing This Book: The First-Principles Systems Stance]
<why-i-am-writing-this-book-the-first-principles-systems-stance>
Every textbook reflects the intellectual lineage, biases, and lens of its author. It is important to be completely upfront about what this book is, what it is not, and the specific viewpoint I bring to the table.

I am not a classical control theorist, nor a mechanical kinematics specialist, and I do not pretend to be one. Readers seeking exhaustive mathematical treatises on Special Euclidean Lie algebra tangent spaces ($S E \( 3 \)$ screw theory), differential geometry, or formal nonlinear Lyapunov stability proofs will find those beautifully developed in the authoritative classical texts by John Craig, Mark Spong, Kevin Lynch & Frank Park, and Jean-Jacques Slotine. Similarly, readers seeking abstract deep learning theory behind glass---probing high-dimensional loss landscapes, optimization dynamics, and generalization bounds---should turn to Ian Goodfellow, Christopher Bishop, and Kevin Murphy.

What I bring to this table is the perspective of a computer systems architect and machine learning systems (MLSys) researcher going after first principles.

For over twenty years---from mobile System-on-Chip performance architecture and edge AI silicon at Google, to co-founding MLCommons/MLPerf to establish empirical benchmarking standards for AI accelerators, to pioneering the TinyML movement to compress neural networks onto milliwatt microcontrollers---my work has lived at the physical boundary where software bits meet physical silicon and energy constraints.

In watching the rapid rise of modern foundation models, vision-language-action (VLA) networks, and generative diffusion policies, I saw a familiar and dangerous disconnect:

- Machine learning researchers build magnificent, multi-billion-parameter neural models assuming time is a synthetic turn counter, compute is infinite, and software errors can be caught with a #NormalTok("try/catch"); block.
- Embedded and robotics engineers build rock-solid real-time motor controllers, but struggle to host heavy, variable-latency stochastic foundation models alongside microsecond control loops without experiencing system lockups or bus contention.

When you bring high-capacity learned models out from behind the glass of the digital sandbox and wire them to physical motor inverters, software bugs become broken hardware. A $100 upright(" ms")$ tail-latency spike on an edge System-on-Chip is not an elastic delay---it is centimeters of unguided kinetic projectile travel ($d = v dot.op Delta t$) that smashes tooling. A stepped waypoint output is not an abstract coordinate---it is an infinite jerk spike ($accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) arrow.r oo$) that shears cycloidal gearbox teeth. A stalled motor current is not an idle thread---it is irreversible Joule heat ($I^2 R$) that melts stator windings.

These failures cannot be resolved by training larger neural networks or by proving more abstract theorems in simulation. They are fundamental computer systems engineering problems:

+ #strong[Time is a Physical Coordinate:] Latency tail distributions ($P_99$), bus crossbar contention, and sensor Direct Memory Access (DMA) pipelines govern closed-loop physical survival.
+ #strong[The Proposal--Permission Privilege Split:] Stochastic, variable-latency neural models running on Linux application processors must be treated as untrusted #emph[proposal engines], while deterministic, zero-heap (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");) bare-metal microcontrollers hold sole #emph[permission authority] to energize actuators.
+ #strong[Canonical, Simple First-Principles Invariants:] The mathematical contracts that protect physical machines do not require baroque notations; they require clean, back-of-the-napkin SI relations---stopping distance physics, information freshness horizons, thermal capacitances, and inductive time constants---evaluated deterministically on every hardware tick.

This textbook is my attempt to share that foundational systems architecture with the next generation of engineers, roboticists, and computer scientists. It is the book I wish my own students had when bridging machine learning systems to the physical world.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Intellectual Lineage and Systems Trajectory]
<intellectual-lineage-and-systems-trajectory>
This first-principles systems trajectory spans three major eras:

- #strong[Silicon Reality & Mobile Architecture:] Grounded in computer architecture, low-power microarchitectures, and mobile System-on-Chip (SoC) design (including edge AI silicon performance architecture at Google). This work addressed the physical realities of computing: memory bus contention under high-bandwidth camera streams, thermal throttling under strict dissipated wattage envelopes, and interrupt latencies under hard real-time deadlines.
- #strong[ML Systems & The TinyML Movement:] Bridging algorithmic machine learning abstractions to silicon execution. He co-founded MLPerf to establish the global standard for benchmarking ML hardware and software, and pioneered the TinyML movement, developing the Harvard CS249r curriculum and the HarvardX TinyML program (reaching over 100,000 learners globally) to bring deep neural models onto milliwatt microcontrollers.
- #strong[Closing the Sense-and-Act Loop in Physical AI:] While TinyML enabled ultra-low-power sensing (open-loop anomaly detection) and cloud MLSys enabled high-throughput serving behind digital sandboxes, Physical AI Systems closes the loop: enabling learned foundation models to exercise delegated physical authority over matter and energy under hard real-time constraints and strict physical invariants.

