=== Cognitive Duality: System 1 and System 2
<cognitive-duality-system-1-and-system-2>
To understand why physical autonomy demands a two-brain architecture, consider how biological organisms solve the simultaneous challenges of high-level deliberate planning and split-second physical survival. In cognitive psychology, Daniel Kahneman (#cite(<kahneman2011thinking>, form: "year")) formalized the fundamental duality between two distinct modes of thought: System 2, the conscious, analytical mind that evaluates complex reasoning, plans multi-step goals, and manipulates abstract semantic concepts; and System 1, the autonomous, instinctual substrate that operates without conscious deliberation, executing continuous motor coordination and instant survival reflexes with microsecond responsiveness.

In human physiology, this cognitive division is directly mirrored in the physical nervous system. When a person reaches to pick up a coffee mug, the cerebral cortex (System 2) perceives the semantic target, estimates 3D depth, and formulates the intentional reaching trajectory over $200 upright("–") 500 upright(" ms")$. However, if your hand unexpectedly brushes against a scorching hot surface or slips on a sharp edge, the spinal reflex arc (System 1) activates in under twenty milliseconds. The motor neurons in the spinal cord immediately contract the biceps and withdraw the arm #emph[long before the sensory pain signal has propagated up the spinal tract to conscious cortical awareness]. Conscious System 2 deliberation #emph[proposes] intentional goals, but autonomic System 1 reflexes hold #emph[absolute physical protection authority].

In digital software engineering and classical robotics, systems were traditionally designed either as monolithic control loops or as flat hierarchical state machines where human-written code governed every level. Physical AI introduces a fundamentally new systems challenge: modern learned policies (such as Vision-Language-Action models and diffusion decoders) are stochastic, high-capacity neural networks running on multi-gigabyte operating systems. These models provide extraordinary semantic competence (interpreting unstructured language instructions, recognizing novel objects, and synthesizing complex multi-step trajectories). However, because they are statistical function approximators trained on finite data, their outputs under rare visual artifacts, specular reflections, or dynamic occlusions are inherently unpredictable and mathematically unverifiable. Attempting to formally prove that a billion-parameter neural network will never output an unsafe motor command is intractable.

The unique contribution of the Physical AI systems lens is resolving this dilemma not by constraining the capacity of the learned model, but by restructuring the architectural privilege boundary. We accept that high-level neural deliberation is stochastic, computationally heavy, and untrusted. We then pair it with an isolated, mathematically verifiable reflex brain that evaluates physical invariants deterministically. Conscious cognitive deliberation proposes candidate trajectories; deterministic bare-metal reflexes hold absolute physical permission authority.

=== The Two-Brain Architecture
<the-two-brain-architecture>
To realize this cognitive and physical separation in silicon, the Physical AI computing substrate is partitioned across a hardware privilege boundary into a Two-Brain Architecture (#ref(<fig-anatomy>, supplement: [Figure]) and #ref(<tbl-processor-comparison>, supplement: [Table])).

The Untrusted Proposal Engine is implemented on high-performance application microprocessors (MPUs) or neural processing units (NPUs) running general-purpose operating systems such as Linux. The proposal engine executes high-capacity foundation models, multimodal vision-language-action (VLA) models at macroscopic cognitive rates ($0.5 upright("–") 2 upright(" Hz")$) @brohan2023rt2@kim2024openvla, and diffusion or transformer action-chunk policies at intermediate rates ($20 upright("–") 50 upright(" Hz")$) @chi2023diffusionpolicy@zhao2023learning. Operating in rich virtual memory with dynamic heap allocation, this tier interprets semantic intent, maintains latent world representations, and generates candidate trajectory chunks $upright(bold(a))_(t : t + H)$. The proposal engine emits time-bounded intent leases accompanied by periodic real-time heartbeats into a shared memory interface. The proposal engine holds zero direct electrical authority over motor inverters, timer registers, or gate-driver enable pins.

The Trusted Permission Authority is implemented on dedicated real-time microcontrollers (MCUs) or isolated deterministic cores running bare-metal firmware or a hard real-time operating system (RTOS) @kopetz2011real@liu1973scheduling. The permission authority executes in zero-wait-state static SRAM with strictly zero dynamic heap allocation (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");). Operating at a deterministic $1000 upright(" Hz")$ loop cadence ($1 upright(" ms") plus.minus 5 thin mu upright("s")$ timing jitter), this tier continuously validates candidate proposals against hard physical safety invariants, kinematic limits, and dynamic stopping boundaries.

To guarantee physical safety while respecting the neural model's high-level semantic intent, the permission authority acts as an active mathematical safety shield. As long as the candidate trajectory remains safely within physical limits, the proposal passes through to the motor drivers without interference. But the instant a proposed action threatens to violate a safety invariant (such as heading toward an unmodeled obstacle or exceeding allowable joint acceleration), the permission supervisor smoothly deflects the trajectory along the safe boundary or commands an immediate deceleration.

The interaction between the two brains is governed by an #emph[expiring intent lease]#footnote[An #emph[Expiring Intent Lease] is a time-bounded spatial and velocity contract issued by the cognitive brain (e.g., "Authorized to reach for cup within corridor $cal(B)$ at $lt.eq 0.5 upright(" m/s")$ for $500 upright(" ms")$"). If not renewed before it expires, the permission authority safely halts the robot.] with a strict three-phase lifecycle:

+ #emph[Lease Issuance:] The MPU packages high-level intent into a bounded contract $cal(L)_(upright("intent")) = \( cal(B) \, v_(upright("max")) \, tau_(upright("lease")) \)$, granting authorization to operate within spatial corridor $cal(B)$ at velocity $lt.eq v_(upright("max"))$ for a finite duration $tau_(upright("lease"))$ (typically $100 upright("–") 500 upright(" ms")$).
+ #emph[Continuous Real-Time Supervision:] The MCU evaluates candidate action slices at $1000 upright(" Hz")$, verifying that each incremental step remains within $cal(B)$ and satisfies dynamic stopping invariants.
+ #emph[Passive Revocation on Expiration:] If the MPU encounters a memory compaction stall, software exception, or scheduler blackout that delays the next lease renewal beyond $tau_(upright("heartbeat")) = 20 upright(" ms")$, the active lease passively expires. The MCU immediately engages a controlled dynamic deceleration trajectory to bring the machine to Safe Torque Off (STO) without requiring any active shutdown command from the crashed host.

While the mathematical formulation of Control Barrier Functions and high-frequency current control loops is developed in Chapter 9 (#emph[Reflexes and Safety Enforcement]) @ames2019control, the architectural contract is established here: the MCU alone holds the electrical privilege to configure PWM timer registers and toggle gate-driver enable lines, and it possesses absolute authority to reshape or refuse any proposal issued by the MPU.

