Under this two-brain contract, refusal is an active, deterministic mechanism rather than an optional design choice. Safe Torque Off (STO) is an internationally certified hardware safety state (IEC 61800-5-2) @iec61800 where power to motor inverter gate drivers is physically cut, guaranteeing that the motor cannot generate rotational torque. This single timing mechanism guarantees that when an upstream artificial intelligence model crashes, hangs, or experiences an operating system memory stall, the physical hardware comes to a safe, controlled stop rather than latching open-loop power.

On representative heterogeneous platforms (such as an application processor paired with an isolated real-time microcontroller, exemplified in dual-brain hardware architectures and industrial robotics controllers), this split is directly observable on the bench. The application processor executes Linux and hosts the untrusted proposal engine, writing candidate action chunks into a shared mailbox. The microcontroller alone is physically connected to the motor timer peripherals and gate-driver enable pins. An engineer can empirically verify this privilege boundary by intentionally terminating the Linux proposal process with a #NormalTok("kill -9"); signal during active trajectory tracking: the motor timers do not latch open-loop current; instead, the microcontroller detects the heartbeat lapse within $20 upright(" ms")$ and safely de-energizes the actuators.

=== Silicon-Level Privilege Isolation
<silicon-level-privilege-isolation>
On modern heterogeneous systems-on-chip, application processor cores and real-time microcontroller cores share common silicon substrate and system interconnects. Consequently, privilege separation cannot rely on software goodwill; it must be enforced through hardware bus firewalls @hennessy2019computer.

Hardware peripheral protection units (such as ARM TrustZone controllers, peripheral protection units, and bus memory management units) physically isolate the memory-mapped I/O (MMIO) registers of advanced motor timers, PWM channels, and GPIO gate-enable pins within the microcontroller bus domain. These register addresses are completely unmapped and physically inaccessible from user-space Linux. The application processor communicates with the real-time microcontroller exclusively through a dedicated, non-cacheable shared SRAM ring buffer#footnote[A #emph[Single-Producer Single-Consumer (SPSC)] ring buffer is a lock-free queue in shared static memory where one processor writes and another reads without mutex locks or thread-blocking overhead.]. Inter-processor communication utilizes lock-free circular mailboxes synchronized with 64-bit monotonic atomic sequence counters and inter-processor interrupts (IPIs). This architecture enables high-bandwidth exchange of candidate trajectory chunks and telemetry while physically preventing the application processor from acquiring a direct bus path to the motor inverters.

