A physical agent is an entirely different engineering object. Its outputs are not text tokens or JSON payloads; they are gate-driver switching commands that draw high currents across inductive windings, accelerate inertial mass, and permanently alter the environment. In the physical domain, errors cannot be caught with a #NormalTok("try/catch"); block once kinetic momentum has been transferred to mechanical linkages.

=== The Dual Criterion of Success
<the-dual-criterion-of-success>
In digital machine learning benchmarks, performance is evaluated along a purely statistical dimension: average test accuracy, top-1 classification rate, or loss across a static dataset. In classical industrial automation, performance is evaluated along a deterministic safety dimension: satisfying hard invariant boundaries within a structured, unchanging workcell.

In Physical AI systems, success is dual, multiplicative, and non-negotiable:

$ upright("Physical AI Success") = underbrace(upright("Semantic Competence"), upright("Task achieved from sensor input")) #h(0em) times #h(0em) underbrace(upright("Physical Invariant Survival"), upright("Zero collisions or safety violations")) $

A vision-language-action model that successfully grasps a delicate target object 99 times out of 100, but shears a cycloidal gearbox or strikes an obstacle on the 100th trial, is not a 99% successful system; it is a total system failure ($1 times 0 = 0$). Physical AI systems engineering requires maximizing semantic competence while mathematically guaranteeing that physical invariant survival cannot be compromised. A naive permission authority could achieve 100% invariant survival by refusing all motion and parking the machine permanently at Safe Torque Off. The core systems challenge of Physical AI is synthesizing safe sets $cal(U)_(upright("safe"))$ that are minimally restrictive, preserving maximal behavioral freedom for the proposal engine to fulfill its semantic mission. This foundational requirement dictates how every stage of cognitive deliberation must be co-designed with physical silicon and mechanical constraints.

== The Physical Agent Co-Design Matrix
<the-physical-agent-co-design-matrix>
In conventional engineering education, robotics, machine learning, and embedded systems are taught in separate silos. Mechanical engineers analyze kinematics and rigid-body dynamics assuming ideal commanded forces; computer scientists train deep neural networks on static offline datasets assuming instantaneous computation and infinite memory bandwidth; embedded systems engineers write real-time firmware for deterministic microcontrollers assuming fixed, hand-crafted control laws. In Physical AI, these three disciplines collide directly at the Causal Boundary. High-capacity neural representations cannot interact with the material world without negotiating with physical inertia, finite electrical time constants, and shared-bus memory contention. Conversely, bare-metal microcontrollers cannot achieve open-world semantic competence without consuming candidate trajectories generated by stochastic foundation models. The #emph[Physical Agent Co-Design Matrix] serves as the master intellectual compass of this textbook, organizing the complex multi-dimensional design space by crossing physical reality against cognitive agency (#ref(<fig-codesign-matrix>, supplement: [Figure])).

