To establish a defensible physical architecture, the Loop Charter defines six non-negotiable systems parameters that must be empirically verified and locked in firmware:

+ #emph[Heartbeat and Lease Timeout Budget ($tau_(upright("heartbeat")) = 20 upright(" ms")$):] The maximum allowable time interval between consecutive host alive pulses before the real-time microcontroller invalidates the active proposal lease and assumes host failure.
+ #emph[Emergency Deceleration Trajectory toward Safe Torque Off (STO):] The deterministic braking profile executed by the microcontroller to bring moving linkages to a complete, controlled stop without exceeding mechanical stress limits upon lease expiration or hardware faults.
+ #emph[Strict Ban on Dynamic Heap Allocation in the Real-Time Loop (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");):] The architectural requirement that all microcontroller memory buffers, ring queues, and state variables reside in statically allocated SRAM, eliminating non-deterministic memory allocator latency and heap fragmentation.
+ #emph[Thermal and $I^2 t$ Continuous Current Clamp:] The firmware and hardware current thresholds that prevent resistive Joule heating from exceeding stator thermal capacitance ($C_(upright("th"))$) and damaging winding insulation ($> 180^compose upright("C")$) or rotor magnets during sustained stall conditions.
+ #emph[Hard Joint Position and Velocity Envelopes:] The physical kinematic bounds ($upright(bold(q))_(upright("min")) lt.eq upright(bold(q)) lt.eq upright(bold(q))_(upright("max"))$) and maximum joint speed limits ($parallel dot(upright(bold(q))) parallel lt.eq dot(upright(bold(q)))_(upright("max"))$) configured directly in microcontroller registers to prevent mechanisms from colliding with mechanical hardstops or exceeding rated gearbox input speeds.
+ #emph[Rejection Threshold for Stale or Out-of-Order Proposals:] The timestamp and sequence verification logic that instantly discards any proposal whose total information age ($Delta t = t_(upright("current")) - t_(upright("transduce"))$) exceeds the freshness wall ($Delta t_(upright("wall"))$) or whose sequence counter indicates transport reordering.

Freezing these six platform parameters ensures that high-level neural experimentation cannot breach the physical survival envelope of the machine. Regardless of what actions an untrusted vision-language model or diffusion policy proposes, the underlying silicon guarantees that every physical invariant remains strictly defended.

== Fallacies and Pitfalls in Physical AI
<fallacies-and-pitfalls-in-physical-ai>
When software engineers and machine learning practitioners transition to Physical AI, they frequently bring assumptions formed in the digital computing domain. In cloud computing and desktop software, computation operates within virtual sandboxes where failures are cheap, execution can be paused, and state can be rolled back. In the physical domain, these assumptions become dangerous fallacies and insidious implementation pitfalls:

#block[
- #strong[The Fallacy:] Wrapping motor command invocations in software exception blocks (#NormalTok("try/catch");) or relying on operating system process termination will safely de-energize physical motor inverters during runtime crashes.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Peripheral timer registers and pulse-width modulation (PWM) controllers operate on autonomous silicon clock trees outside the operating system's virtual memory domain. When a user-space neural process crashes, the Linux kernel reclaims memory but leaves memory-mapped I/O timer registers latched at their last written duty cycle. The inverter continues driving full stall current through stator windings until physical impact occurs or thermal runaway melts the motor. De-energizing hardware requires dedicated bus firewalls and an isolated real-time microcontroller.

] #label("callout-fallacy*-7")
#block[
- #strong[The Fallacy:] Scaling a single end-to-end neural network from raw pixels directly to motor PWM signals eliminates the need for classical real-time operating systems and control barrier supervisors.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Deep neural networks are statistical function approximators susceptible to out-of-distribution hallucinations, specular reflections, and sudden occlusions. Furthermore, general-purpose operating systems suffer multi-millisecond tail latency spikes ($P_99 > 100 upright(" ms")$). Granting an unverified model direct electrical authority over motor inverters guarantees eventual physical destruction. Deterministic safety supervisors executing at $1000 upright(" Hz")$ in zero-heap static SRAM remain non-negotiable.

] #label("callout-fallacy*-8")
#block[
- #strong[The Fallacy:] Optimizing for high average frame rate ($P_50 = 60 upright(" FPS")$) ensures that a physical AI system will operate safely and avoid dynamic collisions in the real world.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Physical dynamical systems evolve continuously along the time axis. In a $50 upright(" Hz")$ control loop executing 180,000 cycles per hour, closed-loop stability is governed strictly by worst-case tail percentiles ($P_99$ and $P_99.9$). A single $250 upright(" ms")$ scheduling blackout while traveling at $2.0 upright(" m/s")$ forces half a meter of completely blind projectile motion, turning a safe path into an unavoidable collision. Systems must be qualified against extreme latency tails.

] #label("callout-fallacy*-9")
#block[
- #strong[The Fallacy:] A policy that successfully avoids collisions and completes manipulation tasks in a rigid-body physics simulator is ready for deployment on real physical hardware.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Simulators routinely abstract away stator winding inductance ($tau_e = L \/ R$), gearbox backlash, structural compliance, shared-DRAM memory bus contention, and irreversible Joule heating ($I^2 R$) @tobin2017domain@peng2018sim@kumar2021rma@hwangbo2019learning. On physical hardware, unmodeled phase lags and discontinuous torque commands generate severe mechanical jerk, excite chassis resonances, chip gearbox teeth, and trigger thermal current clamps.

] #label("callout-fallacy*-10")
== Summary
<summary>
This chapter crossed the foundational threshold from digital computing into Physical AI. In the digital domain, software operates behind glass: bits can be reset, transactions rolled back, and exceptions caught without physical consequence. In Physical AI, software commands energy flows that accelerate mass, dissipate heat, and permanently mutate the environment ($W_t arrow.r W_(t + 1)$). Because every actuation causally reshapes all subsequent sensory observations ($a_t arrow.r u \( t \) arrow.r W_(t + 1) arrow.r o_(t + 1)$), errors cannot be resolved through digital debugging; they must be prevented through rigorous systems co-design.

To reconcile high-capacity stochastic neural deliberation with deterministic real-time safety, physical AI architectures enforce the Proposal and Permission Privilege Split, reserving exclusive electrical motor authority for dedicated, zero-allocation microcontrollers running mathematical safety barriers. The 5x5 Co-Design Matrix organizes the complete engineering discipline, while the Loop Charter establishes the immutable baseline for hardware realization.

In Chapter 2 (#emph[The Physical Constraints: Time, Inertia, Actuation, Energy, Silicon]), we attach physical instruments to every stage of the sense-to-actuation pipeline. We trace the complete latency waterfall (from the arrival of photons at an image sensor through direct memory access transfers, neural network matrix multiplications, inter-processor mailboxes, and inductive current rise times in motor windings), quantifying the precise microsecond budgets and freshness boundaries that dictate physical survival.

