Every embodied intelligent machine, whether an autonomous mobile robot navigating a warehouse (Archetype 1: Mobility), a six-axis arm assembling precision electronics (Archetype 2: Contact Manipulation), or a megawatt battery inverter stabilizing an electrical grid (Archetype 3: Cybernetic Processes), operates within an operating envelope bounded by these five physical columns:

+ #strong[Time] governs information freshness and latency tails, establishing how quickly sensory observations age and determining the geometric Freshness Wall where internal belief diverges from physical reality (#ref(<sec-sense-to-actuation>, supplement: [Section])).
+ #strong[Inertia] dictates momentum and dynamic stopping kinematics, proving why moving mass cannot halt instantaneously and how milliseconds of computational delay translate directly into physical meters of unguided collision hazard (#ref(<sec-inertia-stopping>, supplement: [Section])).
+ #strong[Actuation] bounds electromagnetic torque rise time through stator coil inductance and enforces differential jerk continuity to protect gear teeth and transmissions from mechanical shock shear (#ref(<sec-actuation-jerk>, supplement: [Section])).
+ #strong[Energy] tracks irreversible resistive Joule heating in motor coils and semiconductor power switches, budgeting thermal accumulation to avoid destructive insulation breakdown and silicon thermal throttling (#ref(<sec-energy-thermal>, supplement: [Section])).
+ #strong[Compute] addresses physical memory crossbar contention, signal propagation across wires, and execution determinism, mandating predictable memory bus arbitration, lock-free static SRAM mailboxes, and the Iron Law of Zero Dynamic Heap Allocation in real-time safety paths (#ref(<sec-compute-determinism>, supplement: [Section])).

These five physical dimensions form an interdependent physical chain. When high-level neural software proposes an aggressive motion, Time dictates whether sensory observations remain fresh enough to justify the action; Inertia checks whether the machine has sufficient clearance to stop if an obstacle appears; Actuation verifies that motor coils and gearboxes can deliver the required torque without tooth shear; Energy confirms that copper windings and power stages will not overheat; and Compute guarantees that the underlying compute fabric executes with microsecond determinism. Together, these constraints compile directly into the formal Physical Operating Specification (#ref(<sec-physical-spec>, supplement: [Section])) that freezes physical invariants prior to deployment. We now examine each physical constraint in depth, deriving its governing mathematical laws and observing its consequences on real hardware benchtop systems.

== Time and Information Freshness
<sec-sense-to-actuation>
In Physical AI, time is not an abstract software counter; it is an irreversible physical coordinate. While an onboard neural network deliberates, the physical universe continues evolving along continuous differential equations ($dot(upright(bold(x))) \( t \) = upright(bold(f)) \( upright(bold(x)) \( t \) \, upright(bold(u)) \( t \) \)$), moving masses coast under momentum, and sensory observations age from the microsecond photons strike the camera sensor.

=== The Physical Nature of Time and the Sense-to-Actuation Chain
<the-physical-nature-of-time-and-the-sense-to-actuation-chain>
Digital software operates as a discrete state machine: an instruction executes, updates registers, and halts until the next clock tick. If a cloud model experiences an operating system scheduling pause or memory garbage collection stall, the digital world patiently waits behind a loading spinner. In Physical AI, however, the physical universe serves as the unyielding master clock. Deliberation time is paid for in physical space: at a modest speed of $1.5 upright(" m/s")$, every $100 upright(" ms")$ of computation delay displaces moving mass by $15 upright(" cm")$ before any corrective force can even begin.

When a sensor captures an observation at historical instant $t_(upright("obs"))$, that observation reflects past reality. We define the #strong[Age of Information] ($upright("AoI")$) as the total elapsed time from physical sensor exposure ($t_(upright("obs"))$) to the physical application of torque at the actuator output ($t_(upright("act"))$):

$ upright("AoI") \( t_(upright("act")) \) = t_(upright("act")) - t_(upright("obs")) = tau_(upright("trans")) + tau_(upright("dma")) + tau_(upright("perceive")) + tau_(upright("infer")) + tau_(upright("ipc")) + tau_(upright("enforce")) + tau_(upright("actuator")) $

When instrumented at the hardware level, this causal sense-to-actuation pipeline traces a continuous path from physical photons to electromagnetic torque (#ref(<fig-latency-waterfall>, supplement: [Figure])). The journey begins in the physical domain with optical transduction ($tau_(upright("trans"))$), where photons striking photodiode wells require 8 to 15 ms of charge accumulation. Once digitized, frames are serialized across Direct Memory Access channels into DRAM ($tau_(upright("dma"))$), where the vision backbone and deliberative neural policy extract spatial features and decode candidate trajectories ($tau_(upright("perceive")) + tau_(upright("infer"))$). The candidate plan is then dispatched across inter-processor mailboxes ($tau_(upright("ipc"))$) to a deterministic 1000 Hz safety supervisor running in static SRAM ($tau_(upright("enforce"))$), which validates physical invariants before gating current into motor inverters. Finally, the signal returns to the physical domain, where stator coil inductance requires three electrical time constants ($3 tau_e = 3 L \/ R approx 6 upright("–") 15 upright(" ms")$) for the magnetic field to build commanded torque ($tau_(upright("actuator"))$).

