As illustrated in #ref(<fig-stopping-distance>, supplement: [Figure]), the total dynamic stopping distance ($d_(upright("stop"))$)---measured from the physical instant an obstacle appears until the machine comes to a complete rest---decomposes into two distinct physical phases:

$ underbrace(d_(upright("stop")) \( v_0 \, Delta t_(upright("delay")) \), upright("Total Stopping Distance")) = underbrace(v_0 dot.op Delta t_(upright("delay")), upright("Phase 1: Linear Reaction Drift")) #h(0em) + #h(0em) underbrace(frac(v_0^2, 2 a_(upright("max"))), upright("Phase 2: Quadratic Kinetic Braking")) $

During Phase 1 (#strong[Linear Reaction Drift], $v_0 dot.op Delta t_(upright("delay"))$), the system is traversing its end-to-end sense-to-actuation pipeline ($Delta t_(upright("delay"))$). Because the neural policy and safety layers are still computing, no opposing force has yet reached the physical actuators. By Newton's First Law, the center of mass coasts forward unguided at its initial velocity $v_0$. This exposes the fundamental cyber-physical bridge: #emph[computational delay translates directly into linear unguided projectile travel through physical space].

Once braking torque physically builds in the actuators, Phase 2 (#strong[Quadratic Kinetic Braking], $frac(v_0^2, 2 a_(upright("max")))$) engages. The machine decelerates at maximum available deceleration $a_(upright("max"))$, dissipating its kinetic energy across a quadratic braking distance. Physical braking onsets are further bounded by finite jerk rise times ($Delta t_(upright("jerk")) = a_(upright("max")) \/ j_(upright("max"))$), governed by the electromagnetic coil rise times and transmission shear limits derived in #ref(<sec-actuation-jerk>, supplement: [Section]).

For example, an autonomous mobile platform operating in a warehouse with initial velocity $v_0 = 1.0 upright(" m/s")$, maximum braking deceleration $a_(upright("max")) = 2.0 upright(" m/s")^2$, and nominal latency ($P_50 = 30 upright(" ms")$) exhibits a reaction drift of $3 upright(" cm")$ and kinetic braking distance of $25 upright(" cm")$, yielding a total stopping distance of $d_(upright("stop")) = 28 upright(" cm")$. But if memory bus contention or thread preemption causes a tail latency spike to $P_99.9 = 230 upright(" ms")$, reaction drift expands from $3 upright(" cm")$ to $23 upright(" cm")$. Total stopping distance explodes to $48 upright(" cm")$, breaching a $35 upright(" cm")$ clearance envelope and demonstrating how a $200 upright(" ms")$ compute stall adds $20 upright(" cm")$ of unguided physical travel.

=== Dynamic Safety Clearance Invariants and the Speed Governor
<dynamic-safety-clearance-invariants-and-the-speed-governor>
Because computational delay expands stopping distance linearly, obstacle clearance thresholds must never be hardcoded as static geometric constants (such as maintaining an arbitrary "30 cm buffer"). Instead, safe physical agency requires enforcing two dual physical invariants that link measured latency directly to actuation authority.

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Invariant 1: The Dynamic Clearance Safety Invariant]
<invariant-1-the-dynamic-clearance-safety-invariant>
To guarantee collision-free operation under worst-case tail execution, the available workspace clearance $d_(upright("clearance"))$ must satisfy the scalar safety set condition:

$ h \( upright(bold(x)) \) = d_(upright("clearance")) - (d_(upright("stop")) (v_0 \, Delta t \( P_99.9 \)) + d_(upright("margin"))) gt.eq 0 $

where $d_(upright("margin"))$ is an empirical safety buffer accounting for surface friction variability and chassis geometry. In Chapter 9, we formalize $h \( upright(bold(x)) \)$ as a Control Barrier Function @ames2019control and synthesize deterministic 1 kHz Quadratic Programs that maintain this invariant on real-time microcontrollers without overly conservative trajectory clipping.

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Invariant 2: The Closed-Form Dynamic Speed Governor]
<invariant-2-the-closed-form-dynamic-speed-governor>
By setting $d_(upright("stop")) \( v_(upright("safe")) \, Delta t \) = d_(upright("clearance")) - d_(upright("margin"))$ and solving the quadratic stopping equation for velocity, we obtain the algebraic dual of stopping kinematics---the #strong[Dynamic Speed Governor]:

$ v_(upright("safe")) (d_(upright("clearance")) \, Delta t_(upright("delay"))) = sqrt(\( a_(upright("max")) Delta t_(upright("delay")) \)^2 + 2 a_(upright("max")) (d_(upright("clearance")) - d_(upright("margin")))) - a_(upright("max")) Delta t_(upright("delay")) $

The Speed Governor proves that the maximum permissible operating speed of any autonomous physical machine is explicitly parameterized by its worst-case computational tail latency $Delta t \( P_99.9 \)$. If memory bus contention, thermal throttling, or complex neural beam search inflates tail latency, the machine must automatically derate its operating velocity to guarantee that its physical stopping envelope remains within available clearance.

In real-world deployment, this invariant is implemented as an online velocity clamp in the low-level motion controller. Before dispatching a commanded forward velocity $v_(upright("cmd"))$ to joint inverters, the real-time safety layer queries the current measured obstacle clearance $d_(upright("clearance"))$ and the verified $P_99.9$ tail latency budget, clamping velocity according to:

$ v_(upright("cmd")) arrow.l min (v_(upright("cmd")) \, #h(0em) v_(upright("safe")) \( d_(upright("clearance")) \, Delta t_(P 99.9) \)) $

When the robot enters a constrained passageway or when onboard compute resources experience temporary contention spikes, the speed governor automatically and continuously down-throttles physical velocity, preserving the mathematical guarantee that the machine can always halt before breaching the obstacle boundary.

== Actuation and Mechanical Limits
<sec-actuation-jerk>
Arresting moving mass or tracking a planned trajectory requires applying physical forces through electromechanical actuators: electric motors, power electronics, and mechanical gearboxes. While simulators often treat joint torque $bold(tau)$ as an instantaneous algebraic variable asserted on the next timestep ($bold(tau) \( t \) = bold(tau)_(upright("cmd"))$), real physical actuators are electromagnetic coils and geared transmissions governed by two immutable limits: #strong[Electromagnetic Torque Rise Time] and #strong[Mechanical Jerk Continuity].

=== Stator Inductance and Electrical Rise Time
<stator-inductance-and-electrical-rise-time>
Electric motors generate physical torque through Lorentz forces produced by electric current flowing through stator wire windings ($bold(tau) = K_t upright(bold(I))$). In power electronics, an inductor acts like an #emph[electrical flywheel]: it physically opposes sudden changes in current. Just as you cannot instantly change the rotational velocity of a heavy mechanical flywheel without applying infinite torque, an inverter cannot instantly change current in a wire coil without applying infinite voltage.

The voltage-current dynamics of a motor phase winding follow Faraday's Law of Induction:

$ V \( t \) = R dot.op I \( t \) + L frac(d I \( t \), d t) + K_e dot.op omega \( t \) $

where $V \( t \)$ is the applied inverter phase voltage, $R$ is winding resistance, $L$ is stator coil inductance, $I \( t \)$ is phase current, $K_e$ is the back-electromotive force (back-EMF) constant, and $omega \( t \)$ is rotor angular velocity.

Because torque is proportional to current, commanding a rapid change in torque requires driving a rapid change in current ($frac(d I, d t)$). However, coil inductance $L$ actively induces an opposing counter-voltage that slows current growth. Together, stator inductance and resistance establish the #strong[electrical time constant] $tau_e = L \/ R$. When the inverter applies a step voltage, current rises exponentially ($I \( t \) prop 1 - e^(- t \/ tau_e)$), requiring approximately three electrical time constants ($3 tau_e$) for the magnetic field to build $95 %$ of target torque. For a typical robotic joint actuator with winding resistance $R = 0.60 thin Omega$ and stator inductance $L = 1.8 upright(" mH")$, the electrical time constant is $tau_e = 3.0 upright(" ms")$, requiring $3 tau_e = 9.0 upright(" ms")$ of physical time before commanded torque can physically materialize in the air gap.

Furthermore, as rotor velocity $omega \( t \)$ increases, the spinning permanent magnets generate an opposing back-EMF voltage ($K_e omega$) that subtracts directly from the DC supply voltage. This collapses the available voltage headroom ($Delta V = V_(upright("bus")) - K_e omega$), severely choking the rate of current growth at high speeds. Consequently, the maximum rate of torque generation is bounded by a hardware ceiling:

$ parallel dot(bold(tau)) \( t \) parallel lt.eq dot(tau)_(upright("max")) \( omega \) = frac(K_t (V_(upright("bus")) - K_e omega), L) $

If an upstream motion planner commands an instantaneous step jump in torque, the current controller demands an infinite voltage slew rate ($frac(d V, d t) arrow.r oo$), which saturates power inverter gate drivers and collapses the DC supply bus. To prevent control instability, the torque slew rate limit $dot(tau)_(upright("max")) \( omega \)$ must be frozen into the Physical Operating Specification (#ref(<tbl-physical-spec>, supplement: [Table])) as an immutable hardware boundary.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] In simulation and reinforcement learning, commanded actuator torque $bold(tau)_(upright("cmd"))$ is treated as an algebraic variable instantly asserted on physical joints at the next discrete timestep ($bold(tau) \( t \) = bold(tau)_(upright("cmd")) \( t \)$).
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Actuators are electromagnetic inductors governed by Faraday's Law ($V = I R + L frac(d I, d t) + K_e omega$). Because magnetic field energy ($1 / 2 L I^2$) cannot transition instantaneously, motor torque exhibits an exponential rise time governed by $tau_e = L \/ R$, requiring $3 tau_e$ ($6 upright(" to ") 15 upright(" ms")$) to deliver 95% of target torque. Attempting a step change in torque demands an infinite voltage slew rate ($frac(d V, d t) arrow.r oo$), which saturates power inverter gate drivers and collapses the DC supply bus. When un-smoothed discrete torque setpoints pass into mechanical reducers (harmonic drives or planetary gearheads), the resulting impulsive jerk ($accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) arrow.r oo$) exceeds the shear yield limit ($sigma_(upright("yield"))$) of gear teeth, inducing micro-fractures, tooth pitting, and catastrophic gear shear.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Trajectory generation and low-level control loops must enforce $cal(C)^2$ differential jerk continuity ($parallel accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) \( t \) parallel lt.eq accent(q, ⃛)_(upright("max"))$) with torque slew rates strictly clamped to electrical rise limits and mechanical transmission shear bounds: $parallel dot(bold(tau)) \( t \) parallel lt.eq W_p sigma_(upright("yield")) \/ Delta t_(upright("rise"))$, where $W_p$ is the polar section modulus of the transmission shaft or gear tooth.

