=== Transmissions, Reflected Rotor Inertia, and Jerk Continuity
<transmissions-reflected-rotor-inertia-and-jerk-continuity>
Even if the motor's electrical coils could deliver rapid torque, the mechanical transmission connecting the motor to the robot limb imposes a hard structural limit on how quickly acceleration can change.

Robotic actuators use high-ratio speed reducers---such as strain-wave harmonic drives, cycloidal drives, and planetary gearheads---to amplify motor torque by gear ratio $N in \[ 50 \, 160 \]$. However, gear reducers do not merely scale torque; they amplify the motor rotor's rotational inertia seen at the output joint by #strong[$N^2$]:

$ J_(upright("joint, eff")) = J_(upright("link")) + N^2 J_(upright("rotor")) $

When a discrete neural policy emits piecewise-constant trajectory setpoints at discrete intervals (such as $20 upright(" Hz")$ or $50 upright(" Hz")$), commanded joint acceleration jumps discontinuously across timesteps. In physical calculus, a step change in acceleration ($Delta dot.double(upright(bold(q)))$) across an infinitesimal timestep implies unbounded #strong[mechanical jerk] ($upright(bold(j)) \( t \)$), the third time-derivative of position:

$ upright(bold(j)) \( t \) = accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) \( t \) = frac(d^3 upright(bold(q)), d t^3) arrow.r oo $

Because the high-speed motor rotor must undergo an angular acceleration step of $N Delta dot.double(upright(bold(q)))$, the inertial shock torque transmitted through the gearbox teeth scales with reflected inertia:

$ tau_(upright("shock")) = N^2 J_(upright("rotor")) Delta dot.double(upright(bold(q))) $

Due to the $N^2$ scaling factor, even a lightweight rotor generates massive dynamic shock loads under step acceleration commands. This triggers a destructive mechanical failure cascade. First, transmission components experience peak shear stress ($tau_(upright("shear")) = T \/ W_p$, where $W_p$ is the polar section modulus of the gear tooth or flexspline cup). Impulsive jerk spikes easily exceed the material shear yield strength ($tau_(upright("yield"))$), causing tooth pitting, fatigue micro-fractures, and catastrophic tooth stripping. Second, impulsive shock waves excite unmodeled high-frequency mechanical resonance modes ($1 upright("–") 2 upright(" kHz")$) in the robot's physical linkages. Third, these mechanical vibration transients propagate directly into the chassis, saturating MEMS accelerometers and gyroscopes and corrupting the state estimation pipeline.

To protect mechanical hardware from impulsive shock loads, autonomy architectures must enforce #strong[$cal(C)^2$ differential jerk continuity] ($parallel accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) \( t \) parallel lt.eq accent(q, ⃛)_(upright("max"))$). The planning layer must synthesize kinodynamically smooth trajectory splines across time horizons (as formulated in Chapter 8), while the real-time microcontroller reflex layer enforces microsecond torque slew rate and current clamping (as detailed in Chapter 9). Without these guarantees, an aggressive direction reversal stepping commanded joint acceleration from $+ 12 upright(" rad/s")^2$ to $- 12 upright(" rad/s")^2$ across a single $10 upright(" ms")$ interpolator tick produces an impulsive jerk spike exceeding $2400 upright(" rad/s")^3$. Transmitted through a $100 : 1$ harmonic gearhead, the reflected rotor inertia generates shock torques that can easily shear the gear teeth and permanently destroy the joint actuator.

== Energy and Thermal Limits
<sec-energy-thermal>
To generate peak torques and accelerate heavy masses, motor inverters must drive high electrical currents through stator windings. Because physical conductors possess electrical resistance, current generates irreversible Joule heat ($P_(upright("loss")) = I^2 R$). While software engineers often assume that a $90 %$ battery state-of-charge guarantees unlimited operational headroom, hardware destruction is governed by localized thermal mass ($C_(upright("th"))$) and thermal dissipation resistance ($R_(upright("th"))$).

=== Thermal Time Constants and Continuous Ratings
<thermal-time-constants-and-continuous-ratings>
An electric motor functions thermodynamically like a miniature oven wrapped in insulation. Heat is generated almost instantaneously deep within copper wire windings whenever current flows ($P_(upright("loss")) = I^2 R$). However, conducting that heat out through iron stator teeth and aluminum motor housings into ambient air is a sluggish process governed by the motor's #strong[thermal time constant] $tau_(upright("th")) = R_(upright("th")) C_(upright("th"))$, where $C_(upright("th"))$ is thermal heat capacity and $R_(upright("th"))$ is thermal dissipation resistance.

Embodied AI systems operate across a #strong[triple time-constant hierarchy] spanning five orders of magnitude: electrical dynamics ($tau_e = L \/ R approx 10^(- 3) upright(" s")$) operate orders of magnitude faster than mechanical motion ($tau_m = J \/ b approx 10^(- 1) upright(" s")$), which in turn operates orders of magnitude faster than thermal accumulation ($tau_(upright("th")) = R_(upright("th")) C_(upright("th")) approx 10^1 upright("–") 10^3 upright(" s")$). Because current changes ten thousand times faster than temperature, closed-loop controllers cannot rely on sluggish external temperature sensors for real-time protection; internal coil temperatures can spike to destructive levels during sudden peak-torque maneuvers long before external chassis sensors detect a change.

In steady-state equilibrium, continuous heat generation balances ambient dissipation ($I_(upright("cont"))^2 R = Delta T_(upright("max")) \/ R_(upright("th"))$), establishing the #strong[continuous current rating] ($I_(upright("cont"))$):

$ I_(upright("cont")) = sqrt(frac(Delta T_(upright("max")), R dot.op R_(upright("th")))) $

For short acceleration bursts ($Delta t_(upright("burst")) lt.double tau_(upright("th"))$), actuators operate in the near-adiabatic regime where heat dissipation to the environment is negligible. Total thermal energy absorbed by the coil mass must not exceed the Joule heat generated by peak current $I_(upright("peak"))$, yielding the #strong[Quadratic Burst Scaling Law]:

$ Delta t_(upright("burst, max")) lt.eq tau_(upright("th")) (I_(upright("cont")) / I_(upright("peak")))^2 $

This quadratic relationship exposes an unforgiving physical trade-off: #strong[doubling peak torque demands four times the Joule heat ($I^2$), reducing the permissible burst duration to one-fourth ($25 %$) of the safe window].

Consider a robotic joint motor with winding resistance $R = 0.50 thin Omega$, thermal resistance $R_(upright("th")) = 2.0 upright(" K/W")$, and allowable temperature rise $Delta T_(upright("max")) = 80 upright(" K")$, yielding a continuous rating $I_(upright("cont")) approx 8.94 upright(" A")$. If a dynamic leaping or lifting policy commands peak current $I_(upright("peak")) = 20.0 upright(" A")$ ($2.24 times I_(upright("cont"))$) under thermal time constant $tau_(upright("th")) = 60 upright(" s")$, the maximum safe burst duration is only $Delta t_(upright("burst, max")) lt.eq 60 times \( 8.94 \/ 20.0 \)^2 approx 12.0 upright(" s")$.

Sustaining peak current beyond twelve seconds causes pyrolytic breakdown of wire dielectric enamel ($> 180^compose upright("C")$)#footnote[Standard industrial motor windings adhere to NEMA and IEC 60085 thermal classification standards, where Class F is rated for continuous operation up to $155^compose upright("C")$ and Class H is rated up to $180^compose upright("C")$. Exceeding these thermal thresholds causes pyrolytic breakdown of the wire dielectric enamel, creating inter-turn electrical short circuits.] or irreversible rotor magnet demagnetization ($> 100^compose upright("C")$). Furthermore, copper has a positive temperature coefficient of resistance ($alpha_(upright("Cu")) approx + 0.393 % \/ upright("K")$). As windings heat from room temperature to $180^compose upright("C")$, resistance increases by $+ 63 %$ ($R \( T \) = R_0 \[ 1 + alpha_(upright("Cu")) \( T - T_0 \) \]$), non-linearly accelerating Joule losses in a dangerous thermal positive feedback loop.

Continuous holding torque is therefore strictly bounded by thermodynamics, not battery state-of-charge. High-level planners must explicitly budget thermal duty cycles, alternating high-load actions with low-torque rest phases.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] If a robotic platform has 90% battery state-of-charge remaining and the instantaneous power draw is within the nominal battery pack discharge envelope, high-load maneuvers and stall torques can be sustained without immediate risk.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Battery state-of-charge tracks total chemical energy capacity ($integral P thin d t$), whereas thermal destruction in actuators and silicon is governed by localized thermal mass ($C_(upright("th"))$) and thermal resistance ($R_(upright("th"))$). Resistive Joule losses generate heat as the square of phase current ($P_(upright("loss")) = I^2 R$). Because the thermal time constant ($tau_(upright("th")) = R_(upright("th")) C_(upright("th")) approx 30 upright("–") 120 upright(" s")$) is orders of magnitude slower than electrical dynamics, high peak currents ($I_(upright("peak")) = 3 upright("–") 5 times I_(upright("cont"))$) rapidly overheat stator windings. Safe burst durations decay quadratically: $Delta t_(upright("burst, max")) lt.eq tau_(upright("th")) \( I_(upright("cont")) \/ I_(upright("peak")) \)^2$. A robot with 95% battery will suffer pyrolytic enamel melting ($> 180^compose upright("C")$) or permanent NdFeB magnet demagnetization ($> 100^compose upright("C")$) within 10 to 15 seconds of sustained heavy stall torque.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Actuation authority must be governed by a real-time thermal observer and firmware $I^2 t$ energy accumulator ($E_(upright("thermal")) \[ k \] = max \( 0 \, E_(upright("thermal")) \[ k - 1 \] + \( I^2 \[ k \] - I_(upright("cont"))^2 \) Delta t \)$) that clamps current to $I_(upright("cont"))$ before thermal thresholds ($E_(upright("thermal, max")) = tau_(upright("th")) I_(upright("cont"))^2 = C_(upright("th")) Delta T_(upright("max")) \/ R$) are breached, irrespective of battery charge level.

] #label("callout-fallacy*-14")
=== Firmware $I^2 t$ Thermal Clamping and Silicon Derating
<firmware-i2t-thermal-clamping-and-silicon-derating>
Because physical thermistors mounted on motor casings exhibit a $5 upright("–") 30 upright(" s")$ spatial conduction lag through the motor housing, the real-time microcontroller cannot wait for thermistor readings during sudden stall events; by the time the external casing warms up, internal copper windings are already melting.

To protect hardware in real time, the firmware maintains an #strong[$I^2 t$ thermal energy accumulator] in static SRAM evaluated on every 1 kHz tick:

$ E_(upright("thermal")) \[ k \] = max (0 \, #h(0em) E_(upright("thermal")) \[ k - 1 \] + (I^2 \[ k \] - I_(upright("cont"))^2) Delta t) $

When $E_(upright("thermal"))$ crosses the safe capacity threshold ($E_(upright("thermal, max")) = tau_(upright("th")) I_(upright("cont"))^2 = frac(C_(upright("th")) Delta T_(upright("max")), R)$ in $upright("A")^2 dot.op upright("s")$), the microcontroller autonomously clamps motor current down to $I_(upright("cont"))$ before wire insulation degrades.

Thermal accumulation also governs the embedded edge silicon executing neural models ($P_(upright("SoC")) prop V_(upright("core"))^2 f_(upright("clk")) + I_(upright("leak")) V_(upright("core"))$). When silicon junction temperatures exceed safe thermal thresholds ($85 upright("–") 95^compose upright("C")$), on-die Dynamic Thermal Management automatically triggers Dynamic Voltage and Frequency Scaling (DVFS), cutting processor clock frequencies to prevent thermal runaway.

In enclosed robot chassis without active fan cooling, thermal accumulation within the enclosure pushes junction temperatures beyond safe limits ($85 upright("–") 95^compose upright("C")$). When Dynamic Thermal Management cuts the neural accelerator clock frequency by half, inference latency inflates from a benchtop median of $22 upright(" ms")$ to over $48 upright(" ms")$ ($P_50$) and $120 upright(" ms")$ ($P_99$). The neural policy still produces mathematically valid trajectories, but they arrive after the intent lease has expired, prompting the real-time safety supervisor to reject the stale proposals and trigger emergency braking. Autonomy architectures must account for this #strong[Cold Bench Fallacy]: intent leases and execution timeouts must be co-designed with thermal derating to prevent false-positive safety aborts.

As summarized in #ref(<tbl-thermal-domains>, supplement: [Table]), thermal dynamics and failure thresholds govern both mechanical actuation components and embedded edge silicon across distinct physical time scales.

