== Compute and Determinism
<sec-compute-determinism>
Just as physical motors and power stages are bounded by thermodynamics and inductive current rise times, the computing engine itself is an embodied physical machine. In Physical AI, computation is not an abstract mathematical operation executing in zero time on an idealized Turing machine; it is an embodied physical process governed by the speed of light in dielectric substrates, the parasitic resistance and capacitance of physical wires, and the geometry of the memory hierarchy.

From a computer architect's perspective, all hardware organization---from multi-level caches to heterogeneous interconnects---is a structural response to physical wire limits. To build deterministic computing engines for embodied intelligence, engineers must understand computing from the #strong[wire level to the system level] across four foundational principles: #strong[Wire-Level Parasitics and Data Movement Energy], #strong[The Principle of Locality and the Memory Hierarchy], #strong[Parallelism and Shared Interconnect Contention], and #strong[Spatial Memory Search vs.~Temporal Determinism].

=== Wire-Level Realities: Parasitics, Propagation Delays, and the Energy of Moving Data
<wire-level-realities-parasitics-propagation-delays-and-the-energy-of-moving-data>
Software abstractions treat electrical wires as instantaneous, zero-cost conduits. On real silicon and printed circuit boards (PCBs), a physical wire is a distributed transmission line with parasitic resistance ($R$) and capacitance ($C$).

Signal propagation speed through silicon interconnect metal layers and PCB copper traces is bounded by Maxwell's equations and the dielectric constant ($epsilon.alt_r$) of the insulating substrate ($v_(upright("prop")) = c \/ sqrt(epsilon.alt_r) approx 15 upright(" cm/ns")$, or approximately $67 upright(" picoseconds")$ per centimeter). In a modern processor operating at a $2 upright(" GHz")$ clock frequency, a single clock cycle spans just $0.5 upright(" nanoseconds")$ ($500 upright(" ps")$). In that single clock cycle, an electromagnetic wave in copper travels barely $7.5 upright(" cm")$.

Furthermore, charging and discharging the parasitic capacitance of physical conductors ($E = 1 / 2 C V^2$) dissipates real energy:

- Executing a 32-bit arithmetic floating-point operation within local ALU registers consumes approximately $1 upright("–") 5 upright(" pJ")$.
- Traversing PCB traces to fetch a 32-bit word from off-chip dynamic RAM (DRAM) consumes $1000 upright("–") 2000 upright(" pJ")$.

Shuttling data across physical distance consumes #strong[one thousand times more physical energy] than performing arithmetic on that data. Compute is thermodynamically cheap; data movement across physical distance is slow, power-hungry, and generates the heat that forces silicon thermal downclocking.

=== The Principle of Locality: Geometry of the Memory Hierarchy
<the-principle-of-locality-geometry-of-the-memory-hierarchy>
To mitigate the physical latency and energy penalties of long wires, computer architects organize storage around the #strong[Principle of Locality]: keeping data physically close in space (spatial locality) and reusing it quickly in time (temporal locality). The memory hierarchy is an inescapable geometric consequence of Euclidean distance and the finite speed of light:

- #strong[On-Chip Registers ($< 0.05 upright(" mm")$ distance):] Located immediately adjacent to arithmetic units, register flight times are sub-picosecond, delivering single-cycle access ($< 0.5 upright(" ns")$).
- #strong[On-Chip Static SRAM ($1 upright(" to ") 5 upright(" mm")$ distance):] Integrated directly on the silicon die, static RAM uses six-transistor cells ($6 upright("T")$) to deliver deterministic, single-digit nanosecond latency ($1 upright(" to ") 3 upright(" ns")$). However, $6 upright("T")$ cells require large silicon area ($> 100 thin F^2$), physically capping on-die capacity to tens of megabytes ($< 32 upright("–") 64 upright(" MB")$).
- #strong[Off-Chip Dynamic DRAM ($50 upright(" to ") 100 upright(" mm")$ distance):] Dynamic RAM packs charge into a dense single-transistor trench capacitor ($1 upright("T-1C")$, cell area $approx 6 thin F^2$), providing multi-gigabyte capacity ($> 16 upright(" GB")$). But accessing DRAM requires traversing package pins, solder balls, and PCB traces, demanding $50 upright(" to ") 100 upright(" ns")$ per transaction---a #strong[$100 times$ physical latency penalty] compared to on-chip SRAM.

This physical trade-off creates two direct implications for Physical AI:

+ #strong[Reflex Locality:] Hard real-time safety supervisors must execute strictly within on-chip static SRAM within millimeters of the core. Routing a $1000 upright(" Hz")$ reflex loop across centimeters of PCB copper to off-chip DRAM injects a $100 times$ latency penalty and exposes safety checks to off-chip bus contention.
+ #strong[The Memory-Bandwidth Wall:] High-capacity vision-language-action (VLA) foundation models require gigabytes of parameter weights, far exceeding on-chip SRAM capacity. Because edge neural policies evaluate at real-time batch size 1 with low arithmetic operational intensity ($upright("FLOPs/Byte")$), neural inference is fundamentally #strong[memory-bandwidth bound] ($B_(upright("mem"))$ in Bytes/s) rather than compute bound ($upright("FLOP/s")$): $ t_(upright("infer")) gt.eq frac(upright("Parameter Footprint (Bytes)"), B_(upright("mem")) \( upright("Bytes/s") \)) $

=== Parallelism and the Contention Hazard: Shared Interconnects
<parallelism-and-the-contention-hazard-shared-interconnects>
Embodied systems exploit hardware parallelism by executing multiple specialized processing engines concurrently: camera image signal processors, Direct Memory Access (DMA) ingestion channels, neural network accelerators, general-purpose CPU cores, and real-time microcontroller safety supervisors.

On modern edge System-on-Chips (SoCs), these parallel engines share a common physical dynamic memory subsystem across a single on-die crossbar interconnect. When parallel streams execute concurrently, they compete for the exact same physical wires:

- High-bandwidth perception sensors (such as multi-camera stereo rigs streaming at $60 upright(" FPS")$) continuously blast between $0.75 upright(" and ") 1.5 upright(" GB/s")$ of uncompressed pixel DMA write bursts into DRAM.
- Concurrently, the neural accelerator streams tens of gigabytes of model weights per second ($20 upright("–") 50 upright(" GB/s")$) from DRAM to evaluate trajectory actions.

When these asynchronous streams collide, memory controller queues saturate. Read transactions stall behind queued DMA write bursts, slashing effective bus efficiency by $40 upright(" to ") 60 %$. NPU compute pipelines suffer extensive stall cycles, inflating neural inference latency from a nominal median of $P_50 = 22 upright(" ms")$ to severe tail spikes exceeding $P_99 > 135 upright(" ms")$.

In an embodied machine, unmanaged parallelism creates interconnect contention that directly converts into physical collision hazard by breaching the Freshness Wall ($tau_(upright("world"))$).

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] On modern edge System-on-Chips (SoCs), connecting CPU, GPU, NPU, and camera DMA engines to a shared physical DRAM bus allows zero-copy data sharing without PCIe latency, guaranteeing high-throughput and deterministic neural execution.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] A shared physical memory bus is a shared physical crossbar with finite bandwidth and finite queuing capacity. When multi-camera DMA write bursts (streaming $0.75 upright("–") 1.5 upright(" GB/s")$ of uncompressed pixel data) collide with dense NPU weight-streaming read transactions (demanding $20 upright("–") 50 upright(" GB/s")$), the DRAM memory controller's internal arbiter saturates. Read transactions stall behind queued DMA writes, slashing effective memory bus efficiency by $40 upright("–") 60 %$ and causing neural inference latency to blow out from $P_50 = 22 upright(" ms")$ to severe tail spikes exceeding $P_99 > 135 upright(" ms")$.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Physical memory conduits must enforce strict traffic isolation and priority arbitration---guaranteeing that time-critical control updates and neural weight reads are never starved by bulk perceptual sensor ingestion.

