=== Spatial Memory Search vs.~Temporal Determinism: Zero Dynamic Allocation
<spatial-memory-search-vs.-temporal-determinism-zero-dynamic-allocation>
In standard software engineering, dynamic memory allocation (#NormalTok("malloc");, #NormalTok("free");, #NormalTok("new");, #NormalTok("delete");, and auto-resizing vectors) is standard practice. Programmers allocate objects on the heap at runtime, assuming memory allocation takes negligible time.

In real-time embedded safety systems, dynamic heap allocation is a fatal physical hazard @kopetz2011real@wilhelm2008worst. Searching through a fragmented dynamic heap to find a free memory block is a spatial linked-list traversal process that takes variable, non-deterministic physical time.

Permitting dynamic heap allocation within real-time control silicon introduces three severe physical failure modes:

+ #strong[Timing Jitter and Deadline Misses:] Standard free-list allocators exhibit non-deterministic execution times during spatial linked-list traversal, causing timing stalls that miss hard real-time control deadlines.
+ #strong[Long-Mission Heap Fragmentation:] Continuous allocation and deallocation over multi-hour physical missions fragments the heap, eventually triggering catastrophic out-of-memory allocation failures even when aggregate free memory appears sufficient.
+ #strong[Silent Stack-Heap Collisions:] Real-time microcontrollers lack memory management units (MMUs) to catch memory boundary violations; an expanding dynamic heap silently overwrites descending execution call stack frames, destroying return addresses and causing unhandled processor lockups that leave actuators completely unguided.

This establishes the #strong[Iron Law of Zero Dynamic Heap Allocation (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");)]: zero dynamic memory allocations (#NormalTok("malloc");, #NormalTok("free");, #NormalTok("new");, #NormalTok("delete");, or dynamically resizing data structures) are permitted inside the $1000 upright(" Hz")$ safety supervisor loop, the Control Barrier Function solver, or high-frequency motor control interrupt service routines (enforcing NASA JPL Rule 3 and MISRA C:2012 Rule 21.3). All memory structures on the real-time safety microcontroller must be statically declared at compile time. Every ring buffer, state vector, transformation matrix, and quadratic program workspace resides in dedicated, fixed-size global SRAM sections, guaranteeing mathematically verifiable temporal determinism.

=== The Physical Invariants of Real-Time Compute
<the-physical-invariants-of-real-time-compute>
To summarize Column 5, deterministic computation for Physical AI is governed by three non-negotiable architectural laws:

+ #strong[Spatial Locality:] Hard real-time safety loops must execute strictly within on-chip static SRAM within millimeters of the core, eliminating the $100 times$ off-chip DRAM latency penalty.
+ #strong[Interconnect Isolation:] Time-critical state updates and neural weight reads must never be starved behind bulk sensory DMA transfers on shared memory crossbars.
+ #strong[Static Memory Layouts:] Real-time safety paths must enforce the Iron Law of Zero Dynamic Heap Allocation (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");).

These physical constraints dictate the architecture of the entire system. In #strong[Chapter 4], we formulate the #emph[Multi-Rate Systems Hierarchy] that decouples non-deterministic Linux cognitive deliberation from microsecond bare-metal microcontroller reflexes via lock-free SRAM mailboxes. In #strong[Chapter 10], we translate these principles into concrete silicon placement, configuring hardware interconnect Quality-of-Service (QoS) registers, contiguous memory allocation (CMA) buffers, and thermal execution envelopes.

== The Physical Operating Specification: Freezing Hardware Safety Invariants
<sec-physical-spec>
Every engineering discipline requires a formal specification sheet that establishes the physical operating envelope of a machine prior to deployment. In Physical AI, deploying learned policies without an empirical, hardware-verified specification sheet invites hardware destruction. Before any autonomous agent is permitted to command physical actuators, the engineering team must measure, calculate, and freeze the system's physical operating limits in a formal #emph[Physical Operating Specification].

