== Systems Stress-Tests: Architectural Food for Thought
<sec-constraints-stress-tests>
To transition from abstract equations to systems engineering reasoning, consider four foundational dilemmas where software assumptions collide with the five physical constraints.

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 1: The Reaction Distance Trap (Time & Inertia Columns)]
<stress-test-1-the-reaction-distance-trap-time-inertia-columns>
- #strong[The Scenario:] An autonomous wheeled robot carrying a payload operates in a busy warehouse aisle at a forward speed of $v_0 = 1.5 upright(" m/s")$. The robot senses obstacles using an onboard camera and maintains a physical clearance margin of $0.50 upright(" m")$ ahead. Maximum available braking deceleration is $a_(upright("max")) = 3.0 upright(" m/s")^2$.
- #strong[The Digital vs.~Physical Contrast:] In software simulation, an AI model that processes frames in an average of $20 upright(" ms")$ appears more than fast enough to stop before hitting an obstacle. But on physical hardware, an occasional operating system scheduling pause or memory stall causes a tail latency spike of $250 upright(" ms")$ ($P_99$).
- #strong[The Systems Dilemma:]
  + Calculate the total stopping distance ($d_(upright("stop")) = v_0 dot.op Delta t_(upright("latency")) + frac(v_0^2, 2 a_(upright("max")))$) during a nominal $20 upright(" ms")$ cycle versus a $250 upright(" ms")$ tail latency spike.
  + Explain why the platform halts safely with $12.5 upright(" cm")$ of clearance remaining under nominal conditions, but collides with the obstacle during a latency spike before braking can even begin.
  + If an obstacle is also moving toward the robot, how does the expanding spatial uncertainty envelope define a hard expiration date on sensory information (the Freshness Wall)?
- #strong[The Physical AI Insight:] Average latency ($P_50$) is a dangerous software metric for physical systems. In the physical world, moving mass never pauses for silicon; every millisecond of computational delay translates directly into unguided physical travel, meaning safety margins must always be budgeted against worst-case tail latency ($P_99.9$).

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 2: The Torque-Jerk-Thermal Dilemma (Actuation & Energy Columns)]
<stress-test-2-the-torque-jerk-thermal-dilemma-actuation-energy-columns>
- #strong[The Scenario:] A robotic manipulator must lift a heavy metal workpiece from a conveyor belt within a tight production cycle. To maximize throughput, the motion planner commands maximum motor torque as quickly as possible.
- #strong[The Digital vs.~Physical Contrast:] Digital planners often model actuators as ideal torque sources capable of stepping instantaneously between force levels. In physical reality, stator coils have electrical inductance ($L \/ R$) that resists rapid current changes, transmissions have finite shear strength that fractures under excessive jerk ($accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛)$), and motor windings dissipate resistive Joule heat ($I^2 R$).
- #strong[The Systems Dilemma:]
  + If the controller commands an instantaneous step change in torque, why does the resulting mechanical jerk shock ($accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) arrow.r oo$) threaten gearbox teeth and excite structural vibrations?
  + If the motor is operated continuously at double its continuous current rating ($2 times I_(upright("cont"))$) to hold the heavy payload, by what factor does resistive heat dissipation ($P = I^2 R$) increase?
  + How must the system balance the need for fast motion against the physical limits of gear shear and winding insulation temperature?
- #strong[The Physical AI Insight:] Actuation and Energy form an unyielding physical trade-off. Software cannot command infinite torque slew rates without destroying mechanical transmissions, nor can it sustain peak currents without accumulating irreversible thermal energy that leads to motor burnout.

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 3: The Memory Highway Bottleneck (Compute & Time Columns)]
<stress-test-3-the-memory-highway-bottleneck-compute-time-columns>
- #strong[The Scenario:] An edge computing board on an autonomous mobile robot processes high-resolution stereo camera streams while simultaneously running an onboard neural perception model and a 1000 Hz real-time safety monitor. All three tasks share a single physical memory bus.
- #strong[The Digital vs.~Physical Contrast:] Software frameworks treat unified memory as a frictionless medium where CPU cores, GPU accelerators, and camera interfaces access data without interfering with one another. In hardware reality, the memory bus is a physical set of shared electrical wires with finite bandwidth and arbitration queues.
- #strong[The Systems Dilemma:]
  + When an unexpected visual scene causes the cameras to burst high-bandwidth raw pixel transfers across the memory bus, why do processor memory reads experience queuing stalls?
  + If the 1000 Hz real-time safety loop attempts to read sensor data across a saturated memory bus and suffers a 2-millisecond delay, how does a compute bus bottleneck convert directly into a physical safety violation?
  + Why does physical determinism mandate that real-time safety routines execute in isolated, on-chip static SRAM (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");) rather than relying on shared off-chip DRAM?
- #strong[The Physical AI Insight:] Compute resources are physically bounded. When multiple high-bandwidth components compete for the same physical memory bus, asynchronous sensory traffic can starve real-time control loops, proving that software determinism requires physical hardware isolation.

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 4: The Cross-Column Domino Effect (Whole-System Integration)]
<stress-test-4-the-cross-column-domino-effect-whole-system-integration>
- #strong[The Scenario:] An autonomous delivery rover approaches a steep curb ramp while tracking a delivery path. A sudden visual glare event causes a brief delay in the perception pipeline.
- #strong[The Digital vs.~Physical Contrast:] In an idealized software stack, a perception delay is an isolated software event: the vision module simply finishes slightly later. In an embodied machine, a delay in one column triggers an immediate cascade across all five physical columns.
- #strong[The Systems Dilemma:]
  + #emph[Time to Inertia:] Trace how a $100 upright(" ms")$ perception delay (Column 1) causes the rover to travel further before detecting the ramp, reducing its available stopping and maneuvering distance (Column 2).
  + #emph[Inertia to Actuation:] Because the rover is now closer to the curb, the controller must command a much sharper steering correction and higher braking torque, spiking joint jerk and mechanical stress (Column 3).
  + #emph[Actuation to Energy & Compute:] The sudden peak current draw spikes resistive heating in the motor coils (Column 4), while rapid sensor and state updates flood the communication and memory buses (Column 5).
- #strong[The Physical AI Insight:] The Five Physical Constraints are not independent silos; they form a single interconnected physical system. A delay or failure in any one column cascades immediately through momentum, torque, heat, and silicon, requiring holistic co-design across the entire machine.

== Summary
<sec-constraints-summary>
Physical AI begins where digital software abstractions collide with unyielding physical laws. Throughout this chapter, we established the quantitative instruments, physical constraints, and compute mechanisms that govern the boundary between learned deliberation and physical reality. Unlike digital computing where execution delay is benign, physical dynamical systems evolve along an unyielding time axis, dictating that worst-case tail latency must remain strictly within the physical world deadline ($t_(upright("total")) \( P_99.9 \) + t_(upright("margin")) < tau_(upright("world"))$) to prevent destructive phase margin erosion ($upright("PM") lt.eq 0^compose$).

By tracing the seven-stage sense-to-actuation waterfall, from optical charge integration through neural compute to electromagnetic rise times, we established the Five Physical Columns. We proved why $P_50$ throughput is an illusion, derived the Freshness Wall ($Delta t_(upright("wall"))$), formalized dynamic stopping kinematics ($d_(upright("stop"))$), bounded inductive rise times ($tau_e = L \/ R$), enforced $cal(C)^2$ jerk continuity ($accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛)$), and codified the Iron Law of Zero Dynamic Heap Allocation (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");) in static SRAM. With the physical columns established, #ref(<sec-cognition>, supplement: [Chapter]) turns to the horizontal rows of the Co-Design Matrix (#emph[Perceive], #emph[Remember], #emph[Reason], #emph[Plan], #emph[Execute]), orchestrating multi-rate cognitive execution across heterogeneous hardware.

