As illustrated in #ref(<fig-agent-workflow>, supplement: [Figure]), each of these five canonical stages resolves a distinct physical or representational boundary between the continuous, unyielding physical universe and the discrete, computational silicon substrate. We now formally examine each stage, analyzing its mathematical formulation, its historical evolution from classical robotics to modern Physical AI foundation models, and its specific systems role in the embodied cognitive lifecycle.

== Stage 1: Spatial Perception and Metric Grounding
<stage-1-spatial-perception-and-metric-grounding>
Spatial perception resolves the #emph[Transduction and Projection Boundary], converting uncalibrated physical energy fluxes into geometrically metric, spatially grounded 3D representations.

To build an intuitive physical mental model of perception, consider what an optical camera actually sees. A camera image is a flat, 2D shadow of a three-dimensional world: infinite physical points along a single line-of-sight ray collapse onto the exact same pixel coordinate $\( u \, v \)$. A small $10 upright(" cm")$ hand tool held $0.5 upright(" m")$ in front of the lens subtends the identical angular pixel size as a massive $2 upright(" m")$ steel beam $10 upright(" m")$ away. Driving physical robot motors directly from 2D pixel centroids or bounding box detections is fatal because pixel coordinates contain zero physical depth, spatial orientation, or metric scale.

At the silicon hardware level, the physical world manifests as continuous electromagnetic radiation striking silicon photodiode arrays. Collecting enough photons to form an image requires 8 to 15 milliseconds of physical exposure time ($t_(upright("transduce"))$). Once the charge wells are integrated, analog-to-digital converters serialize the voltages into raw byte arrays, which are streamed across Direct Memory Access (DMA) channels into processor memory.

To transform these ungrounded sensory streams into actionable physical knowledge, the three disciplines contribute distinct layers:

- #strong[Maya (CS/ML):] Leverages Vision Transformers (ViTs) and self-supervised backbones (such as DINOv2) to divide images into pixel patches, projecting them into high-dimensional embedding spaces where self-attention extracts dense semantic feature fields and object affordances invariant to lighting and texture changes.
- #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded):] Provisions zero-copy coherent DMA ring buffers (#NormalTok("dma_alloc_coherent");) to ingest high-bandwidth camera streams directly into unified memory, tagging each frame with an IEEE 1588 PTP hardware timestamp at the exact midpoint of optical exposure.
- #strong[Alex (MechE/Robotics):] Applies projective geometry and Lie group transformations ($S E \( 3 \)$) to unproject 2D pixel rays into 3D metric coordinates and rigidly transform them into the physical robot base frame.

To map a 2D pixel coordinate $\( u \, v \)$ with measured metric depth $d \( u \, v \)$ into a true 3D coordinate $upright(bold(p))_(upright("world")) in bb(R)^3$ in the robot base frame, the perception engine executes the unified inverse pinhole and rigid-body coordinate transformation:

$ mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(p))_(upright("world")); 1) = upright(bold(T))_(upright("world") arrow.l upright("cam")) mat(delim: "[", d \( u \, v \) dot.op upright(bold(K))^(- 1) mat(delim: "[", u; v; 1); 1) $

In this fundamental governing relation:

- $\( u \, v \)$ is the 2D discrete pixel coordinate on the sensor grid.
- $upright(bold(K)) in bb(R)^(3 times 3)$ is the intrinsic camera calibration matrix, containing focal lengths and optical center offsets determined by lens geometry and sensor pixel pitch.
- $d \( u \, v \) in bb(R)^(+)$ is the true metric depth along the optical axis measured by active time-of-flight, structured light, or stereo disparity.
- $upright(bold(T))_(upright("world") arrow.l upright("cam")) in S E \( 3 \)$ is the rigid-body extrinsic transformation matrix, capturing camera orientation ($upright(S O) \( 3 \)$) and position relative to the robot's physical base origin.

Only after this geometric unprojection can downstream planning reason about physical reachability, obstacles, and collision boundaries.

In classical robotics, spatial perception relied upon handcrafted, deterministic feature extractors such as SIFT, SURF, and Harris corner detectors combined with geometric epipolar constraints and multi-view bundle adjustment @hartley2003multiple. While these classical pipelines provided mathematically rigorous metric guarantees, they were notoriously brittle, failing catastrophically under specular reflections, textureless surfaces, dynamic shadows, and atmospheric obscurants. Modern Physical AI transforms perception through large-scale Vision Transformers (ViTs) and self-supervised visual foundation models such as DINOv2 @dosovitskiy2020image@oquab2023dinov2. Rather than tracking isolated, brittle geometric primitives, modern perception backbones extract dense semantic feature fields and spatial affordance tokens directly from high-dimensional sensor arrays. These learned representations capture rich semantic scene context invariant to lighting and texture changes; when fused with calibrated depth sensors or geometric spatial priors, they reliably ground these rich semantics into metric $S E \( 3 \)$ coordinates.

Despite the expressive power of modern perception networks, an instantaneous observation $O_t$ can never serve as a direct command for physical actuation. An observation is fundamentally instantaneous, incomplete, and subject to sensor latency, motion blur, and line-of-sight occlusions. A camera frame reveals only the exposed surfaces of physical objects at the moment of photodiode charge integration ($t_(upright("transduce"))$); it cannot reveal the hidden mass distribution of an occluded object, its continuous velocity vectors (which require temporal finite differences or optical flow gradients across multiple observations), or its future trajectory. To convert instantaneous, fragmented perceptions into dependable operational knowledge, the cognitive pipeline must pass raw spatial representations into persistent temporal memory, establishing the foundation for state estimation analyzed in #ref(<sec-perception>, supplement: [Chapter]).

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] Modern visual foundation models (such as ViTs, CLIP, and DINOv2) can detect, segment, and track objects with high semantic accuracy directly within the pixel array $bb(R)^(H times W times C)$. Therefore, an embodied policy can bypass explicit geometric calibration and directly drive joint actuators toward 2D pixel centroids or optical bounding boxes.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] A 2D camera image represents a lossy projective ray collapse ($S E \( 3 \) arrow.r bb(R)^2$); ungrounded pixel coordinates contain zero metric depth, spatial orientation, or physical scale. Furthermore, optical sensors suffer rolling-shutter spatial shearing under ego-motion, lens distortion, and line-of-sight occlusions. A policy commanding joint torques directly from 2D pixel coordinates cannot distinguish between a $10 upright(" cm")$ tool at $0.5 upright(" m")$ distance and a $2 upright(" m")$ structural hazard at $10 upright(" m")$ distance, inevitably triggering kinematic singularities, unmodeled collisions, or joint limit overruns.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Raw sensory fluxes must be lifted into calibrated metric reference frames ($S E \( 3 \)$) via verified camera extrinsics and geometric depth fusion before entering downstream cognitive stages ($O_t subset S E \( 3 \)$). Never command physical actuation directly from ungrounded 2D pixel space.

] #label("callout-fallacy*-18")
== Stage 2: Temporal Memory and World Modeling
<stage-2-temporal-memory-and-world-modeling>
Temporal memory resolves the #emph[Epistemic and Partial Observability Boundary], bridging the critical divergence between what an agent instantaneously observes and the true physical state of the operating environment ($O_t eq.not S_t$).

To build an intuitive physical mental model of temporal memory, consider navigating your own living room after the lights suddenly go out. You do not instantly forget where the walls, couch, or doorways are located; your brain maintains an internal spatial map of the room that persists even when your eyes receive zero photons. If a family member steps across the room and momentarily occludes a chair, you do not assume the chair has ceased to exist. However, as you take multiple steps in the dark without fresh visual feedback, your internal positional uncertainty grows until you must slow down to avoid stubbing your toe.

In embodied systems, complete observability is a physical impossibility. Dynamic obstacles pass behind structural pillars, camera lenses suffer momentary optical flare, LiDAR beams reflect away from specular metallic surfaces, and communication buses incur scheduling transport delays. If an intelligent agent relied exclusively on its instantaneous sensory observation $O_t$, an obstacle would vanish from existence the instant it slipped behind an occlusion, causing the machine to steer directly into it during subsequent maneuvers.

Furthermore, temporal memory acts as an essential physical buffer against computational deliberation latency ($Delta t$). Because deep neural deliberation and trajectory optimization require non-zero execution time, the physical universe continues moving while algorithms compute. If an autonomous robot designs a motion plan conditioned on an unprojected observation captured at historical instant $t_(upright("capture"))$, by the time the plan arrives at the actuators at $t_(upright("deploy")) = t_(upright("capture")) + Delta t$, the machine has physically traveled forward under momentum. Deploying an action computed for an obsolete pose induces severe trajectory tracking errors, phase-lag oscillations, or structural collisions.

To reconcile these physical challenges, the three disciplines coordinate across temporal memory:

- #strong[Maya (CS/ML):] Develops Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPAs) and Recurrent State-Space Models (RSSMs) that map raw observations into compact latent feature vectors $upright(bold(z)) in bb(R)^d$, predicting how latent physical affordances evolve under candidate actions without wasting compute decoding millions of task-irrelevant background pixels.
- #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded):] Rejects naive multi-frame token concatenation---which triggers quadratic compute growth $cal(O) \( N^2 \)$ and exhausts memory bandwidth with massive Key-Value (KV) cache traffic---and instead enforces fixed-size ring buffers in deterministic memory that execute recursive state updates in strictly bounded $cal(O) \( 1 \)$ time.
- #strong[Alex (MechE/Robotics):] Anchors learned latent states to Lie-algebraic kinematic frame trees and integrates continuous rigid-body dynamics $upright(bold(f)) \( upright(bold(x)) \, upright(bold(u)) \)$ forward across the computational delay $Delta t$, guaranteeing that planned footholds or grasping waypoints align with the robot's future physical pose.

To project the machine's estimated metric state forward across the computational deliberation horizon $Delta t$, the temporal memory stage evaluates the continuous dynamic state projection:

$ hat(upright(bold(x)))_(t + Delta t) = hat(upright(bold(x)))_t + integral_t^(t + Delta t) upright(bold(f)) \( hat(upright(bold(x))) \( tau \) \, upright(bold(u)) \( tau \) \) thin d tau $

In this dynamic projection model, $hat(upright(bold(x)))_t$ represents the estimated metric state vector at the observation timestamp (encompassing 3D position, linear velocity, orientation quaternion, and angular velocity), $upright(bold(f)) \( upright(bold(x)) \, upright(bold(u)) \)$ represents the continuous rigid-body equations of motion governing the platform, and $Delta t$ is the forward computational deliberation horizon.

To understand how forward state projection neutralizes latency in practice, consider an autonomous quadruped robot trotting at forward velocity $v_x = 2.0 upright(" m/s")$. The robot's stereo vision pipeline operates at $20 upright(" Hz")$ ($50 upright(" ms")$ frame period), while high-level semantic deliberation requires $Delta t = 80 upright(" ms")$ inference latency on an embedded neural accelerator. If the downstream planner designs foot placement using the unprojected visual state from $t = 0 upright(" ms")$, the robot physically travels $v_x dot.op Delta t = 2.0 upright(" m/s") times 0.080 upright(" s") = 0.160 upright(" m")$ ($16.0 upright(" cm")$) before the first trajectory waypoint is dispatched. A $16.0 upright(" cm")$ foot placement error on uneven terrain is sufficient to trip the quadruped and cause dynamic roll-over. By integrating forward kinematics across the delay, the temporal memory stage projects the platform pose forward to where the quadruped #emph[will] be upon action deployment.

Historically, classical robotics managed partial observability through Extended Kalman Filters (EKFs), Unscented Kalman Filters, and non-linear factor graph optimizers @kalman1960new@thrun2005probabilistic@barfoot2017state. These algorithms maintained explicit Gaussian belief distributions over kinematic poses and velocities, integrating rigid kinematic frame trees through continuous geometric updates. However, these classical techniques hit an unyielding wall regarding partial observability failure: they required meticulously hand-engineered transition models and struggled to maintain object permanence for complex, non-rigid entities that underwent prolonged visual occlusions.

Modern Physical AI augments these metric kinematic trees with deep predictive world models and Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPAs) @lecun2022path@assran2023self@hafner2023mastering. Instead of falling into the computational trap of predicting raw, high-frequency pixel sequences---which wastes capacity modeling task-irrelevant noise like dynamic shadows or textures---modern latent world models encode observations into compact, geometrically structured latent embeddings that capture task-relevant dynamics, object permanency, and physical affordances. These learned embeddings do not blindly replace classical metric state, but rather wrap around it, grounding abstract semantic predictions back into the metric kinematic tree.

This synthesis of metric kinematic trees and predictive latent representations forms the dynamic core examined in #ref(<sec-state>, supplement: [Chapter]), providing the stable physical foundation necessary for the high-level semantic reasoning stage that follows.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] Modern multimodal transformers feature massive context windows capable of processing millions of tokens. Rather than maintaining explicit state estimators, kinematic frame trees, or dynamic world models, an embodied system can simply concatenate raw historical sensor tokens $\[ O_(t - k) \, dots.h \, O_t \]$ into the model prompt and rely on self-attention to maintain temporal state and object permanence.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Transformer self-attention scales quadratically with sequence length $cal(O) \( N^2 \)$ in compute and $cal(O) \( N \)$ in KV-cache DRAM memory traffic. Concatenating high-bandwidth visual tokens inflates inference latency from tens of milliseconds to multiple seconds, breaching the physical Freshness Wall ($Delta t gt.double Delta t_(upright("wall"))$). More critically, self-attention across token histories cannot enforce rigid-body kinematic transformations ($S E \( 3 \)$), momentum conservation, or numerical velocity integration; when visual occlusions occur, attention weights drift, hallucinating object positions or dropping occluded entities.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Epistemic state belief ($S_t$) must be maintained through recursive state estimation, Lie-algebraic kinematic frame trees, and compact latent world models operating with bounded $cal(O) \( 1 \)$ temporal memory overhead. Historical token accumulation is not a substitute for dynamical state estimation.

] #label("callout-fallacy*-19")
== Stage 3: Semantic Reasoning and Intent Generation
<stage-3-semantic-reasoning-and-intent-generation>
Semantic reasoning resolves the #emph[Grounding and Deliberation Boundary], translating open-world, high-level task specifications into verifiable, time-bounded physical objectives.

To build an intuitive physical mental model of semantic reasoning, consider an architect issuing a signed, time-limited work permit or lease for an active construction zone. The permit specifies the high-level task (e.g., "excavate foundation trench"), defines the exact spatial boundaries within which machinery may operate, and sets strict keepout zones around buried electrical cables. Crucially, the permit contains an unforgeable hardware timestamp and a strict expiration countdown (e.g., valid for 30 minutes). If the operator fails to renew the permit before the countdown reaches zero, all heavy equipment is automatically locked in a safe state. The architect does not grab the hydraulic levers and steer the bulldozer; they establish the authorized operating envelope within which autonomous machinery may work.

In embodied intelligence, the highest tier of cognition requires understanding natural language instructions, evaluating complex multi-step causal dependencies, reasoning over physical affordances, and dynamically re-planning when unexpected environmental anomalies occur. Operating at a deliberative cadence of $0.5 upright(" to ") 2 upright(" Hz")$, this stage interprets the current spatial-temporal state belief $S_t$ to determine #emph[what] physical outcome the agent should pursue over the upcoming task horizon.

To bridge semantic flexibility with physical safety, the three disciplines divide responsibilities:

- #strong[Maya (CS/ML):] Deploys large-scale Vision-Language Models (VLMs) and Vision-Language-Action (VLA) backbones trained on massive multimodal datasets @driess2023palme@brohan2023rt2@kim2024openvla, leveraging their semantic commonsense to decompose ambiguous natural language directives (e.g., "safely clear the spilled chemical vial") into structured spatial waypoints and affordance tokens.
- #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded):] Enforces strict temporal isolation by treating the foundation model as an untrusted proposal generator, running lease expiration watchdogs on hardware timers with monotonic clock timestamps to guarantee that a stalled GPU forward pass never causes unguided actuator runaway.
- #strong[Alex (MechE/Robotics):] Defines certified convex workspace hulls ($cal(S)_(upright("hull"))$) and keepout hazard volumes ($cal(B)_(upright("keepout"))$), verifying that proposed spatial waypoints remain far from kinematic singularities and collision boundaries before granting execution authority.

Because multi-billion parameter autoregressive models exhibit stochastic inference latencies and non-zero hallucination rates, granting them direct, unmitigated electrical authority over physical motor drives violates fundamental safety engineering principles. Physical AI resolves this vulnerability by restricting the semantic reasoning stage to emitting a formal #emph[Expiring Intent Lease] ($I_t = cal(L)_(upright("intent"))$) @gray1989leases:

$ cal(L)_(upright("intent")) = chevron.l cal(G)_(upright("task")) \, thin cal(S)_(upright("hull")) \, thin t_(upright("issue")) \, thin Delta t_(upright("lease")) \, thin upright(bold(w))_(upright("target")) \, thin cal(B)_(upright("keepout")) chevron.r $

In this formal intent lease structure:

- $cal(G)_(upright("task"))$ is the high-level semantic task descriptor or symbolic goal predicate (e.g., #NormalTok("grasp_chemical_vial");).
- $cal(S)_(upright("hull")) subset bb(R)^3 times upright(S O) \( 3 \)$ is the certified spatial operating hull---a bounded volume in workspace coordinates outside of which physical motion is strictly prohibited.
- $t_(upright("issue")) in bb(R)^(+)$ is the synchronized timestamp from the monotonic master hardware clock at the exact moment the lease was signed.
- $Delta t_(upright("lease")) in bb(R)^(+)$ is the explicit validity duration of the lease (typically $0.500 upright(" s")$ for a $2 upright(" Hz")$ reasoner).
- $upright(bold(w))_(upright("target"))$ is the sequence of discrete 3D metric target waypoints defining the proposed intermediate goal.
- $cal(B)_(upright("keepout")) subset bb(R)^3$ represents dynamic keepout hazard volumes identified during semantic scene decomposition.

To guarantee that stochastic foundation models can never breach physical safety invariants, downstream real-time microcontrollers continuously evaluate two mandatory contract invariants:

+ #strong[Freshness Invariant:] $t_(upright("now")) - t_(upright("issue")) lt.eq Delta t_(upright("lease"))$, verifying that the lease has not expired.
+ #strong[Spatial Containment Invariant:] $upright(bold(p)) \( tau \) in cal(S)_(upright("hull")) \\ cal(B)_(upright("keepout")) quad forall tau in \[ t_(upright("issue")) \, t_(upright("issue")) + Delta t_(upright("lease")) \]$, verifying that all proposed motion remains strictly inside the safe hull and outside all keepout zones.

If the high-level neural reasoner experiences a memory stall or scheduling pause, the active lease expires before the next proposal arrives. Acting as a semantic watchdog, the real-time supervisor detects expiration at microsecond precision and smoothly transitions the platform along a pre-certified stopping path to rest inside $cal(S)_(upright("hull"))$, completely preventing unguided actuator runaway.

When operating nominally, this time-bounded intent lease is handed directly to the trajectory planner to be decoded into physical motion, establishing the architectural privilege separation explored in #ref(<sec-intent>, supplement: [Chapter]).

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] If a multimodal foundation model or Vision-Language-Action policy outputs an action token sequence with high softmax probability ($P \( upright("action") \) > 0.99$), the proposed action is safe to execute directly on physical actuators without downstream filtering.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Softmax confidence measures statistical likelihood under the model's training distribution; it is completely blind to physical invariants, multi-body kinematics, friction cones, thermal limits, and real-time obstacle motion. An out-of-distribution observation or subtle optical illusion can cause an autoregressive model to emit a catastrophic command---such as driving an articulated arm through a concrete wall or commanding extreme over-currents---with near $100 %$ statistical confidence.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Statistical confidence is not physical feasibility. High-level semantic reasoning is an unverified proposal engine; all candidate actions must be filtered through deterministic kinematic solvers and Control Barrier Functions running on isolated real-time silicon.

] #label("callout-fallacy*-20")
== Stage 4: Trajectory Planning and Synthesis
<stage-4-trajectory-planning-and-synthesis>
Trajectory planning resolves the #emph[Kinodynamic Feasibility Boundary], converting discrete, high-level semantic intent leases $I_t$ into continuous, collision-free reference trajectories $tau_t$ that strictly respect the mechanical and physical limits of the hardware.

To build an intuitive physical mental model of trajectory planning, consider the difference between an Olympic gymnast performing a fluid tumbling pass versus a stop-motion claymation animator posing a puppet frame by frame. An animator can move a puppet to any position between frames with zero physical consequence. But if a physical robot attempts to jump discontinuously between discrete setpoints, or if an algorithm commands sudden steps in acceleration, the result is infinite theoretical jerk ($accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) arrow.r oo$). In physical transmissions, jerk acts as a mechanical hammer: it slams gear teeth together, excites destructive structural resonances, and strips the delicate teeth of cycloidal and harmonic drives. Furthermore, if a robot evaluates single-step neural policies ($H = 1$), any $20 upright(" ms")$ compute stall leaves the actuators starved of commands, forcing the machine into a jerky, unstable shudder.

To synthesize fluid, kinodynamically safe motion, the three disciplines unite across trajectory planning:

- #strong[Maya (CS/ML):] Deploys generative Action Chunking Transformers (ACT), Diffusion Policies, and Flow Matching backbones @chi2023diffusionpolicy@zhao2023learning@lipman2022flow that predict entire multi-step temporal horizons ($H = 16 upright(" to ") 64$ steps), capturing complex multi-modal trajectory distributions (such as smoothly dodging left or right around an obstacle without fatal mode-averaging collisions).
- #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded):] Streams these multi-step trajectory chunks across lock-free static SRAM circular queues, buffering the low-level motor drives against GPU inference stalls, memory bus contention, and operating system scheduling jitter.
- #strong[Alex (MechE/Robotics):] Enforces $cal(C)^2$ differential continuity (continuous position, velocity, and acceleration) across trajectory waypoints, strictly bounding mechanical jerk ($\| accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) \| lt.eq j_max$) to protect gearboxes and linkages from torque shock.

To eliminate computational starvation and guarantee differential smoothness, modern Physical AI represents the trajectory output as an action-chunked reference queue:

$ tau_t = {upright(bold(q)) \( t + k Delta t_(upright("chunk")) \) \, thin dot(upright(bold(q))) \( t + k Delta t_(upright("chunk")) \) \, thin dot.double(upright(bold(q))) \( t + k Delta t_(upright("chunk")) \)}_(k = 1)^H $

In this trajectory planning formulation:

- $H in bb(N)$ is the action chunk lookahead horizon length (typically $H = 16 upright(" to ") 64$ steps).
- $Delta t_(upright("chunk")) in bb(R)^(+)$ is the discrete step size of the trajectory queue (e.g., $20 upright(" ms")$, corresponding to a $50 upright(" Hz")$ update rate).
- $T_(upright("horizon")) = H dot.op Delta t_(upright("chunk"))$ is the total temporal preview horizon (e.g., $16 times 20 upright(" ms") = 320 upright(" ms")$).
- $upright(bold(q)) \, dot(upright(bold(q))) \, dot.double(upright(bold(q)))$ are the generalized joint position, velocity, and acceleration vectors.
- Successive overlapping action chunks emitted at timestamps $t_i$ are smoothly blended via exponential receding-horizon temporal ensembling: $upright(bold(a))_(upright("ens")) \( t \) = frac(sum w_i \( t \) upright(bold(a))_i \( t \), sum w_i \( t \))$ with exponential decay weight $w_i \( t \) = exp \( - lambda \( t - t_i \) \)$.

With action chunking, the multi-step trajectory queue continuously buffers the joint drives against computational latency jitter: acceleration ramps smoothly along $cal(C)^2$-continuous splines with strictly bounded jerk ($\| accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) \| lt.eq j_max < oo$), while overlapping chunks are smoothly blended to eliminate boundary discontinuities between inference cycles.

Historically, robotics addressed trajectory synthesis through geometric search and sampling-based algorithms, including Rapidly-exploring Random Trees ($upright("RRT")^(*)$), Probabilistic Roadmaps (PRM), and numerical non-linear Model Predictive Control (MPC) @rawlings2017model. While mathematically rigorous and capable of enforcing explicit obstacle bounds, classical optimization techniques often struggle with high-dimensional contact dynamics, deformable objects, and multi-modal trajectory distributions. For example, when an obstacle blocks a direct path, an agent can pass to the left or to the right; classical mean-squared-error regression averages these two valid modes, commanding a fatal straight-ahead collision. Generative diffusion policies and flow matching resolve this by modeling the full multi-modal distribution, sampling a coherent, collision-free trajectory chunk that is then validated against kinodynamic constraints.

Nevertheless, because generative neural policies remain learned statistical approximations, candidate trajectories are treated as unverified proposals until certified by deterministic real-time hardware, motivating the formal kinodynamic synthesis methods established in #ref(<sec-planning>, supplement: [Chapter]).

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] Reinforcement learning and Markov Decision Process formulations assume single-step state-action transitions $a_t tilde.op pi \( s_t \)$. An embodied agent should simply evaluate the policy at every tick, emitting a single instantaneous action $a_t$ for immediate execution.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Evaluating deep neural policies requires non-zero compute latency $Delta t_(upright("inference")) approx 20 upright("–") 100 upright(" ms")$. If an agent dispatches only single-step actions ($H = 1$), the physical hardware is starved of commands between inference cycles, forcing the system to hold stale setpoints (zero-order hold) or coast unguided. Furthermore, stitching together discrete single-step predictions without temporal blending creates severe acceleration discontinuities and infinite jerk ($accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) arrow.r oo$), destroying gearboxes and exciting structural vibrations.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Trajectory generation under non-zero compute latency demands temporal Action Chunking ($H gt.double 1$) and receding-horizon temporal ensembling ($w_i = exp \( - lambda \( t - t_i \) \)$), generating continuous $cal(C)^2$-smooth trajectory queues that buffer actuators against computational jitter.

] #label("callout-fallacy*-21")
== Stage 5: Real-Time Reflex Enforcement
<stage-5-real-time-reflex-enforcement>
Real-time reflex enforcement resolves the #emph[Silicon-to-Physics Invariant Boundary], certifying that every electrical signal dispatched to hardware power stages preserves physical safety invariants under hard real-time deadlines.

To build an intuitive physical mental model of reflex enforcement, consider an active magnetic cushion or virtual forcefield surrounding a physical machine. When the robot operates far inside its safe workspace, the forcefield exerts zero resistance, allowing high-level AI policies full freedom to maneuver. But as the robot approaches a physical obstacle, joint limit, or thermal threshold, the virtual barrier stiffens smoothly and progressively, decelerating the motion to zero before contact can occur. Unlike a physical emergency-stop button or mechanical limit switch---which trips only #emph[after] collision forces have built up---a forward-invariant barrier acts proactively, ensuring the machine never breaches safe physical bounds in the first place.

Operating at the highest system frequency---typically $1000 upright(" Hz")$ or faster---on isolated, deterministic microcontroller cores, this stage executes without dynamic memory allocation (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");) to eliminate operating system jitter and priority inversions @kopetz2011real. It acts as the ultimate physical gatekeeper, evaluating candidate trajectory waypoints $tau_t$ against the instantaneous kinematic state of the machine and formal mathematical barrier certificates.

Here, the three disciplines converge on the physical barrier:

- #strong[Maya (CS/ML):] Recognizes that statistical softmax confidence ($P \( upright("action") \) > 0.99$) is completely blind to physical invariants, multi-body kinematics, friction cones, and thermal limits, happily delegating ultimate execution safety to formal optimization filters.
- #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded):] Implements lightweight, deterministic quadratic programming solvers executing in zero-allocation static SRAM (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");) at $1000 upright(" Hz")$ with sub-250-microsecond execution budgets, directly driving $20 upright(" kHz")$ Pulse-Width Modulation (PWM) gate drivers.
- #strong[Alex (MechE/Robotics):] Formulates Control Barrier Functions (CBFs) that incorporate true multi-body dynamics, joint friction cones, and inductive electrical limits, guaranteeing forward invariance ($cal(C)_(upright("safe"))$) under worst-case hardware operating conditions.

To enforce absolute safety across continuous state spaces, modern Physical AI utilizes Control Barrier Functions formulated as real-time Quadratic Programs (CBF-QPs) @ames2014control@ames2019control. At every 1 kHz control tick, the real-time enforcer solves a convex optimization problem that minimally modifies the proposed high-level acceleration command $upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand")) \( tau_t \)$ to guarantee that the machine remains inside the safe operating set:

$ upright(bold(u))_t^(*) = arg min_(upright(bold(u)) in cal(U)_(upright("safe"))) 1 / 2 parallel upright(bold(u)) - upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand")) \( tau_t \) parallel^2 quad upright("subject to") quad dot(h) \( upright(bold(x)) \, upright(bold(u)) \) + alpha \( h \( upright(bold(x)) \) \) gt.eq 0 $

In this governing execution formulation:

- $upright(bold(x)) in cal(X) subset.eq bb(R)^n$ is the instantaneous continuous physical state vector (positions, velocities, and contact forces).
- $upright(bold(u)) in cal(U) subset.eq bb(R)^m$ is the certified control actuation command.
- $upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand")) \( tau_t \)$ is the candidate unverified reference acceleration or torque proposed by the Stage 4 trajectory planner.
- $h \( upright(bold(x)) \) gt.eq 0$ is a scalar barrier function defining safe workspace clearance.
- $alpha \( h \)$ is a class $cal(K)$ function that dictates permissible approach speeds---permitting rapid movement when far from danger ($h \( upright(bold(x)) \) gt.double 0$) while strictly forcing deceleration to zero as the boundary is approached ($h \( upright(bold(x)) \) arrow.r 0$).

Once certified, the control input $upright(bold(u))_t^(*)$ is converted into physical motor currents and voltages. Because motor coils have an electrical time constant ($tau_e = L \/ R approx 1 upright("–") 2 upright(" ms")$), real-time execution must operate on dedicated bare-metal microcontrollers at $1000 upright(" Hz")$ with $20 upright(" kHz")$ PWM switching to match electrical rise times and prevent current spikes or magnetic saturation.

If a proposed trajectory violates kinematic limits, approaches an obstacle, or if upstream compute nodes experience a latency blowout, the real-time enforcer overrides the learned policy instantly. It synthesizes a certified safe control input $upright(bold(u))_t^(*)$ or executes an emergency stopping trajectory, ensuring that software faults in high-level neural networks never manifest as physical hardware damage. We explore the formal mathematics of barrier functions, real-time architectures, and motor actuation in #ref(<sec-enforcement>, supplement: [Chapter]).

