- #strong[The Common Assumption:] Classical PID controllers and joint limit switches have protected industrial machines for decades. As long as the system clamps motor current and trips an emergency stop when a joint limit or tactile bumper switch is contacted, the machine is physically safe.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Mechanical limit switches and joint current clamps are reactive (post-hoc); they trigger only #emph[after] a physical collision has occurred or after kinetic energy has built up. In high-speed embodied systems ($v > 1 upright(" m/s")$), braking deceleration is physically bounded by friction and torque ($a_max$). If an emergency stop is triggered at the collision boundary ($h = 0$), kinetic momentum carries the machine forward by $d = frac(v^2, 2 a_max)$, crushing the obstacle. Furthermore, joint-level limits are geometrically blind to Cartesian obstacles in workspace coordinates.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Physical safety must be enforced proactively via forward-invariant barrier certificates ($dot(h) \( upright(bold(x)) \, upright(bold(u)) \) + alpha \( h \( upright(bold(x)) \) \) gt.eq 0$) that smoothly decelerate the system #emph[before] the boundary is reached, guaranteeing $cal(C)_(upright("safe"))$ invariance at kilohertz execution rates without relying on reactive post-collision tripping.

