In each domain, the interface between stages is defined by explicit mathematical and temporal contracts:

- #strong[Perception to Memory ($O_t arrow.r S_t$):] Spatial perception delivers timestamped affordance tokens and pose estimates across Direct Memory Access channels, integrating them into global kinematic frame trees using Lie algebra updates.
- #strong[Memory to Reason ($S_t arrow.r I_t$):] Temporal memory continuously filters these observations into persistent, occlusion-free latent world states $S_t$, buffering the deliberative tier against sensor dropouts.
- #strong[Reason to Plan ($I_t arrow.r tau_t$):] Conditioned on this state belief, the semantic reasoner emits a bounded Expiring Intent Lease ($I_t = cal(L)_(upright("intent"))$), establishing spatial bounds and validity horizons for candidate goals.
- #strong[Plan to Execute ($tau_t arrow.r u_t$):] The trajectory planner decodes these leases into smooth, $cal(C)^2$-continuous reference splines $tau_t$ that respect physical torque and acceleration limits.
- #strong[Execute to World ($u_t arrow.r W_(t + 1)$):] Finally, the real-time execution tier filters candidate waypoints through Control Barrier Functions and modulates low-level electrical signals ($u_t$), guaranteeing forward safety invariance while physically transforming the material world ($W_(t + 1)$).

