The enduring systems lesson of the Tempe collision is that safety-critical physical authority must never be subordinate to stochastic neural classifications or high-level software heuristics. In the Uber ATG architecture, the safety braking path was monolithic and centralized: the ability to execute an emergency stop depended on whether a complex, non-deterministic deep neural network correctly stabilized its semantic categories and whether an unverified user-space software timer expired. Physical AI demands a strict Proposal--Permission Boundary. High-level perception backbones and trajectory planners running on application processors (MPUs) or neural processing units (NPUs) at $10 upright("–") 50 upright(" Hz")$ must be treated as untrusted advisory systems that generate candidate trajectory proposals. The authority to grant execution permission must reside strictly within an independent, class-agnostic safety reflex operating at a hard real-time $1000 upright(" Hz")$ cadence on dedicated bare-metal microcontrollers (MCUs). This 1 kHz deterministic reflex continuously evaluates class-agnostic spatial occupancy bounds (derived directly from raw radar returns or direct time-of-flight sensors, completely bypassing the neural vision stack) against dynamic stopping equations ($d_(upright("clearance")) gt.eq d_(upright("stop"))$) in static SRAM with zero dynamic heap allocation (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");). Had such a multi-rate architecture been deployed, the class-agnostic 1 kHz safety monitor would have detected an occupied spatial volume at $T - 4.5 upright(" s")$ and executed full regenerative and friction braking regardless of whether the high-level semantic model classified the obstacle as a car, a bicycle, or static clutter, arresting the vehicle dozens of meters before impact.

== The Three Cadences of Physical AI
<the-three-cadences-of-physical-ai>
The central architectural dilemma of embodied intelligence is that a physical agent cannot execute on a single, uniform computational clock without either sacrificing semantic reasoning capacity or compromising physical safety. In traditional cloud computing, software operates within an artificial, turn-based coordinate system: if a neural network experiences an unexpected garbage collection pause, cache miss storm, or memory bus stall, execution pauses behind a loading indicator while the digital universe patiently waits. In Physical AI, however, the physical universe serves as the unyielding master clock. While silicon deliberates, continuous dynamical equations govern reality: moving mass accumulates linear reaction debt $d_(upright("react")) = v dot.op Delta t$, ungrounded linkages accelerate under gravity, and stator coils accumulate irreversible Joule heat.

To bridge the four orders of magnitude separating high-level open-world comprehension from microsecond motor commutation, embodied architectures partition execution across three asynchronous temporal cadences: System 2 ($0.5 upright("–") 2 upright(" Hz")$), System 1.5 ($20 upright("–") 50 upright(" Hz")$), and System 1 ($1000 upright(" Hz")$), illustrated in #ref(<fig-three-cadences>, supplement: [Figure]) and compared systematically across silicon and authority tiers in #ref(<tbl-three-cadences>, supplement: [Table]). This multi-rate hierarchy is governed by an uncompromising systems invariant: cognitive complexity is strictly decoupled from physical actuation privilege across an asynchronous Proposal--Permission boundary, ensuring that stochastic foundation models propose long-horizon behaviors without ever possessing direct hardware authority over actuator gate drivers.

