=== System 2: Semantic Deliberation
<system-2-semantic-deliberation>
At the deliberative apex of the hierarchy operates System 2, executing at a cadence of $0.5 upright("–") 2 upright(" Hz")$ (cycle periods $Delta t in \[ 500 \, 2000 \] upright(" ms")$) on multi-core Linux Application Processors (MPUs) or offboard compute nodes. This tier ingests rich, high-bandwidth multimodal sensory streams---including multi-view RGB-D video, spatial point clouds, ambient acoustic signals, and natural language mission commands---to execute large Vision-Language-Action (VLA) foundation models scaling from $7 upright("B")$ to $70 upright("B")$ parameters. Its primary computational role is open-world semantic affordance parsing, long-horizon symbolic task decomposition, and spatial reasoning across dynamic environments.

Because deep autoregressive Transformer decoders suffer from stochastic execution latencies, heavy KV-cache memory bandwidth bottlenecks, and non-zero hallucination probabilities, System 2 occupies the privilege tier of an Untrusted Proposal Service. Rather than commanding raw joint angles, torques, or phase voltages, System 2 communicates exclusively by emitting structured Expiring Intent Leases:

$ cal(L)_(upright("intent")) = chevron.l cal(G)_(upright("task")) \, cal(S)_(upright("hull")) \, t_(upright("issue")) \, Delta t_(upright("lease")) \, upright(bold(w))_(upright("target")) \, cal(B)_(upright("keepout")) chevron.r $

where $cal(G)_(upright("task")) in upright(S E) \( 3 \) times bb(R)^k$ encodes the semantic sub-goal (such as a 6-DoF end-effector target pose), $cal(S)_(upright("hull")) subset bb(R)^3$ establishes a mathematically certified spatial operating hull bounding permissible linkage workspace, $t_(upright("issue")) in bb(R)^(+)$ denotes the hardware-synchronized PTP (Precision Time Protocol / IEEE 1588) timestamp of lease generation, $Delta t_(upright("lease")) in bb(R)^(+)$ specifies a finite time-to-live validity duration (typically $50 upright("–") 200 upright(" ms")$), $upright(bold(w))_(upright("target")) in bb(R)^6$ provides a target topological waypoint or desired operational-space contact wrench ($\[ F_x \, F_y \, F_z \, tau_x \, tau_y \, tau_z \]^T$ in $upright("N")$ and $upright("N") dot.op upright("m")$), and $cal(B)_(upright("keepout")) subset bb(R)^3$ specifies dynamic obstacle volumes.

To ground this mathematically, consider an autonomous manipulator inserting a battery cell: System 2 issues a lease specifying sub-goal $cal(G)_(upright("task")) = \[ 0.45 \, 0.10 \, 0.15 \]^T upright(" m")$, spatial hull $cal(S)_(upright("hull"))$ as a cylinder of radius $r = 0.50 upright(" m")$, timestamp $t_(upright("issue")) = 1718000000.120 upright(" s")$, validity $Delta t_(upright("lease")) = 100 upright(" ms")$, nominal downward seating force $upright(bold(w))_(upright("target")) = \[ 0 \, 0 \, - 2.5 upright(" N") \, 0 \, 0 \, 0 \]^T$, and keep-out region $cal(B)_(upright("keepout"))$ bounding the assembly fixture base ($z lt.eq 0.0 upright(" m")$). If an unexpected MPU garbage collection pause or CUDA kernel stall prevents System 2 from renewing the contract before $t_(upright("issue")) + Delta t_(upright("lease")) = 1718000000.220 upright(" s")$, the active lease expires deterministically, forcing downstream planners into a safe hold above the fixture without stalling the physical motor loops.

=== System 1.5: Trajectory Decoding
<system-1.5-trajectory-decoding>
Bridging the gap between discrete semantic intent and continuous mechanical motion is System 1.5, executing at a medium trajectory cadence of $20 upright(" to ") 50 upright(" Hz")$ with cycle periods of $20 upright(" to ") 50 upright(" ms")$ on dedicated Edge Neural Processing Units (NPUs) and pinned real-time Linux threads configured with #NormalTok("PREEMPT_RT"); priority scheduling and #NormalTok("mlockall"); address space locking to eliminate page faults. System 1.5 continuously ingests the active intent lease $cal(L)_(upright("intent"))$ alongside high-rate proprioceptive state estimates $upright(bold(x))_t in bb(R)^n$ to decode continuous multi-step motion sequences using Action Chunking Transformers (ACT), Diffusion Policies, or real-time non-linear Model Predictive Control (MPC). Rather than emitting discrete single-step actions that amplify network jitter and latency instability, System 1.5 generates receding-horizon trajectory chunks $tau_t = { upright(bold(q))_(t + k) \, dot(upright(bold(q)))_(t + k) \, dot.double(upright(bold(q)))_(t + k) }_(k = 1)^H$ spanning a predictive horizon of $H = 16$ waypoints.

To prevent mechanical damage, System 1.5 fits and enforces $cal(C)^2$ polynomial spline continuity across joint space, mathematically constraining the third time-derivative of position, joint jerk:

$ accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) \( t \) = frac(d^3 upright(bold(q)), d t^3) lt.eq upright(bold(j))_(upright("max")) $

where $upright(bold(q)) in bb(R)^n$ represents the joint coordinate vector in radians ($upright("rad")$), $dot(upright(bold(q)))$ is angular velocity ($upright("rad/s")$), $dot.double(upright(bold(q)))$ is acceleration ($upright("rad/s")^2$), $accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛)$ is joint jerk ($upright("rad/s")^3$), and $upright(bold(j))_(upright("max"))$ is the maximum allowable jerk threshold.

Without $cal(C)^2$ continuity, commanding a step change in joint acceleration of $Delta dot.double(q) = 20 upright(" rad/s")^2$ across a single $1 upright(" ms")$ discrete sample period generates an impulsive jerk of $accent(q, ⃛) = 20 \, 000 upright(" rad/s")^3$. For a robot arm with joint inertia $J = 0.5 upright(" kg") dot.op upright("m")^2$, this step discontinuity demands an instantaneous torque rate of $dot(tau) = J accent(q, ⃛) = 10 \, 000 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/s")$, launching acoustic shockwaves through mechanical transmissions, chipping planetary gear teeth, stripping harmonic drive flexsplines, and tripping inverter over-current protections. By enforcing $accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) \( t \) lt.eq upright(bold(j))_(upright("max")) = 100 upright(" rad/s")^3$ via quintic splines, System 1.5 bounds dynamic torque rates to $dot(tau) lt.eq 50 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/s")$, suppressing high-frequency impact stresses and eliminating inductive voltage spikes across stator windings. Operating as a Candidate Trajectory Generator, System 1.5 holds semi-trusted authority: its emitted waypoint chunks represent kinematically feasible and dynamically smooth proposals, but remain entirely subordinate to downstream real-time physical verification.

=== System 1: Real-Time Reflexes
<system-1-real-time-reflexes>
At the foundation of the physical AI hierarchy resides System 1, executing at a hard real-time reflex cadence of $1000 upright(" Hz") plus.minus 5 thin mu upright("s")$ (a strict $1.0 upright(" ms")$ loop period) on dedicated bare-metal microcontrollers, such as ARM Cortex-M7 or dual-core lockstep RISC-V cores. System 1 executes exclusively out of single-cycle Tightly Coupled Memory (ITCM/DTCM) and static SRAM under the strict mandate of Zero Dynamic Heap Allocation (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");), eliminating operating system context switches, translation lookaside buffer (TLB) misses, and memory fragmentation jitter.

The requirement for $1000 upright(" Hz")$ execution is rooted directly in frequency-domain control stability and real-time schedulability:

+ #strong[Closed-Loop Sampling Invariant:] While the classical Nyquist-Shannon sampling theorem requires $f_s gt.eq 2 f_max$ to reconstruct an open-loop continuous signal, stabilizing a closed-loop dynamical system under unmodeled external contact forces requires sampling at ten to twenty times the plant's crossover frequency: $ f_(upright("control")) gt.eq 10 upright("–") 20 times f_(upright("crossover")) $ For an articulated robotic arm with structural contact dynamics and joint compliance exhibiting a crossover frequency of $f_(upright("crossover")) approx 50 upright(" Hz")$ ($omega_c approx 314 upright(" rad/s")$), closed-loop stability dictates a reflex execution rate of $f_(upright("control")) gt.eq 500 upright("–") 1000 upright(" Hz")$.

+ #strong[Phase Lag Decomposition:] Any computational and communication delay $t_(upright("delay"))$ introduces an uncompensated phase lag proportional to the plant's crossover frequency $omega_c$: $ Delta phi.alt = - omega_c t_(upright("delay")) dot.op 180^compose / pi $ To understand phase lag intuitively, imagine steering a high-speed vehicle where the video display has a 200 ms rendering lag: when the car drifts right, by the time you observe it and steer left, the vehicle has already drifted further right; your delayed correction adds energy to the perturbation rather than damping it, sending the vehicle into wild fish-tailing oscillations. For a mechanical system with a responsive bandwidth of $omega_c = 25 upright(" rad/s")$ ($4 upright(" Hz")$) and an available phase margin of $35^compose$, a delay of $t_(upright("delay")) = 25 upright(" ms")$ (typical of non-real-time Linux stacks) introduces $Delta phi.alt approx - 35.8^compose$, consuming the entire phase margin and flipping negative stabilizing feedback into destructive limit-cycle oscillations. By executing at $1000 upright(" Hz")$ with $t_(upright("delay")) lt.eq 1.0 upright(" ms")$, System 1 restricts phase erosion to $Delta phi.alt lt.eq - 1.4^compose$, preserving robust stability margins across dynamic disturbances.

+ #strong[Rate-Monotonic Schedulability Bound:] On dedicated microcontroller firmware, hard real-time tasks are scheduled under Rate-Monotonic Scheduling (RMS), where shorter-period tasks receive higher static priority. Schedulability across $n$ periodic tasks with worst-case execution times $C_i$ and periods $T_i$ is guaranteed if total processor utilization satisfies the Liu and Layland bound: $ U = sum_(i = 1)^n C_i / T_i lt.eq n (2^(1 \/ n) - 1) arrow.r^(n arrow.r oo) ln 2 approx 0.693 $ By bounding total task utilization below $69.3 %$, System 1 mathematically guarantees that every millisecond reflex deadline is met without task preemption starvation or priority inversions.

Operating as the Sole Hardware Permission Authority, System 1 mediates all communication between upstream trajectory generators and the $20 upright(" kHz")$ Field-Oriented Control (FOC) inverter pulse-width modulation (PWM) gate drivers. Every millisecond tick, System 1 evaluates a minimal-intervention safety filter:

$ upright(bold(u))^(*) = arg min_(upright(bold(u)) in cal(U)_(upright("safe"))) 1 / 2 parallel upright(bold(u)) - upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand")) parallel^2 quad upright("subject to") quad dot(h) \( upright(bold(x)) \) gt.eq - gamma h \( upright(bold(x)) \) $

where $h \( upright(bold(x)) \) gt.eq 0$ defines a certified safe envelope (parameterized by active workspace hulls and obstacle keep-out bounds), $upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand"))$ is the candidate torque proposed by System 1.5, and $gamma > 0$ (with SI units of $upright("s")^(- 1)$) acts as a forward speed governor: when distance to an obstacle is $h \( upright(bold(x)) \) = d - d_(upright("min"))$, the condition enforces $v_(upright("approach")) = - dot(h) lt.eq gamma \( d - d_(upright("min")) \)$, smoothly throttling allowable approach velocity to zero at the boundary ($h \( upright(bold(x)) \) arrow.r 0$). For example, if $gamma = 5.0 upright(" s")^(- 1)$ and the remaining clearance to an obstacle is $h = 0.10 upright(" m")$ ($10 upright(" cm")$), the barrier condition constrains the closing speed to $v_(upright("approach")) lt.eq 5.0 times 0.10 = 0.50 upright(" m/s")$\; when clearance shrinks to $h = 0.02 upright(" m")$ ($2 upright(" cm")$), allowable speed is throttled to $0.10 upright(" m/s")$, reaching $0 upright(" m/s")$ at the boundary ($h = 0$).#footnote[The formal derivation of Control Barrier Functions, their Lie derivative expansion $L_f h \( upright(bold(x)) \) + L_g h \( upright(bold(x)) \) upright(bold(u)) gt.eq - alpha \( h \( upright(bold(x)) \) \)$, and their real-time quadratic programming implementation on microcontrollers are developed in #ref(<sec-enforcement>, supplement: [Chapter]).]

Within this sub-millisecond evaluation budget, System 1 continuously verifies dynamic stopping clearance:

$ d_(upright("clearance")) gt.eq d_(upright("stop")) \( v \) = v dot.op tau_(upright("latency")) + frac(v^2, 2 a_(upright("max"))) $

where $v$ is instantaneous velocity ($upright("m/s")$), $tau_(upright("latency"))$ is worst-case sensing transport latency ($upright("s")$), and $a_(upright("max"))$ is maximum emergency deceleration ($upright("m/s")^2$). For a robot moving at $v = 1.5 upright(" m/s")$ with sensing latency $tau_(upright("latency")) = 50 upright(" ms")$ and braking limit $a_(upright("max")) = 3.0 upright(" m/s")^2$, reaction drift burns $v tau = 0.075 upright(" m")$ ($7.5 upright(" cm")$) and quadratic deceleration requires $v^2 \/ \( 2 a_(upright("max")) \) = 0.375 upright(" m")$ ($37.5 upright(" cm")$), requiring a minimum clearance of $d_(upright("stop")) = 0.45 upright(" m")$ ($45 upright(" cm")$). Concurrently, System 1 superimposes closed-loop impedance regulation $bold(tau) = upright(bold(K))_p \( upright(bold(q))_(upright("ref")) - upright(bold(q)) \) + upright(bold(D)) \( dot(upright(bold(q)))_(upright("ref")) - dot(upright(bold(q))) \)$ (where stiffness $upright(bold(K))_p$ in $upright("N") dot.op upright("m/rad")$ acts as a virtual mechanical spring and damping $upright(bold(D))$ in $upright("N") dot.op upright("m") dot.op upright("s/rad")$ acts as a virtual dashpot) alongside dynamic contact damping $bold(tau)_(upright("damp")) = - upright(bold(D)) dot(upright(bold(q)))$ to guarantee passivity and dissipate collision kinetic energy during unexpected physical contact, dynamically clamping or projecting candidate actions into certified safe torque envelopes before energizing motor phase windings.

