- #strong[The Common Assumption:] If an engineering team quantizes a vision-language-action policy (e.g., using INT8 TensorRT) and runs it on a high-end edge accelerator alongside real-time Linux (#NormalTok("PREEMPT_RT");), the entire stack---perception, trajectory generation, and motor torque dispatch---can execute within a single synchronous 1 kHz #NormalTok("while True:"); loop.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Software quantization accelerates average execution ($P_50$) but cannot eliminate hardware non-determinism. Out-of-order execution, DRAM refresh cycles, PCIe arbitration, and OS memory compaction inevitably cause tail latency spikes ($P_99.9 > 100 upright(" ms")$). In a synchronous single-rate loop, a single 100 ms inference stall freezes the motor control loop for 100 consecutive ticks. The physical robot coasts unguided under kinetic momentum ($d_(upright("react")) = v dot.op Delta t$), inducing phase lag inversion ($Delta phi.alt = - omega t_(upright("delay"))$) and tripping over-current hardware clamps.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Physical AI demands an asynchronous multi-rate temporal hierarchy decoupled across isolated silicon domains. Millisecond motor reflexes must execute on bare-metal microcontroller cores completely independent of high-level neural inference threads.

