== The Proposal-Permission Boundary
<the-proposal-permission-boundary>
The integration of deep neural foundation models (such as Vision-Language-Action \[VLA\] architectures, diffusion-based trajectory generators, and predictive world models) into physical robotic platforms creates a fundamental architectural tension between representational expressiveness and deterministic safety. Modern neural policies require heterogeneous multi-core Application Processors (MPUs) or System-on-Chips (SoCs) equipped with specialized Neural Processing Units (NPUs) and multi-gigabyte DRAM subsystems. However, the software execution environment of these high-performance compute engines is inherently stochastic: micro-architectural bottlenecks (such as non-deterministic memory bus arbitration, DRAM page-miss refresh cycles, TLB shootdowns, and PCIe packet contention) and operating system preemption induce substantial latency variance, occasionally resulting in tail latencies exceeding several hundred milliseconds. Furthermore, neural models are fundamentally uncertified approximators prone to out-of-distribution hallucinations, floating-point divergence, and adversarial degradation. In physical systems, an unconstrained computational stall or an erratic torque command is not merely an algorithmic fault; it translates directly into accumulated kinetic energy and momentum. A 75-kilogram humanoid traversing unstructured terrain at an operational velocity of $v = 1.8 upright(" m/s")$ possesses an instantaneous translational kinetic energy of $E_k = 1 / 2 m v^2 approx 121.5 upright(" J")$ and a linear momentum of $upright(bold(p)) = 135 upright(" kg") dot.op upright("m/s")$. An unmediated 100-millisecond compute blackout introduces a physical reaction debt $d_(upright("react")) = v dot.op Delta t = 0.18 upright(" m")$, during which the system drifts ballistically, risking collision, gearbox tooth shear, or operator injury. Consequently, physical AI systems must enforce a strict Proposal--Permission Privilege Split: the upstream neural stack executing on the Linux MPU is mathematically categorized as an #emph[Untrusted Proposal Service], possessing zero authority to pulse motor inverter gates directly, while real-time authority is strictly reserved for a bare-metal Microcontroller Unit (MCU) operating as a #emph[Deterministic Permission Authority].

The formal contract governing this privilege boundary is the #emph[Expiring Intent Lease] ($cal(L)_(upright("intent"))$), a cryptographically validated, temporally bounded tuple transmitted across the physical communication bus (such as PCIe, SPI, or TSN-Ethernet) from the untrusted proposal engine to the real-time permission kernel:

$ cal(L)_(upright("intent")) = chevron.l cal(G)_(upright("task")) \, cal(S)_(upright("hull")) \, t_(upright("issue")) \, Delta t_(upright("lease")) \, upright(bold(w))_(upright("target")) \, cal(B)_(upright("keepout")) chevron.r $

In this formulation, $cal(G)_(upright("task")) in upright(S E) \( 3 \) times bb(R)^k$ represents the high-level task goal parameterizing the 3D position and orientation of the end-effector pose manifold or footstep landing zone, where $upright(S E) \( 3 \)$ denotes the special Euclidean group of 3D rigid transformations and $upright(S O) \( 3 \)$ denotes the special orthogonal group of 3D rotations. The set $cal(S)_(upright("hull")) subset bb(R)^3 times upright(S O) \( 3 \)$ defines a certified spatial convex hull or swept-sphere volume within which the robot's entire kinematic chain is authorized to operate. The hardware timestamp $t_(upright("issue")) in bb(R)^(+)$ denotes the synchronized origin time of the proposal measured against a sub-microsecond IEEE 1588 Precision Time Protocol (PTP) master clock, and $Delta t_(upright("lease")) in bb(R)^(+)$ defines the strict validity lifetime (typically configured between 50 and 200 milliseconds) establishing an uncompromising time-to-live after which the lease irrevocably self-terminates. The vector $upright(bold(w))_(upright("target")) in bb(R)^6$ specifies the nominal desired task-space wrench (the 6-dimensional vector of 3D linear forces and 3D rotational torques $\[ F_x \, F_y \, F_z \, tau_x \, tau_y \, tau_z \]^T$) or operational-space trajectory waypoint sequence, and $cal(B)_(upright("keepout")) subset bb(R)^3$ denotes dynamic obstacle volumes and prohibited self-collision configurations. The intent lease encapsulates bounded intent rather than open-ended control: if the upstream Linux process experiences an unhandled kernel exception, memory thrashing, or thermal throttling, the lease deterministically lapses without requiring any affirmative abort signal from the faulted MPU, guaranteeing that silence from the neural policy results in fail-safe deceleration rather than open-loop runaway.

To guarantee physical safety, the deterministic permission authority executes a fixed-period (1 kHz) interrupt service routine (ISR) on an isolated real-time microcontroller core (e.g., an Arm Cortex-M7 or lockstep RISC-V processor executing from static SRAM). Every millisecond tick, the MCU evaluates four non-negotiable runtime invariants before allowing any candidate control input $upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand"))$ to energize the motor commutators:

+ #strong[Temporal Freshness:] The instantaneous hardware time $t_(upright("now"))$ must satisfy $t_(upright("now")) - t_(upright("issue")) lt.eq Delta t_(upright("lease"))$. If an upstream computation stall or network drop causes the lease to lapse, the MCU immediately triggers a deterministic fallback deceleration. Silence from the proposal engine triggers safety.

+ #strong[Workspace Boundedness (Geofence):] The proposed operational target and forward kinematic swept volume must reside strictly inside the authorized spatial envelope and outside all keep-out zones: $upright(bold(p))_(upright("target")) in cal(S)_(upright("hull")) \\ cal(B)_(upright("keepout"))$.

+ #strong[Dynamic Stopping Clearance:] The available physical clearance to an obstacle $d_(upright("clearance"))$ must strictly exceed the robot's physical stopping distance under maximum emergency braking deceleration: $ d_(upright("clearance")) gt.eq d_(upright("stop")) \( v \) = v dot.op tau_(upright("latency")) + frac(v^2, 2 a_(upright("max"))) $ where $v$ is the instantaneous velocity, $tau_(upright("latency"))$ is the sensing and communication latency delay, and $a_(upright("max"))$ is the maximum allowable deceleration bounded by actuator torque and traction limits.

+ #strong[Forward Safety Barrier (Speed Governor):] For every physical safety boundary modeled by a barrier function $h \( upright(bold(x)) \) gt.eq 0$ (where $h \( upright(bold(x)) \) > 0$ represents the safe operating region and $h \( upright(bold(x)) \) = 0$ is the boundary), the system's approach velocity must satisfy: $ dot(h) \( upright(bold(x)) \) gt.eq - gamma thin h \( upright(bold(x)) \) $ where $gamma > 0$ is a tunable decay parameter. Intuitively, this invariant acts as an automatic speed governor: when the robot is far from danger ($h gt.double 0$), it is free to move at high speed ($dot(h)$ can be negative). As the robot approaches the safety boundary ($h arrow.r 0$), the maximum allowable approach speed towards the obstacle is continuously throttled down to zero, guaranteeing that the machine cannot cross into unsafe territory.#footnote[in #ref(<sec-enforcement>, supplement: [Chapter]), we will explore the complete control-theoretic formulation of Control Barrier Functions (CBFs), their Lie derivative expansions $L_f h \( upright(bold(x)) \) + L_g h \( upright(bold(x)) \) upright(bold(u)) gt.eq - alpha \( h \( upright(bold(x)) \) \)$, and how microcontrollers solve quadratic programs deterministically in static SRAM with zero dynamic heap allocation.]

When all four invariants are satisfied, the Permission Authority operationalizes the candidate action through a real-time #strong[Minimal-Intervention Safety Filter]. Rather than discarding neural proposals or overriding them unnecessarily, the safety filter solves a convex projection for the closest admissible control command $upright(bold(u))^(*)$ in the safe control space $cal(U)_(upright("safe"))$:

$ upright(bold(u))^(*) = arg min_(upright(bold(u)) in cal(U)_(upright("safe"))) 1 / 2 parallel upright(bold(u)) - upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand")) parallel^2 $

This filter embodies the core pair-programming philosophy of Physical AI: during nominal safe operation, when the neural foundation model proposes physically safe trajectories ($upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand")) in cal(U)_(upright("safe"))$), the filter evaluates to the identity projection ($upright(bold(u))^(*) = upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand"))$), fully preserving the behavioral dexterity and compliance learned by the AI. During boundary interventions, when the neural policy hallucinates an unsafe action, drifts out-of-distribution, or approaches an obstacle too quickly, the filter minimally modifies the control command, projecting it onto the nearest safe boundary in under $150 thin mu upright("s")$ within tightly coupled SRAM.

