When an intent lease deterministically expires without renewal, or when aggressive external disturbances render the QP constraint set empty, the permission authority executes an escalation hierarchy structured across standardized functional safety stop categories (#ref(<tbl-safety-stop-escalation>, supplement: [Table])). The initial response is a Category 2 stop, representing a controlled stop where power to the actuator inverter bridges is fully retained: the MCU freezes the target configuration at the boundary of the last valid state $upright(bold(q)) \( t_(upright("freeze")) \)$ and executes localized, high-bandwidth closed-loop impedance regulation to hold posture against gravity while awaiting the arrival of a fresh, valid lease. If the lease lapse persists beyond a secondary threshold $tau_(upright("fault")) > Delta t_(upright("lease"))$, or if the clearance margin continues to degrade, the system escalates to a Category 1 stop (Safe Stop 1, SS1), wherein the MCU commands a certified maximum-torque decelerating trajectory along an admissible braking manifold until total kinetic energy collapses to zero, subsequently engaging mechanical spring-applied friction brakes and de-energizing the pulse-width modulation (PWM) drivers. Finally, upon detection of unrecoverable internal anomalies (such as lockstep core comparison mismatches, DC bus over-current surges, or physical limit switch trips), the system instantly trips a Category 0 stop (Safe Torque Off, STO). In Category 0, dual-channel hardware safety relays physically sever the high-voltage DC supply to the three-phase bridge inverters within microseconds, collapsing the rotating magnetic field and isolating the motor windings from electrical power, ensuring that no software defect, operating system panic, or neural network hallucination can violate the invariant laws of physical mechanics.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] An autonomous system can issue open-ended velocity, position, or torque setpoints to low-level motor controllers, relying on the high-level neural autonomy stack or a supervisory thread to transmit an explicit "abort" or "emergency stop" command when a failure occurs.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] If the high-level application processor experiences an out-of-memory kernel panic, enters an unhandled garbage collection pause, locks up on a socket read, or suffers thermal downclocking, it ceases executing instructions. Because the faulted host cannot transmit an affirmative abort command, the low-level controller continues executing the last latched setpoint indefinitely, driving the physical robot at full power into obstacles, hardstops, or human operators.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] All high-level proposals must be issued as temporally bounded Expiring Intent Leases ($cal(L)_(upright("intent"))$) with hard hardware-enforced expiration times ($Delta t_(upright("lease")) lt.eq 100 upright(" ms")$). Silence from the proposal engine is an immediate fail-safe trigger; if a lease is not proactively renewed before expiration, the real-time microcontroller's hardware watchdog timer deterministically transitions actuators to a safe holding state.

