== Systems Stress-Tests: Architectural Food for Thought
<systems-stress-tests-architectural-food-for-thought-1>
To master multi-rate systems engineering across heterogeneous silicon and physical dynamics, analyze the following four high-stakes architectural dilemmas.

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 1: The Shared UMA Memory Crossbar and the 4K Ingestion Dilemma]
<stress-test-1-the-shared-uma-memory-crossbar-and-the-4k-ingestion-dilemma>
An autonomous humanoid robot's head incorporates four $4 upright("K")$ 60 FPS global-shutter perception cameras streaming uncompressed raw Bayer data directly into a shared LPDDR5 DRAM subsystem across an ARM AXI on-die crossbar ($48.0 upright(" GB/s")$ physical bandwidth). The compute SoC hosts:

- A high-capacity 7B Vision-Language-Action foundation model (System 2, running on an NPU cluster).
- A 50 Hz diffusion trajectory generator (System 1.5, running on an embedded GPU).
- A 1000 Hz real-time safety enforcer (System 1, running on an isolated Cortex-R5 real-time core).

The four camera DMA engines write a continuous $1.49 upright(" GB/s") times 4 approx 5.96 upright(" GB/s")$ to DRAM. Simultaneously, the VLA foundation model performs autoregressive token generation, bursting $32.0 upright(" GB/s")$ of DRAM reads to fetch KV-cache lines and weight matrices, while the diffusion trajectory planner reads $12.0 upright(" GB/s")$ for denoising iterations. Total memory demand spikes to $49.96 upright(" GB/s")$, exceeding the physical DRAM bandwidth by $4.1 %$. The memory controller arbiter enters read-queue saturation.

+ The Cortex-R5 microcontroller, attempting to read the latest joint telemetry payload from a shared DRAM mailbox, experiences an arbitration stall of $1.6 upright(" ms")$, missing its $1.0 upright(" ms")$ real-time control tick. Why does a standard Unified Memory Architecture (UMA) fail in real-time robotics despite having zero-copy software interfaces?
+ How do you configure hardware AXI Quality-of-Service (QoS) registers, non-cacheable DMA memory partitions, and dedicated on-chip Tightly Coupled Memory (TCM/SRAM) to guarantee that the 1000 Hz Control Barrier Function safety enforcer achieves zero-wait-state memory access even when the DRAM crossbar is 100% saturated?

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 2: The Intent Lease Expiry vs.~Dynamic Equilibrium Paradox]
<stress-test-2-the-intent-lease-expiry-vs.-dynamic-equilibrium-paradox>
A bipedal humanoid ($m = 70 upright(" kg")$) is ascending an uneven concrete staircase at $v = 0.8 upright(" m/s")$. The robot is in single-stance phase on its right foot, dynamically balancing its center of mass ($C o M$) forward over the edge of the next stair riser.

The upstream System 2 VLA planner, running on the Linux host, encounters a dynamic memory allocation heap fragmentation stall, delaying the next Expiring Intent Lease by $85 upright(" ms")$. The active intent lease ($Delta t_(upright("lease")) = 100 upright(" ms")$) expires. Under Safety Policy A (Strict Safe Stop 1 / Category 1), the System 1 safety supervisor detects lease expiry and triggers an immediate controlled dynamic brake to rest ($v arrow.r 0$); however, because the robot is in single-stance dynamic fall, bringing joint velocities to zero freezes the stance leg, causing the body momentum ($p = m v$) to pivot over the foot and pitch violently down the staircase. Under Safety Policy B (Open-Ended Horizon Extrapolation), the controller continues forward extrapolation along the old trajectory; however, the stair geometry shifts $5 upright(" cm")$ to the left, which the expired intent lease did not account for.

+ How must the System 1 Reflex Tier be architected with capture point (CP) and zero-moment point (ZMP) dynamic balance controllers so that upon lease expiration, it completes the current dynamic stepping phase to achieve a stable dual-stance equilibrium rather than executing a naive kinematic joint freeze?
+ How should the Expiring Intent Lease tuple ($cal(L)_(upright("intent"))$) formally specify dynamic equilibrium recovery envelopes ($cal(S)_(upright("hull"))$) for legged locomotion versus wheeled mobility?

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 3: The Thermal DVFS Cascade and the Shrinking Action Horizon]
<stress-test-3-the-thermal-dvfs-cascade-and-the-shrinking-action-horizon>
A mobile dual-arm manipulator is performing hazardous chemical decontamination. The System 1.5 diffusion policy generates 16-step action chunks ($H = 16$, spanning an $800 upright(" ms")$ horizon at $20 upright(" Hz")$ execution, with nominal inference time $t_(upright("inference")) = 35 upright(" ms")$).

Ambient room temperature rises to $45^compose upright("C")$, pushing the embedded GPU to $95^compose upright("C")$ and triggering hardware Dynamic Voltage and Frequency Scaling (DVFS). The GPU clock frequency is halved from $1.3 upright(" GHz")$ to $650 upright(" MHz")$, inflating diffusion inference time from $35 upright(" ms")$ to $110 upright(" ms")$. Because the policy runs at $20 upright(" Hz")$ ($50 upright(" ms")$ period), it cannot complete inference within a single cycle. The System 1 queue buffer drains faster than the planner can publish new action chunks (consumer starvation), forcing the low-level controller into an empty mailbox.

+ If the MCU uses polynomial extrapolation beyond the intended $800 upright(" ms")$ chunk horizon, spatial drift accumulates quadratically ($Delta x approx 1 / 2 dot.double(upright(bold(q))) t^2$), risking collisions. If it triggers Category 2 holding brakes every $100 upright(" ms")$, the arm chatters violently, inducing massive mechanical fatigue in harmonic drives. How should this failure mode be resolved?
+ How should a multi-rate system implement adaptive horizon scaling---reducing diffusion denoising steps (e.g., from 16 to 4 steps) or shortening action chunk horizons ($H$)---to maintain deterministic temporal throughput under silicon thermal throttling?

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 4: The Clock Skew Drift and Cross-Cadence Causality Inversion]
<stress-test-4-the-clock-skew-drift-and-cross-cadence-causality-inversion>
A multi-joint industrial manipulator coordinates its joint controllers across an IEEE 802.1AS Time-Sensitive Networking (TSN) Ethernet bus. The host MPU runs an open-loop trajectory publisher on Linux, while six individual joint microcontrollers execute Field-Oriented Control at 10 kHz.

Due to an uncalibrated crystal oscillator drift ($+ 60 upright(" ppm")$) on Joint 3's microcontroller, its local hardware clock advances by $216 upright(" ms")$ over one hour relative to the master PTP clock. When the MPU dispatches a multi-joint trajectory payload timestamped with $t_(upright("issue")) = T_(upright("master"))$, Joint 3 evaluates the temporal freshness check: $Delta t = t_(upright("local")) - t_(upright("issue"))$. Because $t_(upright("local")) > t_(upright("issue")) + Delta t_(upright("lease"))$, Joint 3 rejects the valid trajectory as stale and trips an emergency stop, while Joints 1, 2, 4, 5, and 6 continue tracking the trajectory. The resulting asymmetric joint torque shears the mechanical end-effector workpiece.

+ How must hardware timestamps, monotonic sequence IDs (#NormalTok("write_seq");, #NormalTok("read_seq");), and hardware PTP grandmaster synchronization be bound together within lock-free SRAM mailboxes to make multi-rate execution immune to oscillator drift and clock skew?
+ Why is sequence-number monotonic ordering strictly superior to raw wall-clock time comparisons when certifying causality across asynchronous processor cores?

