=== Rolling Shutter Geometric Distortion
<rolling-shutter-geometric-distortion>
The geometric distortion introduced by optical transduction is compounded by the electronic shutter architecture. While intra-row exposure integration ($t_(upright("exp"))$) creates local edge smearing, standard commercial CMOS image sensors introduce a distinct spatial distortion through sequential row-by-row scanning. Rolling shutters expose and read out pixel lines sequentially from top to bottom across the silicon array. The time delay between reading the first row and the final row (the frame readout time $t_(upright("readout")) approx 10 upright("–") 33 upright(" ms")$) introduces substantial intra-frame temporal skew:

$ Delta theta_(upright("shear")) = parallel bold(omega) parallel dot.op t_(upright("readout")) $

- #strong[Physical Hazard & Motivation:] Under rapid platform rotation, top and bottom rows capture the physical world from different body orientations, bending vertical pillars into curved parabolas and destroying epipolar geometry.

- #strong[Variable & Unit Breakdown:] $parallel bold(omega) parallel$ is the angular yaw/pitch rate ($upright("rad/s")$), $t_(upright("readout"))$ is the total frame scan time ($upright("s")$), and $Delta theta_(upright("shear"))$ is the angular skew across the frame ($upright("rad")$ or degrees).

- #strong[Concrete Numerical Example:] If an autonomous mobile robot rotates at an angular velocity of $omega = 2.0 upright(" rad/s")$ ($114.6^compose \/ upright("s")$) while acquiring a rolling-shutter image with $t_(upright("readout")) = 30 upright(" ms")$, the top and bottom rows diverge by $Delta theta_(upright("shear")) = 2.0 times 0.030 = 0.060 upright(" rad") approx 3.44^compose$. At a working distance of $Z = 2.0 upright(" m")$, this angular distortion translates to a lateral displacement error of $Delta x approx Z tan \( Delta theta_(upright("shear")) \) approx 2.0 times 0.060 = 12.0 upright(" cm")$. In the resulting image, straight vertical structural pillars appear sheared and curved, circular wheels distort into slanted ellipses, and epipolar geometric constraints collapse. Consequently, high-speed embodied platforms must mandate global shutter sensors (wherein every pixel across the array captures charge simultaneously) or apply microsecond-level hardware gyroscope interpolation to unwarp rolling-shutter geometric distortion row by row.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] Standard computer vision pipelines assume that an acquired image matrix $I in bb(R)^(H times W times C)$ represents an instantaneous, globally consistent spatial snapshot of the physical scene frozen at a single timestamp $t$. Triangulation and ray unprojection treat all pixel rays as originating from a single camera pose $upright(bold(T))_(upright("cam")) \( t \) in S E \( 3 \)$.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] CMOS rolling-shutter sensors expose and read out pixel rows sequentially across a finite readout duration ($t_(upright("readout")) approx 10 upright("–") 33 upright(" ms")$). If an embodied platform undergoes angular rotation ($omega$) or high linear acceleration during readout, each row $r$ captures the physical environment from a distinct spatial vantage point $upright(bold(T))_(upright("cam")) \( t_0 + r dot.op Delta t_(upright("row")) \)$. Under a moderate yaw rate of $omega = 2.0 upright(" rad/s")$, top and bottom rows diverge by $Delta theta approx 3.44^compose$, shearing vertical structural supports into curved parabolas and distorting epipolar lines. Unprojecting these warped pixels with a single static camera matrix hallucinates non-existent obstacle geometry and miscalculates grasp target normals by tens of degrees.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] For any physical platform operating with angular rates exceeding $omega dot.op t_(upright("readout")) > theta_(upright("tol"))$ (where $theta_(upright("tol"))$ is the maximum allowable angular calibration error), the perception architecture must either mandate global-shutter sensor hardware or execute line-by-line geometric unwarping by interpolating high-rate ($1000 upright(" Hz")$) IMU gyroscope poses in Lie algebra tangent space $frak(s o) \( 3 \)$ before forwarding frames to spatial encoders.

