=== Non-Visual Transduction Physics and IMU Drift
<non-visual-transduction-physics-and-imu-drift>
Beyond optical cameras, physical transduction mechanisms exhibit unique physical failure modes across non-visual modalities (#ref(<tbl-sensory-modalities>, supplement: [Table])):

+ #strong[Pulsed LiDAR and Time-of-Flight (ToF):] LiDAR sensors emit high-peak-power near-infrared laser pulses ($lambda in { 905 \, 1550 } upright(" nm")$) and measure round-trip time of flight via $d = frac(c dot.op Delta t_(upright("flight")), 2)$. While LiDAR yields direct metric depth, it suffers from severe physical edge cases: specular multi-path reflections on wet asphalt or polished steel reflect laser energy away from the receiver, retroreflective traffic signs saturate photodiode transimpedance amplifiers, and mixed-pixel averaging (occurring when the finite laser beam divergence footprint splits across foreground-background depth silhouettes) injects phantom points floating in free space.
+ #strong[Tactile and Contact Transducers:] Elastomeric cameras (e.g., GelSight) and piezoresistive sensor arrays measure contact pressure and surface shear stress. However, synthetic silicone elastomers exhibit viscoelastic relaxation dynamics and mechanical hysteresis. When a robotic finger breaks contact with a manipulated surface, the elastomer requires tens to hundreds of milliseconds to fully recover its resting geometric profile, generating persistent phantom contact forces that mislead downstream tactile slip controllers.
+ #strong[MEMS Inertial Measurement Units (IMUs):] Silicon micro-machined proof masses and vibrating tuning forks transduce specific force and angular velocity. However, thermal gradients across the silicon substrate and mechanical packaging stresses induce time-varying zero-rate bias drift ($b_a \( t \)$ and $b_g \( t \)$). The stochastic noise profile of an IMU is characterized through Allan Variance analysis ($sigma^2 \( tau \)$), which plots error variance against averaging cluster time $tau$: short cluster windows reveal high-frequency white noise (angle random walk), intermediate plateaus expose flicker bias instability, and long windows capture slow thermal ramp drift (rate random walk). Integrating uncalibrated accelerometer signals produces an unconstrained positional error that grows quadratically with elapsed time:

$ delta upright(bold(p)) \( t \) = 1 / 2 upright(bold(b))_a t^2 $

- #strong[Physical Hazard & Motivation:] Open-loop integration of MEMS accelerometer biases produces positional drift that explodes quadratically over time, rendering inertial dead reckoning blind within seconds.

- #strong[Variable & Unit Breakdown:] $upright(bold(b))_a in bb(R)^3$ is the unmodeled accelerometer zero-rate bias vector ($upright("m/s")^2$), $t$ is the elapsed integration time ($upright("s")$), and $delta upright(bold(p)) \( t \)$ is the resulting metric positional error ($upright("m")$).

- #strong[Concrete Numerical Example:] For a commercial MEMS accelerometer with a modest bias offset of $parallel upright(bold(b))_a parallel = 0.05 upright(" m/s")^2$ ($approx 5.1 upright(" mg")$), integrating over $t = 10 upright(" s")$ yields: $ parallel delta upright(bold(p)) \( 10 upright(" s") \) parallel = 1 / 2 (0.05 upright(" m/s")^2) (10 upright(" s"))^2 = upright(bold(2.5 upright(" meters"))) $ Without continuous fusion with external absolute spatial references (such as visual odometry, LiDAR scan-matching, or GPS), an autonomous mobile agent will drift by multiple meters in seconds, turning open-loop dead reckoning into catastrophic collision.

