== The Memory Ingestion Tax
<the-memory-ingestion-tax>
In academic deep learning, loading sensory data into a neural network is treated as an instantaneous software abstraction (#NormalTok("batch = dataloader.next()");). In embedded Physical AI systems, sensory data ingestion represents a continuous physical tax on silicon memory buses, cache hierarchies, and interconnect crossbars. Modern edge computing platforms (such as automotive SoCs and mobile robotics compute boards) universally employ Unified Memory Architectures (UMA). Under UMA, the multi-core central processing unit (CPU), deep neural processing unit (NPU), graphics processor (GPU), hardware Image Signal Processor (ISP), and Direct Memory Access (DMA) controllers all share a common physical pool of LPDDR5 dynamic RAM through an on-chip crossbar switch.

=== Multi-Camera Ingestion Bandwidth
<multi-camera-ingestion-bandwidth>
To quantify the magnitude of this sensory deluge, consider the raw memory bandwidth required to ingest uncompressed multi-camera video streams across standard autonomous perception configurations:

$ upright("Bandwidth (GB/s)") = upright("Width") times upright("Height") times upright("Bytes/Pixel") times upright("FPS") times N_(upright("cameras")) times 10^(- 9) $

- #strong[Physical Hazard & Motivation:] Streaming high-resolution camera feeds consumes significant DRAM bus capacity, competing with neural accelerator weight fetches and triggering non-deterministic latency spikes in real-time control loops.
- #strong[Variable & Unit Breakdown:]
  - $upright("Width") \, upright("Height")$: Frame dimensions in pixels ($W \, H$).
  - $upright("Bytes/Pixel")$: Memory payload per pixel ($B_(upright("pixel"))$), e.g., $4 upright(" B")$ for RGBA8888 or uncompressed RAW/RGB32, $2 upright(" B")$ for YUV422.
  - $upright("FPS")$: Ingestion frame rate ($upright("Hz")$ or $upright("frames/s")$).
  - $N_(upright("cameras"))$: Count of concurrent synchronized video streams.
- #strong[Concrete Numerical Example:] $ upright("BW")_(upright("720p")) & = 2 times \( 1280 times 720 times 4 upright(" B") \) times 30 upright(" Hz") = 221.2 upright(" MB/s") &  & \( 0.65 % upright(" of ") 34 upright(" GB/s") \)\
  upright("BW")_(upright("1080p")) & = 2 times \( 1920 times 1080 times 4 upright(" B") \) times 60 upright(" Hz") = 995.3 upright(" MB/s") &  & \( 2.93 % upright(" of ") 34 upright(" GB/s") \)\
  upright("BW")_(upright("4K")) & = 2 times \( 3840 times 2160 times 4 upright(" B") \) times 60 upright(" Hz") = upright(bold(3.98 upright(" GB/s"))) &  & \( upright(bold(11.7 % upright(" of ") 34 upright(" GB/s"))) \) $

=== Silicon DRAM Contention Mechanics
<silicon-dram-contention-mechanics>
While an ingestion rate of $3.98 upright(" GB/s")$ appears safely below the nominal theoretical rating of a $34 upright(" GB/s")$ LPDDR5 memory bus, dynamic RAM is not an idealized continuous pipe. Physical DRAM memory controllers organize access around memory channels, ranks, banks, rows, and columns. Picture each DRAM bank as a row of filing cabinet drawers: to read or write data, the controller must physically open a specific drawer (Row Activation, #NormalTok("ACT");, taking $tilde.op 12 upright(" ns")$). If the next request targets the same open drawer, it is a #emph[row-buffer hit] with minimal latency. But if the next access targets a different row in the same bank, the controller must close the active drawer (Row Precharge, #NormalTok("PRE");) before opening the new one (#emph[row-buffer conflict/miss]), imposing a penalty of $t_(upright("RP")) + t_(upright("RCD")) approx 30 upright("–") 50 upright(" ns")$. Furthermore, switching the bidirectional data bus from DMA write bursts to NPU read bursts requires bus turnaround dead cycles ($t_(upright("WTR"))$).

Because camera sensor DMA engines continuously stream uncached pixel bytes across wide physical memory strides, they force the memory controller to constantly switch active rows across all DRAM banks. When the Neural Processing Unit (NPU) attempts to fetch the $350 upright(" MB")$ parameter weights of a Vision Transformer (ViT) at $25 upright(" GB/s")$ to perform real-time patch tokenization, the memory controller encounters severe bank conflicts, row-buffer misses, and bus turnaround stalls ($t_(upright("WTR"))$). Hardware execution pipelines inside the NPU stall in wait-states, waiting for DRAM data lines to clear. Under heavy camera DMA traffic, neural inference tail latency inflates by over six-fold from a nominal median of $P_50 = 22 upright(" ms")$ ($45 upright(" Hz")$) to an unmanageable tail of $P_99.9 = 135 upright(" ms")$ ($7.4 upright(" Hz")$). For an autonomous robot traveling at $2.0 upright(" m/s")$, this latency spike means the vehicle travels nearly $30 upright(" cm")$ completely blind before updating its spatial world model, collapsing feedback phase margins and inducing dangerous trajectory overshoot.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] Edge compute platforms with Unified Memory Architectures (UMA) advertise aggregate LPDDR5 bandwidths of $34 upright("–") 68 upright(" GB/s")$. Streaming dual uncompressed 4K video feeds requires $3.98 upright(" GB/s")$, consuming less than $12 %$ of total theoretical bus capacity. Software engineers assume the remaining $88 %$ bandwidth is fully available for neural accelerator inference.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Dynamic RAM is organized into channels, banks, rows, and columns. Continuous DMA writes from camera ISP engines stream uncached pixel payloads across wide physical memory strides, forcing memory controllers to constantly switch active rows via activate (#NormalTok("ACT");) and precharge (#NormalTok("PRE");) commands. When the NPU initiates high-speed burst reads ($25 upright(" GB/s")$) to stream $350 upright(" MB")$ of Vision Transformer weights, it encounters persistent bank conflicts and write-to-read turnaround delays ($t_(upright("WTR"))$). Under default round-robin AXI bus arbitration, unthrottled camera DMA writes starve NPU weight fetches, causing compute pipelines to stall and blowing out $P_99.9$ inference latency by over $500 %$ ($22 upright(" ms") arrow.r 135 upright(" ms")$).
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] All sensory ingestion pipelines on shared UMA memory buses must enforce hardware-level AXI Quality-of-Service (QoS) bus arbitration (#NormalTok("ARQOS");/#NormalTok("AWQOS");), assigning higher bus priority to real-time safety reflexes (#NormalTok("QoS=15");) and neural weight fetches (#NormalTok("QoS=10");) than to camera ingestion DMA writes (#NormalTok("QoS=4");). Ingested streams must be mapped to pinned, uncached write-combining ring buffers (#NormalTok("dma_alloc_coherent");) to prevent cache thrashing and DRAM bank saturation.

