To prevent sensory ingestion from choking real-time cognitive deliberation, Physical AI architectures implement the #strong[Zero-Copy DMA Ring Buffer Architecture] (#ref(<fig-zerocopy-dma>, supplement: [Figure])):

+ #strong[Kernel Pinned Coherent Allocations:] Rather than allocating memory dynamically in virtual user space via #NormalTok("malloc()");, the ingestion driver allocates physically contiguous DRAM buffers at boot time using the Linux kernel #NormalTok("dma_alloc_coherent()"); interface (via the Contiguous Memory Allocator, CMA). This eliminates Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) misses, guarantees hardware DMA controllers receive direct physical bus addresses, and prevents dynamic memory fragmentation and page-fault latency spikes during runtime execution.
+ #strong[Write-Combining Cache Bypass:] Ingested pixel frames are mapped into memory with uncached, write-combining (WC) page attributes. The hardware MIPI CSI-2 receiver and ISP DMA engines stream incoming pixel payloads directly into physical DRAM without polluting the CPU L1, L2, or L3 cache lines, preserving on-chip cache capacity for neural network activations and real-time control threads.
+ #strong[Hardware AXI Quality-of-Service (QoS) Prioritization:] The SoC on-chip system bus (ARM AMBA AXI crossbar) is configured with explicit, hardware-enforced QoS priority registers (#NormalTok("ARQOS"); for read transactions, #NormalTok("AWQOS"); for write transactions on a 0--15 scale):
  - #strong[Real-Time Safety Reflex (MCU):] #NormalTok("QoS = 15"); (Maximum priority; zero-wait bus arbitration for motor control and safety barrier functions).
  - #strong[Neural Accelerator Weight Streaming (NPU):] #NormalTok("QoS = 10"); (High priority; guaranteed DRAM read bandwidth for deterministic transformer inference).
  - #strong[Sensor Ingestion DMA (ISP / CSI-2):] #NormalTok("QoS = 4"); (Throttled burst writes; buffered in on-chip ISP SRAM FIFOs during active NPU compute bursts).

By enforcing hardware-level bus privilege separation, the system guarantees that camera sensor data ingestion can never starve neural weight streaming or delay safety-critical motor reflex commands.

== 3D Spatial Scene Representations
<d-spatial-scene-representations>
Once sensory bytes are safely ingested into physical DRAM, the perception organ must structure the spatial information into a representation suitable for downstream cognition, world modeling, and kinematic planning. In classical 2D computer vision, images are represented as dense planar tensors $I in bb(R)^(H times W times C)$. While 2D pixel grids excel at semantic classification and text-prompted segmentation, they completely lack Euclidean depth, scale, and volume. An autonomous robot cannot command a joint trajectory based on a 2D bounding box; it requires physical metric geometry in the Special Euclidean group $S E \( 3 \)$.

To bridge this representational gap, Physical AI systems utilize a spectrum of spatial representation paradigms, each trading off metric precision, memory footprint, update latency, and query efficiency (#ref(<tbl-spatial-representations>, supplement: [Table])).

