=== Signed Distance Fields for Real-Time Safety Control
<signed-distance-fields-for-real-time-safety-control>
In contact manipulation and trajectory optimization, the #strong[Signed Distance Field (SDF)] occupies a privileged role. For any query point $upright(bold(x)) in bb(R)^3$, the continuous scalar function $phi.alt \( upright(bold(x)) \)$ computes the signed Euclidean distance to the nearest obstacle boundary $partial cal(O)$:

$ phi.alt \( upright(bold(x)) \) = cases(delim: "{", + min_(upright(bold(y)) in partial cal(O)) parallel upright(bold(x)) - upright(bold(y)) parallel & upright("if ") upright(bold(x)) in cal(F) upright(" (Free space)"), 0 & upright("if ") upright(bold(x)) in partial cal(O) upright(" (Boundary)"), - min_(upright(bold(y)) in partial cal(O)) parallel upright(bold(x)) - upright(bold(y)) parallel & upright("if ") upright(bold(x)) in cal(O) upright(" (Inside obstacle)")) $

- #strong[Physical Hazard & Motivation:] Real-time safety controllers (such as Control Barrier Functions running at $1000 upright(" Hz")$ on bare-metal microcontrollers) cannot afford the non-deterministic memory access and pointer-chasing overhead of $k upright("-d")$ tree point cloud searches ($cal(O) \( log N \)$). Signed Distance Fields provide deterministic $cal(O) \( 1 \)$ distance evaluations and exact analytic gradient vectors for repulsive barrier generation.
- #strong[Variable & Unit Breakdown:]
  - $upright(bold(x)) in bb(R)^3$: 3D query coordinate in robot workspace coordinates ($upright("m")$).
  - $partial cal(O)$: Physical surface boundary of all environmental obstacles.
  - $cal(F)$: Unoccupied free space ($upright(bold(x)) in.not cal(O)$).
  - $phi.alt \( upright(bold(x)) \)$: Signed Euclidean distance ($upright("m")$). Positive in free space, zero at obstacle boundaries, negative within obstacle interiors.
  - $nabla phi.alt \( upright(bold(x)) \)$: Spatial gradient vector. Because the Euclidean SDF satisfies the Eikonal equation $parallel nabla phi.alt \( upright(bold(x)) \) parallel = 1$ almost everywhere, the gradient vector directly yields the outward unit surface normal: $hat(upright(bold(n))) \( upright(bold(x)) \) = nabla phi.alt \( upright(bold(x)) \)$.
- #strong[Concrete Numerical Example:] A robotic end-effector at query position $upright(bold(x)) = \[ 0.42 \, 0.18 \, 0.35 \]^T upright(" m")$ queries a local $64 times 64 times 64$ voxel SDF grid ($1 upright(" cm")$ voxel pitch, $512 upright(" KB")$ table in ARM Cortex-M7 SRAM) near a cylindrical structural column of radius $R = 0.05 upright(" m")$ centered at $\[ 0.50 \, 0.20 \, 0.35 \]^T upright(" m")$.
  + The radial distance to the column axis is $r = sqrt(\( 0.42 - 0.50 \)^2 + \( 0.18 - 0.20 \)^2) = sqrt(\( - 0.08 \)^2 + \( - 0.02 \)^2) = sqrt(0.0068) approx 0.0825 upright(" m")$.

  + The signed surface clearance evaluates to: $ phi.alt \( upright(bold(x)) \) = r - R = 0.0825 upright(" m") - 0.0500 upright(" m") = upright(bold(0.0325 upright(" m"))) = 3.25 upright(" cm") $

  + The unit normal gradient pointing outward away from the column is: $ nabla phi.alt \( upright(bold(x)) \) = frac(\[ 0.42 - 0.50 \, thin 0.18 - 0.20 \, thin 0 \]^T, 0.0825) = mat(delim: "[", - 0.970; - 0.242; 0.000) $

  + When evaluated by a Control Barrier Function enforcer with stiffness gain $k_(upright("rep")) = 500 upright(" N/m")$ and safety margin $d_(upright("safe")) = 0.05 upright(" m")$ ($5 upright(" cm")$), the repulsive barrier force evaluates in $cal(O) \( 1 \)$ time ($< 1 thin mu upright("s")$ via 8 SRAM loads and 7 multiply-accumulate operations): $ upright(bold(F))_(upright("rep")) = - k_(upright("rep")) \( phi.alt \( upright(bold(x)) \) - d_(upright("safe")) \) nabla phi.alt \( upright(bold(x)) \) = - 500 dot.op \( 0.0325 - 0.050 \) mat(delim: "[", - 0.970; - 0.242; 0) = mat(delim: "[", - 8.49; - 2.12; 0.00) upright(" N") $

== Spatial Encoders and Equivariance
<spatial-encoders-and-equivariance>
To extract actionable physical representations from visual inputs, modern Physical AI pipelines replace localized Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) with #strong[Vision Transformers (ViTs)] and self-supervised geometric foundation encoders like #strong[DINOv2].

== Spatial Encoders and Equivariance
<spatial-encoders-and-equivariance-1>
In classical robotics, computer vision pipelines relied on convolutional neural networks (CNNs) with small $3 times 3$ convolutional kernels. Picture a CNN as examining a cluttered manipulation workbench through a narrow drinking straw: its receptive field expands slowly layer by layer ($upright("RF") approx L dot.op \( K - 1 \)$), requiring dozens of cascaded layers before a pixel at the robot gripper can interact with a pixel at the target workpiece. An autonomous robot manipulating a tool on a table needs to evaluate the global multi-body spatial relationship between the tool, the tabletop, obstacle boundaries, and the robot kinematic base simultaneously. Vision Transformers solve this by tokenizing the entire image into spatial patches and computing all-to-all attention from the very first layer.

=== Vision Transformer Patch Tokenization
<vision-transformer-patch-tokenization>
The input image tensor $I in bb(R)^(H times W times C)$ is partitioned into a non-overlapping grid of $P times P$ pixel patches (typically $16 times 16$ or $14 times 14$). Each patch is flattened into a vector $upright(bold(x))_p in bb(R)^(P^2 C)$ and linearly projected through a learned embedding matrix $upright(bold(E)) in bb(R)^(\( P^2 C \) times d)$ into a continuous latent space of dimension $d$. Sinusoidal or learned positional embeddings $upright(bold(E))_(upright("pos")) in bb(R)^(N times d)$ are added to preserve 2D spatial topology:

$ upright(bold(z))_0 = [upright(bold(x))_p^1 upright(bold(E)) \; thin upright(bold(x))_p^2 upright(bold(E)) \; thin dots.h \; thin upright(bold(x))_p^N upright(bold(E))] + upright(bold(E))_(upright("pos")) \, quad N = frac(H W, P^2) $

- #strong[Physical Hazard & Motivation:] Selecting patch size $P$ dictates a strict trade-off between spatial quantization resolution on physical objects and the number of tokens $N$ entering quadratic attention.
- #strong[Variable & Unit Breakdown:]
  - $I in bb(R)^(H times W times C)$: Input image tensor.
  - $P$: Patch width/height in pixels.
  - $upright(bold(x))_p^i in bb(R)^(P^2 C)$: Flattened pixel vector of patch $i$.
  - $upright(bold(E)) in bb(R)^(\( P^2 C \) times d)$: Linear projection matrix.
  - $upright(bold(E))_(upright("pos")) in bb(R)^(N times d)$: Positional encoding matrix.
  - $N$: Total token count entering self-attention.
- #strong[Concrete Numerical Example:] For a standard robotic camera frame of $224 times 224$ pixels with $P = 14$, the image yields $N = \( 224 \/ 14 \)^2 = 256$ tokens with latent dimension $d = 768$ (ViT-B). At a working distance of $Z = 1.0 upright(" m")$ with camera focal length $f = 500 upright(" px")$, each $14 times 14$ patch projects to a physical spatial footprint of: $ Delta X = frac(P dot.op Z, f) = frac(14 times 1.0 upright(" m"), 500) = 0.028 upright(" m") = 2.8 upright(" cm") $ To achieve millimeter-precision grasping on small electronic components ($< 5 upright(" mm")$), the perception pipeline must perform sub-patch feature interpolation or utilize multi-scale foveated cropping.

=== Scaled Dot-Product Self-Attention
<scaled-dot-product-self-attention>
Rather than sliding a local kernel, the transformer computes all-to-all attention across all $N$ spatial patches simultaneously:

$ upright("Attention") \( upright(bold(Q)) \, upright(bold(K)) \, upright(bold(V)) \) = upright("softmax") (frac(upright(bold(Q)) upright(bold(K))^T, sqrt(d_k))) upright(bold(V)) $

where $upright(bold(Q)) = upright(bold(z)) upright(bold(W))_Q$, $upright(bold(K)) = upright(bold(z)) upright(bold(W))_K$, and $upright(bold(V)) = upright(bold(z)) upright(bold(W))_V$.

