=== $S E \( 3 \)$-Equivariant Neural Encoders
<se3-equivariant-neural-encoders>
While ViTs provide dense 2D spatial features, physical manipulation requires reasoning directly in 3D Euclidean space. Picture a coffee mug resting on a workbench: if the mug is rotated by $90^compose$, its physical grasp affordances (the handle position and grasp approach normal) rotate by the exact same $90^compose$. To guarantee that spatial features transform predictably under rigid-body motions without requiring brute-force data augmentation, 3D point cloud and field encoders incorporate #strong[$S E \( 3 \)$-Equivariant Neural Networks] (such as Vector Neurons or Steerable $S E \( 3 \)$-Transformers):

$ Phi (upright(bold(T)) dot.op cal(X)) = rho \( upright(bold(T)) \) dot.op Phi \( cal(X) \) \, quad forall thin upright(bold(T)) = \( upright(bold(R)) \, upright(bold(t)) \) in S E \( 3 \) $

- #strong[Physical Hazard & Motivation:] If a perception model is not equivariant to rigid-body 3D rotations, a policy trained to grasp a tool resting horizontally on a table will fail completely when the tool is presented at a $45^compose$ angle, unless billions of synthetic rotated training examples are collected.
- #strong[Variable & Unit Breakdown:]
  - $cal(X) subset bb(R)^3$: Input 3D point cloud or geometric structure.
  - $upright(bold(T)) = \( upright(bold(R)) \, upright(bold(t)) \) in S E \( 3 \)$: Special Euclidean rigid-body transformation consisting of rotation $upright(bold(R)) in S O \( 3 \)$ and translation $upright(bold(t)) in bb(R)^3$.
  - $Phi \( dot.op \)$: Neural feature extraction operator.
  - $rho \( upright(bold(T)) \)$: Group representation operator acting on the feature latent space.
    - #strong[Trivial Representation ($rho \( upright(bold(T)) \) = 1$):] Used for invariant physical scalars (grasp success probability $hat(p) in \[ 0 \, 1 \]$, friction coefficient $hat(mu)$, object mass $hat(m)$): $Phi \( upright(bold(T)) cal(X) \) = Phi \( cal(X) \)$.
    - #strong[Vector Representation ($rho \( upright(bold(T)) \) = upright(bold(R))$):] Used for 3D directional vectors (surface normals $hat(upright(bold(n))) in bb(S)^2$, grasp approach vectors $hat(upright(bold(v)))$): $Phi \( upright(bold(T)) cal(X) \) = upright(bold(R)) Phi \( cal(X) \)$.
    - #strong[Higher-Order Representations:] Wigner-$D$ matrices acting on spherical harmonics for complex directional torque fields.
- #strong[Concrete Numerical Example:] Consider an object geometry $cal(X)$ rotated by $90^compose$ around the z-axis: $ upright(bold(R)) = upright(bold(R))_z \( 90^compose \) = mat(delim: "[", 0, - 1, 0; 1, 0, 0; 0, 0, 1) \, quad upright(bold(t)) = upright(bold(0)) $ For an initial surface normal prediction $hat(upright(bold(n))) = \[ 1 \, 0 \, 0 \]^T$ on the unrotated object, the equivariant network guarantees that evaluating the rotated object $upright(bold(T)) cal(X)$ outputs: $ hat(upright(bold(n)))_(upright("rot")) = rho \( upright(bold(T)) \) hat(upright(bold(n))) = mat(delim: "[", 0, - 1, 0; 1, 0, 0; 0, 0, 1) mat(delim: "[", 1; 0; 0) = mat(delim: "[", 0; 1; 0) $ The grasp approach vector automatically rotates with the object in Euclidean space with zero loss in prediction confidence and zero additional training data.

