- #strong[The Common Assumption:] Pre-trained 2D Vision Transformers (e.g., ImageNet ViTs or web-scale multi-modal foundation models) exhibit broad semantic invariance across arbitrary object scales, rotations, and perspective crops. Deploying these rich 2D patch features directly into imitation learning or reinforcement learning policies should allow the robot to generalize manipulation policies across diverse camera distances.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Perspective projection in pinhole optics scales inversely with distance ($u = f_x X / Z + c_x$). A coffee cup at $Z = 0.5 upright(" m")$ occupies $4 times$ the pixel area and generates $4 times$ more patch tokens than the exact same cup at $Z = 1.0 upright(" m")$. In 2D pixel space, scale-invariant feature extraction completely strips away metric scale: a $2 times$ resized 2D bounding patch could physically represent a miniature toy at $0.5 upright(" m")$ or a massive industrial drum at $5 upright(" m")$. When policies act on ungrounded 2D embeddings, they command dangerous gripper apertures and incorrect approach speeds because 2D attention matrices lack Euclidean metric awareness.
