== Metric Affordance Grounding
<metric-affordance-grounding>
For a physical agent, high-dimensional latent vectors are unusable without spatial grounding. An actuator cannot apply motor voltage based on an abstract embedding vector $upright(bold(z)) in bb(R)^d$\; it requires a metric grasp pose in Cartesian coordinates $upright(bold(T))_(upright("grasp")) in S E \( 3 \)$, an approach surface normal vector $hat(upright(bold(n))) in bb(S)^2$, and an estimated Coulomb friction coefficient $hat(mu) in bb(R)^(+)$ (#ref(<fig-spatial-tokenization>, supplement: [Figure])).

=== Pinhole Ray Unprojection and Metric Depth Scaling
<pinhole-ray-unprojection-and-metric-depth-scaling>
To translate 2D patch tokens into 3D metric affordances, the perception engine combines calibrated pinhole projective geometry with depth scaling:

$ upright(bold(P))_C = d \( u \, v \) upright(bold(K))^(- 1) mat(delim: "[", u; v; 1) = mat(delim: "[", frac(u - c_x, f_x) d \( u \, v \); frac(v - c_y, f_y) d \( u \, v \); d \( u \, v \)) $

Transforming this unprojected 3D coordinate from the camera optical frame $C$ into the robot kinematic base frame $B$ uses the extrinsic transformation matrix $upright(bold(T))_C^B in S E \( 3 \)$:

$ upright(bold(P))_B = upright(bold(R))_C^B upright(bold(P))_C + upright(bold(p))_C^B $

- #strong[Physical Hazard & Motivation (Resolving Ray Ambiguity):] Under perspective projection, a 2D pixel coordinate $\( u \, v \)$ defines an infinite 1D ray $lambda upright(bold(r))_(upright("cam"))$ ($lambda > 0$). Every 3D point along that line of sight projects onto the exact same pixel. An autonomous gripper cannot close on an ungrounded ray; it must register metric depth $d \( u \, v \)$ to collapse ray ambiguity into a unique Euclidean coordinate $upright(bold(P))_C in bb(R)^3$, and apply extrinsic transformations $upright(bold(T))_C^B$ to ground the target in the robot base frame.
- #strong[Variable & Unit Breakdown:]
  - $\( u \, v \)$: Discrete image pixel coordinates ($upright("px")$).
  - $upright(bold(K)) in bb(R)^(3 times 3)$: Intrinsic camera matrix with focal lengths $\( f_x \, f_y \)$ in $upright("px")$ and optical center $\( c_x \, c_y \)$ in $upright("px")$.
  - $d \( u \, v \) in bb(R)^(+)$: Calibrated metric depth ($upright("m")$) along the camera optical z-axis.
  - $upright(bold(P))_C = \[ X_C \, Y_C \, Z_C \]^T in bb(R)^3$: 3D metric coordinate in camera frame ($upright("m")$).
  - $upright(bold(T))_C^B = mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(R))_C^B, upright(bold(p))_C^B; upright(bold(0))^T, 1) in S E \( 3 \)$: Extrinsic transformation matrix from camera frame $C$ to robot base frame $B$.
  - $upright(bold(P))_B in bb(R)^3$: 3D metric Cartesian position in robot base frame ($upright("m")$).
- #strong[Concrete Numerical Walkthrough:]
  + On a $640 times 480$ depth stream with $f_x = f_y = 500 upright(" px")$ and optical center $\( c_x \, c_y \) = \( 320 \, 240 \)$, a grasp patch centroid is detected at pixel $\( u \, v \) = \( 420 \, 240 \)$ with registered depth $d \( 420 \, 240 \) = 1.20 upright(" m")$.

