  + The camera is mounted on a robot wrist with extrinsic orientation $upright(bold(R))_C^B = mat(delim: "[", 0, 0, 1; 0, 1, 0; - 1, 0, 0)$ (optical axis aligned with base x-axis) and translation $upright(bold(p))_C^B = \[ 0.10 \, 0.00 \, 0.50 \]^T upright(" m")$.

  + The 3D position in the robot base frame evaluates to: $ upright(bold(P))_B = mat(delim: "[", 0, 0, 1; 0, 1, 0; - 1, 0, 0) mat(delim: "[", 0.24; 0.00; 1.20) + mat(delim: "[", 0.10; 0.00; 0.50) = mat(delim: "[", 1.20; 0.00; - 0.24) + mat(delim: "[", 0.10; 0.00; 0.50) = mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(1.30)); upright(bold(0.00)); upright(bold(0.26))) upright(" m") $

=== Surface Normal and Affordance Regression
<surface-normal-and-affordance-regression>
By evaluating spatial gradients across neighboring unprojected points $\( upright(bold(P))_B^(\( i \, j \)) \)$ via discrete finite differences, the perception engine computes local tangent plane vectors and calculates the outward unit surface normal vector facing the robot:

$ hat(upright(bold(n))) = frac(frac(partial upright(bold(P))_B, partial v) times frac(partial upright(bold(P))_B, partial u), ∥frac(partial upright(bold(P))_B, partial v) times frac(partial upright(bold(P))_B, partial u)∥) in bb(S)^2 $

Lightweight Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs) operating atop the frozen DINOv2 patch embeddings predict grasp approach vectors $hat(upright(bold(v))) in bb(S)^2$, gripper width bounds $Delta w_(upright("max"))$, and local friction coefficients $hat(mu)$.

Rather than streaming multi-megabyte image tensors across memory buses, the perception pipeline packages these predictions into compact #strong[3D Spatial Affordance Tokens] (#NormalTok("SpatialAffordanceToken_t");, a 48-byte C-struct in static SRAM: base position $upright(bold(P))_B in bb(R)^3$ \[12 B\], outward normal $hat(upright(bold(n))) in bb(R)^3$ \[12 B\], approach vector $hat(upright(bold(v))) in bb(R)^3$ \[12 B\], gripper aperture $Delta w_(upright("max"))$ \[4 B\], Coulomb friction $hat(mu)$ \[4 B\], and confidence score $p_(upright("grasp"))$ \[4 B\] = 48 bytes total). A prioritized queue of 16 candidate grasp tokens occupies $< 1 upright(" KB")$, fitting entirely within microcontroller L1 cache and streaming directly into $1000 upright(" Hz")$ impedance control loops with zero DRAM bus contention.

== Multi-Modal Sensor Fusion
<multi-modal-sensor-fusion>
A physical AI system rarely relies on a single sensor modality. Autonomous vehicles, humanoids, and industrial arms fuse exteroceptive cameras, LiDAR arrays, and high-frequency IMUs. However, combining sensors operating at disparate physical frequencies ($1000 upright(" Hz")$ IMUs with $30 upright(" Hz")$ cameras) introduces severe temporal and spatial synchronization challenges. In end-to-end multi-modal models, temporal phase mismatch causes cross-attention layers to correlate visual patch tokens with outdated inertial states, hallucinating spurious physical dynamics.

=== The Software Timestamp Illusion and Hardware PTP Synchronization
<the-software-timestamp-illusion-and-hardware-ptp-synchronization>
The most dangerous pitfall in multi-sensor fusion is the #strong[Software Timestamp Illusion] (#ref(<fig-sensor-sync>, supplement: [Figure])). Recording a timestamp in user-space software (e.g., calling #NormalTok("time.time()"); in Python or #NormalTok("std::chrono::system_clock::now()"); in C++) simply logs when the operating system kernel scheduler happened to dispatch the thread after sensor exposure, MIPI serialization, DMA transport, and interrupt latency. This introduces a variable, non-deterministic temporal skew of $Delta t_(upright("skew")) = 10 upright("–") 35 upright(" ms")$. At an operational speed of $v = 2.5 upright(" m/s")$ or an angular rate of $omega = 60^compose \/ upright("s")$, this skew injects $2.5 upright("–") 8.8 upright(" cm")$ of unmodeled spatial displacement and $0.6 upright("–") 2.1^compose$ of orientation phase error into state estimation.

