To eliminate the software timestamp illusion, Physical AI systems implement hardware-level clock synchronization using the Precision Time Protocol (IEEE 1588 PTP) paired with dedicated hardware trigger lines. A master microcontroller emits periodic hardware strobe pulses on GPIO lines, triggering global-shutter camera exposure and latching the absolute physical clock at the exact #strong[exposure midpoint]:

$ t_(upright("mid")) = t_(upright("start")) + 1 / 2 t_(upright("exp")) $

- #strong[Physical Hazard & Motivation:] An optical exposure integrates photons over a finite window $\[ t_(upright("start")) \, t_(upright("start")) + t_(upright("exp")) \]$. The resulting image represents the time-averaged physical state of the world centered at the exposure midpoint $t_(upright("mid"))$. Timestamping frames at software arrival ($t_(upright("recv")) = t_(upright("start")) + t_(upright("exp")) + t_(upright("DMA")) + t_(upright("OS"))$) injects phase lag that destabilizes high-speed feedback control.
- #strong[Variable & Unit Breakdown:]
  - $t_(upright("start"))$: Hardware-latched exposure start timestamp ($mu upright("s")$).
  - $t_(upright("exp"))$: Exposure duration ($mu upright("s")$).
  - $t_(upright("mid"))$: Physical exposure midpoint timestamp ($mu upright("s")$).
- #strong[Concrete Numerical Example:] If a camera triggers at $t_(upright("start")) = 100.000 upright(" ms")$ with exposure $t_(upright("exp")) = 10.000 upright(" ms")$, the optical midpoint is $t_(upright("mid")) = 105.000 upright(" ms")$. If software logs frame arrival at $t_(upright("recv")) = 128.400 upright(" ms")$ after MIPI transmission and DMA interrupt latency, software timestamping introduces $Delta t_(upright("skew")) = 23.400 upright(" ms")$ of unmodeled lag. For a robot moving at $v = 2.5 upright(" m/s")$, this creates an unmodeled position error of $Delta x = 2.5 times 0.0234 = 5.85 upright(" cm")$.

