In this incident, the 45 kg autonomous quadruped traversed an unpaved industrial facility at an operational velocity of $v = 2.5 upright(" m/s")$. Under its baseline $720 upright("p")$ perception pipeline, the self-supervised spatial encoder executed deterministically in $22 upright(" ms")$, consuming only $d_(upright("react")) = 2.5 upright(" m/s") times 0.022 upright(" s") = 5.5 upright(" cm")$ of forward reaction displacement before updating the terrain elevation map.

Because large Vision Transformer model weights ($approx 350 upright(" MB")$) exceed the edge NPU internal on-chip SRAM capacity ($approx 16 upright(" MB")$), parameter weights stream continuously from external LPDDR5 DRAM for every processed frame. When engineers deployed the dual 4K camera upgrade without hardware QoS throttling, continuous DMA write bursts ($2.98 upright(" GB/s")$) saturated the memory crossbar. The memory controller treated video writes with equal priority, starving NPU weight reads and blowing out the perception tail latency to $135 upright(" ms")$ ($P_99.9$).

The quadruped forward reaction distance and resulting dynamic impact force are governed by kinematics and the work-energy theorem:

$ d_(upright("react")) = v dot.op Delta t_(upright("compute")) $

$ F_(upright("impact")) = frac(m v^2, 2 delta) $

- #strong[Physical Hazard & Motivation:] When perception stalls, computational latency ($Delta t_(upright("compute"))$) turns into unmonitored physical displacement ($d_(upright("react"))$). When moving mass collides with an obstacle, the kinetic energy must be absorbed over the mechanical compliance deformation distance ($delta$), generating destructive impact forces that fracture structural linkages.
- #strong[Variable & Unit Breakdown:]
  - $v$: Forward linear velocity ($upright("m/s")$).
  - $Delta t_(upright("compute"))$: End-to-end sense-to-actuation computation latency ($upright("s")$).
  - $d_(upright("react"))$: Unmonitored forward reaction displacement ($upright("m")$).
  - $m$: Total platform mass ($upright("kg")$).
  - $delta$: Mechanical compliance deceleration displacement of the leg and chassis during collision ($upright("m")$).
  - $F_(upright("impact"))$: Peak dynamic collision force ($upright("N")$), derived from the work-energy theorem ($W = F_(upright("impact")) delta = 1 / 2 m v^2$).
- #strong[Concrete Numerical Example:]
