== Systems Stress-Tests: Architectural Food for Thought
<systems-stress-tests-architectural-food-for-thought-2>
Evaluating spatial perception architectures on physical machines requires grappling with non-trivial design trade-offs where optical physics, silicon memory bandwidth, and kinematic safety collide. The following four architectural stress-tests examine critical edge cases across the perception pipeline:

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 1: The High-Resolution vs.~Reaction-Displacement Dilemma]
<stress-test-1-the-high-resolution-vs.-reaction-displacement-dilemma>
An autonomous mobile manipulator operates in an electronics assembly facility, navigating tight aisles at $v = 2.0 upright(" m/s")$ while detecting micro-scale components on conveyor belts. The perception team evaluates two hardware configurations:

+ #strong[High-Resolution Pipeline:] Dual 4K global-shutter cameras ($3840 times 2160$ at 60 FPS) feeding a large Vision Transformer ($350 upright(" MB")$ weights). Patch tokenization resolves $0.5 upright(" mm")$ features, but memory crossbar contention inflates neural inference tail latency to $P_99.9 = 135 upright(" ms")$.
+ #strong[Low-Latency Pipelined Pyramid:] Dual 720p cameras ($1280 times 720$ at 60 FPS) feeding a lightweight spatial encoder with sub-patch bilinear interpolation. Features are resolved to $2.0 upright(" mm")$, but inference finishes deterministically in $22 upright(" ms")$.

While traversing an aisle, a human worker steps into the robot swept path at a distance of $d = 0.50 upright(" m")$. The low-latency pipeline detects the obstacle and updates the world model after $d_(upright("react")) = v dot.op Delta t = 2.0 times 0.022 = 0.044 upright(" m")$ ($4.4 upright(" cm")$), allowing the low-level braking controller to bring the vehicle to a halt within the remaining clearance. The high-resolution pipeline consumes $d_(upright("react")) = 2.0 times 0.135 = 0.270 upright(" m")$ ($27.0 upright(" cm")$) in computational reaction drift before issuing an intent update, leaving insufficient braking distance and causing a collision.

#emph[How should a perception architecture dynamically trade off spatial resolution against reaction displacement across different operational regimes (e.g., stationary precision manipulation vs.~high-speed transport)? How can multi-scale foveated attention or hardware-accelerated image pyramids resolve fine spatial affordances without incurring catastrophic memory crossbar latency tails?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 2: The Multi-Sensor Timestamp Skew and Lie-Algebra Drift Paradox]
<stress-test-2-the-multi-sensor-timestamp-skew-and-lie-algebra-drift-paradox>
An autonomous quadruped negotiates complex terrain under rapid yaw motions ($omega = 120^compose \/ upright("s")$). The perception architecture fuses a $30 upright(" Hz")$ stereo depth camera with a $1000 upright(" Hz")$ IMU. Due to an uncalibrated Linux driver buffer, the camera frames are timestamped in user space upon DMA buffer completion rather than at the physical optical exposure midpoint, introducing an unmodeled temporal delay of $Delta t_(upright("skew")) = 20 upright(" ms")$.

When the perception engine unprojects depth pixels into the robot body frame using the forward-integrated IMU orientation $upright(bold(R)) \( t + Delta t \) = upright(bold(R)) \( t \) exp \( bold(omega)^and Delta t \)$, the $20 upright(" ms")$ timestamp skew injects an angular rotation error of $Delta theta = 120^compose \/ upright("s") times 0.020 upright(" s") = 2.4^compose$. At a distance of $3.0 upright(" m")$, this rotational error displaces the unprojected ground plane elevation by $Delta z approx 3.0 times sin \( 2.4^compose \) approx 12.5 upright(" cm")$. The terrain elevation mapper interprets the flat floor as a steep vertical ledge, commanding an emergency foot retraction that trips the robot.

#emph[Why is software timestamping fundamentally inadequate for dynamic embodied platforms? How do hardware-latched IEEE 1588 PTP exposure strobes eliminate temporal phase skew, and how should an extended Kalman filter detect and isolate timestamp drift before unprojected spatial points corrupt the elevation map?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 3: The Thermal-Vibration Extrinsic Baseline Calibration Drift]
<stress-test-3-the-thermal-vibration-extrinsic-baseline-calibration-drift>
A dual-arm humanoid robot operates continuous 16-hour shifts in an unconditioned warehouse where ambient temperature fluctuates from $10^compose upright("C")$ at dawn to $42^compose upright("C")$ in late afternoon. The robot relies on a head-mounted stereo camera pair ($20 upright(" cm")$ baseline) calibrated to sub-millimeter precision at $20^compose upright("C")$.

As internal actuator heat and solar radiation warm the aluminum chassis, thermal expansion ($alpha approx 23 times 10^(- 6) upright(" K")^(- 1)$) and mechanical motor vibration induce a tiny $0.4^compose$ angular toe-out deflection between the optical axes. At a $4.0 upright(" m")$ working range, this sub-degree deflection expands stereo triangulation depth error from $plus.minus 2.5 upright(" cm")$ to over $plus.minus 22.0 upright(" cm")$. When the robot attempts to grasp a pallet fixture, the end-effector misses the target and collides with the support racking.

#emph[Under what conditions does static factory calibration fail in physical environments? How can real-time epipolar consistency metrics (such as the Sampson error $epsilon.alt_(upright("epi"))$) be computed within the camera ISP to trigger continuous, self-supervised online recalibration without interrupting ongoing manipulation tasks?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 4: The 2D Scale-Equivariance Collapse in Monocular Manipulation]
<stress-test-4-the-2d-scale-equivariance-collapse-in-monocular-manipulation>
An imitation-learning manipulation policy is trained on millions of 2D camera images to grasp common industrial tools. The policy uses a pre-trained 2D Vision Transformer backbone whose self-attention layers are scale-invariant across pixel space. During deployment, an assembly jig is placed $15 upright(" cm")$ closer to the camera than during training.

Because perspective projection scales image area inversely with distance ($u prop 1 \/ Z$), the workpiece appears $30 %$ larger in the 2D image. The scale-invariant 2D ViT extracts high-confidence grasp tokens but predicts a gripper opening aperture tailored to a physically larger object. When the gripper closes, it strikes the edges of the workpiece, toppling the fixture.

#emph[Why does 2D semantic feature richness fail to translate into metric manipulation competence? How must perception representations incorporate explicit $S E \( 3 \)$-equivariant operators and metric ray unprojection to ensure that physical geometry, rather than 2D pixel scale, governs downstream actuation?]

== Chapter Summary and Forward Handoff to #ref(<sec-state>, supplement: [Chapter])
<chapter-summary-and-forward-handoff-to-sec-state>
This chapter developed the sensory foundation of Physical AI, analyzing how physical particle fluxes and analog environmental interactions are transduced, ingested across shared silicon, and structured into discrete 3D metric affordance tokens in $S E \( 3 \)$. Physical perception differs fundamentally from digital image classification: it is an active, latency-taxed pipeline governed by transduction physics, memory crossbar contention, and microsecond clock synchronization.

The architectural discipline of Physical AI perception rests on three core pillars:

+ #strong[Transduction Physics and Sensor Noise Floors:] Optical charge integration windows ($t_(upright("exp"))$), quantum efficiency curves, rolling shutter skew, and IMU Allan variance bias drift determine the fundamental noise floor and temporal lag of raw physical observation.
+ #strong[Memory Bus Determinism and Privilege Separation:] Shared Unified Memory Architectures demand pinned zero-copy DMA ring buffers (#NormalTok("dma_alloc_coherent");), write-combining cache bypass, and hardware AXI QoS bus priority arbitration (#NormalTok("MCU QoS=15 > NPU QoS=10 > Camera DMA QoS=4");) to prevent sensor ingestion from starving neural accelerator weight fetches.
+ #strong[Metric Affordance Grounding:] Distilling dense multi-megabyte image arrays into compact 3D spatial affordance tokens ($o_t = { upright(bold(t o k))_1 \, dots.h \, upright(bold(t o k))_K }$) in $S E \( 3 \)$ reduces bus bandwidth by over $99.9 %$, providing zero-allocation (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");) payloads that feed downstream trajectory planners and control barrier functions within sub-millisecond deadlines.

By anchoring spatial affordance tokens to IEEE 1588 PTP hardware exposure midpoints, the perception organ provides a synchronized, metric snapshot of the immediate physical scene.

However, physical reality is inherently dynamic and partially observable. Manipulated objects pass behind robot linkages, dynamic obstacles traverse the field of view, lens glare blinds optical sensors, and cameras drop frames ($o_t = nothing$). When observation is lost, an embodied agent cannot simply halt its physical inertia or lose track of surrounding geometry.

In #strong[#ref(<sec-state>, supplement: [Chapter]) (Temporal Memory and Latent World Models: Dynamic Frame Trees, Persistence Beyond Occlusion, and JEPAs)], we address what happens when direct observation ends. We explore how an embodied agent maintains spatial permanence across time, tracks kinematic transform trees across moving articulated joints, and predicts dynamic physical interactions using Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures under expanding covariance uncertainty envelopes.

