#emph[Why does an autonomous mobile manipulator drop a delicate glass object the moment an obstacle occludes the overhead camera?]

When a person reaches for a coffee cup while blinking or glancing away, their arm does not freeze in place or drop to the table. The human central nervous system maintains a continuous, internal dynamic mental model---estimating hand velocity, integrating muscle spindle proprioception at high rates, and projecting the spatial coordinates of the table and cup forward through time even when optical vision is temporarily blacked out.

In digital simulation, the physical state of every entity is permanently accessible in global memory arrays. In the physical world, sensory contact is intermittent, noisy, and delayed: objects pass behind occlusions, sensors suffer dropouts, and cameras are blinded by the robot's own moving linkages. Because observation is not state ($o_t eq.not x_t$), an embodied agent cannot rely solely on instantaneous sensory snapshots. Nor can it afford the computational cost of generating raw video pixels in real time. Instead, it must maintain a structured, latent representation of physical reality, anchored by time-indexed coordinate frame trees in $S E \( 3 \)$, forward predictive dynamics in compact latent space, and expanding spatial uncertainty envelopes that mathematically enforce when memory expires.

