== Coordinate Frames and Lie Algebra
<sec-lie-algebra>
To maintain a geometrically consistent model of the physical environment, an embodied agent must track the continuous spatial relationships between its base chassis, articulated linkages, mounted sensors, and external workspace objects. In physical robotics, every sensor, actuator, and tool speaks its own local dialect:

- The wrist-mounted RGB-D camera reports optical coordinates: pixel coordinates $\( u \, v \)$ and depth $d$ in meters.
- The joint encoders report angular shaft positions $upright(bold(q)) = \[ q_1 \, dots.h \, q_6 \]^T$ in radians.
- The vacuum gripper tooling requires Cartesian target coordinates in the static robot base frame $\( x \, y \, z \)$.

Dynamic Kinematic Coordinate Frame Trees ($cal(T)_(upright("frames"))$) act as the geometric Rosetta Stone, where nodes represent spatial coordinate systems and directed edges represent rigid geometric transformations (#ref(<fig-se3-frame-tree>, supplement: [Figure])).

