=== The SE(3) Homogeneous Transformation Matrix
<the-se3-homogeneous-transformation-matrix>
The spatial relationship between any parent coordinate frame $A$ and child coordinate frame $B$ is a member of the Special Euclidean Group $S E \( 3 \)$, represented as a $4 times 4$ homogeneous transformation matrix comprising an orthonormal rotation matrix $upright(bold(R))_B^A in S O \( 3 \)$ and a 3D translational position vector $upright(bold(p))_B^A in bb(R)^3$:

$ upright(bold(T))_B^A = mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(R))_B^A, upright(bold(p))_B^A; upright(bold(0))^T, 1) in S E \( 3 \) \, #h(2em) upright(bold(R))_B^A in S O \( 3 \) arrow.r.double.long \( upright(bold(R))_B^A \)^T upright(bold(R))_B^A = upright(bold(I))_(3 times 3) \, quad det \( upright(bold(R))_B^A \) = + 1 $

where:

- #strong[$upright(bold(T))_B^A in bb(R)^(4 times 4)$:] Homogeneous matrix transforming homogeneous coordinate vectors from frame $B$ into frame $A$.
- #strong[$upright(bold(R))_B^A in bb(R)^(3 times 3)$:] Orthonormal rotation matrix representing the 3D orientation of frame $B$ with respect to frame $A$. Orthonormality ensures distance and angle preservation during coordinate transformations.
- #strong[$upright(bold(p))_B^A = \[ p_x \, p_y \, p_z \]^T in bb(R)^3$:] Position vector locating the Cartesian origin of frame $B$ relative to frame $A$, measured in meters ($upright("m")$).
- #strong[$upright(bold(0))^T = \[ 0 \, 0 \, 0 \]$:] Dimensionless zero row vector enforcing the projective affine constraint.

#emph[Concrete Numerical Example:] Consider an RGB-D camera ($C$) mounted on a robot wrist forearm link ($F$). The camera is offset forward by $0.15 upright(" m")$ along the link's $x$-axis, centered laterally ($y = 0.00 upright(" m")$), elevated by $0.05 upright(" m")$ along $z$, and pitched downward by $90^compose$ ($pi \/ 2 upright(" rad")$) around the $y$-axis ($cos \( pi \/ 2 \) = 0$, $sin \( pi \/ 2 \) = 1$). The transformation matrix $upright(bold(T))_C^F$ evaluates to:

$ upright(bold(T))_C^F = mat(delim: "[", 0, 0, 1, 0.15; 0, 1, 0, 0.00; - 1, 0, 0, 0.05; 0, 0, 0, 1) $

Multiplying an optical detection vector in camera frame $upright(bold(P))_C$ by $upright(bold(T))_C^F$ yields its exact physical coordinates in the forearm frame $F$.

=== Lie Algebra Tangent Space $frak(s e) \( 3 \)$ and Matrix Exponentials
<lie-algebra-tangent-space-mathfrakse3-and-matrix-exponentials>
In software engineering, 3D rotations are often stored as Euler angles (roll, pitch, yaw) or unit quaternions $upright(bold(q)) in bb(R)^4$. However, Euler angles suffer from mathematical singularities known as #strong[gimbal lock]: when the pitch angle reaches $theta = plus.minus 90^compose$, one rotational degree of freedom collapses, causing numerical optimization algorithms to command instantaneous infinite angular velocities ($dot(bold(theta)) arrow.r oo$) that shear mechanical gear teeth. Unit quaternions avoid gimbal lock, but linear numerical integration across time violates the unit norm constraint ($parallel upright(bold(q)) parallel = 1$), causing floating-point truncation under FP32 math to artificially shrink link lengths over millions of cycles.

To solve this, Physical AI architectures execute continuous temporal integration directly in the Lie Algebra Tangent Space $frak(s e) \( 3 \)$ @murray2017mathematical@barfoot2017state.

