To build an intuitive picture of Lie theory, picture walking on the surface of a curved sphere (the Lie group $S O \( 3 \)$). You cannot add two locations on a sphere using standard vector addition ($A + B$) because the straight line segment between them cuts through the interior of the sphere, leaving the valid surface manifold.

However, at the exact point where you stand (the group identity $upright(bold(I))$), the ground beneath your feet is a flat 2D tangent plane (the Lie algebra $frak(s o) \( 3 \)$). On this flat plane, velocities are ordinary vectors that can be scaled, inverted, and added freely. The #strong[matrix exponential] ($exp$) is the mathematical mapping that takes a straight velocity vector on the flat tangent plane and wraps it smoothly along the curved manifold of the sphere for a duration $Delta t$, maintaining exact metric constraints with zero geometric distortion.

