== EKF Estimation on Silicon
<sec-ekf-silicon>
Embodied systems constantly manage a fundamental multi-rate physical mismatch between sensory channels. An autonomous robot relies on two complementary but imperfect windows into reality:

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
To understand state estimation on physical hardware, imagine an #strong[Optimal Confidence Tug-of-War] between two imperfect sensory sources:

+ #strong[Proprioceptive Dead-Reckoning (IMU Gyroscopes & Wheel Encoders):] Streams at high frequency ($1000 upright(" Hz")$, $1.0 upright(" ms")$ tick) with sub-millisecond latency. It provides ultra-smooth relative velocity and angular rate, but like walking blindfolded, dead-reckoning integrates sensor drift ($0.005^compose \/ upright("s") \/ sqrt(upright("Hz"))$) into unbounded spatial error over time.
+ #strong[Exteroceptive Observation (Cameras & LiDAR):] Streams at low frequency ($30 upright(" Hz")$, $33.3 upright(" ms")$ stride) with $30 upright("–") 60 upright(" ms")$ of transport latency. It provides absolute, drift-free world-referenced position, but suffers from optical noise, reflections, and intermittent occlusions.

The Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) is the mathematical arbiter of this tug-of-war. During the $33.3 upright(" ms")$ blind window between camera frames, the filter trusts fast dead-reckoning to propagate state forward. When a delayed camera frame arrives, the Kalman gain $upright(bold(K))$ calculates the exact weighted average based on instantaneous covariance ellipses, pulling the state toward the measurement without injecting violent acceleration spikes.

