To maintain a continuous state belief at $1000 upright(" Hz")$, Physical AI systems employ continuous-discrete Extended Kalman Filters (EKF) @kalman1960new@thrun2005probabilistic. Rather than executing an unconstrained mathematical abstraction, embedded estimators implement a strict real-time systems contract: high-rate dead-reckoning state propagation between measurement arrivals, retroactive temporal fusion of delayed visual observations, and hardware-guaranteed numerical stability in static SRAM (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");).

=== Asynchronous Multi-Rate Fusion and the Continuous Riccati Formulation
<asynchronous-multi-rate-fusion-and-the-continuous-riccati-formulation>
The physical system state $upright(bold(x)) in bb(R)^n$ evolves according to continuous non-linear dynamics driven by control input $upright(bold(u)) \( t \)$ and process noise $upright(bold(w)) \( t \) tilde.op cal(N) \( upright(bold(0)) \, upright(bold(Q)) \( t \) \)$:

$ dot(upright(bold(x))) \( t \) = f \( upright(bold(x)) \( t \) \, upright(bold(u)) \( t \) \) + upright(bold(w)) \( t \) $

$ dot(upright(bold(P))) \( t \) = upright(bold(F)) \( t \) upright(bold(P)) \( t \) + upright(bold(P)) \( t \) upright(bold(F)) \( t \)^T + upright(bold(Q)) \( t \) $

where:

- #strong[$upright(bold(x)) \( t \) in bb(R)^n$:] Continuous physical state vector. For a 12-state rigid-body filter, $upright(bold(x)) = \[ upright(bold(p))^T \, bold(theta)^T \, upright(bold(v))^T \, bold(omega)^T \]^T$ comprises 3D position $upright(bold(p))$ (in $upright("m")$), Euler orientation angles $bold(theta)$ (in $upright("rad")$), linear velocity $upright(bold(v))$ (in $upright("m/s")$), and angular rate $bold(omega)$ (in $upright("rad/s")$).
- #strong[$upright(bold(u)) \( t \) in bb(R)^p$:] Commanded actuator inputs (e.g., motor torques in $upright("N") dot.op upright("m")$).
- #strong[$upright(bold(w)) \( t \) tilde.op cal(N) \( upright(bold(0)) \, upright(bold(Q)) \( t \) \)$:] Zero-mean Gaussian process noise vector.
- #strong[$upright(bold(P)) \( t \) in bb(R)^(n times n)$:] State estimation error covariance matrix ($bb(E) \[ \( upright(bold(x)) - hat(upright(bold(x))) \) \( upright(bold(x)) - hat(upright(bold(x))) \)^T \]$), with diagonal elements representing estimation variances $sigma_i^2$ in $\[ upright("unit") \( x_i \) \]^2$.
- #strong[$upright(bold(F)) \( t \) = frac(partial f, partial upright(bold(x)))\|_(hat(upright(bold(x))) \, upright(bold(u))) in bb(R)^(n times n)$:] Continuous system dynamics Jacobian matrix, measured in $upright("s")^(- 1)$.
- #strong[$upright(bold(Q)) \( t \) in bb(R)^(n times n)$:] Continuous process noise spectral density matrix, representing unmodeled physical disturbances in $\[ \( upright("unit") \( x_i \) \)^2 \/ upright("s") \]$.

#emph[Concrete Numerical Propagation Example:] Consider a 1D position-velocity tracking kinematic model ($dot(p) = v$, $dot(v) = u + w_v$) with state $upright(bold(x)) = \[ p \, v \]^T$. The system Jacobian is $upright(bold(F)) = mat(delim: "[", 0, 1; 0, 0)$, and continuous process noise is $upright(bold(Q)) = upright("diag") \( 0 \, 0.10 \) upright(" m")^2 \/ upright("s")^3$. Suppose the prior covariance at the start of a $1000 upright(" Hz")$ loop ($Delta t = 1.0 upright(" ms") = 0.001 upright(" s")$) is $upright(bold(P)) \( 0 \) = mat(delim: "[", 0.0100, 0.0000; 0.0000, 0.0400) upright(" m")^2$.

The time derivative of the error covariance evaluates to:

$ dot(upright(bold(P))) = upright(bold(F)) upright(bold(P)) + upright(bold(P)) upright(bold(F))^T + upright(bold(Q)) = mat(delim: "[", 0, 0.0400; 0, 0) + mat(delim: "[", 0, 0; 0.0400, 0) + mat(delim: "[", 0, 0; 0, 0.1000) = mat(delim: "[", 0, 0.0400; 0.0400, 0.1000) $

Propagating over the $1.0 upright(" ms")$ interval yields:

$ upright(bold(P)) \( Delta t \) approx upright(bold(P)) \( 0 \) + dot(upright(bold(P))) Delta t = mat(delim: "[", 0.010000, 0.000040; 0.000040, 0.040100) upright(" m")^2 $

State uncertainty expands predictably between measurement arrivals. Complete derivations of continuous error dynamics are cataloged in Appendix C.2.

=== The Joseph-Form Stability Contract on Embedded Silicon
<the-joseph-form-stability-contract-on-embedded-silicon>
In textbook derivations, the a posteriori covariance update is written as $upright(bold(P))_k^(+) = \( upright(bold(I)) - upright(bold(K))_k upright(bold(H))_k \) upright(bold(P))_k^(-)$. While algebraically compact, this standard form is numerically unstable on resource-constrained embedded microcontrollers utilizing 32-bit floating-point (FP32) arithmetic.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
An error covariance matrix $upright(bold(P))$ represents an uncertainty ellipsoid in physical state space. On a 32-bit microcontroller (FP32 with a 24-bit mantissa $approx 7$ decimal digits of precision), subtracting two nearly identical large matrices in the textbook form $upright(bold(P))^(+) = \( upright(bold(I)) - upright(bold(K)) upright(bold(H)) \) upright(bold(P))^(-)$ causes catastrophic cancellation.

When FP32 roundoff truncates small fractional residuals, $upright(bold(P))^(+)$ can lose symmetry ($upright(bold(P)) eq.not upright(bold(P))^T$) and develop negative eigenvalues ($lambda_(upright("min")) \( upright(bold(P)) \) < 0$). A negative eigenvalue represents an uncertainty ellipsoid with an imaginary radius---a physical absurdity. Once this occurs, the filter treats variances as negative numbers, the Kalman gain diverges to infinity, and the microcontroller commands maximum actuator current into endstops.

]
, 
