== Latent World Models
<sec-latent-world-models>
While classical Kalman filters excel at tracking parameterized rigid-body kinematics, they cannot scale to open-world physical scenes containing deformable fabrics, splashing fluids, granular media, or unstructured clutter. An embodied agent needs a world model capable of predicting how complex environments evolve under candidate action sequences.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
To understand why Physical AI avoids generative video models, contrast a #strong[Sketchpad] with a #strong[Movie Studio]:

- #strong[The Movie Studio (Generative Video Models):] Attempts to forecast the future by rendering millions of uncompressed RGB pixels frame by frame ($> 10^13 upright(" FLOPs/s")$). On an edge robot, this saturates the memory bus and expends 90% of its compute power drawing swaying grass, flickering ceiling lights, and background wallpaper. Worse, pixel decoders suffer from visual hallucinations---accidentally erasing thin steel cables or inventing non-existent free space.
- #strong[The Sketchpad (Latent World Models / JEPAs):] Acts like a physicist jotting down coordinates in a notebook. It compresses high-dimensional camera bitmaps into a compact vector of physical invariants $upright(bold(z)) in bb(R)^d$ ($d = 256 upright("–") 1024$), representing object centers of mass, structural affordances, and contact modes. Forward transitions execute in microseconds directly in SRAM with zero pixel rendering overhead.

]
