When a moving target passes behind an obstruction ($o_t = nothing$), the object does not vanish. Its true position drifts within an expanding uncertainty envelope. Assuming a maximum target velocity $v_(upright("target_max"))$, the position uncertainty standard deviation expands over time:

$ sigma_(upright("pos")) \( Delta t \) = sigma_0 + v_(upright("target_max")) dot.op Delta t $

where:

- #strong[$sigma_(upright("pos")) \( Delta t \)$:] Position uncertainty standard deviation at occlusion duration $Delta t$, measured in meters ($upright("m")$).
- #strong[$sigma_0$:] Initial state estimation standard deviation before occlusion, measured in meters ($upright("m")$).
- #strong[$v_(upright("target_max"))$:] Worst-case bounding velocity of the unobserved obstacle/target, measured in $upright("m/s")$.
- #strong[$Delta t$:] Elapsed duration since last valid observation, measured in seconds ($upright("s")$).

Given an available spatial clearance $d_(upright("clearance"))$ to surrounding obstacles and a required safety margin $d_(upright("margin"))$, setting the upper bound of the spatial confidence envelope equal to the allowable clearance defines the #strong[Maximum Occlusion Horizon ($t_(upright("max"))$)]:

$ sigma_(upright("pos")) \( t_(upright("max")) \) lt.eq d_(upright("clearance")) - d_(upright("margin")) arrow.r.double.long t_(upright("max")) = frac(d_(upright("clearance")) - d_(upright("margin")) - sigma_0, v_(upright("target_max"))) $

where:

- #strong[$d_(upright("clearance"))$:] Metric distance between the robot tool/chassis and surrounding obstacles, measured in meters ($upright("m")$).
- #strong[$d_(upright("margin"))$:] Prescribed physical safety buffer (e.g., $0.020 upright(" m")$), measured in meters ($upright("m")$).
- #strong[$t_(upright("max"))$:] Maximum allowable occlusion time window before uncertainty breaches the clearance margin, measured in seconds ($upright("s")$).

#emph[Numerical Walkthrough:] For an autonomous mobile robot or manipulator maintaining an available clearance of $d_(upright("clearance")) = 100 upright(" mm")$ ($0.100 upright(" m")$) around an unobserved dynamic obstacle, with safety margin $d_(upright("margin")) = 20 upright(" mm")$ ($0.020 upright(" m")$), initial position uncertainty $sigma_0 = 5 upright(" mm")$ ($0.005 upright(" m")$), and worst-case target drift velocity $v_(upright("target_max")) = 0.50 upright(" m/s")$ ($500 upright(" mm/s")$), the maximum allowable occlusion horizon evaluates to:

$ t_(upright("max")) = frac(0.100 upright(" m") - 0.020 upright(" m") - 0.005 upright(" m"), 0.50 upright(" m/s")) = frac(0.075 upright(" m"), 0.50 upright(" m/s")) = 0.150 upright(" s") = 150 upright(" ms") $

If a sensory occlusion lasts longer than $t_(upright("max")) = 150 upright(" ms")$, the spatial uncertainty envelope breaches safe physical limits. At this exact threshold, the state estimate's Time-To-Live (TTL) lease expires, forcing the system to halt motion before a collision can occur.

#block[
- #strong[The Seductive Intuition:] Training an unconstrained autoregressive neural network or diffusion world model to predict future latent states ($hat(upright(bold(z)))_(t + 1) = f_theta \( upright(bold(z))_t \, upright(bold(a))_t \)$) allows the robot to "imagine" open-world physical dynamics without needing explicit geometric coordinates, Lie algebra frame trees, or rigid kinematic equations.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Pure neural latent spaces lack metric grounding and conservation-law inductive biases. Without explicit metric coordinate anchoring, multi-step autoregressive rollouts suffer from compounding latent drift ($E \[ parallel upright(bold(z))_t - hat(upright(bold(z)))_t parallel \] prop e^(lambda t)$). Over a 2-second imagination horizon, unanchored latent states drift into physically impossible manifolds---predicting that rigid objects can interpenetrate, that mass vanishes, or that gravity reverses. When a trajectory optimizer plans actions against these ungrounded latent futures, it outputs high-gain actuator commands that drive mechanical linkages into hard obstacles.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Latent world models (JEPAs, RSSMs) must be hybrid-anchored: predictive latent embeddings $upright(bold(z))_t in bb(R)^d$ must be mapped to metric $S E \( 3 \)$ coordinate heads that are continually projected onto physically verified kinematic frame trees and bounded by Lyapunov uncertainty horizons ($t lt.eq t_(upright("max"))$).

