=== Forensic Breakdown of the Stale Transform Skew
<forensic-breakdown-of-the-stale-transform-skew>
In this incident, the 6-DoF articulated manipulator was descending toward a printed circuit board (PCB) fixture at $v = 0.9 upright(" m/s")$ (#ref(<fig-manipulator-arm>, supplement: [Figure])). During the descent, the robot's third linkage rotated across the field of view of the wrist camera, occluding the PCB alignment fiducials for $180 upright(" ms")$.

The software flaw resided in the coordinate transform lookup buffer. The state engine was configured to query the camera-to-target transform $upright(bold(T))_(upright("target"))^(upright("cam"))$ without enforcing strict timestamp matching. Because the vision pipeline stalled during the occlusion, the transform lookup returned the last cached transform from $65 upright(" ms")$ prior, while joint angle encoders were sampled synchronously at $t_(upright("now"))$.

The kinematic engine calculated target position in base coordinates by multiplying non-isochronous transforms:

$ upright(bold(T))_(upright("target"))^(upright("base")) \( t_(upright("now")) \) = upright(bold(T))_(upright("cam"))^(upright("base")) \( t_(upright("now")) \) dot.op upright(bold(T))_(upright("target"))^(upright("cam")) \( t_(upright("now")) - 65 upright(" ms") \) $

This temporal mismatch injected an uncompensated spatial skew along the vertical descent axis:

$ Delta z_(upright("skew")) = v_(upright("descent")) dot.op Delta t_(upright("stale")) = 0.9 upright(" m/s") times 0.065 upright(" s") = 0.0585 upright(" m") = 5.85 upright(" cm") $

Assuming the workpiece lay $5.85 upright(" cm")$ below its true position, the trajectory planner commanded the manipulator to continue descending at high speed. The vacuum gripper struck the solid aluminum fixture at $0.9 upright(" m/s")$, generating an instantaneous impact force of $1.8 upright(" kN")$ that fractured the ceramic tip and sheared mounting pins (#ref(<tbl-telemetry-ch06>, supplement: [Table])).

