== Systems Stress-Tests: Architectural Food for Thought
<systems-stress-tests-architectural-food-for-thought-3>
State estimation and world modeling in Physical AI require resolving difficult design tensions where partial observability, non-linear mechanics, and embedded execution limits intersect. The following four architectural stress-tests examine critical edge cases across temporal memory and state persistence:

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 1: The Asynchronous Camera Stride and Kinematic Warp Dilemma]
<stress-test-1-the-asynchronous-camera-stride-and-kinematic-warp-dilemma>
A 6-DoF collaborative robot executes high-speed pick-and-place cycles at $v_(upright("tool")) = 1.2 upright(" m/s")$. The robot relies on a wrist-mounted RGB-D camera delivering 30 Hz object detections with an average image processing latency of $Delta t_(upright("vision")) = 45 upright(" ms")$, while joint encoders stream at $1000 upright(" Hz")$.

During a rapid descent, the vision system detects a target bin at time $t_(upright("capture"))$, but the detection message is processed by the kinematic state engine at $t_(upright("now")) = t_(upright("capture")) + 45 upright(" ms")$. If the state engine naively multiplies the current base-to-camera transform $upright(bold(T))_(upright("cam"))^(upright("base")) \( t_(upright("now")) \)$ by the target transform $upright(bold(T))_(upright("target"))^(upright("cam")) \( t_(upright("capture")) \)$, the $45 upright(" ms")$ latency mismatch injects a vertical spatial skew of $Delta z = 1.2 times 0.045 = 5.4 upright(" cm")$, causing the gripper to slam into the bin rim at full speed.

#emph[How must the state estimation ring buffer structure historical transforms in SRAM to support exact geodesic interpolation in Lie algebra space $frak(s e) \( 3 \)$? When sensory processing latency fluctuates non-deterministically ($Delta t in \[ 30 \, 90 \] upright(" ms")$), how should the state engine determine whether to forward-project the target using historical velocity models or revoke the intent lease entirely?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 2: The Contact Discontinuity and Process Covariance Collapse]
<stress-test-2-the-contact-discontinuity-and-process-covariance-collapse>
An autonomous quadruped transitions from a high-speed running trot ($v = 3.0 upright(" m/s")$) on asphalt to an unmodeled sheet of ice ($mu approx 0.05$). The onboard Extended Kalman Filter maintains a constant process noise covariance $upright(bold(Q))_(upright("trot"))$ tuned for solid terrain adhesion.

Upon contacting the ice, the robot's front-left foot experiences continuous slip ($Delta v_(upright("foot")) = 2.5 upright(" m/s")$). Because the EKF assumes low process noise on leg kinematics, it treats the high foot velocity as legitimate forward vehicle acceleration rather than unmodeled slip. The filter updates the body state estimate with an inflated velocity ($hat(v) = 5.5 upright(" m/s")$), causing the balance controller to command aggressive retarding torques on the remaining legs. This uncoordinated torque injection destabilizes the pitch axis and flips the robot onto its chassis.

#emph[Why do static Gaussian noise assumptions fail across hybrid dynamic contact transitions? How should hybrid contact state estimators combine high-rate tactile force sensing with adaptive Joseph-form covariance updates to prevent covariance matrix collapse during sudden friction changes?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 3: The Generative Latent Drift vs.~Reactive Halting Trade-Off]
<stress-test-3-the-generative-latent-drift-vs.-reactive-halting-trade-off>
A mobile manipulator uses a non-generative Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA) world model to forecast target affordances during sensory occlusions. The robot moves at $v = 1.0 upright(" m/s")$ toward an assembly station when a passing forklift occludes its primary stereo cameras ($o_t = nothing$).

The JEPA model continues rolling out latent predictions $hat(upright(bold(z)))_(t + k) = P_phi.alt \( hat(upright(bold(z)))_(t + k - 1) \, upright(bold(a))_(t + k - 1) \)$ in open loop. The ensemble variance across its 4 prediction heads grows from $sigma_(upright("epistemic")) = 0.02$ at $t = 0$ to $sigma_(upright("epistemic")) = 0.35$ after $150 upright(" ms")$. If the robot halts immediately upon camera occlusion, it creates a traffic hazard in the aisle. If it trusts the JEPA forecast for $300 upright(" ms")$, the spatial prediction error grows to $plus.minus 8.5 upright(" cm")$, risking a collision with surrounding tooling.

#emph[How should systems architects compute the strict Maximum Occlusion Horizon ($t_(upright("max"))$) from geometric clearance envelopes and maximal Lyapunov exponents? At what exact epistemic variance threshold should the supervisory contract force the system from predictive continuation into active epistemic abstention?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 4: The Static SRAM Footprint vs.~Non-Linear Transform Graph Scale]
<stress-test-4-the-static-sram-footprint-vs.-non-linear-transform-graph-scale>
An autonomous drone with 12 articulated inspection degrees of freedom is constrained to run its state estimator on an embedded ARM Cortex-M7 with only $512 upright(" KB")$ of DTCM SRAM. The software architecture requires maintaining a 30-node dynamic transform tree with 64-bit PTP timestamps and full $6 times 6$ spatial covariance matrices across a $1000 upright(" ms")$ ring buffer history.

Allocating full 6D covariance histories for 30 nodes at $1000 upright(" Hz")$ requires $30 times 1000 times \( 16 times 4 + 36 times 4 \) upright(" Bytes") approx 6.24 upright(" MB")$---exceeding available SRAM by more than $12 times$. If the engineering team moves the transform history to external LPDDR DRAM, shared bus contention with the video pipeline inflates transform lookup latency from $12 thin mu upright("s")$ to $1.8 upright(" ms")$, violating the 1 kHz control loop deadline.

#emph[How should systems engineers design hierarchical transform storage (e.g., storing full SE(3) transforms in SRAM at reduced temporal keyframes with Lie algebra spline interpolation) to achieve sub-millisecond query determinism within strict static memory constraints (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");)?]

== Chapter Summary and Forward Handoff to #ref(<sec-intent>, supplement: [Chapter])
<chapter-summary-and-forward-handoff-to-sec-intent>
This chapter established how embodied physical agents maintain structured spatial belief and temporal continuity across inevitable sensory occlusions. Physical state is not a stateless text token sequence safely confined to a digital context window; it is a metric, dynamic coordinate tree mathematically integrated in Lie algebra tangent space and strictly bounded by constantly expanding uncertainty covariances. By pairing advanced latent world models on high-performance compute arrays with deterministic hardware ring buffers and real-time proprioceptive state estimators on edge microcontrollers, systems engineers ensure that transient sensory blackouts never result in open-loop physical destruction.

Having firmly anchored the agent's perception into a mathematically sound, temporally persistent world model, the architectural pipeline is now primed to generate purposeful action. In the following chapter, #strong[#ref(<sec-intent>, supplement: [Chapter]) (Intent and Reasoners: System 2 Multimodal VLAs, Spatial Grounding, and Expiring Intent Leases)], we will explore how physical agents reason about task goals over these state beliefs. We will examine how high-level semantic instructions are translated into bounded, physically verified kinematic proposals, and how the system rigorously enforces expiring intent leases before authorizing multi-joint motion in the real world.

