== The Semantic Reasoning Layer
<sec-reasoning-layer>
In biological motor control, physical action operates through a strict temporal and physical hierarchy. When a human decides to pick up a mug from a tabletop, conscious cortical deliberation formulates a macroscopic goal over fractions of a second ($0.5 upright("–") 2 upright(" Hz")$). The conscious brain does not calculate individual motor unit firing rates, biceps tendon tensions, or shoulder joint reaction torques. Instead, high-level intent delegates to spinal motor pools and cerebellar reflex circuits executing at hundreds of hertz. Those low-level reflex loops automatically adjust for muscle fatigue, maintain grip stability, and arrest arm motion if the fingers strike an unexpected rigid obstacle. If the conscious cortex instead attempted to command raw electrical pulses directly to muscle fibers without spinal reflex stabilization, reaching for an object in a dark room would tear ligaments and smash knuckles against the tabletop.

In Physical AI architectures, this cognitive duality is formalized by positioning high-capacity Multimodal Vision-Language Models (VLMs) and Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models at the deliberative apex of the compute hierarchy (#ref(<fig-locator-ch07>, supplement: [Figure])). Executing on multi-core Linux Application Processors (MPUs) or offboard compute clusters, System 2 deliberation ingests multimodal sensory observations $O_t$, natural language task prompts $cal(P)_(upright("prompt"))$ (such as #emph["retrieve the heated calibration bolt behind the fixture"]), and persistent world state representations $S_t$ from the temporal memory organ. The model's primary objective is to translate unstructured semantic prompts into an ordered, executable High-Level Task Graph ($cal(G)_(upright("task"))$).

In physical environments, executing multi-step behavioral primitives without strict causal ordering and state verification leads to mechanical damage. If an ungrounded high-level reasoner commands a robot to close its gripper before reaching the workpiece, or commands a rapid arm retraction before releasing a mechanical latch, moving linkages collide with rigid fixtures or drop fragile components. We formalize this dependency structure as a directed acyclic task graph:

$ cal(G)_(upright("task")) = chevron.l cal(V)_(upright("subtasks")) \, cal(E)_(upright("causal")) \, cal(C)_(upright("preconditions")) chevron.r $

where each vertex $v_i in cal(V)_(upright("subtasks"))$ represents a discrete behavioral primitive (such as #NormalTok("approach_fixture");, #NormalTok("align_gripper");, #NormalTok("close_fingers");, #NormalTok("retract_arm");), directed edges $e_(i j) = \( v_i \, v_j \) in cal(E)_(upright("causal"))$ specify required execution order, and $cal(C)_(upright("preconditions")) \( v_i \) arrow.r { upright("true") \, upright("false") }$ represents a boolean predicate over measured physical state that must evaluate to true before primitive $v_i$ is authorized to execute.

#emph[Operational Walkthrough:] Consider an automated electronics assembly cell executing a 4-node task graph: $v_1 = upright("approach") \( upright(bold(p))_1 \, v_(upright("max")) = 0.40 upright(" m/s") \)$, $v_2 = upright("align_orientation") \( upright(bold(R))_(upright("target")) \)$, $v_3 = upright("insert_pin") \( F_z = 20.0 upright(" N") \)$, and $v_4 = upright("release_and_retract") \( Delta z = 0.15 upright(" m") \)$. If tactile telemetry during $v_3$ registers lateral resistance $F_(upright("lateral")) = 35.0 upright(" N") > 10.0 upright(" N")$ (indicating mechanical jamming), the precondition predicate $cal(C)_(upright("preconditions")) \( v_4 \)$ evaluates to false. Directed edge $e_34$ inhibits $v_4$, preventing the arm from applying excessive vertical pull that would snap the delicate tooling pin.

In digital software engineering, foundation models operate within an unconstrained virtual environment. If an autoregressive language model generates an illogical token sequence or hallucinates an imaginary entity, the software simply prints invalid text to a console. In Physical AI, however, granting an ungrounded foundation model direct authority over actuator inverters is catastrophic. Large neural networks are statistical pattern matchers trained on Internet-scale datasets; they possess zero intrinsic awareness of joint torque envelopes, gear ratios, link inertia, or physical reachability boundaries.

Mastering this deliberative reasoning layer requires bridging three engineering perspectives:

- #strong[Maya (CS/ML):] Evaluates how multimodal foundation models tokenize vision and language, while discovering why single-batch autoregression is memory-bandwidth bound ($< 1.0 upright(" FLOP/byte")$) on edge silicon and why discrete action quantization causes high-frequency mechanical chatter.
- #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded):] Architects the Proposal--Permission boundary between Linux Application Processors (MPUs) and bare-metal Microcontrollers (MCUs), designing zero-allocation shared-memory mailboxes (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");) and hardware watchdogs that enforce deterministic Category 1 stops upon communication timeout.
- #strong[Alex (Robotics/MechE):] Analyzes the geometry of metric affordance grounding in $S E \( 3 \)$, constructing morphological workspace hulls ($cal(W)_(upright("reach"))$) and Jacobian singularity filters ($kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \) lt.eq 50.0 \, w \( upright(bold(q)) \) gt.eq 0.05$) to protect harmonic drive flexsplines from destructive torque shocks.

To bridge semantic intelligence to real-time physical mechanisms without compromising hardware integrity, this chapter establishes the architectural discipline of intent generation. We begin by examining the computational mechanics of Vision-Language-Action networks, tracing how cross-modal attention and discrete action binning generate candidate behaviors while exposing the Key-Value cache memory bottleneck on edge silicon (#ref(<sec-vla-models>, supplement: [Section])). We then resolve the metric grounding problem, lifting dimensionless 2D semantic tokens into calibrated 3D coordinates in $S E \( 3 \)$ and filtering proposals through morphological reachability hulls and Jacobian singularity metrics (#ref(<sec-metric-grounding>, supplement: [Section])).

To bridge the latency chasm between slow deliberative reasoning and fast physical dynamics, we evaluate temporal action chunking across multi-step horizons and formulate receding-horizon polynomial blending to guarantee smooth acceleration (#ref(<sec-action-chunking>, supplement: [Section])). We formalize the Proposal--Permission boundary into Expiring Intent Leases, defining the four runtime invariants and hardware watchdog mechanisms that execute in static microcontroller memory to enforce deterministic Category 2, 1, and 0 fail-safe stops (#ref(<sec-intent-leases>, supplement: [Section])). We then analyze how conformal prediction and calibrated uncertainty detect spatial hallucinations to trigger safe epistemic abstention (#ref(<sec-failure-modes>, supplement: [Section])). Finally, we conduct a forensic autopsy of an industrial arm flexspline fracture caused by ungrounded reach proposals (#ref(<sec-autopsy-intent>, supplement: [Section])), freeze the intent interface specification ledger (#ref(<sec-contract-intent>, supplement: [Section])), stress-test the architecture against high-stakes reasoning dilemmas (#ref(<sec-stress-intent>, supplement: [Section])), and provide the clean forward handoff to trajectory synthesis (#ref(<sec-summary-intent>, supplement: [Section])).

