For #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded)] and #strong[Alex (Robotics/MechE)], a Vision-Language-Action model can be understood as a multimodal sequence transducer that converts heterogeneous sensory signals into discrete behavioral tokens across four sequential stages:

+ #strong[Linguistic Tokenization:] The natural language prompt $cal(P)_(upright("prompt"))$ is partitioned into subword units using Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE), mapping text strings to discrete token indices $upright(bold(t))_(upright("text")) in bb(N)^(L_(upright("text")))$ and projecting them through an embedding lookup matrix into continuous latent vectors $upright(bold(E))_(upright("text")) in bb(R)^(L_(upright("text")) times d)$ (where $d$ is the transformer hidden dimension, typically $d = 4096$).
+ #strong[Visual Patch Tokenization:] Visual frames from calibrated RGB-D cameras (e.g., $224 times 224 times 3$ pixels) are divided into non-overlapping $P times P$ pixel patches (where $P = 14 upright(" px")$, yielding $N_(upright("patches")) = \( 224 \/ 14 \)^2 = 256$ patches). A self-supervised vision transformer backbone (such as DINOv2 or SigLIP) projects each flattened patch into visual feature tokens $upright(bold(E))_(upright("vision")) in bb(R)^(N_(upright("patches")) times d_v)$ (where $d_v = 1024$) that capture dense geometric contours and semantic textures.
+ #strong[Cross-Modal Alignment Adapters:] Because the visual backbone dimension ($d_v = 1024$) differs from the language model's hidden dimension ($d = 4096$), a projection adapter (typically a two-layer Multi-Layer Perceptron or a Perceiver Resampler) maps visual tokens into language space: $upright(bold(Z))_(upright("vis")) = upright("MLP") \( upright(bold(E))_(upright("vision")) \) in bb(R)^(N_(upright("patches")) times d)$.
+ #strong[Causal Transformer Decoding:] The concatenated token sequence $upright(bold(Z))_0 = \[ upright(bold(Z))_(upright("vis")) \; upright(bold(E))_(upright("text")) \] in bb(R)^(\( N_(upright("patches")) + L_(upright("text")) \) times d)$ passes through an autoregressive transformer decoder consisting of dozens of causal self-attention layers.

In physical systems, motor intent generation at time step $t$ must respect the arrow of time: a model cannot attend to future observations that have not yet occurred without violating physical causality. Furthermore, in high-dimensional latent spaces, dot products between unscaled queries and keys grow large, driving softmax activations into saturated regions where gradients vanish. Causal self-attention enforces temporal irreversibility and numerical stability:

$ upright("Attention") \( upright(bold(Q)) \, upright(bold(K)) \, upright(bold(V)) \) = upright("softmax") (frac(upright(bold(Q)) upright(bold(K))^T, sqrt(d_k)) + upright(bold(M))_(upright("causal"))) upright(bold(V)) $

where $upright(bold(Q)) in bb(R)^(L times d_k)$ represents the queries (what information each token seeks), $upright(bold(K)) in bb(R)^(L times d_k)$ represents the keys (what features each token contains), $upright(bold(V)) in bb(R)^(L times d_v)$ represents the values (the extracted content), $d_k$ is the projection dimension per attention head, and $upright(bold(M))_(upright("causal")) in bb(R)^(L times L)$ is an upper-triangular causal mask with entries $M_(i j) = 0$ for $i gt.eq j$ and $M_(i j) = - oo$ for $i < j$, ensuring that predicted tokens depend strictly upon past observations.

#emph[Numerical Walkthrough:] For a prompt-observation sequence length of $L = 512$ tokens with key dimension $d_k = 64$, the scaling factor is $sqrt(d_k) = sqrt(64) = 8.0$. Unscaled dot products of magnitude $upright(bold(q))_i upright(bold(k))_j^T approx 72.0$ would result in extreme logit overflow in half-precision floating-point ($e^72 arrow.r oo$ in FP16), collapsing softmax into an un-trainable one-hot argmax. Scaling by $8.0$ normalizes the logit to $72.0 / 8.0 = 9.0$, yielding smooth, differentiable attention weights across the $256$ visual patch tokens.

To emit continuous robot actions from an autoregressive architecture designed for discrete vocabulary tokens, VLA models (such as RT-2 and OpenVLA) discretize continuous control actions into #strong[Action Bins] @brohan2023rt2@kim2024openvla. Robotic actuators, however, operate on continuous currents and smooth trajectories. Discretizing continuous physical actions into uniform bins introduces quantization error that excites drivetrain resonances if fed directly to motors.

A continuous 7-DoF robot action vector $upright(bold(a)) = \[ Delta x \, Delta y \, Delta z \, Delta phi.alt_x \, Delta phi.alt_y \, Delta phi.alt_z \, upright("gripper") \]^T in bb(R)^7$ (representing Cartesian end-effector translation deltas, rotation deltas, and normalized gripper aperture) is normalized to $\[ a_(upright("min")) \, a_(upright("max")) \] = \[ - 1 \, 1 \]$ and mapped into $B = 256$ uniform discrete bins:

$ upright("bin") \( a_i \) = upright("clip") (⌊frac(a_i - a_(upright("min")), a_(upright("max")) - a_(upright("min"))) dot.op B⌋ \, 0 \, B - 1) \, #h(2em) hat(a)_i = a_(upright("min")) + frac(k + 0.5, B) \( a_(upright("max")) - a_(upright("min")) \) $

During inference, the predicted discrete action token index $k in \[ 0 \, B - 1 \]$ is un-binned back into continuous metric space via linear de-quantization to the bin center.

#emph[Numerical Walkthrough:] For an end-effector velocity bound of $\[ - 0.5 \, + 0.5 \] upright(" m/s")$ ($a_(upright("min")) = - 0.5 upright(" m/s") \, a_(upright("max")) = + 0.5 upright(" m/s")$) with $B = 256$ discrete bins, the discretization resolution is $Delta v = 1.0 / 256 approx 3.91 upright(" mm/s")$. If the high-level reasoner commands $v = 0.100 upright(" m/s")$, the bin index is:

$ k = ⌊frac(0.100 - \( - 0.5 \), 1.0) times 256⌋ = floor.l 0.600 times 256 floor.r = floor.l 153.6 floor.r = 153 $

De-quantizing bin index $k = 153$ yields:

$ hat(v) = - 0.5 + frac(153 + 0.5, 256) \( 1.0 \) = - 0.5 + 153.5 / 256 = - 0.5 + 0.5996 = 0.0996 upright(" m/s") $

introducing an unavoidable steady-state quantization offset of $delta v = 0.100 - 0.0996 = 0.0004 upright(" m/s") = 0.4 upright(" mm/s")$.

This action binning formulation generalizes across all physical archetypes: mobile robots discretize body-frame velocities $upright(bold(a))_(upright("mobile")) = \[ v_x \, v_y \, omega_z \]^T$ (e.g., speed bins of $Delta v approx 7.8 upright(" mm/s")$ over $\[ - 1 \, 1 \] upright(" m/s")$), while cyber-physical energy managers quantize active and reactive power dispatch adjustments $upright(bold(a))_(upright("cyber")) = \[ Delta P_(upright("grid")) \, Delta Q_(upright("var")) \]^T$. The language model's vocabulary is extended with special action tokens ($chevron.l upright("act_0") chevron.r \, dots.h \, chevron.l upright("act_255") chevron.r$), allowing the transformer to emit physical action trajectories using standard cross-entropy loss.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] Discretizing continuous 7-DoF control vectors into $B = 256$ uniform bins allows autoregressive transformers to model robot motion with standard cross-entropy loss, treating physical actions identically to discrete vocabulary tokens.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Uniform binning enforces a fixed quantization resolution ($Delta v = frac(v_(upright("max")) - v_(upright("min")), 256) approx 3.91 upright(" mm/s")$ for a $1.0 upright(" m/s")$ range). In precision contact tasks (such as peg-in-hole insertion or holding position against gravity), the policy cannot output an exact zero velocity or fine-trim torque. Quantization error causes the discrete token to alternate between adjacent bins, injecting high-frequency limit-cycle chattering that excites drivetrain mechanical resonances ($20 upright("–") 80 upright(" Hz")$) and accelerates gearbox backlash wear.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Discrete action tokens emitted by high-level foundation models represent coarse macroscopic behavioral goals, not raw actuator setpoints. Downstream trajectory planners must apply continuous polynomial interpolation, and low-level microcontroller loops must execute continuous impedance control to filter quantization chatter.

] #label("callout-fallacy*-42")
While action binning enables foundation models to express multimodal behaviors, autoregressive token generation suffers from severe micro-architectural bottlenecks on edge silicon. For #strong[Maya (CS/ML)], who is accustomed to training large models with batch sizes of 64 or 128 where weights loaded into SRAM are reused across thousands of operations (achieving high arithmetic intensity of $> 100 upright(" FLOP/byte")$), single-robot inference presents a harsh physical constraint. In real-time physical control, batch size is strictly $N = 1$. Emitting a single 7-DoF action vector requires the model to execute $7$ sequential forward passes, generating tokens $chevron.l upright("act_0") chevron.r$ through $chevron.l upright("act_6") chevron.r$ one by one.

Because single-batch token generation has an arithmetic intensity of less than $1.0 upright(" FLOP/byte")$, execution is fundamentally memory-bandwidth bound: the processor spends over $90 %$ of its time waiting for weights to stream from DRAM into on-chip cache. For a 7-billion parameter model in 16-bit precision ($14 upright(" GB")$ of weights), generating 7 sequential tokens requires streaming $7 times 14 upright(" GB") = 98 upright(" GB")$ across the memory crossbar. To avoid recomputing Key-Value activations across multi-frame observation histories, the runtime maintains a #strong[Key-Value (KV) Cache]. In an embedded System-on-Chip (SoC) using Unified Memory Architecture (UMA) LPDDR5 DRAM (such as an NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin or Apple Silicon), the GPU/NPU, CPU cores, and camera Direct Memory Access (DMA) controllers all share the same physical memory bus.

When autoregressive weight and KV-cache streaming saturates $85 upright("–") 90 %$ of the DRAM crossbar bandwidth, real-time camera DMA ring buffers experience bus arbitration stalls. This memory contention drops sensory frames and inflates $P_99$ deliberative tail latency from nominal $P_50 = 450 upright(" ms")$ to an unmanageable $P_99 = 1800 upright(" ms")$. This severe latency jitter confirms why slow autoregressive VLAs must never be placed inside real-time motor control loops.

== Metric Affordance Grounding
<sec-metric-grounding>
When an autoregressive VLA model generates a discrete action sequence, the resulting tokens represent dimensionless numerical indices. Before these tokens can command physical mechanisms, they must be grounded into continuous, metric coordinate frames within the physical workspace (#ref(<fig-reachability-filter>, supplement: [Figure])).

#figure([
#box(image("chapters/07-intent/figures/fig06_kinematic_reachability_filter.svg", width: 100.0%))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
#strong[Kinematic Reachability and Singularity Screening Filter.] Candidate goal poses generated by foundation models are screened through three non-negotiable geometric gates: (1) 3D Workspace Bounding Hull ($cal(W)_(upright("reach"))$), (2) Keep-Out Collision Volumes ($cal(B)_(upright("keepout"))$), and (3) Singularity Manipulability Envelopes ($kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \) lt.eq 50 \, w \( upright(bold(q)) \) gt.eq 0.05$). Proposals breaching reachability boundaries are rejected before passing to trajectory generators.
