<fig-reachability-filter>


In computer vision pipelines, neural detectors output bounding boxes or keypoints in 2D pixel coordinates $\( u \, v \)$. A pixel is dimensionless light on an imaging sensor: commanding a robot arm to move to pixel $\( 480 \, 360 \)$ without calibrated optics and registered depth commands an undefined physical target, causing the arm to plunge into the workbench or sweep through empty air. Lifting 2D semantic affordance predictions into 3D metric space begins by combining camera intrinsic calibration with optical depth registration $d \( u \, v \)$ and extrinsic frame transformations:

$ upright(bold(p))_(upright("target"))^(upright("world")) = upright(bold(T))_(upright("cam"))^(upright("world")) mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(K))^(- 1) mat(delim: "[", u; v; 1) d \( u \, v \); 1) = upright(bold(T))_(upright("cam"))^(upright("world")) mat(delim: "[", frac(u - c_x, f_x) d \( u \, v \); frac(v - c_y, f_y) d \( u \, v \); d \( u \, v \); 1) $

where $\( u \, v \)$ are pixel coordinates in the 2D image plane ($upright("pixels")$), $\( c_x \, c_y \)$ is the optical principal point ($upright("pixels")$), $\( f_x \, f_y \)$ are focal lengths ($upright("pixels")$), $d \( u \, v \)$ is registered metric depth ($upright("meters")$), $upright(bold(K)) in bb(R)^(3 times 3)$ is the intrinsic camera matrix, and $upright(bold(T))_(upright("cam"))^(upright("world")) in S E \( 3 \)$ is the rigid $4 times 4$ extrinsic transform from camera optical frame to robot base frame. The approach orientation $upright(bold(R))_(upright("approach")) in S O \( 3 \)$ aligns the gripper approach vector antiparallel to the surface normal $hat(upright(bold(n)))$, completing the 6-DoF target pose $upright(bold(T))_(upright("target"))^(upright("world")) = mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(R))_(upright("approach")), upright(bold(p))_(upright("target"))^(upright("world")); upright(bold(0))^T, 1) in S E \( 3 \)$.

#emph[Numerical Walkthrough:] On a $640 times 480$ RGB-D camera with focal lengths $f_x = f_y = 525.0 upright(" px")$, principal point $\( c_x \, c_y \) = \( 320.0 \, 240.0 \) upright(" px")$, and measured depth $d \( 480 \, 360 \) = 0.650 upright(" m")$, the 3D position in the camera frame is:

$ x_c = frac(480.0 - 320.0, 525.0) times 0.650 = 160.0 / 525.0 times 0.650 approx 0.198 upright(" m") $

$ y_c = frac(360.0 - 240.0, 525.0) times 0.650 = 120.0 / 525.0 times 0.650 approx 0.148 upright(" m") \, quad z_c = 0.650 upright(" m") $

Given a camera mounted on the robot base at offset $upright(bold(p))_(upright("cam")) = \[ 0.10 \, 0.00 \, 0.50 \]^T upright(" m")$ with identity rotation ($upright(bold(R))_(upright("cam"))^(upright("world")) = upright(bold(I))$), the target in world coordinates is $upright(bold(p))_(upright("target"))^(upright("world")) = \[ 0.10 + 0.198 \, 0.00 + 0.148 \, 0.50 + 0.650 \]^T = \[ 0.298 \, 0.148 \, 1.150 \]^T upright(" m")$.

Once a metric target frame $upright(bold(T))_(upright("target"))^(upright("world"))$ is constructed, the reasoning engine must verify that the proposed pose is physically reachable. The workspace of an articulated manipulator is strictly bounded by its kinematic linkage lengths, joint travel limits, and mounting geometry, defining the #strong[Reachable Workspace Hull] ($cal(W)_(upright("reach")) subset bb(R)^3$). Furthermore, obstacle geometry extracted from the temporal world model defines prohibited #strong[Keep-Out Volumes] ($cal(B)_(upright("keepout")) subset bb(R)^3$). Any target pose residing outside the bounding hull ($upright(bold(p))_(upright("target")) in.not cal(W)_(upright("reach"))$) or intersecting a keep-out volume is immediately rejected. This morphological gating applies universally: mobile robots verify that 2D/3D navigation waypoints lie within traversable ground-plane corridors subject to non-holonomic steering bounds, while cybernetic processes verify that power and thermal dispatch setpoints reside within the admissible operating polytope defined by generator capability curves ($upright(bold(A))_(upright("grid")) upright(bold(x)) lt.eq upright(bold(b))$).

Physical reachability extends beyond positional coordinates: the robot must be capable of generating motion without driving its actuators into kinematic singularities. For #strong[Maya (CS/ML)] and #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded)], who may be encountering differential kinematics for the first time, the geometric Jacobian matrix $upright(bold(J)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) in bb(R)^(6 times n)$ represents the instantaneous physical transmission ratio mapping joint angular velocities $dot(upright(bold(q))) in bb(R)^n$ ($upright("rad/s")$) to end-effector Cartesian linear and angular velocities $dot(upright(bold(x))) = \[ upright(bold(v))^T \, bold(omega)^T \]^T in bb(R)^6$ ($\[ upright("m/s") \, upright("rad/s") \]^T$):

$ dot(upright(bold(x))) = upright(bold(J)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) dot(upright(bold(q))) arrow.r.double.long dot(upright(bold(q))) = upright(bold(J))^dagger \( upright(bold(q)) \) dot(upright(bold(x))) $

where $upright(bold(J))^dagger = upright(bold(J))^T \( upright(bold(J)) upright(bold(J))^T \)^(- 1)$ is the right Moore-Penrose pseudo-inverse for redundant manipulators ($n gt.eq 6$).

When an articulated arm approaches a kinematic singularity (such as full elbow extension or wrist gimbal alignment), the manipulator loses the physical capability to move in one or more Cartesian directions. Mathematically, the matrix $upright(bold(J)) upright(bold(J))^T$ loses rank, and its minimum singular value collapses ($sigma_(upright("min")) \( upright(bold(J)) \) arrow.r 0$). Attempting to execute even a minute Cartesian velocity in a singular direction requires inverting this near-zero singular value, commanding joint angular velocities that spike toward infinity ($parallel dot(upright(bold(q))) parallel arrow.r oo$).

To prevent destructive acceleration whiplash, the semantic reasoning engine evaluates candidate configurations using the #strong[Yoshikawa Manipulability Index] ($w \( upright(bold(q)) \)$) and the #strong[Jacobian Condition Number] ($kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \)$):

$ w \( upright(bold(q)) \) = sqrt(det (upright(bold(J)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) upright(bold(J))^T \( upright(bold(q)) \))) = product_(i = 1)^6 sigma_i \( upright(bold(J)) \) gt.eq w_(upright("min")) = 0.05 \, #h(2em) kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \) = frac(sigma_(upright("max")) \( upright(bold(J)) \), sigma_(upright("min")) \( upright(bold(J)) \)) lt.eq kappa_(upright("max")) = 50.0 $

The Yoshikawa index $w \( upright(bold(q)) \)$ measures the volume of the 6D manipulability ellipsoid; as $w \( upright(bold(q)) \) arrow.r 0$, the ellipsoid flattens into a degenerate plane, signifying loss of mobility degrees of freedom. The condition number $kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \)$ evaluates the ratio of maximum to minimum ellipsoid principal axes, measuring the velocity amplification and numerical distortion of the inverse kinematic solve.

#emph[Numerical Walkthrough:] In a well-conditioned nominal configuration $upright(bold(q))_(upright("nom"))$, a 6-DoF arm achieves singular values $bold(sigma) \( upright(bold(J)) \) = \[ 0.85 \, 0.72 \, 0.54 \, 0.40 \, 0.32 \, 0.20 \]$, yielding $w \( upright(bold(q)) \) = product sigma_i approx 0.0084$ (normalized metric $w_(upright("norm")) approx 0.12 gt.eq 0.05$) and condition number $kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \) = 0.85 / 0.20 = 4.25 lt.eq 50.0$. Near full elbow extension, singular values collapse to $sigma_(upright("min")) = 0.0008$, dropping manipulability to $w \( upright(bold(q)) \) approx 3.5 times 10^(- 5) lt.double 0.05$ and exploding the condition number to $kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \) = 0.85 / 0.0008 = 1 \, 062.5 gt.double 50.0$, immediately triggering kinematic proposal rejection.

Any proposal that breaches these bounds is rejected in software before candidate trajectory generation begins, guaranteeing that singular configurations never translate into saturated motor currents or sheared gearboxes.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] Any 3D spatial coordinate within the manipulator's maximum radial reach sphere ($r lt.eq R_(upright("max"))$) is physically achievable, allowing a vision-language model to command arbitrary Cartesian target poses inside the workspace envelope.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] The physical workspace boundary and internal kinematic singular configurations cause the manipulator Jacobian $upright(bold(J)) \( upright(bold(q)) \)$ to lose rank ($sigma_(upright("min")) \( upright(bold(J)) \) arrow.r 0$), driving the condition number to extreme values ($kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \) > 1000$). Direct numerical pseudo-inversion ($dot(upright(bold(q))) = upright(bold(J))^dagger dot(upright(bold(x)))$) near singular boundaries demands near-infinite joint angular velocities ($parallel dot(upright(bold(q))) parallel arrow.r oo$). This saturates motor phase currents ($I_(upright("phase")) gt.eq 24 upright(" A")$), whips linkages violently across the cell, and causes plastic tooth shear in harmonic gearboxes.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Semantic target poses must be screened through condition number ceilings ($kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \) lt.eq 50.0$) and Yoshikawa manipulability floors ($w \( upright(bold(q)) \) gt.eq 0.05$) in software before candidate trajectory generation or inverse kinematics solves are authorized.

