== Temporal Action Chunking
<sec-action-chunking>
In early embodied AI implementations, researchers attempted to command robots by running foundation models in a tight single-step feedback loop: at each control step $t$, the VLA ingested a camera frame and emitted a single action $a_t$.

In physical hardware, single-step autoregressive execution fails completely due to computational latency. Evaluating a multi-billion parameter VLA requires $500 upright("–") 1000 upright(" ms")$ on edge accelerators ($0.5 upright("–") 2 upright(" Hz")$). If an autonomous manipulator moving at $v = 1.0 upright(" m/s")$ waits for single-step model inferences, the robot executes a jerky, stop-and-go motion, pausing for an entire second between millimeter movements. During each inference stall, moving mass accumulates unguided reaction displacement ($d_(upright("react")) = v dot.op Delta t = 1.0 upright(" m")$), destroying dynamic stability.

Physical AI resolves this latency chasm through #strong[Temporal Action Chunking] @zhao2023learning@chi2023diffusionpolicy. Rather than predicting a single instantaneous action autoregressively, the model deploys non-autoregressive parallel decoders or diffusion heads to generate a contiguous sequence of $H$ future action waypoints in a single forward execution pass:

$ upright(bold(A))_(t : t + H) = [upright(bold(a))_t \, upright(bold(a))_(t + 1) \, upright(bold(a))_(t + 2) \, dots.h \, upright(bold(a))_(t + H - 1)] in bb(R)^(H times d_a) $

where each action waypoint $upright(bold(a))_(t + k) = \[ upright(bold(q))_(t + k) \, dot(upright(bold(q)))_(t + k) \, bold(tau)_(t + k) \, upright("grip")_(t + k) \]$ specifies target joint configurations ($upright("rad")$), feedforward velocities ($upright("rad/s")$), expected interaction wrenches ($upright("N") \, upright("N") dot.op upright("m")$), and gripper states.

#emph[Numerical Walkthrough:] Consider a chunk horizon of $H = 16$ waypoints sampled at discrete intervals of $Delta t = 20 upright(" ms")$. The action chunk spans a temporal lookahead window of $T_(upright("chunk")) = H dot.op Delta t = 16 times 0.020 = 0.320 upright(" s") = 320 upright(" ms")$. If an embedded neural processing unit (NPU) evaluates the parallel diffusion or transformer decoder in $T_(upright("infer")) = 25 upright(" ms")$ ($40 upright(" Hz")$ throughput), the model synthesizes $320 upright(" ms")$ of continuous kinodynamic motion every $25 upright(" ms")$. This completely eliminates the latency starvation bottleneck, continuously buffering verified future setpoints for downstream $1000 upright(" Hz")$ motor control loops.

Selecting the optimal action chunk horizon $H$ involves navigating a fundamental systems trade-off (#ref(<tbl-action-chunk-tradeoffs>, supplement: [Table])). A short horizon ($H = 4$, $tilde.op 80 upright(" ms")$) provides high responsiveness to sudden environmental perturbations but requires high-frequency model inference that overwhelms edge NPUs and drains battery power. A long horizon ($H = 64$, $tilde.op 1280 upright(" ms")$) amortizes inference latency and yields exceptionally smooth trajectories but renders the robot blind to dynamic obstacle motion occurring mid-chunk.

