When transitioning between consecutive action chunks, abruptly switching from the active chunk to a newly arrived chunk creates an instantaneous velocity step ($Delta dot(upright(bold(q))) > 0$). In physical drivetrains, differentiating a step change in velocity yields theoretical infinite acceleration ($dot.double(upright(bold(q))) arrow.r oo$) and infinite mechanical jerk ($accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) arrow.r oo$), which excites high-frequency drivetrain resonances ($20 upright("–") 80 upright(" Hz")$) and damages gear teeth.

To eliminate boundary jerk, the downstream trajectory planner executes #strong[Receding-Horizon Boundary Stitching]. Action chunks are queried asynchronously at an update period $K < H$ (typically $K = H \/ 2$). When a fresh action chunk $upright(bold(A))_(t + K : t + K + H)^(upright("new"))$ arrives, the planner blends the tail of the active trajectory with the head of the new trajectory using a temporal weighting kernel $w \( tau \)$:

$ upright(bold(a))_(upright("blended")) \( tau \) = \( 1 - w \( tau \) \) dot.op upright(bold(a))_(upright("active")) \( tau \) + w \( tau \) dot.op upright(bold(a))_(upright("new")) \( tau \) \, #h(2em) w \( tau \) = 3 (tau / T_(upright("blend")))^2 - 2 (tau / T_(upright("blend")))^3 $

where $tau in \[ 0 \, T_(upright("blend")) \]$ is the elapsed time within the blending window $T_(upright("blend"))$. The cubic Hermite weighting kernel $w \( tau \) in \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ satisfies boundary conditions $w \( 0 \) = 0 \, w \( T_(upright("blend")) \) = 1$ and zero endpoint derivatives $w' \( 0 \) = w' \( T_(upright("blend")) \) = 0$. This guarantees $cal(C)^1$ velocity continuity across chunk transitions, eliminating instantaneous position jumps and bounding torque spikes. For high-precision mechanisms demanding $cal(C)^2$ jerk continuity, a quintic polynomial $w_5 \( tau \)$ is substituted:

$ w_5 \( tau \) = 6 (tau / T_(upright("blend")))^5 - 15 (tau / T_(upright("blend")))^4 + 10 (tau / T_(upright("blend")))^3 $

which enforces $w_5 \( 0 \) = 0 \, w_5 \( T_(upright("blend")) \) = 1$, $w'_5 \( 0 \) = w'_5 \( T_(upright("blend")) \) = 0$, and $w''_5 \( 0 \) = w''_5 \( T_(upright("blend")) \) = 0$, ensuring that acceleration is strictly continuous across chunk boundaries.

#emph[Numerical Walkthrough:] For a blending duration of $T_(upright("blend")) = 100 upright(" ms")$, at the midpoint $tau = 50 upright(" ms")$ ($tau \/ T_(upright("blend")) = 0.5$), the cubic kernel evaluates to $w \( 0.5 \) = 3 \( 0.5 \)^2 - 2 \( 0.5 \)^3 = 3 \( 0.25 \) - 2 \( 0.125 \) = 0.75 - 0.25 = 0.500$. If the active trajectory commands velocity $v_(upright("active")) = 0.40 upright(" m/s")$ and the newly arrived chunk commands $v_(upright("new")) = 0.60 upright(" m/s")$, the blended velocity is $v_(upright("blended")) \( 50 upright(" ms") \) = \( 1 - 0.500 \) \( 0.40 \) + 0.500 \( 0.60 \) = 0.20 + 0.30 = 0.50 upright(" m/s")$. Because the polynomial derivative begins at zero ($w' \( 0 \) = 0$), the transition initiates with zero initial acceleration jump, protecting speed reducers from torque shock.

== Expiring Intent Leases
<sec-intent-leases>
To operationalize the architectural privilege separation between untrusted foundation models on Linux MPUs and trusted permission supervisors on bare-metal MCUs, the reasoning engine communicates exclusively through the #strong[Expiring Intent Lease] ($cal(L)_(upright("intent"))$) (#ref(<fig-expiring-lease>, supplement: [Figure])).

