An Expiring Intent Lease is a formal, time-bounded tuple emitted by the high-level reasoning engine:

$ cal(L)_(upright("intent")) = chevron.l cal(G)_(upright("task")) \, cal(S)_(upright("hull")) \, t_(upright("issue")) \, Delta t_(upright("lease")) \, upright(bold(w))_(upright("target")) \, cal(B)_(upright("keepout")) \, upright("CRC32") chevron.r $

where $cal(G)_(upright("task"))$ defines the active subtask primitive, $cal(S)_(upright("hull")) subset bb(R)^3$ represents the convex polyhedral corridor within which the mechanism is authorized to move (e.g., a 3D tubular workspace volume for an arm, a 2D traversability corridor for an AGV, or a bounded operational manifold for a microgrid), $t_(upright("issue"))$ is the IEEE 1588 PTP hardware timestamp of issuance, $Delta t_(upright("lease")) in \[ 100 \, 500 \] upright(" ms")$ defines the strict operational lifetime, $upright(bold(w))_(upright("target"))$ specifies the nominal target wrench $\[ F_x \, F_y \, F_z \, tau_x \, tau_y \, tau_z \]^T$, operational-space velocity setpoint, or cybernetic dispatch target, $cal(B)_(upright("keepout"))$ denotes active obstacle or hazard boundaries, and $upright("CRC32")$ is a 32-bit cyclic redundancy check verifying shared-memory transport integrity.

The deterministic permission authority running at $1000 upright(" Hz")$ on the isolated microcontroller evaluates four runtime invariants before allowing any candidate control input $upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand"))$ to pass to the motor inverters:

+ #strong[Temporal Freshness Invariant:] The instantaneous hardware time $t_(upright("now"))$ must strictly satisfy:

$ t_(upright("now")) - t_(upright("issue")) lt.eq Delta t_(upright("lease")) $

If an operating system stall, memory bus contention spike, or VLM inference delay prevents the MPU from publishing a renewed lease before $Delta t_(upright("lease"))$ elapses, the active lease deterministically expires. The MCU immediately initiates an autonomous controlled stop without requiring any explicit abort command from the faulted host.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ #strong[Spatial Boundedness Invariant:] The forward kinematic swept volume $cal(S)_(upright("cand"))$ generated by candidate trajectory tracking must remain entirely enclosed within the authorized corridor:
]

$ cal(S)_(upright("cand")) subset.eq cal(S)_(upright("hull")) \\ cal(B)_(upright("keepout")) $

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 3)
+ #strong[Dynamic Stopping Clearance Invariant:] The Euclidean distance to the corridor boundary $d_(upright("clearance")) \( upright(bold(x)) \)$ must strictly exceed the worst-case kinematic stopping distance:
]

$ d_(upright("clearance")) \( upright(bold(x)) \) gt.eq d_(upright("stop")) \( v \) = v dot.op tau_(upright("tail")) + frac(v^2, 2 a_(upright("max"))) $

where $tau_(upright("tail"))$ is the total hardware reaction latency (the $1 upright(" ms")$ MCU control cycle plus inverter drive latency, typically $tau_(upright("tail")) lt.eq 20 upright(" ms")$), $v = parallel dot(upright(bold(x))) parallel$ is the instantaneous end-effector velocity ($upright("m/s")$), and $a_(upright("max"))$ is the maximum allowable deceleration ($upright("m/s")^2$) under actuator torque limits.

#emph[Numerical Walkthrough:] At a transport velocity of $v = 1.0 upright(" m/s")$ with hardware reaction delay $tau_(upright("tail")) = 20 upright(" ms")$ ($0.020 upright(" s")$) and maximum braking deceleration $a_(upright("max")) = 5.0 upright(" m/s")^2$, unguided reaction drift consumes $v dot.op tau_(upright("tail")) = 1.0 times 0.020 = 0.02 upright(" m")$ ($2 upright(" cm")$), while mechanical braking work requires $frac(v^2, 2 a_(upright("max"))) = 1.0^2 / 10.0 = 0.10 upright(" m")$ ($10 upright(" cm")$). The minimum required boundary clearance is:

$ d_(upright("stop")) = 0.02 + 0.10 = 0.12 upright(" m") = 12 upright(" cm") $

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 4)
+ #strong[Control Barrier Function Safety Invariant:] The candidate control input must satisfy forward invariance for all active barrier certificates $h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) gt.eq 0$:
]

$ L_f h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) + L_g h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) upright(bold(u)) + alpha \( h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) \) gt.eq 0 $

where $h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) gt.eq 0$ defines the barrier certificate for obstacle boundary $i$ (where $h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) > 0$ defines safe free space and $h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) = 0$ defines the physical boundary), $L_f h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) = nabla h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) dot.op f \( upright(bold(x)) \)$ and $L_g h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) = nabla h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) dot.op g \( upright(bold(x)) \)$ are Lie derivatives projecting system drift and control vector fields along the safety gradient, and $alpha \( h \) = gamma h$ (with $gamma > 0$, in units of $upright("s")^(- 1)$) is an extended class-$cal(K)$ function governing boundary approach rate. Because this constraint is strictly affine in the control input $upright(bold(u))$, the $1000 upright(" Hz")$ MCU evaluates it as a linear half-space constraint in a convex Quadratic Program (QP) safety filter:

$ upright(bold(u))^(*) = arg min_(upright(bold(u))) 1 / 2 parallel upright(bold(u)) - upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand")) parallel^2 quad upright("s.t.") quad L_g h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) upright(bold(u)) gt.eq - L_f h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) - gamma h_i \( upright(bold(x)) \) quad forall i $

which modulates candidate controls $upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand"))$ minimally in sub-millisecond static SRAM (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");).

When an invariant is breached or a lease expires, the real-time supervisor maps the fault to one of three standardized emergency stop categories defined by industrial safety standards (IEC 60204-1 and ISO 13849):

- #strong[Category 2 Stop (Controlled Pause with Power Retained):] The system brings the mechanism to a controlled stop while maintaining electrical drive power to motor inverters, holding the current joint positions actively in closed-loop feedback. This transition is used for temporary operational pauses, operator hold requests, or minor epistemic ambiguity where immediate resumption is anticipated.
- #strong[Category 1 Stop (Controlled Deceleration with Power Cutoff at Rest):] The system decelerates the mechanism along a safe trajectory to zero velocity using active regenerative braking within the authorized corridor, and then immediately cuts electrical power to the motor inverters while engaging mechanical friction brakes. This transition is the default fail-safe response when an Expiring Intent Lease lapses without renewal or when the MPU encounters an operating system kernel panic.
- #strong[Category 0 Stop (Immediate Uncontrolled Power Interruption):] The system immediately cuts all electrical power to actuator gate drivers via hardware electromechanical interlock relays, leaving the mechanism to decelerate solely under passive friction, gravity, and mechanical brakes. This stop is reserved for catastrophic emergency events, such as physical safety fence breaches, detected hard collisions, or hardware watchdog processor resets.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] An intent command emitted by a high-level cognitive planner remains valid indefinitely until the planner explicitly computes and transmits an #NormalTok("ABORT"); or #NormalTok("NEW_GOAL"); message across the inter-process bus.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] High-level Linux host processes running large neural models are vulnerable to unannounced operating system stalls, memory allocation paging locks, GPU driver faults, and $P_99$ latency spikes exceeding $1800 upright(" ms")$. If the host process hangs while the robot is moving at $v = 1.0 upright(" m/s")$, an active-abort architecture leaves the actuators executing the last valid command indefinitely, traveling $1.8 upright(" m")$ unguided into obstacles before human operators can intervene.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Autonomous physical systems must enforce fail-safe #emph[passive expiration] rather than #emph[active cancellation]. All high-level intent must be encapsulated within an Expiring Intent Lease ($cal(L)_(upright("intent"))$) with a hardware-timestamped Time-To-Live ($Delta t_(upright("lease")) lt.eq 300 upright(" ms")$). If upstream software fails to refresh the lease before expiration, the real-time microcontroller autonomously executes a Category 1 controlled stop in static SRAM.

