== Semantic Grounding Failure Modes
<sec-failure-modes>
When deploying large foundation models to govern physical intent, systems engineers confront three archetypal semantic failure modes that never arise in classical control theory:

+ #strong[Spatial Hallucination:] Autoregressive vision-language models frequently hallucinate 3D coordinates in empty air or inside solid objects. In manipulation, an ungrounded VLA predicts grasp targets $20 upright(" cm")$ behind solid fixture surfaces due to monocular depth training bias; in mobility, a navigation model hallucinates drivable free space over descending steps; in cybernetics, an ungrounded policy commands generator power dispatch exceeding line thermal capacity.
+ #strong[Affordance and Material Mismatch:] A high-level model may correctly identify a visual target (e.g., a glass beaker) but propose an invalid interaction parameter, such as commanding a $50 upright(" N")$ pinch grip on fragile glassware or attempting to lift a $25 upright(" kg")$ steel billet with an arm rated for a $5 upright(" kg")$ continuous payload.
+ #strong[Semantic Ambiguity and Multi-Modal Mode Collapse:] When presented with an ambiguous natural language instruction (e.g., #emph["clean the table"] in a scene containing two identical mugs), standard $cal(L)_2$ regression heads minimize conditional expectation ($bb(E) \[ upright(bold(y)) divides upright(bold(x)) \]$), mathematically averaging the two distinct trajectory modes and driving the end-effector directly into the space between them. While diffusion policies natively represent multimodal distributions, downstream temporal chunk ensembling can inadvertently blend distinct sample trajectories across consecutive queries, creating identical collision hazards.

To safeguard against these failure modes, Physical AI architectures implement #strong[Conformal Prediction and Calibrated Uncertainty Filtering] @vovk2005algorithmic. Multimodal foundation models output uncalibrated softmax logits that suffer from severe overconfidence under out-of-distribution glare, lens smudges, or novel workpiece geometry ($P > 0.99$ on hallucinated targets).

Given a calibration dataset of $n$ ground-truth physical scenes $cal(D)_(upright("cal")) = { \( x_i \, y_i \) }_(i = 1)^n$, we define a non-conformity score measuring prediction error:

$ s_i = parallel upright(bold(p))_(upright("pred")) \( x_i \) - upright(bold(p))_(upright("true")) \( y_i \) parallel in bb(R)_(gt.eq 0) $

For a user-selected error tolerance $epsilon.alt in \( 0 \, 1 \)$ (such as $epsilon.alt = 0.15$, guaranteeing $1 - epsilon.alt = 85 %$ statistical coverage), the empirical $\( 1 - epsilon.alt \)$ quantile threshold over calibration residuals is:

$ hat(q)_(1 - epsilon.alt) = upright("Quantile") ({ s_1 \, dots.h \, s_n } \, frac(ceil.l \( n + 1 \) \( 1 - epsilon.alt \) ceil.r, n)) $

For a fresh test observation $x_(upright("test"))$, the calibrated prediction set is constructed as:

$ cal(C) \( x_(upright("test")) \) = {upright(bold(p)) in bb(R)^3 : parallel upright(bold(p)) - upright(bold(p))_(upright("pred")) \( x_(upright("test")) \) parallel lt.eq hat(q)_(1 - epsilon.alt)} $

which guarantees distribution-free coverage in the physical workspace:

$ bb(P) (upright(bold(p))_(upright("true")) in cal(C) \( x_(upright("test")) \)) gt.eq 1 - epsilon.alt $

#emph[Numerical Walkthrough:] An electronics assembly cell is calibrated on $n = 500$ ground-truth grasp trials. For a target coverage of $1 - epsilon.alt = 0.85$ ($85 %$), the calibration rank index is $k = ceil.l \( 500 + 1 \) times 0.85 ceil.r = ceil.l 425.85 ceil.r = 426$. Sorting calibration residuals yields the 426th error: $hat(q)_0.85 = 0.024 upright(" m")$ ($2.4 upright(" cm")$). During deployment under intense overhead glare, the VLA predicts grasp coordinate $upright(bold(p))_(upright("pred")) = \[ 0.45 \, 0.20 \, 0.35 \]^T upright(" m")$ with an ensemble rollout spatial variance of $sigma_(upright("spatial")) = 0.048 upright(" m")$. Because the spatial uncertainty radius exceeds the conformal calibration bound ($0.048 upright(" m") > 0.024 upright(" m")$), the normalized confidence metric drops to $gamma_(upright("conf")) = hat(q) / sigma_(upright("spatial")) = 0.024 / 0.048 = 0.50 lt.double 0.85$.

If the confidence score drops below the safety threshold ($gamma_(upright("conf")) < 0.85$), or if spatial variance across sample rollouts exceeds the dynamic clearance margin ($sigma_(upright("spatial")) > d_(upright("clearance"))$), the reasoning engine suppresses execution. The system transitions into an active epistemic abstention state, commanding the pan-tilt camera head to gather multi-view observations to resolve ambiguity, holding current position safely, or querying the human operator for linguistic clarification before resuming physical motion.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] If a multimodal foundation model outputs a high softmax probability score ($P > 0.95$) for a semantic target detection or affordance token, the system has high certainty and can safely execute the physical action without additional verification.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Softmax probabilities are relative normalizations of uncalibrated neural network logits across a closed vocabulary. Under real-world optical glare, camera lens smudges, or out-of-distribution physical objects, models are notoriously overconfident, assigning $P > 0.99$ to spatial coordinates located in empty air or inside solid metal fixtures.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Safety gating requires distribution-free statistical coverage guarantees derived via Conformal Prediction ($gamma_(upright("conf")) gt.eq 0.85$) and spatial variance screening across rollout distributions ($sigma_(upright("spatial")) lt.eq d_(upright("clearance"))$). When conformal confidence bounds are breached, the system must trigger active epistemic abstention.

