#emph[Why does an AI policy that outputs smooth trajectories in simulation snap gear teeth and shake violently on a physical robot?]

In digital machine learning, sequential decision-making is framed as a Markov decision process where policies emit a single discrete action per time step. On physical hardware, this single-step paradigm breaks down because neural forward passes require tens of milliseconds, during which actuators starve for fresh setpoints. When delayed actions arrive, discrete step changes command instantaneous acceleration steps, injecting theoretical infinite jerk ($accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) arrow.r oo$) that excites mechanical resonances, fatigues harmonic drive flexsplines, and shears gearbox pins. Physical AI resolves this throughput mismatch through action chunking: predicting multi-step future horizons that amortize computational latency, blending overlapping predictions across physical time through exponential recency ensembling, and interpolating waypoints via $cal(C)^2$ quintic splines to respect the physical limits of mass, torque, and transmission integrity.

