== Trajectory Synthesis and Cadence
<sec-trajectory-cadence>
Physical action takes place across fundamentally incompatible time scales. When an autonomous embodied agent operates in an unstructured physical environment, high-level semantic reasoning and deliberate task decomposition operate at macroscopic intervals ($0.5 upright("–") 2.0 upright(" Hz")$), constrained by the heavy arithmetic burden and memory bandwidth of multi-billion-parameter vision-language models (#ref(<sec-intent>, supplement: [Chapter])). Conversely, stabilizing physical contact, damping structural vibrations, and regulating magnetic flux in brushless motor windings demands microsecond determinism ($1000 upright("–") 20 \, 000 upright(" Hz")$, Chapter 9). Between these two extremes sits the trajectory planning organ, designated in this textbook as System 1.5 (#ref(<fig-locator-ch08>, supplement: [Figure])).

=== The Cadence Mismatch and the Single-Step Bottleneck
<the-cadence-mismatch-and-the-single-step-bottleneck>
In standard digital machine learning frameworks, control policies evaluate a single sensory observation and emit a single control vector ($H = 1$). For #strong[Maya (CS/ML)], trained on discrete-time Markov Decision Processes (MDPs), this setup seems entirely natural: observe state $upright(bold(s))_t$, sample action $upright(bold(a))_t tilde.op pi \( dot.op \| upright(bold(s))_t \)$, and transition to $upright(bold(s))_(t + 1)$.

On physical hardware, however, this single-step assumption triggers a catastrophic dynamical failure. A deep neural network running on an embedded accelerator requires a finite inference window of $T_(upright("infer")) in \[ 20 \, 50 \] upright(" ms")$, capping policy throughput at $20 upright("–") 50 upright(" Hz")$. Meanwhile, a $1000 upright(" Hz")$ motor inverter updates every $1.0 upright(" ms")$. If the low-level controller is driven directly by this single-step policy, it is forced to hold each commanded setpoint constant across 20 to 50 consecutive control cycles.

To understand why this is fatal, consider two intuitive physical mental models: #strong[The Musician's Motor Chunk] and #strong[The Delayed Steering Wheel].

When a concert pianist executes a rapid 16-note arpeggio or an acoustic guitarist plays a fast flamenco rasgueado, they do not perceive, deliberate, and decide note-by-note with 50-millisecond cognitive feedback pauses between each individual finger pluck. If the musician stopped to deliberatively plan every strike, the musical performance would immediately collapse into jagged, unplayable stutter. Instead, cognitive intent compiles and triggers a multi-step #emph[motor chunk]---a continuous 500-to-800 ms forward trajectory ribbon that the spinal neuromuscular reflex arc executes fluidly at 1000 Hz. While the fingers sweep across the physical keys, the brain's higher cognitive centers concurrently evaluate the acoustics of the hall and compose the subsequent musical phrase.

Conversely, consider what happens when a machine lacks this continuous motor buffering: #strong[The Delayed Steering Wheel]. Imagine driving a high-speed vehicle on an icy road while your windshield camera displays footage delayed by a quarter of a second, and your steering wheel only accepts an update every 50 milliseconds. By the time your visual cortex detects that the vehicle has begun to drift rightward, the car has already rotated significantly. When your corrective left-steering command finally reaches the wheels, it arrives too late, applying torque long after the car has rotated past center. Your delayed correction oversteers the vehicle into a violent leftward skid, setting off sustained, amplifying zig-zag oscillations until the tires lose traction completely.

=== The Mathematics of Phase Lag under Zero-Order Hold
<the-mathematics-of-phase-lag-under-zero-order-hold>
In physical control systems, holding a commanded position or velocity setpoint constant between policy updates is known as a #strong[Zero-Order Hold (ZOH)]. A ZOH introduces an uncompensated transport delay into the feedback loop.

When an actuator operates on delayed setpoints, its control action lags behind the true physical state of the plant. In closed-loop systems, transport delay erodes the control loop's phase margin. If the phase lag approaches the system's stability boundary, nominal feedback amplification turns negative feedback into positive reinforcement, transforming stable trajectory tracking into violent mechanical hunting, overshoot, and sustained limit cycles.

In classical frequency-domain analysis, an ideal continuous transport delay of duration $tau_d$ possesses the transfer function $G_(upright("delay")) \( s \) = e^(- s tau_d)$. Evaluating this transfer function along the imaginary frequency axis $s = j omega$ yields the frequency response:

$ G_(upright("delay")) \( j omega \) = e^(- j omega tau_d) = cos \( omega tau_d \) - j sin \( omega tau_d \) $

The corresponding phase lag $angle G_(upright("delay")) \( j omega \)$ introduced into the open-loop transfer function is governed by:

$ angle G_(upright("delay")) \( j omega \) = - omega tau_d = - omega (1 / 2 T_(upright("infer"))) $

where:

- $omega = 2 pi f$ is the dynamic frequency (or bandwidth) of the commanded motion in radians per second ($upright("rad/s")$). A fast arm sweep at $f = 1.6 upright(" Hz")$ corresponds to $omega approx 10.0 upright(" rad/s")$.
- $T_(upright("infer"))$ is the forward inference latency of the neural network policy in seconds ($upright("s")$).
- $tau_d = 1 / 2 T_(upright("infer"))$ is the effective average transport delay of the zero-order hold in seconds ($upright("s")$), representing the average age of the held command across the sampling interval.
- $Delta phi.alt = - omega tau_d$ is the resulting phase erosion in radians ($upright("rad")$) or degrees ($""^compose$). Because phase subtracts directly from the system's phase margin ($upright("PM") = 180^compose + angle L \( j omega_c \)$), every degree of phase lost brings the physical plant closer to destructive instability.

Consider an articulated robot arm executing a high-speed assembly sweep with a dominant motion bandwidth of $omega = 10.0 upright(" rad/s")$ ($f approx 1.59 upright(" Hz")$). The vision-based neural policy requires $T_(upright("infer")) = 50.0 upright(" ms")$ ($0.050 upright(" s")$), establishing an effective ZOH transport delay of $tau_d = 0.025 upright(" s")$ ($25.0 upright(" ms")$). Evaluating the phase loss yields:

$ Delta phi.alt = - omega tau_d = - 10.0 upright(" rad/s") times 0.025 upright(" s") = - 0.25 upright(" rad") = - 0.25 times (180^compose / pi) approx - 14.3^compose $

If the low-level joint position controller was tuned with a typical industrial phase margin of $upright("PM") = 35.0^compose$, this single inference delay erodes over $40 %$ of the entire stability reserve ($upright("PM")_(upright("effective")) = 35.0^compose - 14.3^compose = 20.7^compose$). The robot begins to vibrate audibly, overshooting target waypoints by several centimeters.

=== The System 1.5 Solution: Action Chunking
<the-system-1.5-solution-action-chunking>
System 1.5 resolves this cadence mismatch by replacing single-step outputs with multi-step trajectory chunks ($H in \[ 16 \, 32 \]$). By predicting a continuous temporal horizon of future waypoints in a single parallel inference pass, the planner amortizes the computational cost of neural inference across physical time. The resulting stream of future setpoints is buffered into shared memory, evaluated through kinodynamic boundary constraints, and interpolated to supply the $1000 upright(" Hz")$ real-time execution tier with unbroken, jerk-continuous reference commands.

Crucially, this trajectory planning paradigm generalizes across all three Physical AI archetypes:

- #strong[Manipulation (Articulated Arms):] 7-DoF joint angle trajectory chunks ($upright(bold(q))_(t : t + H)$) prevent high-frequency acceleration steps, protecting precision harmonic drives and cycloidal speed reducers from shear fracture.
- #strong[Mobility (Wheeled AMRs & Quadrupeds):] Continuous linear velocity $v \( t \)$, yaw rate $omega \( t \)$, and foot trajectory chunks prevent tire slip, steering chatter, and dynamic rollover during high-speed maneuvers.
- #strong[Cybernetics (Battery Energy Storage & Microgrids):] Active and reactive power setpoint chunks ($\[ P \( t \) \, Q \( t \) \]$) limit power ramp rates ($d P \/ d t$), preventing transformer saturation, inductive voltage spikes, and battery thermal runaway.

Mastering this mid-rate planning organ requires bridging three engineering perspectives:

- #strong[Maya (CS/ML):] Evaluates why single-step autoregression fails on edge memory buses and how generative decoders (ACT and Diffusion Policies) synthesize multi-step action chunks filtered through receding-horizon temporal ensembling.
- #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded):] Architects lock-free Single-Producer Single-Consumer (SPSC) ring buffers, triple-buffering mailboxes, and zero-allocation Horner's evaluations in static SRAM to guarantee sub-microsecond timing within the 1 kHz timer ISR.
- #strong[Alex (Robotics/MechE):] Analyzes the physical mechanics of mechanical jerk ($accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛)$), formulating closed-form $cal(C)^2$ quintic polynomial splines that strictly enforce joint jerk bounds ($parallel accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) parallel lt.eq 25.0 upright(" rad/s")^3$) and torque rate limits ($parallel dot(bold(tau)) parallel lt.eq 150.0 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/s")$).

The architecture of this planning pipeline unfolds systematically across this chapter. We first examine generative trajectory decoders and the mechanics of receding-horizon temporal ensembling (#ref(<sec-generative-decoders>, supplement: [Section])). We then lower these mathematical horizons into deterministic silicon memory layouts and lock-free static queues (#ref(<sec-realtime-queues>, supplement: [Section])). Next, we derive the physics of $cal(C)^2$ quintic polynomial splines, establishing how boundary acceleration matching protects precision transmissions from destructive mechanical jerk and examining the forensic autopsy of the Sheared Cycloidal Drive Pin (#ref(<sec-jerk-splines>, supplement: [Section]) and #ref(<sec-cycloidal-autopsy>, supplement: [Section])). We formalize the candidate trajectory engineering contract (#strong[PLAN-01]) and audit the planning organ across the co-design checkpoint ledger (#ref(<sec-planning-spec>, supplement: [Section])). We stress-test the planning architecture against high-stakes physical trade-offs in Systems Stress-Tests (#ref(<sec-planning-stress-tests>, supplement: [Section])), before establishing the clean forward handoff to bare-metal safety enforcement (#ref(<sec-planning-summary>, supplement: [Section])).

== Generative Action Decoders
<sec-generative-decoders>
Modern Physical AI systems replace deterministic point-regression policies with generative trajectory decoders capable of capturing complex, multi-modal human demonstration distributions (#ref(<fig-three-speeds-latency>, supplement: [Figure])). When a robot encounters an obstacle or manipulates an object with multiple grasping modes, regression-to-the-mean policies produce non-viable averaged trajectories (e.g., commanding an arm to drive straight through an obstacle). Generative trajectory synthesis resolves this by sampling distinct behavioral modes from learned distributions. Two architectures dominate modern embodied deployments: #strong[Action Chunking with Transformers (ACT)] and #strong[Conditional Diffusion Policies].

