=== Why Single-Step Autoregression Fails on Physical Hardware
<why-single-step-autoregression-fails-on-physical-hardware>
In natural language processing, autoregressive transformers generate tokens sequentially, appending each output token to the key-value (KV) cache to condition the subsequent step. For #strong[Maya (CS/ML)], the natural instinct is to treat continuous robotic control as another autoregressive sequence generation task ($H = 1$). On physical hardware, however, sequential single-step autoregression fails for two fundamental physical reasons:

+ #strong[Computational Latency and Memory-Bandwidth Saturation:] Generating a trajectory horizon of $H = 16$ steps autoregressively requires $16$ sequential forward passes through the transformer decoder. On edge silicon (such as an NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin or Google TPU Edge), single-batch token generation has an arithmetic intensity of less than $1.0 upright(" FLOP/byte")$. Each sequential token requires loading all model weights from DRAM into on-chip cache. If a single forward pass takes $12.0 upright(" ms")$, autoregressive decoding consumes $16 times 12.0 = 192.0 upright(" ms")$. This latency inflates the Age of Information (AoI) and expands the dynamic stopping distance.
+ #strong[Compounding Covariate Shift:] When a policy is trained via behavioral cloning on expert demonstrations, minor prediction errors at step $t$ shift the robot into states slightly outside the training distribution support. Under sequential single-step autoregression without feedback correction, these unmitigated errors compound quadratically over time ($cal(O) \( T^2 \)$, Ross et al. (#cite(<ross2011reduction>, form: "year"))), causing the physical robot to drift irrevocably into unstable configurations.

To quantify the physical hazard of inference latency on stopping clearance, recall the #strong[Dynamic Stopping Distance Law] established in Chapter 2:

$ d_(upright("stop")) = v dot.op tau_(upright("delay")) + frac(v^2, 2 a_(upright("max"))) $

where $v$ is the forward velocity in meters per second ($upright("m/s")$), $tau_(upright("delay"))$ is the end-to-end computational and transmission latency in seconds ($upright("s")$), and $a_(upright("max"))$ is the maximum allowable emergency braking deceleration in meters per second squared ($upright("m/s")^2$). The linear term $v dot.op tau_(upright("delay"))$ represents the #emph[reaction drift distance] traversed blindly before braking begins, while $frac(v^2, 2 a_(upright("max")))$ represents the #emph[quadratic physical braking distance] required to dissipate kinetic energy.

Consider an autonomous mobile robot traveling at $v = 1.0 upright(" m/s")$ with emergency braking limit $a_(upright("max")) = 2.0 upright(" m/s")^2$:

- #strong[Under Single-Pass Action Chunking ($H = 16$):] Neural inference runs in parallel once every $20 upright(" Hz")$ cycle, with pipeline latency bounded to $tau_(upright("delay")) = 25.0 upright(" ms") = 0.025 upright(" s")$. The reaction drift is $d_(upright("drift")) = 1.0 times 0.025 = 0.025 upright(" m")$ ($2.5 upright(" cm")$), and total stopping distance is: $ d_(upright("stop, chunk")) = 0.025 + frac(1.0^2, 2 \( 2.0 \)) = 0.025 + 0.250 = 0.275 upright(" m") quad \( 27.5 upright(" cm") \) $

- #strong[Under 16-Step Sequential Autoregression:] Cumulative latency explodes to $tau_(upright("delay")) = 192.0 upright(" ms") = 0.192 upright(" s")$. The reaction drift balloons to $d_(upright("drift")) = 1.0 times 0.192 = 0.192 upright(" m")$ ($19.2 upright(" cm")$), inflating the total stopping distance to: $ d_(upright("stop, autoreg")) = 0.192 + 0.250 = 0.442 upright(" m") quad \( 44.2 upright(" cm") \) $

This represents a $61 %$ increase in stopping distance ($16.7 upright(" cm")$ of extra unguided drift), easily breaching safety clearance margins in human-occupied workspaces. Action chunking eliminates both failure modes: by predicting all $H$ future waypoints simultaneously in a single parallel inference pass, the model achieves high arithmetic intensity, eliminates iterative KV-cache DRAM fetches, and bounds inference latency to a deterministic $20 upright("–") 25 upright(" ms")$ budget.

=== Action Chunking with Transformers (ACT)
<action-chunking-with-transformers-act>
The Action Chunking with Transformers (ACT) architecture structures trajectory generation as a conditional sequence-to-sequence problem parameterized by a Conditional Variational Autoencoder (CVAE) @zhao2023learning.

For #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded)] and #strong[Alex (MechE/Robotics)], who may be less familiar with variational generative models, the core intuition is the #strong[Fork in the Road Dilemma]. Suppose a robotic arm is trained on human demonstrations where the operator grasps an object by reaching either around the left side or around the right side of an obstacle. A traditional mean-squared-error (MSE) regression network attempts to satisfy both modes by averaging the output coordinates: commanding the gripper directly down the middle, smashing straight into the obstacle.

ACT resolves this by introducing a continuous style latent variable $upright(bold(z)) tilde.op cal(N) \( bold(mu) \, bold(Sigma) \) in bb(R)^(d_z)$. During training, an encoder compresses full demonstration trajectories into $upright(bold(z))$, teaching the latent space to distinguish discrete execution styles (e.g., left sweep vs.~right sweep). During deployment on the robot, the encoder is discarded, and $upright(bold(z))$ is sampled from the standard Gaussian prior $upright(bold(z)) tilde.op cal(N) \( upright(bold(0)) \, upright(bold(I)) \)$ (or set to the mean mode $upright(bold(z)) = upright(bold(0))$).

The transformer decoder ingests the sampled style vector $upright(bold(z))$, the current proprioceptive joint state $upright(bold(q))_t$, and spatial affordance patch tokens extracted from visual backbones. Rather than generating waypoints sequentially, the decoder feeds $H$ learned query positional embeddings simultaneously into its self-attention layers. The decoder outputs all $H$ future joint waypoints in a single parallel matrix multiplication across output heads:

$ upright(bold(A))_(t : t + H) = [upright(bold(a))_t \, upright(bold(a))_(t + 1) \, dots.h \, upright(bold(a))_(t + H - 1)] in bb(R)^(H times d_a) $

Because all $H$ waypoints are decoded in a single forward pass, ACT maximizes GPU tensor core utilization and completes full horizon synthesis ($H = 16 \, d_a = 7$) in under $25.0 upright(" ms")$ on edge embedded processors.

=== Conditional Diffusion Policies and Flow Matching
<conditional-diffusion-policies-and-flow-matching>
Conditional Diffusion Policies formulate trajectory synthesis as a stochastic denoising process @chi2023diffusionpolicy. For #strong[Carlos] and #strong[Alex], an intuitive mental model is #strong[Sculpting a Trajectory Ribbon from Marble]. Instead of calculating an exact trajectory in a single shot, the model begins with a block of random white Gaussian noise spanning the entire $H$-step trajectory ribbon ($upright(bold(A))^K tilde.op cal(N) \( upright(bold(0)) \, upright(bold(I)) \)$). Over $K$ discrete denoising iterations, a 1D Temporal U-Net gradually chisels away high-frequency noise, guided by sensory conditioning, until a smooth, kinodynamically viable trajectory $upright(bold(A))^0$ emerges.

The discrete reverse diffusion iteration that steps from noise level $k$ to $k - 1$ is governed by:

$ upright(bold(A))^(k - 1) = 1 / sqrt(alpha_k) (upright(bold(A))^k - beta_k / sqrt(1 - macron(alpha)_k) bold(epsilon.alt)_theta \( upright(bold(A))^k \, k \, upright(bold(O))_t \)) + sigma_k upright(bold(z)) $

where:

- $upright(bold(A))^k in bb(R)^(H times d_a)$ is the noisy trajectory chunk at diffusion iteration $k in { K \, K - 1 \, dots.h \, 1 }$.
- $upright(bold(O))_t$ represents observation conditioning containing visual patch tokens, spatial affordances, and proprioception measured at current time $t$.
- $bold(epsilon.alt)_theta \( upright(bold(A))^k \, k \, upright(bold(O))_t \)$ is the learned neural noise prediction network (typically a 1D Temporal Convolutional U-Net).
- $beta_k in \( 0 \, 1 \)$ is the variance schedule at step $k$, with $alpha_k = 1 - beta_k$ and cumulative product $macron(alpha)_k = product_(j = 1)^k alpha_j$.
- $sigma_k upright(bold(z))$ is optional injected Gaussian noise ($upright(bold(z)) tilde.op cal(N) \( upright(bold(0)) \, upright(bold(I)) \)$). In real-time deterministic deployment, numerical solvers set $sigma_k = 0$ (as in Denoising Diffusion Implicit Models, DDIM) to produce repeatable trajectories.

Consider generating a trajectory chunk for a 7-DoF manipulator ($d_a = 7$) over horizon $H = 16$ ($16 times 7 = 112$ scalar values). Standard diffusion models use $K = 100$ denoising steps, which would take over $400 upright(" ms")$ on an embedded GPU---far too slow for real-time control. Modern Physical AI implementations deploy accelerated DDIM or Flow Matching solvers requiring only $K = 4 upright("–") 8$ denoising steps. On an NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin running TensorRT with FP16 precision, an 8-step DDIM solve completes in exactly $18.5 upright(" ms")$, generating all 112 joint setpoints well within the $50.0 upright(" ms")$ ($20 upright(" Hz")$) planning cycle. Complete mathematical derivations of score-matching variational bounds and diffusion loss functions are cataloged in Appendix C.10.

=== Receding-Horizon Temporal Ensembling
<receding-horizon-temporal-ensembling>
Because generative diffusion policies rely on stochastic sampling, successive policy evaluations generated from consecutive time steps exhibit high-frequency sampling jitter. If an agent executes Chunk $k$ to completion and abruptly switches to Chunk $k + 1$ at boundary time $T$, the robot experiences high-frequency chattering at the seam (#ref(<fig-action-chunking-ensembling>, supplement: [Figure])).

