=== Cross-Archetype Generality of Trajectory Horizons
<cross-archetype-generality-of-trajectory-horizons>
The mathematical structure of the action chunk $upright(bold(A))_(t : t + H) in bb(R)^(H times d_a)$ directly maps across all three Physical AI archetypes:

+ #strong[Manipulation ($d_a = 7$):] The action vector represents 7-DoF target joint angles $upright(bold(q)) in bb(R)^7$ (or 6-DoF end-effector pose deltas $Delta upright(bold(x)) in bb(R)^6$ plus 1-DoF gripper aperture). The $H = 16$ chunk ($800.0 upright(" ms")$ forward window) amortizes visual processing latency while ensembling smooths multi-finger grasping transitions.
+ #strong[Mobility ($d_a = 2 upright("–") 3$):] For non-holonomic mobile robots and legged quadrupeds, the action vector contains linear surge velocity $v_x$, lateral sway velocity $v_y$, and angular yaw rate $omega_z$. Temporal ensembling prevents sudden steering angle jumps that would break tire traction or induce dynamic tipping.
+ #strong[Cybernetics ($d_a = 2$):] In grid-scale Battery Energy Storage Systems (BESS) and industrial power converters, the action vector commands active power $P \( t \)$ and reactive power $Q \( t \)$. Temporal ensembling ensures that power setpoints transition smoothly, preventing rapid current slew rates ($d I \/ d t$) that trip grid protection relays or degrade battery chemistry.

== Real-Time Trajectory Queues
<sec-realtime-queues>
Lowering mathematical trajectory chunks to physical silicon requires uncompromising hardware and memory management. The architectural boundary between the Linux-hosted neural planner (System 1.5 running on a multi-core MPU or GPU accelerator) and the bare-metal microcontroller executive (System 1 running on an ARM Cortex-M7 or Cortex-R5F core) spans heterogeneous compute substrates, asymmetric memory buses, and asynchronous clock domains.

For #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded)], the central challenge is guaranteeing deterministic worst-case execution time (WCET) while transferring high-dimensional trajectory chunks across this domain boundary without thread contention, priority inversion, or data corruption.

=== Lock-Free SPSC Ring Buffers in Static SRAM
<lock-free-spsc-ring-buffers-in-static-sram>
To exchange trajectory chunks across cores without operating system mutexes or blocking synchronization primitives, systems deploy #strong[Single-Producer Single-Consumer (SPSC)] circular ring buffers mapped into on-chip Static RAM (SRAM) or Tightly-Coupled Memory (DTCM).

For #strong[Maya (CS/ML)], who is accustomed to dynamic memory management in Python and standard C++ (#NormalTok("malloc");, #NormalTok("new");, #NormalTok("std::vector");), understanding why dynamic allocation is banned on bare metal is essential. On a non-real-time operating system, #NormalTok("malloc()"); is fast on average, but its #emph[worst-case execution time] is completely unbounded: the allocator must traverse heap bin lists, handle page table faults, and acquire memory locks. In a $1000 upright(" Hz")$ Interrupt Service Routine (ISR) with a hard $1.0 upright(" ms")$ ($1000 thin mu upright("s")$) deadline, a single $500 thin mu upright("s")$ heap stall causes the microcontroller to miss its control deadline, leaving motor bridge inverters un-serviced and causing immediate mechanical loss of control. Therefore, dynamic allocation is strictly prohibited in the real-time path (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");).

The trajectory queue is organized as a statically declared, cache-line-aligned array of chunk slots in shared SRAM:

+ #strong[Cache Line Alignment and False Sharing Prevention:] Modern multi-core processors read and write memory in discrete 64-byte blocks called #emph[cache lines]. If Core 0 (Linux NPU) and Core 1 (MCU) modify different variables that happen to reside within the same 64-byte memory block, the hardware cache coherency protocol (such as MESI) continuously invalidates and reloads the cache line between cores. This phenomenon, known as #strong[false sharing] or #emph[cache bouncing], severely degrades bus throughput. To prevent this, every trajectory chunk slot is explicitly padded and aligned to 64-byte boundaries (#NormalTok("alignas(64)");), ensuring each core operates on independent cache lines.
+ #strong[Atomic Head/Tail Pointers:] The producer writes solely to #NormalTok("head");, while the consumer reads solely from #NormalTok("tail");. The queue is empty when #NormalTok("head == tail"); and full when #NormalTok("(head + 1) % QUEUE_SLOTS == tail");. Because only one core modifies each pointer, no mutexes or spinlocks are required.
+ #strong[Hardware Memory Barriers:] Modern pipelined processors and write-combining memory controllers reorder store instructions to maximize bus throughput. Without synchronization, the CPU might commit the updated #NormalTok("head"); pointer to SRAM #emph[before] the trajectory chunk's floating-point waypoint payload has finished writing to memory. If the consumer reads the slot immediately, it receives corrupted, half-written data (a #emph[torn read]). To prevent this, the producer executes an explicit Data Memory Barrier (#NormalTok("__DMB()");) or Data Synchronization Barrier (#NormalTok("__DSB()");) immediately before updating #NormalTok("head");. The consuming MCU executes a memory barrier before reading payload slots to guarantee cache coherency.

=== Triple-Buffering Mailbox Protocol
<triple-buffering-mailbox-protocol>
When trajectory chunks are synthesized asynchronously by an edge neural accelerator (NPU/GPU) at $20 upright("–") 50 upright(" Hz")$, writing directly into an active queue slot could cause torn reads if the 1 kHz consumer accesses the slot concurrently. To guarantee absolute read isolation without blocking locks, systems implement a #strong[Triple-Buffering Mailbox Protocol]:

+ #strong[Active Slot:] Owned exclusively by the MCU real-time consumer for 1 kHz spline evaluation.
+ #strong[Committed Slot:] Holds the most recently validated, fully assembled trajectory chunk from System 1.5.
+ #strong[Staging Slot:] Owned exclusively by the Linux NPU thread for assembling and ensembling incoming diffusion waypoints.

When the NPU finishes synthesizing and verifying a new chunk, it atomically updates the committed pointer to reference the staging slot and reclaims the old committed slot for the next staging cycle. At the start of each spline segment, the MCU timer interrupt checks the committed pointer. If a newer validated chunk is present, it atomically claims the committed slot as its new active buffer, completing the handoff with zero thread contention and zero data copying overhead.

=== Zero-Allocation Real-Time Spline Evaluation
<zero-allocation-real-time-spline-evaluation>
In the 1 kHz timer Interrupt Service Routine (ISR) of the microcontroller, the execution engine evaluates the active polynomial spline to generate target joint angles, velocities, and feedforward torques for the motor inverters.

Evaluating a 5th-order polynomial $s \( tau \) = sum_(k = 0)^5 a_k tau^k$ naively requires computing powers ($tau^2 \, tau^3 \, tau^4 \, tau^5$) and performing 15 floating-point multiplications and 5 additions for position alone. Across 7 kinematic joints, evaluating position, velocity, and acceleration requires over 120 floating-point operations. On an embedded MCU with limited clock speed, naive evaluation consumes tens of microseconds, risking ISR overrun and jitter.

To minimize arithmetic operations and maximize numerical stability, polynomial evaluations are structured using #strong[Horner's Method], which factorizes polynomials into nested single-cycle multiplications:

$ s \( tau \) = a_0 + tau #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\(] a_1 + tau #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] a_2 + tau \( a_3 + tau \( a_4 + tau a_5 \) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\)] $

$ dot(s) \( tau \) = a_1 + tau #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\(] 2 a_2 + tau #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 3 a_3 + tau \( 4 a_4 + tau dot.op 5 a_5 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\)] $

$ dot.double(s) \( tau \) = 2 a_2 + tau #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\(] 6 a_3 + tau #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 12 a_4 + tau dot.op 20 a_5 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] #scale(x: 180%, y: 180%)[\)] $

where:

- $tau = t - t_(upright("start"))$ is the elapsed physical time within the current spline segment in seconds ($upright("s")$), where $0 lt.eq tau lt.eq T$.
- $a_0 \, a_1 \, dots.h \, a_5$ are the pre-computed polynomial coefficients stored in static SRAM.
- By structuring the polynomial through Horner's nested form, computing position requires $5$ Fused Multiply-Adds (FMAs), velocity requires $4$ FMAs, and acceleration requires $3$ FMAs---totaling exactly $12$ FMAs per joint axis. A hardware FMA instruction executes $x arrow.l \( a dot.op b \) + c$ in a single clock cycle with single-rounding precision.

Consider evaluating Joint 1 at time $tau = 0.025 upright(" s")$ ($25.0 upright(" ms")$) within a $T = 0.050 upright(" s")$ segment with pre-computed coefficients:

$ upright(bold(a)) = \[ a_0 = 0.0 \, a_1 = 1.0 \, a_2 = 0.0 \, a_3 = 400.0 \, a_4 = - 8000.0 \, a_5 = 48000.0 \] $

Evaluating position $s \( 0.025 \)$ via Horner's steps:

$ upright("Inner")_1 = a_4 + 0.025 times a_5 = - 8000.0 + 1200.0 = - 6800.0 $

$ upright("Inner")_2 = a_3 + 0.025 times upright("Inner")_1 = 400.0 - 170.0 = 230.0 $

$ upright("Inner")_3 = a_2 + 0.025 times upright("Inner")_2 = 0.0 + 5.75 = 5.75 $

$ upright("Inner")_4 = a_1 + 0.025 times upright("Inner")_3 = 1.0 + 0.14375 = 1.14375 $

$ s \( 0.025 \) = a_0 + 0.025 times upright("Inner")_4 = 0.0 + 0.02859 = 0.02859 upright(" rad") $

Across all 7 kinematic joints, evaluating this quintic formulation requires just $12 upright(" FMAs") times 7 = 84 upright(" instructions")$. On an ARM Cortex-M7 running at $480 upright(" MHz")$, executing 84 single-cycle FMA instructions takes $84 \/ \( 480 times 10^6 \) approx 0.175 thin mu upright("s")$. Including SRAM register loads and stores, total spline evaluation completes in under $1.2 thin mu upright("s")$---consuming just $0.12 %$ of the $1.0 upright(" ms")$ control cycle and leaving $> 99.8 %$ of CPU bandwidth for safety enforcers and Field-Oriented Control (FOC).

=== Cross-Archetype Embedded Memory Implementations
<cross-archetype-embedded-memory-implementations>
This lock-free silicon memory architecture is identical across all three embodied archetypes:

- #strong[Manipulation:] The SPSC queue transfers 7-DoF joint position, velocity, and acceleration coefficients to CAN-FD or EtherCAT motor drive transceivers.
- #strong[Mobility:] In autonomous mobile robots (AMRs) and automated guided vehicles (AGVs), the queue maps $\[ v_x \, v_y \, omega_z \]$ velocity trajectories into bare-metal motor traction controllers over an ISO 11898 CAN bus.
- #strong[Cybernetics:] In grid-tied battery storage systems, the queue transfers active/reactive power ramps $\[ P \( t \) \, Q \( t \) \]$ directly to Digital Signal Processors (DSPs) driving high-voltage Silicon Carbide (SiC) inverter bridge gates.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] If the high-level neural trajectory generator experiences an occasional inference latency spike or memory bus stall, the real-time microcontroller can simply hold the last received waypoint via a Zero-Order Hold (ZOH) until the next action chunk arrives.

- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Holding a position or velocity setpoint constant across multiple control cycles while physical mass is in motion introduces an unmodeled transport delay ($tau_d = 1 / 2 Delta t_(upright("pause"))$). In frequency-domain analysis, this delay produces an exponential phase lag ($angle G_(upright("delay")) = - omega tau_d$). For a manipulator moving at high speed, a $50 upright(" ms")$ compute pause introduces $> 14^compose$ of phase erosion into the closed-loop feedback path, destroying control gain margins and inducing violent limit-cycle hunting. If the queue starves completely while moving at $v = 1.0 upright(" m/s")$, an un-cleared ZOH latches non-zero motor currents, commanding the machine into hard mechanical boundaries.

- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Real-time trajectory queues must never apply unconstrained Zero-Order Holds during queue underflow. If the lock-free SPSC buffer starves, the real-time microcontroller must execute an autonomous fallback: transitioning into a $cal(C)^2$ quintic polynomial deceleration profile to safely bring joint velocities to zero within the active intent lease boundary.

