== Jerk Continuity and Quintic Splines
<sec-jerk-splines>
A common and dangerous failure mode in physical robot deployments is the dispatch of discrete, un-interpolated neural network waypoints directly to motor controllers. When a generative decoder outputs an action chunk, the trajectory consists of discrete setpoints spaced at $Delta t_(upright("chunk")) = 50.0 upright(" ms")$. If a low-level controller commands step changes in velocity between these waypoints across single control cycles ($Delta t = 1.0 upright(" ms")$), the commanded joint acceleration experiences an instantaneous Dirac delta impulse ($dot.double(upright(bold(q))) arrow.r oo$).

For #strong[Alex (Robotics/MechE)], this impulse represents an immediate threat to mechanical transmission life and structural integrity.

=== The Physical Mechanics of Mechanical Jerk
<the-physical-mechanics-of-mechanical-jerk>
To understand why un-interpolated commands destroy mechanical hardware, consider two tangible mechanical mental models: #strong[The Hammer on a Tuning Fork] and #strong[The Rev-Matched Automotive Clutch].

A robot arm is not an infinitely rigid mathematical stick; it is an elastic multi-body structure coupled with compliant gearboxes (harmonic drives and cycloidal reducers). When an actuator commands an instantaneous step change in acceleration, the required motor torque jumps discontinuously ($dot(bold(tau)) arrow.r oo$). This torque impulse acts exactly like striking a steel tuning fork with a heavy hammer: it injects broad-band frequency energy that excites the structural resonant modes of the linkage, causing gear teeth to hammer against each other across backlash gaps, inducing fatigue, and shearing pins.

Conversely, consider what happens when you shift gears in a high-performance manual car: #strong[The Rev-Matched Clutch]. If you drop the clutch abruptly without matching engine and transmission speeds, the instantaneous change in shaft speed sends a violent torque shock through the drivetrain, shuddering the chassis and stressing gear splines. If you smoothly rev-match and engage the clutch progressively, torque transfers seamlessly with zero driveline shock.

In multi-body dynamics, the dynamic torque vector $bold(tau) in bb(R)^n$ required by an $n$-DoF articulated linkage is governed by the rigid-body equations of motion:

$ bold(tau) = upright(bold(M)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) dot.double(upright(bold(q))) + upright(bold(C)) \( upright(bold(q)) \, dot(upright(bold(q))) \) dot(upright(bold(q))) + upright(bold(g)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) $

where $upright(bold(M)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) in bb(R)^(n times n)$ is the symmetric positive-definite mass matrix, $upright(bold(C)) \( upright(bold(q)) \, dot(upright(bold(q))) \) dot(upright(bold(q))) in bb(R)^n$ is the vector of Coriolis and centrifugal torques, and $upright(bold(g)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) in bb(R)^n$ is the gravitational torque vector.

Differentiating this equation with respect to time yields the #strong[Torque Slew Rate] ($dot(bold(tau))$):

$ dot(bold(tau)) = upright(bold(M)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) + dot(upright(bold(M))) \( upright(bold(q)) \) dot.double(upright(bold(q))) + frac(d, d t) [upright(bold(C)) \( upright(bold(q)) \, dot(upright(bold(q))) \) dot(upright(bold(q))) + upright(bold(g)) \( upright(bold(q)) \)] approx upright(bold(M)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) upright(bold(j)) $

where $upright(bold(j)) = accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) = frac(d^3 upright(bold(q)), d t^3)$ is the joint jerk vector in radians per second cubed ($upright("rad/s")^3$).

The structural natural resonance frequency $f_n$ of a joint linkage with torsional stiffness $k_(upright("joint"))$ and rotational inertia $I_(upright("link"))$ is:

$ f_n = frac(1, 2 pi) sqrt(k_(upright("joint")) / I_(upright("link"))) $

where $k_(upright("joint"))$ is the torsional spring stiffness of the joint gearbox in Newton-meters per radian ($upright("N") dot.op upright("m/rad")$) and $I_(upright("link"))$ is the effective rotational inertia of the distal link in kilogram-meters squared ($upright("kg") dot.op upright("m")^2$). When control commands contain spectral energy near $f_n$, the joint acts as a mechanical amplifier, sustaining destructive vibrations.

Consider Joint 2 of a precision manipulator arm carrying a fixture payload, with combined link inertia $I_(upright("link")) = 0.045 upright(" kg") dot.op upright("m")^2$ and harmonic drive torsional stiffness $k_(upright("joint")) = 4 \, 500 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/rad")$. The joint natural resonance frequency is:

$ f_n = frac(1, 2 pi) sqrt(frac(4 \, 500 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/rad"), 0.045 upright(" kg") dot.op upright("m")^2)) = frac(1, 2 pi) sqrt(100 \, 000) = 316.23 / 6.2832 approx 50.33 upright(" Hz") approx 50.3 upright(" Hz") $

Because the neural policy updates at $20 upright(" Hz")$ ($50 upright(" ms")$) and the motor inverter updates at $1000 upright(" Hz")$ ($1.0 upright(" ms")$), un-interpolated step commands inject sharp high-frequency harmonic content spanning $20 upright("–") 80 upright(" Hz")$. This directly excites the $50.3 upright(" Hz")$ resonant mode, amplifying transmission vibrations and fatiguing flexspline teeth.

