=== The Asynchronous State Re-Anchoring Handshake
<the-asynchronous-state-re-anchoring-handshake>
When an independent safety filter (such as a Control Barrier Function on the MCU) intervenes to prevent a collision or position limit violation, it clamps motor commands or applies dynamic braking, freezing the physical robot at position $upright(bold(q))_(upright("veto"))$.

To understand why this creates a severe architectural hazard, consider the #strong[Blindfolded Runner at a Crosswalk] mental model.

Imagine running through a city blindfolded, counting your strides in your head to track where you are. If a safety guard suddenly grabs your shoulder and halts you in place at a crosswalk for three seconds, your mental countdown keeps advancing if you don't realize you were stopped. When the guard lets go and you open your eyes, you cannot simply leap forward expecting to land where your mental clock imagined you would be---doing so would pitch you headfirst onto the pavement. Instead, you must open your eyes, re-anchor your feet to your true physical position $upright(bold(q))_(upright("actual"))$, and initiate a new stride from zero velocity ($dot(upright(bold(q)))_0 = upright(bold(0)) \, dot.double(upright(bold(q)))_0 = upright(bold(0))$).

If the upstream neural planner on the Linux MPU continues to execute in open loop during an MCU safety veto, it will generate subsequent trajectory chunks conditioned on where it #emph[believed] the robot would be ($upright(bold(q))_(upright("expected"))$), rather than where the robot is physically stopped. If the safety veto clears while this divergence exists, dispatching the new chunk causes a violent snap-back motion as the controller attempts to bridge the positional gap in a single control cycle.

