To prevent this windup failure, Physical AI architectures enforce the #strong[Asynchronous State Re-Anchoring Handshake] (#ref(<fig-reanchoring-handshake>, supplement: [Figure])):

+ #strong[Veto Assertion:] Upon detecting a constraint violation ($h \( upright(bold(x)) \) < 0$), the MCU safety filter overrides the motor setpoints, sets an atomic #NormalTok("VETO_ACTIVE = 1"); flag in the shared SRAM mailbox, and captures the true physical encoder state $upright(bold(q))_(upright("actual"))$.
+ #strong[Interrupt Assertion:] The MCU raises a dedicated hardware inter-processor interrupt (or Non-Maskable Interrupt, NMI) to notify the Linux MPU.
+ #strong[Pipeline Flush:] The Linux planning thread flushes all unexecuted waypoints from the SPSC ring buffer, invalidates its internal forward trajectory cache, and clamps diffusion conditioning to $upright(bold(q))_(upright("actual"))$.
+ #strong[Re-Anchored Generation:] The planner generates a fresh trajectory chunk seeded strictly from $upright(bold(q))_(upright("actual"))$ with boundary velocities initialized to zero ($dot(upright(bold(q)))_0 = upright(bold(0)) \, dot.double(upright(bold(q)))_0 = upright(bold(0))$).
+ #strong[Resumption:] Once the new verified chunk is committed to the mailbox, the MCU clears the #NormalTok("VETO_ACTIVE"); flag and resumes $cal(C)^2$ spline execution without positional snap-back.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] Checking that commanded joint torque $bold(tau)$ is within static motor datasheet limits ($parallel bold(tau) parallel lt.eq tau_(upright("peak"))$) at waypoint keyframes guarantees that the trajectory is dynamically executable by the motor inverters.

- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Motor torque generation is constrained by electromagnetic and thermal dynamics. Stator coils have non-zero electrical inductance $L$ and resistance $R$, establishing an electrical time constant $tau_e = L \/ R$. When rotational angular velocity $dot(upright(bold(q)))$ is high, back-electromotive force ($e_(upright("bemf")) = K_e dot(q)_(upright("motor"))$) opposes the bus voltage, collapsing the available voltage headroom: $ Delta V = V_(upright("bus")) - e_(upright("bemf")) - R i_q $ The maximum rate of torque change is strictly bounded by winding inductance: $ dot(tau)_(upright("max")) = K_t (frac(V_(upright("bus")) - K_e N_(upright("gear")) dot(q) - R i_q, L)) $ At high joint speeds, available voltage headroom $Delta V$ approaches zero, severely choking torque slew rates ($dot(tau) arrow.r 0$). A trajectory that satisfies static torque bounds but demands high torque slew rates ($parallel dot(bold(tau)) parallel > 150.0 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/s")$) causes severe torque tracking lag, inverter overcurrent faults, or DC bus voltage collapse.

- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Trajectory planners must enforce dynamic torque-rate limits ($parallel dot(bold(tau)) parallel lt.eq dot(tau)_(upright("max")) \( dot(upright(bold(q))) \)$) and evaluate electro-mechanical voltage headroom envelopes before committing candidate splines to real-time motor inverters.

] #label("callout-fallacy*-52")
