=== Closing the Planning Row: Binding versus Non-Binding Constraints
<closing-the-planning-row-binding-versus-non-binding-constraints>
Following the co-design framework, we evaluate the depth to which each physical column constrains the trajectory planning organ:

+ #strong[Actuation Limits and Jerk (DOMINANT):] This is the center of gravity for #ref(<sec-planning>, supplement: [Chapter]). The mathematical boundary matching of position, velocity, and acceleration directly dictates whether physical transmissions survive or shear under neural control commands.
+ #strong[Time and Freshness (SECONDARY):] Binds through the latency amortization mechanism. Predicting multi-step horizons is the sole reason high-capacity neural networks can govern physical mechanisms without starving low-level control loops.
+ #strong[Inertia and Stopping (THIN):] Binds through the requirement that trajectory horizons must not command accelerations that violate dynamic braking envelopes ($d_(upright("stop"))$). If an obstacle enters the swept volume, the plan must truncate to allow safe deceleration.
+ #strong[Silicon Determinism (THIN):] Binds through the memory layout of static SRAM ring buffers. The trajectory queue must be lock-free and statically allocated to prevent non-deterministic thread contention across heterogeneous processor cores.
+ #strong[Energy and Thermal (NOT BINDING):] Does not directly bind the trajectory planning organ. While rapid motor accelerations dissipate Joule heat ($I^2 R$), the thermal protection of motor windings and power inverters is enforced at the 1 kHz and 20 kHz execution tiers (#ref(<sec-enforcement>, supplement: [Chapter])), not within the trajectory spline geometry.

=== Silent Failure and Online Diagnostic Detectors
<silent-failure-and-online-diagnostic-detectors>
A pervasive and dangerous failure mode in trajectory planning is #strong[Chunk Boundary Divergence], where the neural model continues to emit syntactically valid trajectory chunks, but the boundary velocity $dot(upright(bold(q)))_H$ of Chunk $k$ diverges substantially from the initial velocity $dot(upright(bold(q)))_0$ of Chunk $k + 1$. This failure produces high-frequency mechanical vibration and gear wear without raising software exceptions or kernel panics.

To detect this silent failure before un-smoothed commands reach motor inverters, the real-time microcontroller evaluates the boundary derivative jump norms at each chunk handoff:

$ Delta dot(upright(bold(q)))_(upright("boundary")) = parallel dot(upright(bold(q)))_0^(\( k + 1 \)) - dot(upright(bold(q)))_H^(\( k \)) parallel \, #h(2em) Delta dot.double(upright(bold(q)))_(upright("boundary")) = parallel dot.double(upright(bold(q)))_0^(\( k + 1 \)) - dot.double(upright(bold(q)))_H^(\( k \)) parallel $

where:

- $dot(upright(bold(q)))_H^(\( k \))$ and $dot.double(upright(bold(q)))_H^(\( k \))$ are the terminal joint velocity and acceleration vectors of the active Chunk $k$ evaluated at $t = t_0 + H Delta t$.
- $dot(upright(bold(q)))_0^(\( k + 1 \))$ and $dot.double(upright(bold(q)))_0^(\( k + 1 \))$ are the initial joint velocity and acceleration vectors of the newly committed candidate Chunk $k + 1$.
- $Delta dot(upright(bold(q)))_(upright("boundary"))$ is the Euclidean velocity discontinuity across all $n$ kinematic joints in radians per second ($upright("rad/s")$).
- $Delta dot.double(upright(bold(q)))_(upright("boundary"))$ is the Euclidean acceleration discontinuity in radians per second squared ($upright("rad/s")^2$).

Consider an industrial robot arm with boundary acceptance thresholds set to $Delta dot(q)_(upright("thresh")) = 0.20 upright(" rad/s")$ and $Delta dot.double(q)_(upright("thresh")) = 5.0 upright(" rad/s")^2$.

When an un-ensembled diffusion chunk arrives with $Delta dot(upright(bold(q)))_(upright("boundary")) = 0.45 upright(" rad/s") > 0.20 upright(" rad/s")$, the MCU boundary checker flags the chunk seam in under $0.8 thin mu upright("s")$. Rather than executing the discontinuous waypoint, the MCU rejects Chunk $k + 1$, triggers an atomic pipeline re-anchoring interrupt, and generates a smooth quintic deceleration profile that arrests joint motion safely to zero. This online detector provides over $99.5 %$ diagnostic coverage against transmission-damaging trajectory discontinuities.

=== When This Row Is Absorbed: End-to-End Latent Trajectory Policies
<when-this-row-is-absorbed-end-to-end-latent-trajectory-policies>
In research settings, emerging end-to-end foundation models attempt to absorb trajectory planning directly into large multimodal networks that map raw sensory pixels to low-level motor torques. When this occurs, the explicit trajectory planning organ appears to vanish from the software diagram.

However, absorbing the planning module does not eliminate the physical constraint. A neural network outputting direct torques at $50 upright(" Hz")$ still commands discrete step changes across physical time. The physical plant still possesses mass, the motor windings still possess inductive time constants ($tau_e = L \/ R$), and the gearbox still shears under unbounded torque derivatives. When the trajectory planner is absorbed, the verification checks (jerk bounding, torque rate limiting, and state re-anchoring) do not disappear; they simply relocate to the deterministic bare-metal safety filter (#ref(<sec-enforcement>, supplement: [Chapter])) at the boundary to physical hardware.

== Systems Stress-Tests: Architectural Food for Thought
<sec-planning-stress-tests>
Evaluating trajectory synthesis and mid-rate planning architectures requires confronting multi-disciplinary trade-offs where generative sampling statistics, silicon memory determinism, and mechanical transmission limits collide. The following four architectural stress-tests examine critical edge cases across the System 1.5 planning tier:

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 1: The Long-Horizon Amortization vs.~Reaction-Drift Dilemma]
<stress-test-1-the-long-horizon-amortization-vs.-reaction-drift-dilemma>
An autonomous mobile manipulator navigates dynamic hospital corridors at $v = 1.5 upright(" m/s")$. The engineering team evaluates two diffusion trajectory policy configurations:

+ #strong[Long Horizon ($H = 32$ steps, $50 upright(" ms")$ interval, $1.6 upright(" s")$ duration):] Evaluated at $2 upright(" Hz")$ ($500 upright(" ms")$ inference latency). Amortizes compute, keeping NPU power dissipation at $8 upright(" W")$ and delivering exceptionally smooth long-range paths.
+ #strong[Short Horizon ($H = 8$ steps, $50 upright(" ms")$ interval, $400 upright(" ms")$ duration):] Evaluated at $20 upright(" Hz")$ ($50 upright(" ms")$ inference latency). Requires high-frequency inference drawing $28 upright(" W")$, heating the embedded SoC near the thermal throttling threshold.

While the robot is moving under the Long Horizon configuration, a pedestrian suddenly steps out from an intersecting doorway $0.80 upright(" m")$ ahead. Because the active chunk has $1.1 upright(" s")$ of execution remaining, the robot cannot incorporate fresh camera observations until the next $2 upright(" Hz")$ cycle. By the time the next inference completes ($500 upright(" ms")$ later), moving mass has drifted forward by $d_(upright("react")) = 1.5 times 0.500 = 0.75 upright(" m")$, leaving only $0.05 upright(" m")$ of clearance---making collision unavoidable.

#emph[How should systems architects design hierarchical planning architectures that dynamically modulate chunk horizon length $H$ and query frequency based on local spatial clearance and velocity? When an unexpected obstacle intrudes mid-chunk, how should the real-time MCU safety supervisor truncate the active spline without inducing destructive jerk?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 2: The SPSC Queue Starvation and Inductive Hunting Paradox]
<stress-test-2-the-spsc-queue-starvation-and-inductive-hunting-paradox>
A dual-arm industrial assembly robot executes precision insertions at high speed. The System 1.5 planner runs on an embedded Linux MPU, depositing $H = 16$ quintic spline chunks into a lock-free SPSC ring buffer in shared SRAM. The real-time safety MCU reads waypoints at $1000 upright(" Hz")$ to drive Field-Oriented Control (FOC) inverters.

Due to a heavy background disk logging operation on the Linux host, the MPU scheduler encounters a $65 upright(" ms")$ thread preemption stall. The SPSC queue starves of new chunks while the robot arm is traveling at $dot(q)_2 = 1.2 upright(" rad/s")$. If the MCU applies a Zero-Order Hold (ZOH) on the last commanded velocity, the $65 upright(" ms")$ delay injects an uncompensated phase lag of $Delta phi.alt = omega dot.op frac(Delta t, 2) approx 18^compose$ into the joint position loop, causing the current controller to oscillate violently at $45 upright(" Hz")$ and exciting the robot's second structural harmonic. If the MCU instead immediately clamps velocity to zero across a single 1 ms tick, the instantaneous deceleration ($dot.double(q) = 1 \, 200 upright(" rad/s")^2$) shatters the harmonic drive flexspline.

#emph[What deterministic fallback state machine must execute in zero-allocation SRAM when a trajectory queue starves? How does a real-time executive calculate a dynamically feasible quintic deceleration profile that arrests motion within remaining geometric clearance while strictly respecting torque derivative bounds ($parallel dot(bold(tau)) parallel lt.eq 150 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/s")$)?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 3: The Diffusion Sampling Multimodality and Ensembling Seam Tear]
<stress-test-3-the-diffusion-sampling-multimodality-and-ensembling-seam-tear>
A bimanual robot is tasked with clearing obstacles from a workbench. Directly ahead lies a rigid vertical stanchion. The generative diffusion policy is trained on human demonstrations that either swerve left (Mode A) or swerve right (Mode B).

At time $t_0$, the diffusion model samples Mode A (swerve left). At time $t_0 + 50 upright(" ms")$, the receding-horizon temporal ensembler queries the diffusion model again, but due to stochastic noise sampling, the second forward pass samples Mode B (swerve right). The exponential ensembling filter computes a weighted average of the two overlapping chunks: $upright(bold(a))^(*) \( t \) = 0.6 upright(bold(a))_(upright("left")) + 0.4 upright(bold(a))_(upright("right"))$. The resulting blended trajectory commands the robot to drive straight down the middle---directly into the solid stanchion.

#emph[Why do standard temporal ensembling algorithms fail when generative policies exhibit multimodal output distributions? How must trajectory planning architectures implement mode-consistent clustering or trajectory selection before applying temporal smoothing filters across successive prediction horizons?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 4: The Dynamic Torque Slew Rate and Back-EMF Voltage Collapse]
<stress-test-4-the-dynamic-torque-slew-rate-and-back-emf-voltage-collapse>
A high-payload legged robot attempts a dynamic jumping maneuver. The trajectory planner computes a kinematically valid $cal(C)^2$ quintic spline that commands a rapid knee extension from $dot(q) = 2.0 upright(" rad/s")$ to $dot(q) = 12.0 upright(" rad/s")$ in $40 upright(" ms")$, with a peak torque requirement of $tau = 65 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m")$. The motor's static torque rating is $80 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m")$.

As joint velocity reaches $dot(q) = 10.0 upright(" rad/s")$, the motor's rotational back-electromotive force rises to $e_(upright("bemf")) = K_e dot(q) = 0.045 times \( 10.0 times 25 \) = 11.25 upright(" V")$ (accounting for gearbox ratio). The inverter DC bus is $24 upright(" V")$. The available voltage headroom to drive stator current collapses to $Delta V = V_(upright("bus")) - e_(upright("bemf")) = 12.75 upright(" V")$. Given the stator inductance $L = 250 thin mu upright("H")$, the maximum current slew rate is severely restricted:

$ frac(d i_q, d t) lt.eq frac(Delta V - R i_q, L) $

The motor cannot ramp torque fast enough to track the spline's acceleration profile. The lag in torque generation causes the robot to miss its takeoff trajectory, crashing into the landing surface.

#emph[Why is kinematic $cal(C)^2$ jerk continuity necessary but insufficient for high-speed dynamic maneuvers? How must trajectory synthesis engines incorporate electro-mechanical actuator models (including back-EMF, winding inductance, and inverter DC bus voltage limits) to certify that planned acceleration profiles are physically achievable before committing commands to hardware?]

== Chapter Summary and Forward Handoff to #ref(<sec-enforcement>, supplement: [Chapter])
<sec-planning-summary>
This chapter established the necessity and mechanics of trajectory action chunking as the architectural bridge between cognitive inference and mechanical reality. We diagnosed the dynamical instability caused by single-step Markovian latency and demonstrated how generative decoders (including ACT and Diffusion Policies) amortize this latency by producing multi-step prediction horizons ($H = 16$). Through receding-horizon temporal ensembling and $cal(C)^2$ quintic polynomial splines, discrete neural predictions are mathematically transformed into continuous, jerk-bounded trajectories that safely drive high-frequency motor inverters without triggering mechanical resonances or shearing gearbox pins.

Across our engineering disciplines:

- #strong[Maya (CS/ML)] mastered how generative sequence-to-sequence decoders and temporal ensembling transform stochastic diffusion samples into smooth, low-pass filtered control horizons without autoregressive KV-cache bottlenecks.
- #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded)] engineered the lock-free static SRAM queues, triple-buffering mailboxes, and zero-allocation Horner's evaluations that execute in under $1.2 thin mu upright("s")$ within the 1 kHz timer ISR.
- #strong[Alex (Robotics/MechE)] derived the closed-form $cal(C)^2$ quintic spline boundary conditions that strictly enforce joint jerk bounds ($parallel accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) parallel lt.eq 25.0 upright(" rad/s")^3$) and torque rate limits ($parallel dot(bold(tau)) parallel lt.eq 150.0 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/s")$), preventing catastrophic gear pin shear.

With the candidate trajectory chunk smoothed, verified, and deposited into the lock-free shared SRAM mailbox, authority transfers to the lowest tier of the physical computing stack. In #strong[#ref(<sec-enforcement>, supplement: [Chapter]) (Reflexes and Safety Enforcement: System 1 1000 Hz Control Barrier Functions, Compliance, and Motor FOC)], we examine bare-metal microcontroller execution. We explore how deterministic real-time execution engines ingest these polynomial splines, evaluate hard mathematical Control Barrier Function Quadratic Programs (CBF-QPs) in static SRAM, regulate contact impedance, and command high-frequency Field-Oriented Control (FOC) space-vector PWM inverters at $20 upright(" kHz")$ to guarantee absolute physical survival.

