== Real-Time Safety Enforcement
<real-time-safety-enforcement>
In #ref(<sec-planning>, supplement: [Chapter]), we engineered the System 1.5 trajectory planning organ, which generates continuous, multi-step action chunks ($H = 16$) to bridge the cadence gap between high-level semantic reasoning ($1 upright(" Hz")$) and low-level actuator control (#ref(<fig-locator-ch09>, supplement: [Figure])). However, passing these generative trajectories directly to physical motor drives introduces a lethal vulnerability. In conventional robotics stacks, motion planning, trajectory synthesis, and safety monitoring are frequently bundled into a monolithic application process executing under a standard operating system. In modern Physical AI architectures, where high-level reasoning and trajectory synthesis are driven by multi-billion parameter foundation models and diffusion decoders, this monolithic design is fatal.

=== The Physical Analogy: Hydraulic Brake Assist and the Silicon Shield
<the-physical-analogy-hydraulic-brake-assist-and-the-silicon-shield>
To understand why software-only safety fails, consider a tangible physical mental model: #strong[The Hydraulic Brake Assist Overriding the Electronic Accelerator]. In a modern drive-by-wire automobile, the driver or autonomous cruise-control computer may depress the electronic throttle pedal, requesting full engine acceleration. However, a dedicated hydraulic anti-lock braking system (ABS) module---hardwired directly to wheel-speed sensors and hydraulic brake calipers---holds physical override authority. The instant millimeter-wave radar detects a stopped obstacle within the physical stopping distance, the hydraulic ABS valves pulse fluid pressure into the calipers, arresting the vehicle's momentum regardless of the electronic throttle command. The software throttle #emph[proposes], but the physical hydraulic circuit holds absolute #emph[permission] authority.

In Physical AI, we implement this exact separation across heterogeneous silicon:

A general-purpose operating system such as standard Linux is engineered to optimize average throughput across competing processes. In doing so, it routinely introduces non-deterministic latency spikes: kernel memory compaction pauses, dirty page background flushes, thread preemption, and bus contention across shared DRAM crossbars (#ref(<fig-enforcer-timing>, supplement: [Figure])). If safety monitoring resides in user-space software on the Linux host, any operating system stall halts the safety monitor simultaneously with the neural policy. During that stall, unserviced motor command registers remain latched while moving mass continues to coast forward under unguided momentum ($d_(upright("coast")) = v dot.op Delta t$). For a robotic manipulator traveling at an operational velocity of $v = 1.5 upright(" m/s")$, a modest $140 upright(" ms")$ Linux kernel memory compaction stall results in $d_(upright("coast")) = 210 upright(" mm")$ ($21 upright(" cm")$) of completely unmonitored displacement, converting software scheduling jitter directly into a destructive physical collision.

