In this industrial workcell incident, the 6-DoF articulated manipulator was executing high-speed palletizing maneuvers at $v = 1.8 upright(" m/s")$. Safety monitoring and trajectory generation were co-located in a user-space process executing under standard Linux on an x86-64 host computer.

At timestamp $T - 0.140 upright(" s")$, an optical time-of-flight sensor detected an unexpected fixture protrusion at a distance of $d_(upright("clearance")) = 0.280 upright(" m")$. However, at that exact millisecond, the Linux kernel initiated a background memory compaction cycle to service a multi-gigabyte dirty page writeback. The user-space planning and safety process was preempted and suspended for $140 upright(" ms")$.

Because the motor inverters were governed by unmanaged user-space setpoints, the actuators held the last commanded velocity. The arm coasted forward unguided:

$ d_(upright("coast")) = v dot.op Delta t_(upright("pause")) = 1.8 upright(" m/s") times 0.140 upright(" s") = 0.252 upright(" m") $

When the Linux kernel finally resumed the safety process at $T - 0.010 upright(" s")$, the remaining physical clearance had collapsed to:

$ d_(upright("remaining")) = 0.280 upright(" m") - 0.252 upright(" m") = 0.028 upright(" m") = 2.8 upright(" cm") $

The safety software immediately commanded maximum emergency braking deceleration ($a_(upright("max")) = 20.0 upright(" m/s")^2$). However, the quadratic braking distance required to arrest moving mass from $v = 1.8 upright(" m/s")$ was:

$ d_(upright("brake")) = frac(v^2, 2 a_(upright("max"))) = frac(1.8^2, 2 times 20.0) = 3.24 / 40.0 = 0.081 upright(" m") = 8.1 upright(" cm") $

Because the required stopping distance ($8.1 upright(" cm")$) exceeded the remaining physical clearance ($2.8 upright(" cm")$), the collision was physically unavoidable. The manipulator struck the solid steel fixture plate at an impact velocity of $v_(upright("impact")) approx 0.92 upright(" m/s")$, generating a dynamic shock load of $F_(upright("impact")) approx 3.4 upright(" kN")$. The shock load fractured the precision flexspline teeth of the Joint 4 harmonic drive speed reducer, bent the mounting flange, and damaged internal motor bearings, incurring a $7 \, 200 upright(" USD")$ overhaul cost. The flight-recorder telemetry ledger is detailed in #ref(<tbl-telemetry-ch09>, supplement: [Table]).

