The post-incident investigation brought together the three engineering perspectives to reconstruct why the failure occurred:

- #strong[Maya (CS/ML):] #emph["Our reinforcement learning policy had reached zero collision rate in training and possessed high confidence. But because the safety monitor ran in Python on the same Linux host, the model was completely blinded during the kernel writeback pause. We must never treat software assertions as physical safety guarantees."]
- #strong[Carlos (ECE/Embedded):] #emph["The fatal flaw was architectural: allowing an OS-managed thread to control motor registers directly without an independent hardware watchdog. In our refactored architecture, candidate trajectories are guarded by a $50 upright(" ms")$ intent lease in static SRAM. When the OS paused for $140 upright(" ms")$, the microcontroller's $1000 upright(" Hz")$ timer ISR would have expired the lease at $T - 0.075 upright(" s")$ and executed a Category 1 dynamic stop with $0.18 upright(" m")$ of clearance to spare."]
- #strong[Alex (MechE/Robotics):] #emph["The $3.4 upright(" kN")$ impact load easily sheared the cup flexspline teeth rated for $180 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m")$. Moving mass cannot be commanded to stop without respecting $d_(upright("stop")) = v tau + v^2 \/ 2 a_(upright("max"))$. By embedding dynamic stopping envelopes into the bare-metal microcontroller, we guarantee that velocity is throttled before clearance falls below the braking threshold."]

