== Systems Stress-Tests: Architectural Food for Thought
<systems-stress-tests-architectural-food-for-thought-4>
Evaluating the real-time reflex and safety enforcement tier requires navigating difficult trade-offs where control barrier theory, microsecond embedded execution, and high-power inverter dynamics intersect. The following four architectural stress-tests examine critical edge cases across bare-metal safety enforcement:

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 1: The Relative-Degree Deadlock and Actuator Saturation Dilemma]
<stress-test-1-the-relative-degree-deadlock-and-actuator-saturation-dilemma>
An autonomous forklift carrying a $1200 upright(" kg")$ pallet approaches a warehouse wall at $v = 3.5 upright(" m/s")$. The safety microcontroller solves a 1 kHz Minimal-Intervention CBF-QP enforcing an obstacle clearance barrier $h \( upright(bold(p)) \) = parallel upright(bold(p)) - upright(bold(p))_(upright("wall")) parallel^2 - r_(upright("safe"))^2 gt.eq 0$.

Because position has relative degree 2 with respect to drive motor torque, the CBF inequality constrains instantaneous braking acceleration. At distance $d = 1.2 upright(" m")$, the QP solver finds an instantaneous torque command that satisfies $dot(h) gt.eq - gamma h$ at $t_k$. However, this state lies outside the viability kernel: at $t_(k + 1)$, satisfying the barrier requires a deceleration of $a_(upright("req")) = 8.5 upright(" m/s")^2$, but tire-ground Coulomb friction limits maximum braking to $a_(upright("friction")) = mu g = 0.6 times 9.81 = 5.88 upright(" m/s")^2$. The QP solver becomes primal infeasible ($cal(U)_(upright("safe")) = nothing$) inside the 1 ms ISR.

#emph[When an active-set QP solver encounters mathematical infeasibility due to actuator saturation, what deterministic fallback command must execute within the remaining $175 thin mu upright("s")$ window? How must systems engineers design High-Order Control Barrier Functions (HOCBFs) to ensure that the admissible set $cal(C)$ accounts for state-dependent friction limits before high-speed trajectories are authorized?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 2: The Zero-Allocation Active-Set Iteration Bound vs.~Primal Divergence]
<stress-test-2-the-zero-allocation-active-set-iteration-bound-vs.-primal-divergence>
An 8-DoF collaborative humanoid arm operates with 14 active Control Barrier Functions (joint limits, self-collision spheres, workspace boundaries, and thermal barriers). The safety filter runs on an ARM Cortex-M7 with a strict execution budget of $upright("WCET") lt.eq 175 thin mu upright("s")$ at $1000 upright(" Hz")$.

During a fast multi-link reaching motion, 6 barrier constraints activate simultaneously. The active-set solver performs iterative Givens rotations in static DTCM SRAM (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");). Due to near-parallel constraint hyperplanes (ill-conditioned active set), the solver requires 18 active-set iterations to converge, consuming $240 thin mu upright("s")$ and overrunning the timer ISR deadline. If firmware enforces a hard iteration ceiling ($N_(upright("iter")) lt.eq 10$), the solver terminates with a suboptimal, non-invariant control command that violates the self-collision boundary.

#emph[How should systems architects structure barrier hierarchies and constraint pruning (e.g., evaluating only the $K$-nearest geometric barriers based on spatial distance) to guarantee that active-set factorizations converge within a fixed, deterministic clock-cycle bound across all operational configurations?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 3: The Category 1 Dynamic Braking vs.~Inverter DC-Bus Overvoltage Spikes]
<stress-test-3-the-category-1-dynamic-braking-vs.-inverter-dc-bus-overvoltage-spikes>
A high-speed pick-and-place delta robot moving a $15 upright(" kg")$ payload at $v = 4.0 upright(" m/s")$ experiences an intent lease expiration fault. The safety MCU triggers a Category 1 Safe Stop 1 (SS1), commanding maximum counter-electromotive braking torque ($tau = - K_t i_q$) to arrest motion in $45 upright(" ms")$.

As the motor phases generate counter-torque, mechanical kinetic energy ($E_k = 1 / 2 m v^2 = 120 upright(" J")$) is regenerated back through the inverter bridge into the $48 upright(" V")$ DC bus capacitor bank. The regenerated current surges to $I_(upright("regen")) = 55 upright(" A")$, causing the DC bus voltage to spike from $48 upright(" V")$ to $72 upright(" V")$ in $8 upright(" ms")$ ($Delta V = frac(I Delta t, C)$). This overvoltage exceeds the $60 upright(" V")$ breakdown rating of the inverter MOSFETs, triggering the hardware overvoltage protection circuit to fire an immediate Category 0 Safe Torque Off (STO). With the inverter bridge floating ($0 upright(" Nm")$ torque), the robot coasts un-braked at $4.0 upright(" m/s")$ into the conveyor frame.

#emph[How must real-time safety enforcers co-design dynamic braking deceleration profiles ($a_(upright("brake"))$) with power electronics constraints (DC bus capacitance, brake chopper resistors, and regenerative current absorption) to prevent emergency stops from triggering secondary electrical hardware trips?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 4: The Tactile Impedance Relaxation vs.~Hard Position Enclosure]
<stress-test-4-the-tactile-impedance-relaxation-vs.-hard-position-enclosure>
A collaborative robot arm is equipped with joint torque sensors executing Cartesian impedance control ($M dot.double(upright(bold(x))) + D dot(upright(bold(x))) + K \( upright(bold(x)) - upright(bold(x))_(upright("ref")) \) = upright(bold(F))_(upright("ext"))$) to safely co-exist with human operators. A hard Control Barrier Function simultaneously enforces a virtual safety fence ($x_(upright("tcp")) lt.eq x_(upright("wall"))$).

A human worker inadvertently leans against the robot arm, applying an external contact force of $upright(bold(F))_(upright("ext")) = 80 upright(" N")$ toward the virtual wall. The impedance controller yields compliantly to protect the human from bruising, deflecting the end-effector toward the boundary. As the position approaches the safety fence, the CBF-QP overrides the compliance controller, commanding maximum counter-torque ($K_(upright("stiff")) arrow.r oo$) to prevent the barrier breach. This instantaneous stiffness step exerts a rigid $450 upright(" N")$ reactionary force against the human operator's arm, causing soft-tissue injury.

#emph[How should systems engineers unify compliant physical human-robot interaction with inviolable geometric barrier certificates? Can Control Barrier Functions natively modulate impedance stiffness and damping parameters rather than commanding rigid kinematic projections when interacting with compliant biological bodies?]

