#part[Integration and Release]
Building an autonomous agent that operates successfully during a curated demonstration in a controlled laboratory is straightforward. Engineering a physical AI machine that can be safely released into the physical world alongside humans and critical infrastructure is an entirely different discipline.

When an autonomous system operates in production, three critical failure modes emerge:

+ #strong[Resource Contention and Thermal Throttling:] When perception, world modeling, and trajectory generation run simultaneously, memory bus contention on unified memory architectures can cause tail latency spikes that trigger safety watchdog timeouts.
+ #strong[Policy Endogeneity and Covariate Shift:] Fine-tuning learned models on operational logs that contain human safety interventions induces severe distributional collapse. The system inadvertently learns that dangerous states are normal, amplifying future errors.
+ #strong[Unmodeled Physical Edge Cases:] Simulation benchmarks cannot prove that a system is safe against dirty camera lenses, dropped communication packets, microsecond clock skews, or sudden operating system kernel panics.

Part III shockproofs the complete physical AI system, establishes human-machine governance, and constructs the empirical safety case required for an accountable release verdict.

