This four-tier hierarchy applies across all three canonical Physical AI archetypes. In #strong[Locomotion and Mobility] (such as autonomous transport AGVs and quadrupeds), Tier 4 manages fleet coordination and global warehouse maps ($0.01 upright(" Hz")$), Tier 3 processes multi-camera spatial scene graphs ($2 upright(" Hz")$), Tier 2 runs receding-horizon diffusion trajectory decoders ($20 upright(" Hz")$), and Tier 1 evaluates Control Barrier Functions and commands motor inverters at a deterministic $1000 upright(" Hz")$. In #strong[Contact Manipulation] (such as multi-joint robotic arms and dexterous hands), Tier 3 handles semantic task decomposition, Tier 2 executes 50 Hz action-chunked spline policies, and Tier 1 enforces Cartesian contact compliance and joint torque limits in tightly coupled SRAM. In #strong[Cybernetic Process and Energy Regulation] (such as battery energy storage systems and smart inverters), Tier 3 logs degradation trends, Tier 2 runs neural state-of-charge estimators, and Tier 1 bare-metal DSPs execute 20 kHz Space-Vector PWM switching while guarding electrochemical safe sets.

To build an embodied system that thrives within these physical boundaries, this chapter establishes the end-to-end discipline of heterogeneous workload placement. We begin by examining micro-architectural contention across shared on-chip memory crossbars and configuring hardware AXI Quality-of-Service arbitration (Section 10.2). We then construct zero-copy, lock-free SRAM mailboxes that bridge asymmetric Linux host cores and bare-metal microcontrollers without OS scheduling jitter (Section 10.3). Next, we derive the physics of data movement using the Roofline model to balance arithmetic intensity against DRAM access energy (Section 10.4) and model dynamic thermal dissipation to prevent DVFS downclocking cliffs (Section 10.5). We quantify multi-dimensional re-placement ripple effects across latency, power, and safety (Section 10.6), dissect the forensic autopsy of the Insulated Cold-Vault Collision where a 5G wireless blackout caused a 1.2-ton machine to strike a structural pillar (Section 10.7), freeze the formal workload placement specification (#strong[PLACE-01]) that bridges systems architecture to physical integration (Section 10.8), and stress-test the heterogeneous architecture against high-stakes placement dilemmas (Section 10.9).

== Interconnects and Bus Contention
<interconnects-and-bus-contention>
In modern embedded Systems-on-Chip (SoCs)---such as the NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin, Qualcomm Robotics RB5, or Apple M-series silicon---heterogeneous compute engines (CPU, GPU, NPU, ISP, and DMA engines) share a single pool of physical LPDDR5 DRAM through a #strong[Unified Memory Architecture (UMA)] (#ref(<fig-uma-contention>, supplement: [Figure])).

Unlike desktop workstations where CPUs and discrete GPUs possess separate physical memory pools connected across dedicated PCIe expansion buses, an embedded UMA SoC routes all memory traffic through a shared silicon crossbar. While UMA eliminates explicit PCIe data copies, it introduces a severe micro-architectural hazard: #strong[Memory Crossbar Contention].

To visualize this hazard, consider the #strong[Single-Lane Highway Tollbooth Bottleneck]. Imagine a multi-lane highway feeding into a single shared toll plaza. Under normal conditions, traffic flows smoothly. But when a fleet of wide, slow-moving freight trucks enters the plaza, they occupy every toll lane with long transaction times. If an emergency ambulance approaches with an urgent patient, it cannot pass: it is trapped in gridlock behind the trucks.

In an embedded SoC, the shared memory crossbar is that toll plaza. High-bandwidth camera DMA write streams are the convoy of freight trucks, blasting gigabytes of raw pixel data every second. The real-time microcontroller reads and NPU tensor weight fetches are the emergency vehicles. Without hardware priority sirens that preempt the freight trucks at the toll gate, the emergency control loop is starved of memory access.

