=== Micro-Architectural Head-of-Line Blocking
<micro-architectural-head-of-line-blocking>
When an unthrottled camera DMA burst saturates the memory crossbar, DRAM access latency for CPU and MCU cores spikes by $300 upright("–") 800 %$. The multi-channel memory controller's command queue fills with long-burst transactions (such as 256-byte incrementing bursts, #NormalTok("INCR256");), blocking urgent high-priority requests.

Total memory access latency $t_(upright("access"))$ under bus contention is modeled as:

$ t_(upright("access")) = t_(upright("prop")) + t_(upright("arbitration")) + t_(upright("block")) $

where $t_(upright("prop"))$ is the baseline silicon propagation delay, $t_(upright("arbitration"))$ is the DRAM row-buffer activation and command arbitration overhead, and $t_(upright("block"))$ represents head-of-line blocking delay:

$ t_(upright("block")) = frac(N_(upright("burst")) dot.op L_(upright("burst")), W_(upright("bus")) dot.op f_(upright("bus"))) $

where:

- $N_(upright("burst"))$ is the number of pending lower-priority burst transactions queued in the memory controller \[dimensionless count\].
- $L_(upright("burst"))$ is the burst length per transaction \[$upright("Bytes")$\] (e.g., $256 upright(" bytes")$ for an AXI #NormalTok("INCR256"); transaction).
- $W_(upright("bus"))$ is the physical data width of the crossbar interconnect in bytes per clock cycle \[$upright("Bytes/cycle")$\] (e.g., $16 upright(" bytes")$ for a 128-bit crossbar).
- $f_(upright("bus"))$ is the interconnect clock frequency \[$upright("Hz")$ or $upright("cycles/s")$\] (e.g., $800 upright(" MHz")$).

#strong[Concrete Numerical Hazard:] Consider an unmanaged memory crossbar where an unthrottled 4K camera DMA write stream queues $N_(upright("burst")) = 16$ pending $256 upright("-byte")$ bursts across a 128-bit ($W_(upright("bus")) = 16 upright(" bytes")$) interconnect running at $f_(upright("bus")) = 800 upright(" MHz")$. The bus transfer blocking time evaluates to:

$ t_(upright("block")) = frac(16 times 256 upright(" bytes"), 16 upright(" bytes/cycle") times 800 times 10^6 upright(" cycles/s")) = frac(4 \, 096 upright(" bytes"), 12.8 times 10^9 upright(" bytes/s")) = 320 upright(" ns") $

Factoring in DRAM bank conflict and row-activation delay ($t_(upright("arbitration")) approx 530 upright(" ns")$), single-transaction access latency spikes to $t_(upright("access")) approx 850 upright(" ns")$. For a $1000 upright(" Hz")$ control loop requiring 20 distinct encoder and waypoint memory reads per millisecond tick, aggregate bus blocking accumulates over:

$ Delta t_(upright("contention")) = 20 times 850 upright(" ns") = 17.0 thin mu upright("s") $

In a hard real-time safety Interrupt Service Routine (ISR) with a certified worst-case budget of $upright("WCET") lt.eq 175 thin mu upright("s")$, this $17.0 thin mu upright("s")$ blocking delay consumes nearly $10 %$ of the entire execution window solely waiting on memory crossbar stalls.

=== Hardware AXI QoS Priority Arbitration
<hardware-axi-qos-priority-arbitration>
To eliminate memory crossbar starvation, Physical AI architectures configure hardware #strong[AXI Quality-of-Service (QoS)] arbitration registers (#NormalTok("ARQOS"); read and #NormalTok("AWQOS"); write 4-bit signals) at the silicon interconnect fabric:

$ upright("AXI QoS Priority Invariant:") quad upright("QoS") \( upright("MCU Real-Time Reads") \) > upright("QoS") \( upright("NPU Weight Fetches") \) > upright("QoS") \( upright("Camera DMA Writes") \) $

+ #strong[Real-Time MCU SRAM/DRAM Reads (#NormalTok("QoS = 15");):] Absolute highest interconnect priority (value #NormalTok("0b1111");). AXI crossbar arbiters immediately preempt lower-priority transactions at the next 64-bit beat boundary ($1.25 upright(" ns")$) and insert MCU read requests at the head of the DRAM controller scheduler queue, guaranteeing transaction service within $< 45 upright(" ns")$ and total loop memory delay $< 0.90 thin mu upright("s")$.
+ #strong[NPU Tensor Weight Fetches (#NormalTok("QoS = 10");):] Medium-high priority (value #NormalTok("0b1010");), guaranteeing sustained memory throughput ($15 upright("–") 30 upright(" GB/s")$) to meet $20 upright("–") 50 upright(" Hz")$ action chunking deadlines without starving the real-time controller.
+ #strong[Camera Sensor DMA Writes (#NormalTok("QoS = 4");):] Background priority (value #NormalTok("0b0100");). Camera DMA streams are buffered in dedicated on-chip hardware FIFO buffers and paced onto the crossbar using token-bucket rate limiters only during memory bus idle cycles, completely eliminating latency tail blowouts for critical safety loops.

