== Lock-Free Shared SRAM Mailboxes
<lock-free-shared-sram-mailboxes>
To pass high-rate trajectory chunks ($50 upright(" Hz")$) and low-latency safety telemetry ($1000 upright(" Hz")$) between the Linux host MPU and the bare-metal MCU without thread blocking, Physical AI rejects traditional operating system IPC primitives (such as Unix domain sockets, POSIX message queues, or kernel mutexes).

Under Linux, acquiring a contested mutex involves kernel #NormalTok("futex"); system calls, CPU thread context switches, and core migrations that routinely introduce $10 upright("–") 50 upright(" ms")$ of non-deterministic scheduling jitter. Crucially, a bare-metal microcontroller executing in zero-allocation static SRAM has no operating system kernel, cannot block on user-space semaphores, and cannot interact with Linux scheduler runqueues.

To visualize how these asymmetric processors communicate safely, consider the #strong[Physical Post-Office Lockbox Hatch]. Imagine a physical pass-through hatch built into the wall between two separate rooms. In the deliberative planning room (the Linux host), an architect draws up a new trajectory map every $20.0 upright(" ms")$ ($50 upright(" Hz")$) and drops it into the hatch, stamping it with a monotonically increasing serial number. In the machine shop (the bare-metal MCU), a machinist peeks into the hatch every $1.0 upright(" ms")$ ($1000 upright(" Hz")$), grabs the latest stamped blueprint, and immediately executes the machine toolpath.

Crucially, the machinist never rings a bell, never knocks on the door, and never waits for the architect to finish drawing before cutting metal. If no new blueprint has arrived, the machinist continues smoothly executing the current plan; when a newer blueprint arrives, old intermediate sketches are instantly bypassed without dynamic memory allocation or thread stalling.

In silicon, this mechanism is implemented using #strong[Zero-Copy Lock-Free Single-Producer Single-Consumer (SPSC) Ring Buffers] residing in physical internal static SRAM (Tightly Coupled Memory - TCM).

=== Memory Ordering, False Sharing, and Cache Maintenance
<memory-ordering-false-sharing-and-cache-maintenance>
Operating across asymmetric silicon domains (e.g., an out-of-order 64-bit ARM Cortex-A78 running Linux and a deterministic 32-bit ARM Cortex-M7 running bare-metal) requires strict memory isolation:

+ #strong[Cache Line Alignment and False Sharing:] Modern CPU cores manage memory in 64-byte cache lines. If producer and consumer control variables reside within the same 64-byte window, the hardware cache coherency interconnect continuously bounces the line between cores (cache line ping-pong via MESI snooping), degrading throughput by over $10 times$. All mailbox control structures and payload slots are strictly isolated using 64-byte alignment (#NormalTok("alignas(64)");).
+ #strong[Atomic Monotonic Sequence Counters:] The producer increments #NormalTok("head_sequence"); using 64-bit atomic operations with release memory barriers (#NormalTok("atomic_store_explicit(&mailbox->head_sequence, seq, memory_order_release)"); or ARM #NormalTok("dmb ish");). The consumer reads the sequence using acquire memory barriers (#NormalTok("atomic_load_explicit(&mailbox->head_sequence, memory_order_acquire)"); or ARM #NormalTok("dmb ish");), guaranteeing that payload data writes are fully committed across the memory fabric before the sequence counter updates.
+ #strong[Explicit Cache Line Invalidation to Point of Coherency (PoC):] Because the Cortex-A processor operates with hierarchical L1/L2 data caches while the Cortex-M microcontroller accesses tightly coupled SRAM without hardware cache snooping, software must explicitly maintain cache coherency:

$ upright("Producer Write Sequence:") quad upright("write_payload") \( upright("slot") \) arrow.r.double.long upright("clean_dcache_range") \( upright("addr") \, upright("size") \) arrow.r.double.long upright("atomic_store") \( upright("head") \) $

$ upright("Consumer Read Sequence:") quad upright("atomic_load") \( upright("head") \) arrow.r.double.long upright("invalidate_dcache_range") \( upright("addr") \, upright("size") \) arrow.r.double.long upright("read_payload") \( upright("slot") \) $

=== Latest-Frame Sampling Semantics
<latest-frame-sampling-semantics>
The lock-free SPSC ring buffer naturally bridges the cadence mismatch between multi-rate cognitive organs. When Maya's 50 Hz trajectory planner publishes 16-waypoint action chunks faster than Carlos's 1000 Hz MCU reflex loop consumes entire batches, the consumer does not process an unbounded FIFO queue. Instead, it executes #strong[Latest-Frame Sampling]:

$ upright("slot_idx") = upright("head_sequence") med \( mod med N_(upright("slots")) \) $

$ tau_(upright("staleness")) = t_(upright("sample")) - t_(upright("publish")) lt.eq 1 / f_(upright("producer")) = T_(upright("chunk")) $

where:

- $upright("slot_idx")$ is the physical buffer index into the triple-buffered SRAM mailbox \[dimensionless integer $in { 0 \, 1 \, 2 }$\].
- $upright("head_sequence")$ is the monotonically increasing 64-bit atomic transaction counter \[count\].
- $N_(upright("slots")) = 3$ is the triple-buffered slot allocation preventing writer-reader memory collisions.
- $tau_(upright("staleness"))$ is the maximum age of the ingested trajectory data \[$upright("seconds")$\].
- $f_(upright("producer"))$ is the publishing cadence of the neural trajectory planner \[$upright("Hz")$\] ($50 upright(" Hz") arrow.r.double.long T_(upright("chunk")) = 20.0 upright(" ms")$).

#strong[Concrete Operational Mechanics:] If the producer publishes at $f_(upright("producer")) = 50 upright(" Hz")$ ($T_(upright("chunk")) = 20.0 upright(" ms")$) into a 3-slot ring buffer, and the consumer samples at $f_(upright("consumer")) = 1000 upright(" Hz")$ ($T_(upright("cycle")) = 1.0 upright(" ms")$), the latest-frame sampling mechanism drops intermediate stale waypoints without memory allocation or blocking. It guarantees that the sampled trajectory chunk is never older than $20.0 upright(" ms")$, feeding smooth quintic spline interpolators on the microcontroller with zero queue lag.

