== Arithmetic Intensity and the Roofline Model
<arithmetic-intensity-and-the-roofline-model>
In physical systems design, the total electrical power budget is constrained by onboard battery capacity and thermal dissipation limits. To maximize operational battery life and prevent thermal runaway, algorithms must be optimized for #strong[Arithmetic Intensity] ($I_(upright("arith"))$), defined as the ratio of computational floating-point operations to memory access traffic @hennessy2019computer@williams2009roofline:

$ I_(upright("arith")) = Phi_(upright("FLOPs")) / upright("BW")_(upright("DRAM")) = upright("Total Operations (FLOPs)") / upright("Total Memory Traffic (Bytes)") quad [upright("FLOPs") / upright("Byte")] $

To grasp why arithmetic intensity dictates physical survival, consider the #strong[Data Movement Shipping Tax]. Imagine manufacturing a precision mechanical assembly. Performing an arithmetic operation on data already stored in local on-chip registers is like using a hand tool already in your grasp: it takes negligible physical energy ($E_(upright("op")) approx 0.2 upright(" pJ")$ for an INT8 multiply-accumulate). In contrast, fetching a single byte of weights from off-chip DRAM across $50 upright(" mm")$ of printed circuit board traces is like ordering raw materials shipped across town on a diesel truck: charging the long PCB copper traces and memory I/O pads consumes $150 upright(" pJ")$ per byte---a #strong[$750 times$ physical energy disparity].

If an algorithm fetches billions of weight bytes but performs only a handful of arithmetic operations per byte ($I_(upright("arith")) lt.double beta_(upright("ridge"))$), it spends $99 %$ of its energy budget purely paying the shipping tax. The processor's arithmetic ALUs sit idle while memory I/O pads dissipate Joulean heat, draining the battery and driving silicon toward thermal breakdown.

The hardware Roofline model dictates whether a neural policy is bound by computational throughput or memory bus bandwidth:

$ upright("Attainable Performance (FLOPs/s)") = min (pi_(upright("peak")) \, #h(0em) I_(upright("arith")) times beta_(upright("mem"))) $

The transition between operational regimes is defined by the hardware #strong[Ridge Point] ($beta_(upright("ridge"))$):

$ beta_(upright("ridge")) = pi_(upright("peak")) / beta_(upright("mem")) quad [upright("FLOPs") / upright("Byte")] $

where:

- $pi_(upright("peak"))$ is the peak theoretical compute throughput of the accelerator \[$upright("FLOPs/s")$\].
- $beta_(upright("mem"))$ is the peak sustained memory bus bandwidth \[$upright("Bytes/s")$\].
- $beta_(upright("ridge"))$ is the hardware ridge point separating memory-bound from compute-bound regimes \[$upright("FLOPs/Byte")$\].
- $Phi_(upright("FLOPs"))$ is the operational floating-point execution rate \[$upright("FLOPs/s")$\].
- $upright("BW")_(upright("DRAM"))$ is the off-chip DRAM memory bus bandwidth \[$upright("Bytes/s")$\].

=== Memory-Bound vs.~Compute-Bound Regimes
<memory-bound-vs.-compute-bound-regimes>
The hardware Roofline curve divides all neural workloads into two operational regimes:

+ #strong[Memory-Bandwidth Bound ($I_(upright("arith")) < beta_(upright("ridge"))$):] Computational throughput is strictly limited by the rate at which bytes can be fetched across off-chip memory traces. The accelerator's tensor ALUs spend $> 90 %$ of their clock cycles stalled waiting for DRAM operands, while memory bus PHY I/O pads dissipate substantial electrical power.
+ #strong[Compute Bound ($I_(upright("arith")) gt.eq beta_(upright("ridge"))$):] Tensor ALUs operate at peak saturation. Memory bus transfer times are completely hidden behind arithmetic execution pipelines.

Total electrical power dissipated by compute and memory silicon is governed by:

$ P_(upright("total")) = P_(upright("ALU")) + P_(upright("DRAM_IO")) = \( Phi_(upright("FLOPs")) times E_(upright("op")) \) + \( upright("BW")_(upright("DRAM")) times E_(upright("DRAM_byte")) \) $

where $E_(upright("op"))$ is the energy consumed per arithmetic operation \[$upright("Joules/FLOP")$\] ($approx 0.2 upright(" pJ")$ for an INT8 multiply-accumulate operation in modern $7 upright(" nm")$ silicon), and $E_(upright("DRAM_byte"))$ is the physical I/O pad and DRAM bank access energy per transferred byte \[$upright("Joules/Byte")$\] ($approx 150 upright(" pJ/Byte")$ in LPDDR5 DRAM).

=== Side-by-Side Architectural Evaluation
<side-by-side-architectural-evaluation>
Consider a concrete numerical comparison between two deployment architectures on an autonomous mobile platform:

- #strong[Architecture A: Unquantized Single-Token VLA on DRAM:] An unquantized 7-billion-parameter model executing autoregressively fetches $7.0 upright(" GB")$ of FP16 model weights per action token. Generating 10 tokens per second ($10 upright(" Hz")$) demands $70.0 upright(" GB/s")$ of continuous DRAM memory bandwidth ($upright("BW")_(upright("DRAM")) = 70.0 times 10^9 upright(" Bytes/s")$). At $E_(upright("DRAM_byte")) approx 150 upright(" pJ/byte")$, the memory I/O interface alone dissipates: $ P_(upright("DRAM_IO")) = 70.0 times 10^9 upright(" Bytes/s") times 150 times 10^(- 12) upright(" J/Byte") = 10.50 upright(" Watts") $ Because single-token generation executes approximately $14.0 upright(" GFLOPs/token")$ ($14.0 times 10^9 upright(" FLOPs/s")$), the operational arithmetic intensity is: $ I_(upright("arith")) = frac(14.0 times 10^9 upright(" FLOPs/s"), 70.0 times 10^9 upright(" Bytes/s")) = 0.20 upright(" FLOPs/Byte") lt.double beta_(upright("ridge")) approx 120 upright(" FLOPs/Byte") $ This $10.50 upright(" W")$ memory bus power draw occurs entirely in PHY I/O pads and DRAM banks before arithmetic is performed, trapping the processor in the memory-bound regime where ALUs are stalled $> 95 %$ of the time.

- #strong[Architecture B: Quantized Action-Chunked Policy in On-Chip SRAM:] An INT8-quantized 1D Temporal U-Net diffusion policy ($6.5 upright(" MB")$ total parameter footprint) resides permanently inside the neural accelerator's $8 upright(" MB")$ on-chip static SRAM. Generating a 16-step action chunk requires $1.2 upright(" GFLOPs")$ of computation ($60.0 upright(" GFLOPs/s")$ at $50 upright(" Hz")$) while generating zero off-chip DRAM traffic ($upright("BW")_(upright("DRAM")) = 0 upright(" GB/s")$). At internal SRAM access energy $E_(upright("SRAM")) approx 1.5 upright(" pJ/byte")$, total data movement power is: $ P_(upright("SRAM_IO")) = 50 upright(" Hz") times 6.5 times 10^6 upright(" Bytes") times 1.5 times 10^(- 12) upright(" J/Byte") = 0.00049 upright(" Watts") = 0.49 upright(" mW") $ Operational arithmetic intensity surges to $I_(upright("arith")) > 180 upright(" FLOPs/Byte") > beta_(upright("ridge"))$, operating squarely in the compute-bound regime, slashing data movement heat by $99.9 %$, and enabling sustained $50 upright(" Hz")$ inference within a compact $8 upright(" W")$ total SoC power envelope.

To achieve maximum computational efficiency, Physical AI architectures apply three silicon optimizations:

+ #strong[On-Chip SRAM Weight Tiling:] High-frequency trajectory decoders (such as 1D Temporal U-Nets for Diffusion Policies in Contact Manipulation) are quantized to INT8 or FP8 precision, enabling the entire active model weights ($< 8 upright(" MB")$) to reside permanently inside the NPU's on-chip static SRAM.
+ #strong[Elimination of Off-Chip DRAM Round-Trips:] By keeping model weights and intermediate activations in on-chip SRAM across layers, memory bus traffic is reduced to zero during trajectory generation, increasing arithmetic intensity to $I_(upright("arith")) > 150 upright(" FLOPs/Byte")$.
+ #strong[Joulean Energy Savings:] Operating out of SRAM ($E_(upright("SRAM")) approx 1.5 upright(" pJ")$) cuts data movement energy by $98.5 %$, allowing the neural accelerator to sustain $50 upright(" Hz")$ action chunking while drawing under $12 upright(" Watts")$ of total package power.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] Sizing a foundation model for embedded robotics based solely on total parameter count ($N$) and theoretical FLOP throughput ensures the model will execute within the edge compute budget.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Computational throughput (FLOPs/s) is useless if the workload is trapped in the memory-bandwidth-bound regime of the Roofline curve ($I_(upright("arith")) < beta_(upright("ridge"))$). Single-batch autoregressive token generation fetches multi-gigabyte weight tensors across DRAM for every individual step ($I_(upright("arith")) approx 1.0 upright(" FLOPs/Byte")$). At $150 upright(" pJ/Byte")$ DRAM access energy, memory PHY I/O pads dissipate $10.5 upright(" W")$ of heat purely shuffling weights, driving silicon junction temperature past $85^compose upright("C")$ and triggering hardware DVFS downclocking that halves inference throughput.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Trajectory generation policies deployed on power-constrained edge SoCs must be designed for the compute-bound Roofline regime ($I_(upright("arith")) gt.eq 120 upright(" FLOPs/Byte")$), leveraging temporal action chunking ($H gt.eq 16$) and model parameter footprints small enough ($< 8 upright(" MB")$) to tile permanently in on-chip static SRAM.

