== Thermal Envelopes and Dynamic Throttling
<thermal-envelopes-and-dynamic-throttling>
All electrical energy consumed by silicon computing substrates is ultimately converted into thermal heat via Joule heating ($P = I^2 R$). In an embodied robot, compute silicon is frequently housed inside a sealed, IP67-rated water-resistant chassis lacking heavy active cooling fans (#ref(<fig-thermal-derating>, supplement: [Figure])).

To understand why thermal dissipation threatens real-time stability, consider the #strong[Thermal Mass Cooking Over Time] analogy. Think of a heavy cast-iron skillet placed on a stovetop flame. The skillet does not reach $200^compose upright("C")$ immediately; because iron has high thermal mass (thermal capacitance $C_(upright("th"))$), it absorbs heat slowly over several minutes ($tau_(upright("th")) = R_(upright("th")) C_(upright("th"))$). A cook who touches the skillet after five seconds might conclude the pan is safe. But under sustained heat input, the temperature climbs inexorably until it burns whatever touches it.

In an embedded robot, the silicon die, copper heat spreader, and aluminum chassis form that exact thermal mass. When Carlos benchmarks a $25.0 upright(" W")$ neural model on an open lab bench for 30 seconds, the die remains deceptively cool---this is the #strong[Cold Bench Fallacy]. But when Alex seals that same board inside an IP67 aluminum enclosure on a $45.0^compose upright("C")$ factory floor, continuous inference pumps heat into the chassis. After two minutes, junction temperature breaches the $85.0^compose upright("C")$ safety limit. At that instant, hardware DVFS circuits trigger: processor clocks are slashed by $60 %$, doubling inference latency mid-mission and dropping real-time control frames.

