The thermodynamic heat dissipation of the compute die is governed by the lumped-parameter thermal RC differential equation:

$ C_(upright("th")) frac(d T_j \( t \), d t) = P_(upright("dynamic")) \( t \) + P_(upright("leakage")) \( T_j \) - frac(T_j \( t \) - T_(upright("ambient")), R_(upright("th"))) $

where:

- $T_j \( t \)$ is the instantaneous silicon junction temperature \[$""^compose upright("C")$\].
- $T_(upright("ambient"))$ is the ambient enclosure temperature \[$""^compose upright("C")$\].
- $C_(upright("th"))$ is the lumped thermal capacitance of the silicon die and copper heat spreader \[$upright("Joules") \/^compose upright("C")$ or $upright("J/K")$\].
- $R_(upright("th"))$ is the thermal resistance from junction to ambient air through the aluminum chassis \[$upright("K") \/ upright("W")$\].
- $tau_(upright("th")) = R_(upright("th")) C_(upright("th"))$ represents the thermal time constant of the silicon package \[$upright("seconds")$\].
- $P_(upright("dynamic")) = alpha C V^2 f$ is the dynamic switching power dissipated by active transistor gates \[$upright("Watts")$\].
- $P_(upright("leakage")) \( T_j \) prop T_j^2 e^(- q V_(upright("th")) \/ k T_j)$ is static sub-threshold leakage power that grows exponentially with junction temperature \[$upright("Watts")$\].

Under sustained neural inference workload $P_(upright("total")) = P_(upright("dynamic")) + P_(upright("leakage"))$, the transient thermal step response starting from ambient equilibrium $T_j \( 0 \) = T_(upright("ambient"))$ is:

$ T_j \( t \) = T_(upright("ambient")) + P_(upright("total")) R_(upright("th")) (1 - e^(- t \/ tau_(upright("th")))) $

The asymptotic steady-state equilibrium junction temperature reaches:

$ T_(upright("ss")) = T_(upright("ambient")) + P_(upright("total")) R_(upright("th")) $

The elapsed operating time $t_(upright("trip"))$ required to reach a critical thermal shutdown or throttling threshold $T_(upright("trip"))$ is:

$ t_(upright("trip")) = - tau_(upright("th")) ln (1 - frac(T_(upright("trip")) - T_(upright("ambient")), P_(upright("total")) R_(upright("th")))) $

=== Concrete Numerical Evaluation
<concrete-numerical-evaluation>
Consider an autonomous mobile robot with an onboard compute module enclosed in an IP67 sealed aluminum chassis ($T_(upright("ambient")) = 45.0^compose upright("C")$ on a warm factory floor). The compute package exhibits a thermal resistance of $R_(upright("th")) = 1.80 upright(" K/W")$ and a thermal capacitance of $C_(upright("th")) = 25.0 upright(" J/K")$, yielding a thermal time constant of $tau_(upright("th")) = R_(upright("th")) C_(upright("th")) = 45.0 upright(" seconds")$.

- #strong[Unmanaged High-Load Inference ($P_(upright("total")) = 25.0 upright(" W")$):] Under continuous, unthrottled neural inference, the equilibrium steady-state junction temperature evaluates to: $ T_(upright("ss")) = 45.0^compose upright("C") + \( 25.0 upright(" W") times 1.80 upright(" K/W") \) = 45.0 + 45.0 = 90.0^compose upright("C") $ Because $T_(upright("ss")) = 90.0^compose upright("C") > 85.0^compose upright("C")$, the silicon will breach the critical safety threshold ($T_(upright("trip")) = 85.0^compose upright("C")$). The time elapsed before throttling trips is: $ t_(upright("trip")) = - 45.0 ln (1 - frac(85.0 - 45.0, 25.0 times 1.80)) = - 45.0 ln (1 - 40.0 / 45.0) = - 45.0 ln (1 / 9) = 45.0 ln \( 9 \) approx 98.88 upright(" s") approx 98.9 upright(" s") $ At $t = 98.9 upright(" seconds")$, the hardware DVFS downclocking cliff triggers, forcing clock frequencies down by $60 %$ and halving inference throughput mid-mission.

- #strong[Proactive Cadence Scaling ($P_(upright("total")) arrow.r 12.0 upright(" W")$):] When firmware detects $T_j gt.eq 75.0^compose upright("C")$ and proactively expands the chunk horizon ($H = 16 arrow.r 32$) while scaling cadence ($50 upright(" Hz") arrow.r 20 upright(" Hz")$), total power drops to $12.0 upright(" W")$. The new equilibrium stabilizes at: $ T_(upright("ss")) = 45.0^compose upright("C") + \( 12.0 upright(" W") times 1.80 upright(" K/W") \) = 45.0 + 21.6 = 66.6^compose upright("C") $ This safely caps package heating well below $80.0^compose upright("C")$, completely preventing DVFS clock throttling while preserving real-time control deadlines.

=== The DVFS Downclocking Cliff
<the-dvfs-downclocking-cliff>
Compute SoCs implement hardware #strong[Dynamic Voltage and Frequency Scaling (DVFS)] thermal trip points:

+ #strong[Nominal Operating Regime ($T_j lt.eq 75^compose upright("C")$):] The accelerator operates at maximum rated boost clock frequency ($f_(upright("boost")) = 1.3 upright(" GHz")$), sustaining $50 upright(" Hz")$ diffusion policy inference ($T_(upright("infer")) = 18.0 upright(" ms")$).
+ #strong[Thermal Trip Threshold ($T_j gt.eq 85^compose upright("C")$):] To prevent permanent semiconductor lattice degradation, the SoC's hardware Power Management IC (PMIC) triggers thermal throttling, forcing the clock frequency down by $60 %$ ($f_(upright("throttle")) = 520 upright(" MHz")$).
+ #strong[The Real-Time Deadline Violation:] Downclocking causes diffusion inference latency to jump from $18.0 upright(" ms")$ to $45.0 upright(" ms")$. If the System 1.5 trajectory planner requires a new action chunk every $20.0 upright(" ms")$ ($50 upright(" Hz")$), the throttled accelerator drops half its execution frames, starving the $1000 upright(" Hz")$ real-time trajectory queue. Moving mass coasts unguided through missing spline segments, triggering emergency safety stops.

To avoid the DVFS downclocking cliff, Physical AI systems implement #strong[Proactive Thermal Cadence Scaling]:

$ T_j gt.eq 75^compose upright("C") arrow.r.double.long upright("Reduce Cadence from ") 50 upright(" Hz ") \( H = 16 \) arrow.r 20 upright(" Hz ") \( H = 32 \) $

By doubling the action chunk horizon from $H = 16$ to $H = 32$, the policy halves its forward inference rate ($50 upright(" Hz") arrow.r 20 upright(" Hz")$), cutting dynamic switching power ($P_(upright("dynamic"))$) by $60 %$. Silicon junction temperature stabilizes below $78^compose upright("C")$, maintaining continuous, deterministic real-time control without tripping hardware thermal downclocking.

#block[
- #strong[The Common Assumption:] Benchmarking an edge compute SoC on an open laboratory bench at $22^compose upright("C")$ ambient temperature proves that a 25 W neural workload is thermally viable for production robotics.
- #strong[The Physical Reality:] Silicon thermal dynamics follow a first-order differential response governed by thermal resistance $R_(upright("th"))$ and thermal capacitance $C_(upright("th"))$ ($tau_(upright("th")) = R_(upright("th")) C_(upright("th")) approx 45 upright(" s")$). Short bench tests evaluate only the transient thermal capacitance phase, failing to reach steady-state junction equilibrium ($T_(upright("ss")) = T_(upright("ambient")) + P_(upright("total")) R_(upright("th"))$). In an IP67-sealed chassis operating in a $45^compose upright("C")$ ambient warehouse, sustained inference at $P_(upright("total")) = 25 upright(" W")$ drives $T_(upright("ss"))$ to $90^compose upright("C")$, triggering hardware DVFS downclocking at $t approx 99 upright(" s")$. The sudden $60 %$ drop in clock frequency doubles inference latency and starves control loops mid-operation.
- #strong[The Systems Invariant:] Embedded thermal envelopes must be validated under continuous worst-case steady-state equilibrium ($t gt.eq 5 tau_(upright("th"))$). Firmware must implement Proactive Thermal Cadence Scaling ($T_j gt.eq 75^compose upright("C") arrow.r.double.long 50 upright(" Hz") arrow.r 20 upright(" Hz")$) to stabilize junction temperatures below the DVFS downclocking cliff.

