The authority engine defines four mutually exclusive operating states:

+ #strong[#NormalTok("MANUAL"); State:] The human operator holds exclusive control authority through physical joysticks, pendant controllers, or direct teleoperation master arms. Autonomous neural proposals are completely ignored, though low-level safety barriers (such as dynamic stopping distance clamps $d_(upright("stop"))$) remain active to prevent operator error.
+ #strong[#NormalTok("SHARED_ADMITTANCE"); State:] The human and the autonomous policy jointly guide the mechanism. The robot behaves as a virtual mass-spring-damper system, complying with physical forces exerted by a human collaborator while the autonomous policy provides gentle feedforward trajectory guidance.
+ #strong[#NormalTok("AUTONOMOUS"); State:] The autonomous neural policy (System 1.5 Action Chunking and System 1 CBF-QPs) holds primary trajectory execution authority, subject to continuous Expiring Intent Leases ($cal(L)_(upright("intent"))$) and real-time clearance invariants.
+ #strong[#NormalTok("ESTOP"); State:] The system is in an un-resettable safety hold. Gate drive power to motor inverters is physically severed via optocoupler-isolated Safe Torque Off (STO) lines, and mechanical brakes are clamped.

=== Admittance Dynamics in Shared Authority
<admittance-dynamics-in-shared-authority>
When operating in the #NormalTok("SHARED_ADMITTANCE"); state, the robot must not fight the human operator with stiff position control. Instead, the bare-metal controller enforces second-order admittance dynamics:

$ upright(bold(M))_d \( dot.double(upright(bold(x))) - dot.double(upright(bold(x)))_d \) + upright(bold(D))_d \( dot(upright(bold(x))) - dot(upright(bold(x)))_d \) + upright(bold(K))_d \( upright(bold(x)) - upright(bold(x))_d \) = upright(bold(F))_(upright("human")) $

where $upright(bold(x)) \, dot(upright(bold(x))) \, dot.double(upright(bold(x))) in bb(R)^6$ are the actual Cartesian tool pose, velocity, and acceleration vectors (in meters and radians); $upright(bold(x))_d \, dot(upright(bold(x)))_d \, dot.double(upright(bold(x)))_d in bb(R)^6$ are the reference trajectory and derivatives proposed by the autonomous policy; $upright(bold(M))_d in bb(R)^(6 times 6)$ is the desired virtual inertia matrix ($upright("kg")$ and $upright("kg") dot.op upright("m")^2$); $upright(bold(D))_d in bb(R)^(6 times 6)$ is the desired virtual damping matrix ($upright("N") dot.op upright("s/m")$ and $upright("N") dot.op upright("m") dot.op upright("s/rad")$); $upright(bold(K))_d in bb(R)^(6 times 6)$ is the desired virtual stiffness matrix ($upright("N/m")$ and $upright("N") dot.op upright("m/rad")$); and $upright(bold(F))_(upright("human")) in bb(R)^6$ is the external contact wrench measured in real time by a 6-axis wrist force-torque sensor.

To see how admittance transforms a heavy industrial machine into a compliant, feather-light mechanism, consider a collaborative 6-DoF manufacturing cell. The virtual parameters along the tool translation axis are configured to a light virtual mass $M_d = 2.0 upright(" kg")$, virtual damping $D_d = 40.0 upright(" N") dot.op upright("s/m")$, and virtual stiffness $K_d = 0.0 upright(" N/m")$ (pure admittance float). When a human technician exerts an external guidance force of $F_(upright("human")) = 20.0 upright(" N")$ along the tool axis, the initial instantaneous tool acceleration is $dot.double(x) \( 0 \) = F_(upright("human")) / M_d = frac(20.0 upright(" N"), 2.0 upright(" kg")) = 10.0 upright(" m/s")^2$. As velocity builds, the virtual damping term $upright(bold(D))_d dot(upright(bold(x)))$ opposes the motion until acceleration reaches zero ($dot.double(x) arrow.r 0$), settling into a steady-state compliance velocity of $v_(upright("ss")) = F_(upright("human")) / D_d = frac(20.0 upright(" N"), 40.0 upright(" N") dot.op upright("s/m")) = 0.50 upright(" m/s")$. This allows the human operator to guide a massive 50 kg industrial robot smoothly and effortlessly with one hand, while the autonomous policy provides subtle virtual fixture constraints.

=== The Preemption Privilege Hierarchy and Hardware Containment
<the-preemption-privilege-hierarchy-and-hardware-containment>
To guarantee safety under all operating conditions, the state machine enforces a strict #strong[Preemption Privilege Hierarchy]:

$ upright("Preemption Order:") quad upright("ESTOP") succ upright("MANUAL") succ upright("SHARED_ADMITTANCE") succ upright("AUTONOMOUS") $

Any assertion of an emergency stop line immediately forces the system into #NormalTok("ESTOP"); in $< 5 thin mu upright("s")$. If an operator touches a capacitive dead-man switch or moves a joystick beyond a certified deadband threshold ($parallel upright(bold(u))_(upright("human")) parallel > epsilon.alt_(upright("deadband"))$), the microcontroller executes an instantaneous hardware preemption, revoking autonomous policy authority and transitioning to #NormalTok("MANUAL"); or #NormalTok("SHARED"); mode.

To guard against software lockups, the bare-metal MCU and the Linux host processor exchange bidirectional #strong[Dual-Heartbeat Watchdogs] at a strict $20 upright(" ms")$ period. If the Linux host fails to refresh its heartbeat before the timer expires, the MCU automatically initiates a staged safety fallback: escalating from a Category 2 trajectory hold (zero-velocity stop along current path) to a Category 1 controlled deceleration profile, and finally to a Category 0 hardware Safe Torque Off if dynamic clearance is breached @iec60204.

- #strong[For Maya (CS/ML):] The state machine establishes a deterministic boundary around learned models. The neural network never directly controls motor voltages; it issues candidate trajectory proposals that are continuously gated by the MCU authority engine.
- #strong[For Carlos (ECE/Embedded):] The $20 upright(" ms")$ dual-heartbeat watchdog guarantees that even if a Linux kernel panic or GPU driver hang occurs, the bare-metal MCU safely catches the fault within a single control frame.
- #strong[For Alex (MechE/Robotics):] Admittance control transforms rigid high-ratio planetary gearboxes into compliant, human-safe virtual springs and dampers.

== $cal(C)^2$ Bumpless Transfer Filtering: Eliminating Mechanical Takeover Shock
<sec-bumpless-transfer>
When an operator seizes control from an autonomous policy during high-speed motion, the commanded control input abruptly switches from the autonomous policy's output $upright(bold(u))_(upright("policy")) \( t \)$ to the human operator's command $upright(bold(u))_(upright("human")) \( t \)$.

To understand the physical danger of this transition, consider an automotive transmission. If a driver drops a mechanical clutch instantaneously at high engine RPM, angular momentum dumps across the drivetrain in an infinitesimal fraction of a second. The resulting shock wave shears drive pins, strips transmission gear teeth, and snaps half-shafts. Modern dual-clutch transmissions avoid this destruction by using electronically modulated hydraulic pressure curves to blend torque continuously across a 150 ms transition envelope.

In robotic actuation, an unblended discrete switch ($Delta upright(bold(u)) = upright(bold(u))_(upright("human")) - upright(bold(u))_(upright("policy"))$) acts like an instantaneous clutch dump: the commanded motor torque experiences an infinite rate of change ($dot(bold(tau)) arrow.r oo$) (#ref(<fig-bumpless-transfer>, supplement: [Figure])). In physical hardware, this step discontinuity induces violent mechanical jerk ($accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) arrow.r oo$), trips inverter overcurrent fault registers, and generates destructive dynamic shear forces across precision cycloidal and harmonic gearbox gear teeth.

