=== The Mathematics of $cal(C)^2$ Continuity
<the-mathematics-of-mathcalc2-continuity>
To eliminate mechanical takeover shock, Physical AI implements #strong[$cal(C)^2$ Smoothstep Bumpless Transfer Filtering]. When an intervention is triggered at time $t_(upright("takeover"))$, the microcontroller blends the two control signals over a calibrated transition window $T_(upright("blend")) in \[ 100 \, 200 \] upright(" ms")$:

$ upright(bold(u))_(upright("cmd")) \( t \) = \( 1 - S \( alpha \) \) dot.op upright(bold(u))_(upright("policy")) \( t \) + S \( alpha \) dot.op upright(bold(u))_(upright("human")) \( t \) $

$ alpha \( t \) = frac(t - t_(upright("takeover")), T_(upright("blend"))) in \[ 0 \, 1 \] $

where $S \( alpha \)$ is the 5th-order (quintic) smoothstep polynomial:

$ S \( alpha \) = 6 alpha^5 - 15 alpha^4 + 10 alpha^3 $

Evaluating the first and second time-derivatives of the blending polynomial confirms:

$ dot(S) \( alpha \) = 30 alpha^4 - 60 alpha^3 + 30 alpha^2 = 30 alpha^2 \( 1 - alpha \)^2 arrow.r.double.long dot(S) \( 0 \) = 0 \, quad dot(S) \( 1 \) = 0 $

$ dot.double(S) \( alpha \) = 120 alpha^3 - 180 alpha^2 + 60 alpha = 60 alpha \( 1 - alpha \) \( 1 - 2 alpha \) arrow.r.double.long dot.double(S) \( 0 \) = 0 \, quad dot.double(S) \( 1 \) = 0 $

Because both the first derivative (velocity / torque-rate) $dot(S)$ and the second derivative (acceleration / torque-acceleration) $dot.double(S)$ evaluate to exactly zero at both boundary endpoints ($alpha = 0$ and $alpha = 1$), the transition is guaranteed to be #strong[$cal(C)^2$-continuous].

=== Why Linear ($cal(C)^0$) and Cubic ($cal(C)^1$) Blending Fail
<why-linear-mathcalc0-and-cubic-mathcalc1-blending-fail>
To appreciate why quintic $cal(C)^2$ smoothing is physically necessary, consider what happens with simpler blending functions:

+ #strong[Linear Blending ($cal(C)^0$):] Ramping $alpha \( t \) = t \/ T_(upright("blend"))$ yields a constant slope $dot(alpha) = 1 \/ T_(upright("blend"))$. While position and torque are continuous, the first derivative of torque jumps discontinuously from $0$ to $frac(Delta tau, T_(upright("blend")))$ at $t = 0$. Taking the derivative of this step produces a Dirac delta impulse in torque acceleration ($dot.double(upright(bold(u))) arrow.r oo$), exciting drivetrain resonance modes ($20 upright("–") 80 upright(" Hz")$) and producing audible acoustic ringing.
+ #strong[Cubic Smoothstep ($cal(C)^1$):] Using $S_3 \( alpha \) = 3 alpha^2 - 2 alpha^3$ achieves $dot(S)_3 \( 0 \) = dot(S)_3 \( 1 \) = 0$. However, its second derivative evaluates to $dot.double(S)_3 \( alpha \) = 6 - 12 alpha$. At the moment takeover begins ($alpha = 0$), $dot.double(S)_3 \( 0 \) = 6 \/ T_(upright("blend"))^2 eq.not 0$. This non-zero second derivative injects an instantaneous step change in acceleration at $t_(upright("takeover"))$, creating a mechanical jerk discontinuity that vibrates robot linkages and causes vehicle suspension bounce.

=== Derivation of Peak Slew Rate and Numerical Verification
<derivation-of-peak-slew-rate-and-numerical-verification>
Evaluating the maximum time-derivative of the commanded control vector:

$ dot(upright(bold(u)))_(upright("cmd")) \( t \) = frac(d S, d alpha) frac(d alpha, d t) \( upright(bold(u))_(upright("human")) - upright(bold(u))_(upright("policy")) \) = frac(30 alpha^2 \( 1 - alpha \)^2, T_(upright("blend"))) Delta upright(bold(u)) $

The maximum of $alpha^2 \( 1 - alpha \)^2$ occurs at the midpoint $alpha = 0.5$, where $\( 0.5 \)^2 \( 0.5 \)^2 = 0.0625 = 1 / 16$. Thus, $max dot(S) \( alpha \) = 30 times 1 / 16 = 15 / 8 = 1.875$, and the peak rate of torque change evaluates to:

$ parallel dot(bold(tau)) parallel_(upright("max")) = 15 / 8 frac(Delta tau, T_(upright("blend"))) $

To see the physical protection provided by quintic smoothing on bench hardware, consider an emergency teleoperation override on a heavy manipulator arm experiencing a torque step discrepancy of $Delta tau = 60.0 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m")$ across a calibrated transition window of $T_(upright("blend")) = 150.0 upright(" ms")$ ($0.15 upright(" s")$). The peak rate of torque change evaluates to $parallel dot(bold(tau)) parallel_(upright("max")) = 1.875 times frac(60.0 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m"), 0.15 upright(" s")) = 750.0 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/s")$. For a joint with reflected inertia $J = 0.8 upright(" kg") dot.op upright("m")^2$, the raw mechanical jerk evaluates to $parallel accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) parallel_(upright("max")) = frac(750.0 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/s"), 0.8 upright(" kg") dot.op upright("m")^2) = 937.5 upright(" rad/s")^3$, which the low-level trajectory generator safely clamps to the certified mechanical bound of $parallel accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) parallel lt.eq 25.0 upright(" rad/s")^3$. In sharp contrast, executing an unblended discrete step across a single $1 upright(" ms")$ control tick ($Delta t = 0.001 upright(" s")$) produces an unmitigated shock spike of $parallel dot(bold(tau)) parallel_(upright("unblended")) = frac(60.0 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m"), 0.001 upright(" s")) = 60 \, 000 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/s")$---an $80 times$ surge that instantly trips inverter gate overcurrent registers and subjects precision cycloidal gear teeth to destructive shear stresses.

