== The Policy Endogeneity Trap: Why Retraining on Emergency Interventions Causes Collapse
<sec-policy-endogeneity>
In classical web-scale machine learning, deploying a "data flywheel" is straightforward: user interactions are logged passively, aggregated in cloud data lakes, and used to fine-tune foundation models via supervised behavioral cloning:

$ cal(L)_(upright("BC")) \( theta \) = bb(E)_(\( s_t \, a_t^(*) \) tilde.op cal(D)) [∥pi_theta \( s_t \) - a_t^(*)∥_2^2] $

In Physical AI, this passive flywheel design triggers a fatal systemic failure mode known as the #strong[Policy Endogeneity Trap] (#ref(<fig-policy-flywheel>, supplement: [Figure])).

To grasp why endogeneity destroys learning in physical systems, consider the acoustic feedback screech that occurs when a live microphone is placed in front of its own loudspeaker. The microphone picks up the speaker's amplified output, feeds it back into the amplifier, and within milliseconds the audio loop saturates into a deafening, uncontrollable howl.

In Physical AI, an embodied agent operates inside a physical closed loop where every motor command actively mutates the environment and dictates the future sensory distribution ($s_t arrow.r^(pi_theta) a_t arrow.r^(upright("Physics")) s_(t + 1)$). When an autonomous policy drifts dangerously off-course and a human safety driver grabs the physical controls to swerve away from a wall, an undemarcated telemetry logger records both the dangerous drift and the human evasive swerve as a single continuous expert demonstration. Retraining on this closed feedback loop teaches the neural network that approaching a wall at high speed is the necessary causal precondition that triggers a turn. The data flywheel enters an algorithmic feedback screech: the retrained policy actively accelerates toward obstacles because it was trained on its own un-demarcated failure-recovery cycle.

