== Systems Stress-Tests: Architectural Food for Thought
<sec-governance-stresstests>
Evaluating human-machine authority arbitration and governed telemetry engines across physical embodiments requires confronting high-stakes tensions between mechanical transmission limits, causal learning integrity, and hardware fault tolerance. The following four architectural stress-tests examine critical edge cases across runtime governance:

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 1: Takeover Transition Duration ($T_(upright("blend"))$) vs.~Clearance Enclosure Margin]
<stress-test-1-takeover-transition-duration-t_textblend-vs.-clearance-enclosure-margin>
An engineering team is configuring the authority takeover parameters for a $1 \, 200 upright(" kg")$ autonomous mobile forklift operating in narrow warehouse aisles ($v = 2.0 upright(" m/s")$, obstacle clearance $d_(upright("clearance")) = 0.80 upright(" m")$).

The team faces an architectural dilemma:

+ #strong[Short Blending Window ($T_(upright("blend")) = 20 upright(" ms")$):] The human operator's evasive steering command takes full effect almost instantaneously ($Delta t = 20 upright(" ms")$), enabling rapid obstacle avoidance with minimal unguided drift ($d_(upright("drift")) lt.eq 0.04 upright(" m")$). However, the peak torque rate jumps to $parallel dot(bold(tau)) parallel_(upright("max")) = 5 \, 625 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/s")$, exceeding the transmission dynamic rating by $4.6 times$ and risking structural pin shear.
+ #strong[Long Blending Window ($T_(upright("blend")) = 250 upright(" ms")$):] Quintic $cal(C)^2$ filtering guarantees torque rates remain below $450 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/s")$, perfectly protecting gearboxes and suppressing chassis rocking. However, during the $250 upright(" ms")$ handoff, the vehicle travels $0.50 upright(" m")$ along the compromised autonomous vector, consuming $62.5 %$ of the total available clearance margin before the human's evasive maneuver takes full authority.

#emph[How should systems engineers mathematically couple the bumpless blending window $T_(upright("blend"))$ to real-time lidar stopping clearance ($d_(upright("clearance")) \( t \)$) and vehicle momentum? If an imminent collision envelope violates $d_(upright("clearance")) < v dot.op T_(upright("blend"))$, under what formal criteria should the governance engine bypass $cal(C)^2$ smoothing and command an immediate Category 0 STO trip?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 2: Emergency Intervention Poisoning in Continuous Self-Supervised Retraining]
<stress-test-2-emergency-intervention-poisoning-in-continuous-self-supervised-retraining>
A commercial fleet of 50 collaborative robotic assembly arms performs precision peg-in-hole insertion ($100 thin mu upright("m")$ clearance). When parts are misaligned or grasp slip occurs, technicians seize the robot tool-tip and manually guide the peg into the socket.

The fleet telemetry pipeline uploads all continuous joint positions, motor torques, and wrist camera video to a cloud GPU cluster for daily policy retraining:

+ #strong[Pipeline A (Naive Unbroken Ingestion):] Ingests all recorded telemetry as successful human demonstrations. Over three weeks, insertion success rates degrade from $96 %$ to $68 %$. The retrained policy actively wiggles pegs aggressively against chamfer edges at high force before inserting, because it learned that contact jamming is the necessary causal precursor that triggers alignment.
+ #strong[Pipeline B (DAgger Episode Slicing):] Excises pre-takeover contact sequences ($t_(upright("takeover")) - 1.5 upright(" s") lt.eq t < t_(upright("takeover"))$) into failure datasets ($cal(D)_(upright("failure"))$), and trains the policy only on isolated human recovery segments ($cal(D)_(upright("recovery"))$). However, because human demonstrations start from high-contact jammed states ($s tilde.op d^(pi_(upright("drift")))$), the policy never learns how to approach the hole smoothly from nominal free-space configurations.

#emph[How must the data flywheel pipeline mathematically balance nominal demonstrations ($cal(D)_(upright("nominal"))$) and DAgger recovery segments ($cal(D)_(upright("recovery"))$) in the loss objective to eliminate the Policy Endogeneity Trap without introducing distribution shift during nominal free-space approach?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 3: Telemetry Ring Buffer Sizing Under Asynchronous Bus Saturation]
<stress-test-3-telemetry-ring-buffer-sizing-under-asynchronous-bus-saturation>
A multi-axis surgical robot records continuous tamper-evident flight-recorder logs across 7 joints at $100 upright(" Hz")$. Each frame contains 64-bit IEEE 1588 PTP timestamps, joint kinematics, commanded motor currents, and a SHA-256 hash chaining to the previous frame ($256 upright(" bytes/frame")$, $25.6 upright(" KB/s")$ sustained stream).

Telemetry frames are written from bare-metal SRAM to a shared LPDDR5 DRAM ring buffer via DMA, where a background Linux logging daemon writes them to an onboard NVMe SSD:

- During standard operations, the NVMe write latency is $< 2.0 upright(" ms")$.
- During flash block garbage collection or heavy OS thread preemption, NVMe write latency spikes to $450 upright(" ms")$.
- If the DRAM telemetry ring buffer fills during the stall, the system must choose between:
  - #strong[Option 1 (Drop Telemetry Frames):] Drops telemetry frames to preserve real-time control execution, breaking the cryptographic SHA-256 hash chain and forfeiting forensic non-repudiation during post-incident investigations.
  - #strong[Option 2 (Block Real-Time Producer):] Stalls the real-time control thread until buffer space is freed, guaranteeing complete cryptographic logs but causing the 1 kHz safety enforcer to miss its hard real-time interrupt deadline.

#emph[How must systems architects size lock-free SRAM ring buffers, configure memory bus AXI QoS priorities, and design non-volatile storage controllers to guarantee both $100 %$ cryptographic hash chain continuity and zero deadline misses for hard real-time motor control loops?]

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Stress-Test 4: Dual-Bank A/B Firmware Rollback Deadlines During Field OTA Updates]
<stress-test-4-dual-bank-ab-firmware-rollback-deadlines-during-field-ota-updates>
A fleet of 200 autonomous mobile delivery robots receives an over-the-air (OTA) firmware update to their low-level microcontroller safety executive. The MCU utilizes dual-bank flash memory (Bank A: Active Golden Image, Bank B: Target Update Partition).

Upon rebooting into Bank B, the hardware bootloader arms a hardware watchdog timer with a timeout of $T_(upright("watchdog")) = 50 upright(" ms")$. The newly booted firmware must initialize hardware peripherals, verify SRAM memory checksums, and successfully exchange its first bidirectional heartbeat with the Linux host processor before clearing the watchdog line.

Under nominal conditions, firmware startup completes in $18 upright(" ms")$. However, in 12 field robots operating in sub-zero winter temperatures ($- 20^compose upright("C")$), external crystal oscillator startup latency is prolonged by $38 upright(" ms")$, causing total boot time to reach $56 upright(" ms")$. The hardware watchdog trips at $50 upright(" ms")$, triggering an automatic rollback to Bank A. On the subsequent boot into Bank A, the cold crystal again takes $56 upright(" ms")$, tripping the watchdog again and locking the 12 robots into an infinite reboot loop.

#emph[How must embedded systems architects design multi-tier bootloader watchdog protocols, thermal-aware startup timing margins, and non-volatile boot attempt counters to guarantee fail-safe rollback capabilities without inducing infinite reboot lockouts in extreme physical environments?]

== Fallacies and Pitfalls in Runtime Governance and Data Flywheels
<sec-governance-fallacies>
Deploying governed physical systems requires dismantling software-centric assumptions regarding human interventions, fail-safe architectures, and data ingestion pipelines.

#block[
#strong[The Seductive Intuition:] Teleoperation demonstrations and human emergency interventions recorded by expert operators are inherently optimal and safe, and can be directly ingested into supervised behavioral cloning pipelines without pre-processing.

#strong[The Physical Reality:] Human operators exhibit non-negligible neuromuscular reaction latency ($200 upright("–") 400 upright(" ms")$), physical hand tremors, and panic over-corrections. During emergency interventions, human inputs represent high-gain evasive maneuvers initiated from deep out-of-distribution states, not steady-state optimal demonstrations. Retraining behavioral cloning models on un-demarcated intervention logs causes the policy to learn spurious causal dependencies between dangerous boundary states and corrective swerves, leading directly to the Policy Endogeneity Trap and fleet-wide crashes @ross2011reduction.

#strong[The Systems Invariant:] Automated data flywheels must never treat raw intervention logs as unbroken expert demonstrations; telemetry ingestion pipelines must mathematically decouple pre-takeover failure trajectories ($cal(D)_(upright("failure"))$) from post-takeover recovery maneuvers ($cal(D)_(upright("recovery_dagger"))$).

] #label("callout-fallacy*-71")
#block[
#strong[The Seductive Intuition:] Mapping a keyboard shortcut, UI button, or ROS2 topic to an emergency stop routine provides adequate safety assurance for experimental and production robotic platforms.

#strong[The Physical Reality:] Software-based stop routines depend on healthy OS thread scheduling, intact memory crossbars, functioning device drivers, and non-congested communication buses. If a Linux kernel enters a deadlock, an NPU memory leak freezes the PCIe bus, or an unhandled exception hangs the runtime, software-level stop commands will never reach the motor inverters.

#strong[The Systems Invariant:] Certified physical safety requires hardwired, optocoupler-isolated #strong[Safe Torque Off (STO)] circuits that physically cut gate-drive power to inverter power MOSFETs and clamp mechanical holding brakes in $< 5 thin mu upright("s")$ @iec61800@iso13849.

] #label("callout-fallacy*-72")
