=== Rung 1: Historical Replay Testing
<rung-1-historical-replay-testing>
The candidate policy is evaluated against millions of frames of historical fleet telemetry recorded across previous real-world operations. This open-loop evaluation verifies regression accuracy, confirming that model updates maintain behavioral parity on known edge cases, obstacle maneuvers, and emergency takeover scenarios:

- #strong[Mobility (Rover):] Replaying thousands of recorded sidewalk hours, verifying that the candidate policy matches golden human expert steering and braking on pedestrian crosswalks (Action $upright("MSE") lt.eq 0.015 upright(" rad")$).
- #strong[Manipulation (Arm):] Replaying high-speed component insertions, confirming that predicted end-effector grasp trajectories do not drift into fixturing walls.
- #strong[Cybernetics (Inverter):] Replaying historical power grid frequency excursions, verifying that neural droop controllers maintain stable phase synchronization.
- #strong[Persona Lens (Maya):] Maya tracks counterfactual action drift and validation loss curves. While Rung 1 cannot evaluate closed-loop compounding errors, it acts as a fast, zero-risk continuous integration filter.

=== Rung 2: Physics-Based Domain Randomization
<rung-2-physics-based-domain-randomization>
The candidate policy is placed in closed-loop control within high-fidelity physics simulators. Physical parameters are randomized across broad distributions: link mass ($plus.minus 20 %$), surface friction ($mu in \[ 0.20 \, 1.20 \]$), ambient lighting ($10 upright("–") 50 \, 000 upright(" lux")$), and sensor noise:

- #strong[Mobility:] Simulating slippery ice ($mu = 0.15$), wet grass ($mu = 0.35$), and dry asphalt ($mu = 0.85$) with randomized obstacle velocities across $10 \, 000$ episodes.
- #strong[Manipulation:] Randomizing peg-in-hole contact friction, surface restitution, joint backlash ($0.1 upright("–") 0.5 upright(" mm")$), and workpiece mass distribution.
- #strong[Cybernetics:] Injecting wide impedance variations and multi-generator grid faults in real-time electromagnetic transient (EMT) models.
- #strong[Persona Lens (Alex):] Alex verifies that the policy's control manifold is robust against parametric variation in dynamic models and does not rely on unrealistically sharp contact friction spikes.

=== Rung 3: Hardware-in-the-Loop (HIL) Silicon & Dynamometer Emulation
<rung-3-hardware-in-the-loop-hil-silicon-dynamometer-emulation>
The candidate policy binary is flashed onto the exact target System-on-Chip (SoC) and microcontroller hardware mounted on high-power dynamometer test benches. Hardware timers, memory crossbars, DMA channels, and motor gate drivers execute in physical real time against microsecond FPGA plant emulators:

- #strong[Mobility:] The rover's Jetson Orin and Cortex-M7 drive physical wheel hub motors coupled to opposing load dynamometers that electronically simulate road rolling resistance and sudden hydroplaning.
- #strong[Manipulation:] Industrial arm joint actuators are mounted to dynamometer load cells that physically emulate contact reaction forces, revealing motor thermal saturation and harmonic drive torque ripple.
- #strong[Cybernetics:] Inverter control boards connect to real-time OPAL-RT or Typhoon HIL power grid emulators, subjecting gate drivers to microsecond grid voltage sags and switching spikes.
- #strong[Persona Lens (Carlos):] Carlos instruments hardware logic analyzers and AXI trace units to confirm that real-time safety ISRs execute within $upright("WCET") lt.eq 175 thin mu upright("s")$ under maximum LPDDR5 memory crossbar load.

=== Rung 4: Shadow Fleet Deployment
<rung-4-shadow-fleet-deployment>
The candidate build is deployed to physical machines operating in real-world environments in passive shadow mode. The policy ingests live sensor feeds and decodes trajectory chunks, but its outputs are routed to telemetry logging buffers rather than motor gate drivers:

- #strong[Mobility:] Candidate rover policies predict path trajectories in live urban sidewalks while the certified baseline controller actively drives the chassis.
- #strong[Manipulation:] Candidate assembly policies decode multi-step action chunks alongside production assembly lines without physical tool actuation.
- #strong[Cybernetics:] Candidate neural inverter controllers evaluate optimal Volt-VAR dispatch in real-time substations while certified classical PLLs govern physical power flow.
- #strong[Persona Synthesis:] Maya, Carlos, and Alex inspect real-world disagreement distributions: when candidate proposals deviate by $> 5 %$ from certified baselines or suggest actions violating Control Barrier Functions, telemetry triggers automated issue logs before actuation authority is ever granted.

