== Claims-Arguments-Evidence Framework and the Safety Case Architecture
<claims-arguments-evidence-framework-and-the-safety-case-architecture>
Release qualification culminates in the synthesis of a formal #strong[Claims-Arguments-Evidence (CAE)] Safety Assurance Case (#ref(<fig-cae-safety-case>, supplement: [Figure])) @leveson2011engineering.

In civil engineering, a dam or suspension bridge is not certified based solely on median river flow or average daily wind speeds. Civil engineers must guarantee structural survival against the 100-year flood or the 1-in-a-million extreme storm gust. Because an engineering team cannot wait 10,000 years to observe tail weather events, they record empirical high-water exceedances and fit rigorous statistical tail distributions to extrapolate extreme return levels.

In commercial aviation, civil airworthiness standards (such as FAA DO-178C Level A and ARP4754A) mandate that the probability of a catastrophic failure condition leading to hull loss must be less than $10^(- 9)$ per flight hour. Yet accumulating $10^9$ empirical flight hours would require continuously operating an aircraft for more than $114 \, 000$ years. Aviation certification does not rely on impossible brute-force logging; it relies on a structured #strong[Safety Assurance Case] that rigorously proves top-level claims through deterministic hardware isolation, formal mathematical proofs, and empirical test ledgers.

