=== Extreme Value Theory (EVT) and Peak-Over-Threshold Reliability Proofs
<extreme-value-theory-evt-and-peak-over-threshold-reliability-proofs>
In safety-critical physical systems, standard Gaussian or Student-$t$ statistical distributions systematically underestimate tail risk. Real-world collisions, memory bus stalls, and mechanical over-torque events occur at the extreme, fat tail of the physical distribution ($P_99.9999$).

To rigorously prove that the probability of catastrophic hazard occurrence satisfies the top-level safety claim ($P \( upright("Hazard") \) < 10^(- 6) upright(" / hour")$), Physical AI applies #strong[Extreme Value Theory (EVT)] using the #strong[Peak-Over-Threshold (POT)] methodology @coles2001introduction.

Let $X_1 \, X_2 \, dots.h \, X_N$ be a sequence of independent and identically distributed physical measurements (such as sense-to-actuation latency $tau_(upright("delay"))$, tracking error $parallel upright(bold(e)) parallel$, or stopping clearance violation $Delta d$) with continuous distribution function $F \( x \)$. Given a high safety threshold $u in bb(R)$, define the threshold exceedance $Y = X - u$ for $X > u$.

==== The Pickands-Balkema-de Haan Theorem
<the-pickands-balkema-de-haan-theorem>
The fundamental theorem of Extreme Value Theory states that for a broad class of underlying physical distributions $F \( x \)$, as threshold $u$ approaches the upper physical endpoint $x_F$, the conditional excess distribution $F_u \( y \) = P \( X - u lt.eq y divides X > u \)$ converges uniformly to the #strong[Generalized Pareto Distribution (GPD)]:

$ lim_(u arrow.r x_F) sup_(0 lt.eq y < x_F - u) lr(|F_u \( y \) - G \( y \; sigma \, xi \)|) = 0 $

where the Generalized Pareto cumulative distribution function $G \( y \; sigma \, xi \)$ is defined by:

$ G \( y \; sigma \, xi \) = cases(delim: "{", 1 - (1 + frac(xi y, sigma))^(- 1 \/ xi) & upright("for ") xi eq.not 0, 1 - exp (- y / sigma) & upright("for ") xi = 0) $

parameterized by scale parameter $sigma > 0$ and shape parameter (tail index) $xi in bb(R)$:

- #strong[$xi > 0$ (Heavy-Tailed / Fréchet):] Models non-deterministic systems with operating system scheduling stalls and memory crossbar contention where tail latencies decay polynomially.
- #strong[$xi = 0$ (Exponential / Gumbel):] Models memoryless random processes.
- #strong[$xi < 0$ (Bounded / Weibull):] Models hard real-time silicon with deterministic physical upper limits.

==== Derivation of the High-Quantile Safety Return Level
<derivation-of-the-high-quantile-safety-return-level>
Let $N_u = sum_(i = 1)^N bb(I) \( X_i > u \)$ denote the number of recorded exceedances above threshold $u$ in a sample of size $N$. Inverting the cumulative Generalized Pareto tail model yields the #strong[High-Quantile Safety Return Level] $x_p$:

$ underbrace(x_p, upright("Extreme Tail Return Level")) = underbrace(u, upright("High Safety Threshold")) #h(0em) + #h(0em) underbrace(sigma / xi [(N / N_u p)^(- xi) - 1], upright("Generalized Pareto Tail Extrapolation")) $

==== Numerical Calculation of Per-Cycle Failure Probability ($p_(upright("cycle"))$)
<numerical-calculation-of-per-cycle-failure-probability-p_textcycle>
For an embodied physical agent operating at real-time control cadence $f_s = 1000 upright(" Hz")$, one operating hour contains:

$ N_(upright("hour")) = 3 \, 600 upright(" s/hr") times 1000 upright(" cycles/s") = 3.6 times 10^6 upright(" cycles") $

To satisfy the top-level safety claim $P \( upright("Hazard") \) < 10^(- 6) upright(" per operating hour")$, the allowable per-cycle hazard probability $p_(upright("cycle"))$ evaluates to:

$ p_(upright("cycle")) = frac(P \( upright("Hazard") \), N_(upright("hour"))) = frac(10^(- 6), 3.6 times 10^6) = 2.78 times 10^(- 13) $

Directly verifying a failure rate of $2.78 times 10^(- 13)$ via brute-force empirical testing would require executing more than $10^13$ cycles---equivalent to over $317$ years of continuous hardware testing.

Extreme Value Theory provides the mathematically rigorous foundation to extrapolate tail risk from $N = 100 \, 000$ Hardware-in-the-Loop test cycles ($27.8 upright(" hours")$ of testing). By setting threshold $u$ at the $99.9 upright("th")$ percentile ($N_u = 100$ tail exceedances) and estimating parameters $\( sigma \, xi \)$, we calculate the extreme return level $x_(p_(upright("cycle")))$, proving that worst-case physical clearance margins or latencies remain strictly bounded within safe limits.

=== Distribution-Free Perception Guarantees: Conformal Prediction
<distribution-free-perception-guarantees-conformal-prediction>
To ensure that learned spatial tokens fed into the safety case possess rigorous statistical validity, Physical AI complements EVT with #strong[Conformal Prediction] @vovk2005algorithmic. Rather than relying on uncalibrated heuristic confidence scores from deep neural networks, conformal prediction provides exact, distribution-free finite-sample coverage guarantees:

$ P (upright(bold(y))_(n + 1) in hat(cal(C))_(1 - alpha) \( upright(bold(x))_(n + 1) \)) gt.eq 1 - alpha $

where $upright(bold(x))_(n + 1)$ is a new camera observation, $upright(bold(y))_(n + 1)$ is the true physical bounding volume or 3D affordance position, and $hat(cal(C))_(1 - alpha)$ is a calibrated prediction set constructed such that the true state is contained with user-specified confidence $1 - alpha$ (for instance, $99.9 %$). If environmental lighting degradation causes the conformal prediction volume $hat(cal(C))_(1 - alpha)$ to expand beyond available physical clearance margins ($d_(upright("clearance"))$), the system immediately detects epistemic uncertainty saturation and commands a proactive velocity derate.

=== System-Theoretic Process Analysis (STPA) Formalism
<system-theoretic-process-analysis-stpa-formalism>
To identify all causal mechanisms that could violate Claim 1.0, Physical AI integrates #strong[System-Theoretic Process Analysis (STPA)] @leveson2011engineering@leveson2012stpa. STPA treats safety not as a component reliability problem, but as a hierarchical dynamic control problem.

+ #strong[System Losses ($cal(L)$):]
  - $L_1$: Human fatality or bodily injury.
  - $L_2$: Structural damage to the machine or customer environment.
  - $L_3$: Critical mission failure or grid electrical blackout.
+ #strong[System Hazards ($cal(H)$):] A system hazard $H_k = chevron.l cal(S)_k \, cal(C)_k chevron.r$ is an unsafe internal state $cal(S)_k subset cal(X)$ combined with worst-case environmental context $cal(C)_k$ that leads to system loss:
  - $H_1$: Robot moving mass overruns clearance envelope ($d_(upright("clearance")) < d_(upright("stop"))$) $arrow.r.double.long L_1 \, L_2$.
  - $H_2$: Manipulator exerts excessive contact force ($F_(upright("contact")) > 200 upright(" N")$) on fixture $arrow.r.double.long L_2$.
  - $H_3$: Inverter phase current exceeds thermal demagnetization limit ($I_(upright("phase")) > I_(upright("max"))$) $arrow.r.double.long L_2 \, L_3$.
+ #strong[Hierarchical Control Graph ($cal(G) = \( cal(V) \, cal(E) \)$):] Modeling interactions between Cloud ($V_4$), Host MPU ($V_3$), Edge NPU ($V_2$), Bare-Metal MCU ($V_1$), Actuators, and Plant.
+ #strong[The Four Canonical Classes of Unsafe Control Actions (UCAs):] Every control action $upright(bold(u))_j$ emitted across the hierarchy is audited across four failure modes: $ upright("UCA")_(j \, 1) \( upright(bold(x)) \, t \) & = \( upright(bold(u))_j \( t \) = nothing \) and cal(P)_(upright("req")) \( upright(bold(x)) \( t \) \) quad &  & \( upright("Control Action Not Provided when needed") \)\
  upright("UCA")_(j \, 2) \( upright(bold(x)) \, upright(bold(u)) \, t \) & = \( upright(bold(u))_j \( t \) = upright(bold(u))_(upright("unsafe")) \) and cal(P)_(upright("hazard")) \( upright(bold(x)) \( t \) \, upright(bold(u))_j \) quad &  & \( upright("Unsafe Action Provided") \)\
  upright("UCA")_(j \, 3) \( upright(bold(x)) \, upright(bold(u)) \, t \) & = \( upright(bold(u))_j \( t \) eq.not nothing \) and \( t < t_(upright("early")) or t > t_(upright("late")) \) quad &  & \( upright("Action Too Early, Too Late, or Wrong Order") \)\
  upright("UCA")_(j \, 4) \( upright(bold(x)) \, upright(bold(u)) \, Delta t \) & = \( Delta t < Delta t_(upright("min")) or Delta t > Delta t_(upright("max")) \) quad &  & \( upright("Action Stopped Too Soon or Applied Too Long") \) $

Every identified $upright("UCA")_(j \, k)$ is mapped directly to a deterministic, hardware-isolated Control Barrier Function guard or timer interrupt in static SRAM (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");), guaranteeing that unsafe control proposals are vetoed before reaching physical gate drivers.

