== Autonomous Fleets and Swarms
<sec-fleet-swarms>
When Physical AI expands from single isolated machines to large-scale multi-agent deployments (such as autonomous transport fleets, robotic warehousing swarms, agricultural combines, and smart electrified microgrids), the systems architecture broadens into #strong[Distributed Embodied Intelligence].

Consider the physical reality of a busy logistics warehouse where 50 automated guided vehicles (AGVs) transport heavy pallet loads across crossing aisles. The physical space is surrounded by tall steel storage racks that reflect and attenuate radio signals, creating severe radio-frequency (RF) multipath fading and localized signal shadows. As two 400 kg AGVs approach a blind intersection at $1.8 upright(" m/s")$, they exchange position estimates and trajectory intents over a wireless mesh. If radio packets drop or network latency spikes from $15 upright(" ms")$ to $200 upright(" ms")$, neither vehicle can halt instantaneously. Moving mass carries momentum ($upright(bold(p)) = m upright(bold(v))$), and waiting for an acknowledgment packet while coasting forward blind erodes safe braking margins.

Scaling to multi-agent fleets introduces three major systems challenges:

+ #strong[Distributed State Estimation under Band-Limited RF Communication:] In dense warehouse fleets, robots must share dynamic obstacle maps and cooperative intent without saturating wireless spectrum. Fleet architectures deploy distributed factor graph smoothing and spatial covariance consensus, exchanging compact 3D bounding hulls and expiring trajectory corridors ($cal(L)_(upright("corridor"))$) rather than raw point clouds or video streams.

+ #strong[Multi-Agent Collision Avoidance via Decentralized Control Barrier Functions:] When multiple autonomous agents operate in shared workspaces, their safety barrier certificates become coupled across the wireless network. To guarantee mutual clearance without a centralized arbiter, each agent solves a local Quadratic Program on its onboard microcontroller, evaluating pairwise #strong[Decentralized Control Barrier Functions (D-CBFs)] of the form $L_f h_(i j) \( upright(bold(x))_i \, hat(upright(bold(x)))_j \) + L_(g_i) h_(i j) upright(bold(u))_i + L_(g_j) h_(i j) hat(upright(bold(u)))_j + alpha \( h_(i j) \) gt.eq 0$, where $hat(upright(bold(x)))_j$ and $hat(upright(bold(u)))_j$ represent the neighbor's state and policy received over the wireless mesh. Under network delay and packet drops, agents dynamically inflate their pairwise safety barrier radius ($R_(i j)^(upright("net"))$):

$ R_(i j)^(upright("net")) \( tau_(upright("net")) \) = r_i + r_j + d_(upright("margin")) + v_(upright("rel")) tau_(upright("net")) + 1 / 2 a_(upright("max")) tau_(upright("net"))^2 $

where $r_i$ and $r_j$ are the physical vehicle radii, $d_(upright("margin"))$ is the static safety buffer, $v_(upright("rel")) = parallel upright(bold(v))_i - upright(bold(v))_j parallel$ is the relative closing speed, $a_(upright("max"))$ is the maximum braking deceleration, and $tau_(upright("net"))$ is the measured one-way communication latency.

Consider two autonomous transport AGVs approaching an intersection, each with a physical footprint radius of $r_i = r_j = 0.40 upright(" m")$, a static clearance buffer of $d_(upright("margin")) = 0.20 upright(" m")$, a relative closing speed of $v_(upright("rel")) = 3.60 upright(" m/s")$ ($1.80 upright(" m/s")$ per vehicle), and a maximum emergency deceleration of $a_(upright("max")) = 3.0 upright(" m/s")^2$. Under nominal wireless mesh conditions with a communication latency of $tau_(upright("net")) = 50.0 upright(" ms")$, the linear drift displacement is $3.60 times 0.050 = 0.180 upright(" m")$ and the acceleration term is $0.0038 upright(" m")$, yielding a dynamic safety radius of $R_(i j)^(upright("net")) = 1.18 upright(" m")$. When steel storage racks induce RF multipath congestion and network latency spikes to $tau_(upright("net")) = 200.0 upright(" ms")$, the unguided closing displacement expands to $0.720 upright(" m")$ and the second-order term reaches $0.060 upright(" m")$. The total required safety radius inflates to $R_(i j)^(upright("net")) = 1.78 upright(" m")$, representing a $51 %$ expansion. The local microcontroller QP enforcer senses the delayed packet timestamps, expands the barrier boundary, and smoothly initiates early braking before either vehicle reaches the blind corner.

