== Physical Foundation Scaling Laws
<sec-scaling-laws>
In natural language processing and computer vision behind glass, empirical scaling laws dictate that model performance improves predictably as a power-law of compute FLOPs ($C$), parameter count ($N$), and dataset token volume ($D$):

$ L \( N \, D \) approx (N_c / N)^(alpha_N) + (D_c / D)^(alpha_D) $

In Physical AI, scaling confronts the physical nature of time, mass, and energy. While internet text tokens can be harvested in trillions ($> #h(-1em) 10^13$ tokens) at near-zero marginal cost, physical robot data is fundamentally bounded by the tick of real time ($1 upright(" hour of physical interaction") = 3 \, 600 upright(" seconds")$). Every action token represents a motor command that generates heat in copper coils, stresses cycloidal gear reducers, and drains batteries.

The physical world imposes three fundamental constraints that challenge naive parameter scaling:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("        DISEMBODIED AI (Text / Pixels)         PHYSICAL AI (Mass / Matter)");],
[#NormalTok("     ┌────────────────────────────────┐     ┌────────────────────────────────┐");],
[#NormalTok("     │ • Trillions of web tokens      │     │ • 1 hour = 3,600 physical sec  │");],
[#NormalTok("     │ • Zero cost per sample         │     │ • Gearbox wear & thermal limits│");],
[#NormalTok("     │ • Arbitrary token permutations │     │ • Strict SE(3) Euclidean laws  │");],
[#NormalTok("     │ • Scale = compute + parameters │     │ • Scale = sample efficiency    │");],
[#NormalTok("     └────────────────────────────────┘     └────────────────────────────────┘");],));
+ #strong[The Physical Sample Complexity Bottleneck:] Digital text tokens are cheap, abundant, and virtual. In contrast, physical robot interaction hours are expensive, slow, and mechanically bounded. A commercial fleet of 100 industrial robot arms operating 16 hours per day at a $10 upright(" Hz")$ trajectory logging cadence generates $100 times 16 times 3 \, 600 times 365 times 10 = 2.10 times 10^10$ action tokens per year. While 21 billion tokens represents a large robotics dataset, it constitutes less than $0.15 %$ of the token volume found in web-scale text corpora (exceeding $1.5 times 10^13$ tokens), while incurring millions of dollars in electrical power, actuator wear, and physical fixturing maintenance.

+ #strong[The Geometry of Invariance:] Unlike linguistic tokens, physical space possesses strict Euclidean geometric symmetries governed by the Special Euclidean Lie group $S E \( 3 \) = S O \( 3 \) times.l bb(R)^3$. Physical models that incorporate explicit $S E \( 3 \)$-equivariant neural backbones satisfy the symmetry law:

$ f \( g dot.op upright(bold(x)) \) = g dot.op f \( upright(bold(x)) \) quad forall g in S E \( 3 \) $

By embedding $S E \( 3 \)$ equivariance directly into neural policy architectures, a robotic manipulation task requiring $5 \, 000$ real-world teleoperation demonstrations under an unconstrained transformer converges in just $50$ demonstrations under an $S E \( 3 \)$-equivariant diffusion policy, representing a $100 times$ reduction in physical training time and hardware wear.

