#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[Mathematical and Physical Foundations for Systems Engineers]
<mathematical-and-physical-foundations-for-systems-engineers>
This appendix provides a self-contained, systems-oriented reference for the mathematical and control-theoretic formulations used across this book.

For systems, embedded, and software engineers, mathematical formulas in Physical AI are not abstract analytical exercises; they are the #strong[specifications for real-time silicon execution]. Every equation maps directly into memory access patterns, execution cycle budgets ($upright("WCET")$), numerical precision requirements, and physical safety bounds.

#horizontalrule

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[1. Kinematics and Stopping Envelopes]
<kinematics-and-stopping-envelopes>
#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[The Physical Derivation of Stopping Distance]
<the-physical-derivation-of-stopping-distance>
When an embodied robot maneuvers at linear velocity $v \( t \)$, the distance required to bring its physical mass to a complete, controlled stop consists of two decoupled components:

+ #strong[Reaction Drift ($d_(upright("drift"))$):] The distance traversed while sensory signals propagate through transduction, bus serialization, neural inference, and communication queues before the actuators can apply braking torque.
+ #strong[Kinematic Braking ($d_(upright("brake"))$):] The distance traversed while physical friction or counter-electromotive braking forces decelerate the moving mass at maximum safe deceleration $a_(upright("max"))$.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("|<----------------------- Total Stopping Distance d_stop ------------------------>|");],
[#NormalTok("|                                                |                                 |");],
[#NormalTok("|  Reaction Drift (d_drift = v * tau_latency)    |  Kinematic Braking (v^2 / 2a)   |");],
[#NormalTok("|===============================================>|================================>|");],
[#NormalTok("t = 0 (Hazard Appears)                     t = tau (Brakes Engage)          t = t_stop (V=0)");],));
Integrating Newtonian dynamics under constant deceleration $a \( t \) = - a_(upright("max"))$:

$ v \( t \) = v_0 - a_(upright("max")) t arrow.r.double.long t_(upright("brake")) = v_0 / a_(upright("max")) $

$ d_(upright("brake")) = integral_0^(t_(upright("brake"))) \( v_0 - a_(upright("max")) t \) thin d t = v_0 t_(upright("brake")) - 1 / 2 a_(upright("max")) t_(upright("brake"))^2 = frac(v_0^2, 2 a_(upright("max"))) $

Accounting for total sensorimotor transport latency $tau_(upright("latency"))$, the #strong[Total Dynamic Stopping Envelope] is:

$ d_(upright("stop")) \( v \) = underbrace(v dot.op tau_(upright("latency")), upright("Reaction Drift")) + underbrace(frac(v^2, 2 a_(upright("max"))), upright("Kinematic Braking")) $

where: \* $v$: Current operational velocity ($upright("m/s")$). \* $tau_(upright("latency"))$: Total end-to-end transport delay ($upright("s")$), encompassing camera exposure, bus serialization (CAN-FD/PCIe), operating system scheduling tail latency, and inverter current rise time. \* $a_(upright("max"))$: Maximum permissible physical braking deceleration ($upright("m/s")^2$), constrained by tire friction coefficient ($mu g$), payload tipping limits, or motor peak torque.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Parabolic Safe Velocity Clamping]
<parabolic-safe-velocity-clamping>
Given a measured physical clearance $d_(upright("clearance"))$ to an obstacle and a safety margin $d_(upright("margin"))$, the maximum safe operational velocity $v_(upright("safe"))$ is derived by solving the quadratic inequality $d_(upright("stop")) \( v \) lt.eq d_(upright("clearance")) - d_(upright("margin"))$:

$ frac(1, 2 a_(upright("max"))) v^2 + tau_(upright("latency")) v - \( d_(upright("clearance")) - d_(upright("margin")) \) lt.eq 0 $

Applying the quadratic formula and selecting the positive root:

$ v_(upright("safe")) = sqrt(a_(upright("max"))^2 tau_(upright("latency"))^2 + 2 a_(upright("max")) \( d_(upright("clearance")) - d_(upright("margin")) \)) - a_(upright("max")) tau_(upright("latency")) $

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Microcontroller Execution Complexity and Timing Bounds]
<microcontroller-execution-complexity-and-timing-bounds>
In real-time firmware, evaluating $v_(upright("safe"))$ and $d_(upright("stop")) \( v \)$ executes at $1000 upright(" Hz")$ in static SRAM with zero dynamic memory allocation (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");). Because the formulation requires only a single square-root operation and basic floating-point arithmetic, its Worst-Case Execution Time (WCET) on a $480 upright(" MHz")$ ARM Cortex-M7 with a hardware Floating Point Unit (FPU) is bounded to $upright("WCET") lt.eq 1.2 thin mu upright("s")$, consuming less than $0.15 %$ of the $1.0 upright(" ms")$ control interval.

#horizontalrule

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[2. Spatial Geometry and Lie Algebra]
<spatial-geometry-and-lie-algebra>
Physical AI agents operate in three-dimensional Euclidean space $bb(R)^3$. To transform sensor observations, object affordances, and robot kinematics across coordinate frames, systems rely on the Special Euclidean Group $S E \( 3 \)$.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Homogeneous Transformation Matrices]
<homogeneous-transformation-matrices>
A rigid-body transformation from coordinate frame $A$ to coordinate frame $B$ consists of a 3D rotation $upright(bold(R))_A^B in S O \( 3 \)$ and a 3D translation $upright(bold(p))_A^B in bb(R)^3$, unified into a $4 times 4$ homogeneous matrix $upright(bold(T))_A^B in S E \( 3 \)$:

$ upright(bold(T))_A^B = mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(R))_A^B, upright(bold(p))_A^B; upright(bold(0))_(1 times 3), 1) in bb(R)^(4 times 4) $

Points in 3D space are transformed using homogeneous coordinates $tilde(upright(bold(x))) = \[ upright(bold(x))^T \, 1 \]^T$:

$ tilde(upright(bold(x)))_B = upright(bold(T))_A^B tilde(upright(bold(x)))_A arrow.r.double.long mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(x))_B; 1) = mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(R))_A^B upright(bold(x))_A + upright(bold(p))_A^B; 1) $

Matrix inversion in $S E \( 3 \)$ has a closed-form structure that avoids general $4 times 4$ Gaussian elimination:

$ \( upright(bold(T))_A^B \)^(- 1) = upright(bold(T))_B^A = mat(delim: "[", \( upright(bold(R))_A^B \)^T, - \( upright(bold(R))_A^B \)^T upright(bold(p))_A^B; upright(bold(0))_(1 times 3), 1) $

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Quaternions for Singularity-Free Rotations]
<quaternions-for-singularity-free-rotations>
While Euler angles (Roll, Pitch, Yaw) suffer from kinematic singularities (#strong[Gimbal Lock] when pitch approaches $plus.minus 90^compose$, causing division by zero in coordinate transformations), unit quaternions provide a continuous, computationally robust 4-parameter representation:

$ upright(bold(q)) = mat(delim: "[", w; x; y; z) = mat(delim: "[", cos \( theta \/ 2 \); upright(bold(u)) sin \( theta \/ 2 \)) \, quad parallel upright(bold(q)) parallel_2 = w^2 + x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1 $

where $upright(bold(u)) in bb(R)^3$ is the unit rotation axis and $theta$ is the rotation angle.

The #strong[Hamilton Quaternion Product] ($upright(bold(q))_c = upright(bold(q))_a times.circle upright(bold(q))_b$) compounds successive 3D rotations:

