$ upright(bold(P))_(k \| k) = \( upright(bold(I)) - upright(bold(K))_k upright(bold(H))_k \) upright(bold(P))_(k \| k - 1) \( upright(bold(I)) - upright(bold(K))_k upright(bold(H))_k \)^T + upright(bold(K))_k upright(bold(R))_k upright(bold(K))_k^T $

#strong[Mathematical Proof of Global Stability:] Let $upright(bold(y)) in bb(R)^n$ be an arbitrary non-zero test vector. The quadratic form evaluates to:

$ upright(bold(y))^T upright(bold(P))_(k \| k) upright(bold(y)) = upright(bold(y))^T \( upright(bold(I)) - upright(bold(K))_k upright(bold(H))_k \) upright(bold(P))_(k \| k - 1) \( upright(bold(I)) - upright(bold(K))_k upright(bold(H))_k \)^T upright(bold(y)) + upright(bold(y))^T upright(bold(K))_k upright(bold(R))_k upright(bold(K))_k^T upright(bold(y)) $

Defining transformed vectors $upright(bold(u))_1 = \( upright(bold(I)) - upright(bold(K))_k upright(bold(H))_k \)^T upright(bold(y))$ and $upright(bold(u))_2 = upright(bold(K))_k^T upright(bold(y))$:

$ upright(bold(y))^T upright(bold(P))_(k \| k) upright(bold(y)) = upright(bold(u))_1^T upright(bold(P))_(k \| k - 1) upright(bold(u))_1 + upright(bold(u))_2^T upright(bold(R))_k upright(bold(u))_2 $

Because $upright(bold(P))_(k \| k - 1) succ.curly.eq 0$ and $upright(bold(R))_k succ 0$:

$ upright(bold(u))_1^T upright(bold(P))_(k \| k - 1) upright(bold(u))_1 gt.eq 0 \, #h(2em) upright(bold(u))_2^T upright(bold(R))_k upright(bold(u))_2 gt.eq 0 arrow.r.double.long upright(bold(y))^T upright(bold(P))_(k \| k) upright(bold(y)) gt.eq 0 quad forall upright(bold(y)) in bb(R)^n $

Thus, $upright(bold(P))_(k \| k)$ is strictly positive semi-definite ($upright(bold(P))_(k \| k) succ.curly.eq 0$) regardless of floating-point truncation errors or suboptimal gain matrices.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Mahalanobis Innovation Gating]
<mahalanobis-innovation-gating>
The measurement innovation $tilde(upright(bold(y)))_k = upright(bold(z))_k - h \( hat(upright(bold(x)))_(k \| k - 1) \)$ represents the new information introduced by the sensor. Under Gaussian assumptions, $tilde(upright(bold(y)))_k tilde.op cal(N) \( upright(bold(0)) \, upright(bold(S))_k \)$.

The squared Mahalanobis distance scales the innovation residual by its expected uncertainty:

$ d_M^2 = tilde(upright(bold(y)))_k^T upright(bold(S))_k^(- 1) tilde(upright(bold(y)))_k tilde.op chi_m^2 $

where $m = dim \( upright(bold(z))_k \)$. The gating threshold is evaluated against the cumulative Chi-Square distribution:

$ upright("Decision") = cases(delim: "{", upright("Accept Measurement and Apply Update") \, & d_M^2 lt.eq chi_(m \, 1 - alpha)^2, , upright("Reject Outlier and Propagate Open-Loop") \, & d_M^2 > chi_(m \, 1 - alpha)^2) $

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Real-Time Kalman Filter Determinism and Complexity]
<real-time-kalman-filter-determinism-and-complexity>
In embedded state estimation pipelines, the Continuous-Discrete EKF executes on dedicated microcontroller hardware or real-time DSPs in static DTCM SRAM with zero dynamic memory allocation (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");).

For a 6-dimensional kinematic state ($n = 6$) with 3-dimensional position observations ($m = 3$), the propagation step requires $O \( n^3 \)$ operations ($216$ multiply-accumulates), and the Joseph-form measurement update requires matrix inversion of the $3 times 3$ innovation covariance $upright(bold(S))_k$, followed by outer product updates $upright(bold(P))_k = \( upright(bold(I)) - upright(bold(K))_k upright(bold(H))_k \) upright(bold(P))_k^(-) \( upright(bold(I)) - upright(bold(K))_k upright(bold(H))_k \)^T + upright(bold(K))_k upright(bold(R))_k upright(bold(K))_k^T$.

On an ARM Cortex-M7 with hardware SIMD and single-precision FPU, the complete propagation and Joseph update cycle executes in under $18.5 thin mu upright("s")$ ($upright("WCET") lt.eq 25.0 thin mu upright("s")$), ensuring that state estimates and uncertainty bounds are updated with deterministic microsecond precision before feeding downstream Control Barrier Functions.

