#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[5. Field-Oriented Motor Control]
<field-oriented-motor-control>
To translate high-level torque commands into physical electromagnetic forces, brushless permanent-magnet synchronous motors (PMSM) rely on #strong[Field-Oriented Control (FOC)] executing in dedicated hardware timer peripherals at $20 upright(" kHz")$ ($Delta t = 50 thin mu upright("s")$).

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Clarke Transformation]
<clarke-transformation>
Transforms balanced three-phase stator currents $\( i_a \, i_b \, i_c \)$ into a stationary two-phase orthogonal coordinate system:

$ mat(delim: "[", i_alpha; i_beta) = 2 / 3 mat(delim: "[", 1, - 1 / 2, - 1 / 2; 0, sqrt(3) / 2, - sqrt(3) / 2) mat(delim: "[", i_a; i_b; i_c) $

Using the zero-sum property $i_a + i_b + i_c = 0$, this simplifies to:

$ i_alpha = i_a \, quad i_beta = 1 / sqrt(3) \( i_a + 2 i_b \) $

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Park Transformation]
<park-transformation>
Rotates the stationary $\( alpha \, beta \)$ currents into the rotor's rotating reference frame using the electrical rotor angle $theta_e = P dot.op theta_(upright("mech"))$ (where $P$ is the motor pole-pair count):

$ mat(delim: "[", i_d; i_q) = mat(delim: "[", cos theta_e, sin theta_e; - sin theta_e, cos theta_e) mat(delim: "[", i_alpha; i_beta) $

- #strong[Direct-Axis Current ($i_d$):] Aligns parallel with the rotor permanent magnet flux. Setting $i_d^(*) = 0$ eliminates wasteful radial forces and maximizes electrical efficiency.
- #strong[Quadrature-Axis Current ($i_q$):] Aligns $90^compose$ electrical ahead of rotor flux, producing pure orthogonal electromagnetic torque:

$ tau_(upright("motor")) = 3 / 2 P lambda_(upright("pm")) i_q = K_t dot.op i_q $

where $lambda_(upright("pm"))$ is the permanent magnet flux linkage ($upright("Wb")$) and $K_t$ is the motor torque constant ($upright("N") dot.op upright("m/A")$).

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Thermal Energy Accumulator]
<thermal-energy-accumulator>
To prevent motor stator insulation breakdown ($T > 155^compose upright("C")$), the motor driver integrates Joulean thermal dissipation in real time:

$ E_(upright("thermal")) \( t \) = integral_0^t I_(upright("phase"))^2 \( tau \) R_(upright("stator")) thin d tau $

When $E_(upright("thermal"))$ exceeds the continuous thermal rating $E_(upright("continuous"))$, the controller linearly derates peak torque current $i_q^(upright("limit")) arrow.l i_q^(upright("nominal")) dot.op (1 - frac(T_j - 85^compose upright("C"), 155^compose upright("C") - 85^compose upright("C")))$.

#horizontalrule

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[6. Frequency-Domain Stability and Phase Margin]
<frequency-domain-stability-and-phase-margin>
In classical digital control, converting continuous commands to discrete hardware outputs introduces uncompensated phase lag that erodes the system's stability margin.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Zero-Order Hold (ZOH) Phase Lag]
<zero-order-hold-zoh-phase-lag>
A digital-to-analog converter holding a commanded setpoint constant over sample period $T$ is modeled by the Zero-Order Hold transfer function:

$ G_(upright("ZOH")) \( s \) = frac(1 - e^(- s T), s) $

Evaluating its frequency response $s = j omega$:

$ G_(upright("ZOH")) \( j omega \) = T dot.op upright("sinc") (frac(omega T, 2)) e^(- j frac(omega T, 2)) $

The magnitude attenuation is $lr(|upright("sinc") (frac(omega T, 2))|)$, while the #strong[exact phase lag] is:

$ Delta phi.alt_(upright("ZOH")) = - frac(omega T, 2) quad \( upright("radians") \) = - omega dot.op T / 2 dot.op 180^compose / pi quad \( upright("degrees") \) $

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[The Phase Margin Stability Criterion]
<the-phase-margin-stability-criterion>
Let $omega_c$ be the open-loop gain crossover frequency ($parallel G \( j omega_c \) parallel = 1$) of the physical mechanism, and $upright("PM")_(upright("nominal"))$ be the nominal phase margin. Total sensorimotor transport delay $tau_(upright("delay")) = tau_(upright("compute")) + 1 / 2 T_(upright("sample"))$ introduces phase lag:

$ Delta phi.alt = - omega_c dot.op tau_(upright("delay")) dot.op 180^compose / pi $

The effective closed-loop phase margin is:

$ upright("PM")_(upright("effective")) = upright("PM")_(upright("nominal")) - omega_c tau_(upright("delay")) dot.op 180^compose / pi $

- #strong[Stability Limit:] If $tau_(upright("delay")) gt.eq upright("PM")_(upright("nominal")) / omega_c dot.op pi / 180^compose$, then $upright("PM")_(upright("effective")) lt.eq 0^compose$. Stabilizing negative feedback flips into positive feedback, triggering explosive limit-cycle oscillations.

