#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[8. Camera Projection and Epipolar Geometry]
<camera-projection-and-epipolar-geometry>
In physical perception, an optical camera does not produce 3D coordinates directly. It captures a 2D projection of 3D Euclidean space onto a semiconductor photodiode grid. To transform pixel observations into metric affordance tokens in $S E \( 3 \)$ that an actuator can reach, systems rely on calibrated projective geometry, depth unprojection, and epipolar multi-view constraints.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Pinhole Projection and Intrinsic Matrix]
<pinhole-projection-and-intrinsic-matrix>
Consider a 3D point $upright(bold(P))_C = \[ X_C \, Y_C \, Z_C \]^T in bb(R)^3$ in the camera optical reference frame, where the $+ Z_C$ axis points forward along the optical axis, $+ X_C$ points to the right, and $+ Y_C$ points downward. Under ideal perspective projection, light rays pass through the optical center (pinhole aperture) and project onto the image sensor plane at focal distance $f$:

$ tilde(x) = X_C / Z_C \, quad tilde(y) = Y_C / Z_C $

where $\( tilde(x) \, tilde(y) \)$ are dimensionless normalized image plane coordinates.

To convert normalized coordinates into discrete sensor pixel indices $\( u \, v \)$, the optical projection is parameterized by the #strong[Camera Intrinsic Matrix] $upright(bold(K)) in bb(R)^(3 times 3)$:

$ upright(bold(K)) = mat(delim: "[", f_x, s, c_x; 0, f_y, c_y; 0, 0, 1) $

where: \* $f_x = f / p_w \, #h(0em) f_y = f / p_h$: Effective focal lengths expressed in pixel units, where $p_w \, p_h$ are the physical horizontal and vertical pixel pitches ($mu upright("m/pixel")$) on the CMOS silicon die. \* $\( c_x \, c_y \)$: Pixel coordinates of the #strong[principal point] (the intersection of the optical axis with the sensor array, ideally $\( W \/ 2 \, H \/ 2 \)$). \* $s$: Pixel grid skew coefficient (identically zero, $s = 0$, for modern lithographic rectangular silicon sensors).

Under zero skew ($s = 0$), the projective mapping from a 3D camera point $upright(bold(P))_C$ to homogeneous pixel coordinates $tilde(upright(bold(x))) = \[ u \, v \, 1 \]^T$ is:

$ Z_C mat(delim: "[", u; v; 1) = upright(bold(K)) upright(bold(P))_C = mat(delim: "[", f_x, 0, c_x; 0, f_y, c_y; 0, 0, 1) mat(delim: "[", X_C; Y_C; Z_C) = mat(delim: "[", f_x X_C + c_x Z_C; f_y Y_C + c_y Z_C; Z_C) $

Expanding into inhomogeneous Cartesian pixel coordinates:

$ u = f_x X_C / Z_C + c_x \, #h(2em) v = f_y Y_C / Z_C + c_y $

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Optical Ray Unprojection and Metric Cartesian Coordinates]
<optical-ray-unprojection-and-metric-cartesian-coordinates>
Because prospective projection collapses 3D rays onto a 2D plane, a single pixel coordinate $\( u \, v \)$ defines an infinite angular ray rather than a discrete physical location. Recovering the 3D metric coordinate requires unprojecting along the ray using registered orthogonal depth $Z = d \( u \, v \) in bb(R)^(+)$ (measured via active stereo, structured light, or time-of-flight).

The upper-triangular intrinsic matrix $upright(bold(K))$ has a direct closed-form inverse:

$ upright(bold(K))^(- 1) = mat(delim: "[", 1 / f_x, 0, - c_x / f_x; 0, 1 / f_y, - c_y / f_y; 0, 0, 1) $

Multiplying the homogeneous pixel vector by $upright(bold(K))^(- 1)$ reconstructs the normalized unit-depth ray vector $upright(bold(r))_(upright("cam"))$ in the camera optical frame:

$ upright(bold(r))_(upright("cam")) = upright(bold(K))^(- 1) mat(delim: "[", u; v; 1) = mat(delim: "[", frac(u - c_x, f_x); frac(v - c_y, f_y); 1) $

Scaling this ray vector by the registered depth $Z = d \( u \, v \)$ yields the 3D metric position $upright(bold(P))_C$ in the camera optical frame:

$ upright(bold(P))_C = Z dot.op upright(bold(r))_(upright("cam")) = mat(delim: "[", X_C; Y_C; Z_C) = mat(delim: "[", frac(u - c_x, f_x) Z; frac(v - c_y, f_y) Z; Z) $

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Rigid Extrinsic Transform to Robot Base Frame]
<rigid-extrinsic-transform-to-robot-base-frame>
To enable physical manipulation, navigation, or obstacle avoidance, the camera-frame point $upright(bold(P))_C$ must be transformed into the robot's stationary physical base frame $B$. Applying the extrinsic homogeneous transformation matrix $upright(bold(T))_C^B = \( upright(bold(R))_C^B \, upright(bold(p))_C^B \) in S E \( 3 \)$:

$ upright(bold(P))_B = upright(bold(R))_C^B upright(bold(P))_C + upright(bold(p))_C^B = upright(bold(R))_C^B (Z dot.op upright(bold(K))^(- 1) mat(delim: "[", u; v; 1)) + upright(bold(p))_C^B $

where $upright(bold(R))_C^B in S O \( 3 \)$ is the $3 times 3$ rotation matrix aligning the camera optical axes to the robot base coordinate axes, and $upright(bold(p))_C^B in bb(R)^3$ is the physical lever-arm displacement vector from the base origin to the optical center.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Surface Normal Estimation from Discrete Depth Gradients]
<surface-normal-estimation-from-discrete-depth-gradients>
For robotic grasping and contact mechanics, the agent requires the outward unit surface normal vector $hat(upright(bold(n))) in bb(S)^2$ at the contact target. Considering unprojected points $upright(bold(P))_B \( u \, v \)$ as a 2D parameterized surface, the tangent vectors spanning the local contact plane are given by spatial partial derivatives:

$ upright(bold(t))_u = frac(partial upright(bold(P))_B, partial u) approx frac(upright(bold(P))_B \( u + Delta u \, v \) - upright(bold(P))_B \( u - Delta u \, v \), 2 Delta u) $

$ upright(bold(t))_v = frac(partial upright(bold(P))_B, partial v) approx frac(upright(bold(P))_B \( u \, v + Delta v \) - upright(bold(P))_B \( u \, v - Delta v \), 2 Delta v) $

The outward unit normal vector $hat(upright(bold(n)))$ facing the robot sensor is computed via the normalized vector cross-product:

$ hat(upright(bold(n))) = frac(upright(bold(t))_v times upright(bold(t))_u, parallel upright(bold(t))_v times upright(bold(t))_u parallel_2) in bb(S)^2 $

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Multi-View Epipolar Geometry and Sampson Residual Metric]
<multi-view-epipolar-geometry-and-sampson-residual-metric>
When fusing multi-camera observations or tracking stereo visual odometry, corresponding pixel points across two calibrated viewpoints must satisfy geometric coplanarity.

Let $upright(bold(P)) in bb(R)^3$ be a physical 3D scene point observed by Camera 1 as $upright(bold(P))_1$ and by Camera 2 as $upright(bold(P))_2$. The two cameras are related by relative rigid-body rotation $upright(bold(R)) in S O \( 3 \)$ and baseline translation $upright(bold(t)) in bb(R)^3$, such that $upright(bold(P))_2 = upright(bold(R)) upright(bold(P))_1 + upright(bold(t))$.

The vectors $upright(bold(P))_2$, $upright(bold(t))$, and $upright(bold(R)) upright(bold(P))_1$ are coplanar. Expressing this geometric condition as a scalar triple product:

$ upright(bold(P))_2^T (upright(bold(t)) times \( upright(bold(R)) upright(bold(P))_1 \)) = 0 arrow.r.double.long upright(bold(P))_2^T \[ upright(bold(t)) \]_times upright(bold(R)) upright(bold(P))_1 = 0 $

Defining the #strong[Essential Matrix] $upright(bold(E)) in bb(R)^(3 times 3)$:

$ upright(bold(E)) = \[ upright(bold(t)) \]_times upright(bold(R)) $

Substituting the normalized image coordinates $upright(bold(x))_1 = upright(bold(K))_1^(- 1) tilde(upright(bold(x)))_1$ and $upright(bold(x))_2 = upright(bold(K))_2^(- 1) tilde(upright(bold(x)))_2$ defines the #strong[Fundamental Matrix] $upright(bold(F)) in bb(R)^(3 times 3)$:

$ tilde(upright(bold(x)))_2^T upright(bold(F)) tilde(upright(bold(x)))_1 = 0 \, #h(2em) upright(bold(F)) = upright(bold(K))_2^(- T) upright(bold(E)) upright(bold(K))_1^(- 1) = upright(bold(K))_2^(- T) \[ upright(bold(t)) \]_times upright(bold(R)) upright(bold(K))_1^(- 1) $

- #strong[Epipolar Line Invariant:] A pixel point $tilde(upright(bold(x)))_1$ in Camera 1 defines an epipolar line $upright(bold(l))_2 = upright(bold(F)) tilde(upright(bold(x)))_1$ in Camera 2 upon which the corresponding point $tilde(upright(bold(x)))_2$ must lie: $tilde(upright(bold(x)))_2^T upright(bold(l))_2 = 0$.

During active robot operation, mechanical shocks and thermal expansion degrade camera extrinsic parameters. To quantify geometric consistency without re-triangulating 3D points, the online calibration filter computes the #strong[Sampson Epipolar Error] $epsilon.alt_(upright("Sampson"))$ (the first-order geometric distance approximation from the points to their respective epipolar lines):

$ epsilon.alt_(upright("Sampson")) \( tilde(upright(bold(x)))_1 \, tilde(upright(bold(x)))_2 \) = frac(\( tilde(upright(bold(x)))_2^T upright(bold(F)) tilde(upright(bold(x)))_1 \)^2, \( upright(bold(F)) tilde(upright(bold(x)))_1 \)_1^2 + \( upright(bold(F)) tilde(upright(bold(x)))_1 \)_2^2 + \( upright(bold(F))^T tilde(upright(bold(x)))_2 \)_1^2 + \( upright(bold(F))^T tilde(upright(bold(x)))_2 \)_2^2) $

where $\( upright(bold(v)) \)_i$ denotes the $i$-th element of vector $upright(bold(v))$. If $epsilon.alt_(upright("Sampson")) > epsilon.alt_(upright("threshold"))$ across tracked feature pairs, the estimator flags extrinsic baseline drift and triggers real-time bundle adjustment.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Thermal Baseline Expansion and Triangulation Error Sensitivity]
<thermal-baseline-expansion-and-triangulation-error-sensitivity>
In stereo perception, metric depth $Z$ is triangulated from stereo disparity $d = u_1 - u_2$ across physical baseline $b = parallel upright(bold(t)) parallel$:

$ Z = frac(f dot.op b, d) $

Differentiating with respect to disparity $d$ and baseline length $b$:

$ delta Z = frac(partial Z, partial d) delta d + frac(partial Z, partial b) delta b = - frac(f dot.op b, d^2) delta d + f / d delta b = - frac(Z^2, f dot.op b) delta d + Z / b delta b $

- #strong[Quadratic Distance Sensitivity:] Triangulation depth uncertainty grows quadratically ($prop Z^2$) with distance. At short ranges ($Z = 0.5 upright(" m")$), a $0.5 upright(" px")$ disparity noise induces millimeter error; at far ranges ($Z = 5.0 upright(" m")$), the exact same noise induces tens of centimeters of spatial uncertainty.

Chassis aluminum expands under operational temperature rise $Delta T$ with thermal coefficient $alpha_(upright("Al")) approx 23 times 10^(- 6) upright(" K")^(- 1)$:

$ delta b = b_0 dot.op alpha_(upright("Al")) dot.op Delta T $

Additionally, uneven thermal expansion or motor torque vibration induces an angular baseline tilt $delta theta$, shifting feature disparity by $delta d_(upright("tilt")) = f dot.op tan \( delta theta \) approx f dot.op delta theta$. The resulting depth error is:

$ delta Z_(upright("tilt")) approx - Z^2 / b delta theta $

For a stereo camera with baseline $b = 0.20 upright(" m")$, an angular deflection of just $delta theta = 0.5^compose = 0.00873 upright(" rad")$ produces: \* At working distance $Z = 1.0 upright(" m")$: $delta Z = 1.0^2 / 0.20 times 0.00873 = 0.0436 upright(" m") approx 4.4 upright(" cm")$. \* At obstacle distance $Z = 5.0 upright(" m")$: $delta Z = 5.0^2 / 0.20 times 0.00873 = 1.091 upright(" m") approx 109.1 upright(" cm")$.

This reveals why static calibration matrices fail in physical deployment and mandates continuous online epipolar tracking.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Real-Time Metric Unprojection Complexity]
<real-time-metric-unprojection-complexity>
In embedded perception and spatial affordance engines, metric unprojection from 2D depth arrays into 3D robot base frames ($upright(bold(p))_B = upright(bold(R))_C^B upright(bold(K))^(- 1) \[ u \, v \, 1 \]^T Z + upright(bold(p))_C^B$) is implemented using pre-computed inverse focal length constants ($f_x^(- 1) \, f_y^(- 1)$) in static memory (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");).

