#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[9. Differential Kinematics and Jacobians]
<differential-kinematics-and-jacobians>
In articulated manipulation, high-level intent proposals specify desired operational-space trajectories for the end-effector ($upright(bold(x))_e in S E \( 3 \)$), while physical motors exert joint torques and velocities ($upright(bold(q)) \, dot(upright(bold(q))) in bb(R)^n$). The fundamental differential mapping between joint velocity space and Cartesian spatial twist is governed by the #strong[Geometric Jacobian].

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Forward Differential Kinematics and Geometric Jacobian Formulation]
<forward-differential-kinematics-and-geometric-jacobian-formulation>
Consider an $n$-DoF open kinematic chain composed of revolute joints with generalized joint coordinates $upright(bold(q)) = \[ q_1 \, q_2 \, dots.h \, q_n \]^T in bb(R)^n$. The position and orientation of each link frame $i in { 1 \, dots.h \, n }$ relative to the stationary base frame $0$ is computed via cumulative homogeneous transformation matrices:

$ upright(bold(T))_i^0 \( upright(bold(q)) \) = upright(bold(T))_1^0 \( q_1 \) upright(bold(T))_2^1 \( q_2 \) dots.h.c upright(bold(T))_i^(i - 1) \( q_i \) = mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(R))_i^0 \( upright(bold(q)) \), upright(bold(p))_i^0 \( upright(bold(q)) \); upright(bold(0))_(1 times 3), 1) $

where $upright(bold(R))_i^0 in S O \( 3 \)$ represents the rotation matrix and $upright(bold(p))_i^0 in bb(R)^3$ denotes the origin position of frame $i$.

The instantaneous spatial twist of the end-effector $bold(xi)_e = \[ upright(bold(v))_e^T \, bold(omega)_e^T \]^T in bb(R)^6$ (combining linear velocity $upright(bold(v))_e = dot(upright(bold(p)))_e in bb(R)^3$ and angular velocity $bold(omega)_e in bb(R)^3$) is linearly related to the joint velocity vector $dot(upright(bold(q)))$:

$ bold(xi)_e = mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(v))_e; bold(omega)_e) = upright(bold(J)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) dot(upright(bold(q))) $

where $upright(bold(J)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) in bb(R)^(6 times n)$ is the #strong[Geometric Jacobian Matrix].

For a kinematic chain consisting entirely of revolute joints, each joint $i$ contributes to the end-effector velocity by rotating about its instantaneous unit axis $upright(bold(z))_(i - 1) = upright(bold(R))_(i - 1)^0 hat(upright(bold(z)))$. The $i$-th column $upright(bold(J))_i \( upright(bold(q)) \) in bb(R)^6$ is computed using the vector cross product:

$ upright(bold(J))_i \( upright(bold(q)) \) = mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(J))_(v \, i); upright(bold(J))_(omega \, i)) = mat(delim: "[", upright(bold(z))_(i - 1) times \( upright(bold(p))_e - upright(bold(p))_(i - 1) \); upright(bold(z))_(i - 1)) $

where $upright(bold(p))_e = upright(bold(p))_n^0$ is the current position of the end-effector flange, and $upright(bold(p))_(i - 1)$ is the position of the origin of joint frame $i - 1$ expressed in the base frame.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Inverse Differential Kinematics and the Singularity Barrier]
<inverse-differential-kinematics-and-the-singularity-barrier>
To compute the joint velocities required to achieve a commanded end-effector twist $bold(xi)_(upright("cmd"))$, the system inverts the differential kinematic relationship:

$ dot(upright(bold(q))) = upright(bold(J))^dagger \( upright(bold(q)) \) bold(xi)_(upright("cmd")) $

For redundant manipulators with more joint degrees of freedom than task-space dimensions ($n > 6$), the standard minimum-norm solution uses the Moore-Penrose right pseudo-inverse:

$ upright(bold(J))^dagger \( upright(bold(q)) \) = upright(bold(J))^T \( upright(bold(J)) upright(bold(J))^T \)^(- 1) in bb(R)^(n times 6) $

When the manipulator approaches a #strong[Kinematic Singularity] (such as full arm reach extension, shoulder-wrist coaxial alignment, or wrist gimbal lock), the Jacobian matrix loses rank ($upright("rank") \( upright(bold(J)) \) < 6$). As a consequence:

$ det (upright(bold(J)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) upright(bold(J))^T \( upright(bold(q)) \)) arrow.r 0 $

The matrix $\( upright(bold(J)) upright(bold(J))^T \)$ becomes singular, and its smallest singular value collapses ($sigma_(upright("min")) \( upright(bold(J)) \) arrow.r 0$). Attempting to invert an ill-conditioned Jacobian forces the pseudo-inverse gain toward infinity ($parallel upright(bold(J))^dagger parallel arrow.r oo$). Even an infinitesimal Cartesian tracking error or commanded velocity results in unbounded joint velocity commands ($parallel dot(upright(bold(q))) parallel arrow.r oo$), causing immediate actuator phase current saturation, violent mechanical whiplash, and transmission gear shear.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Damped Least-Squares (Levenberg-Marquardt) Regularization]
<damped-least-squares-levenberg-marquardt-regularization>
To preserve numerical stability near singular configurations, real-time trajectory runtimes replace the unconstrained pseudo-inverse with #strong[Damped Least-Squares (DLS)] inversion:

$ upright(bold(J))^(*) \( upright(bold(q)) \) = upright(bold(J))^T (upright(bold(J)) upright(bold(J))^T + lambda^2 upright(bold(I))_(6 times 6))^(- 1) $

The damping parameter $lambda in bb(R)^(+)$ regularizes the inversion by reformulating the problem as an unconstrained optimization trading off tracking precision against joint velocity magnitude:

$ min_(dot(upright(bold(q)))) (parallel bold(xi)_(upright("cmd")) - upright(bold(J)) dot(upright(bold(q))) parallel_2^2 + lambda^2 parallel dot(upright(bold(q))) parallel_2^2) $

To maintain exact kinematic tracking in well-conditioned regions while providing robust damping near singularities, the runtime modulates $lambda$ dynamically based on the instantaneous Yoshikawa manipulability index $w \( upright(bold(q)) \)$:

$ lambda^2 = cases(delim: "{", 0 & upright("if ") w \( upright(bold(q)) \) gt.eq w_0, lambda_0^2 (1 - frac(w \( upright(bold(q)) \), w_0))^2 & upright("if ") w \( upright(bold(q)) \) < w_0) $

where $w_0$ is the manipulability threshold below which damping engages, and $lambda_0$ is the maximum damping factor.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Singular Value Decomposition, Manipulability Ellipsoids, and Condition Numbers]
<singular-value-decomposition-manipulability-ellipsoids-and-condition-numbers>
Applying Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) to the Jacobian matrix provides complete geometric insight into the manipulator's instantaneous dexterity:

$ upright(bold(J)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) = upright(bold(U)) bold(Sigma) upright(bold(V))^T = sum_(i = 1)^6 sigma_i upright(bold(u))_i upright(bold(v))_i^T $

where $upright(bold(U)) = \[ upright(bold(u))_1 \, dots.h \, upright(bold(u))_6 \] in bb(R)^(6 times 6)$ and $upright(bold(V)) = \[ upright(bold(v))_1 \, dots.h \, upright(bold(v))_n \] in bb(R)^(n times n)$ are orthonormal matrices spanning Cartesian twist directions and joint velocity directions, respectively, and $bold(Sigma) = \[ upright("diag") \( sigma_1 \, dots.h \, sigma_6 \) divides upright(bold(0))_(6 times \( n - 6 \)) \] in bb(R)^(6 times n)$ contains the ordered singular values $sigma_1 gt.eq sigma_2 gt.eq dots.h gt.eq sigma_6 gt.eq 0$.

Mapping a unit hypersphere in joint velocity space ($parallel dot(upright(bold(q))) parallel_2 lt.eq 1$) into Cartesian space defines the #strong[Manipulability Ellipsoid]:

$ bold(xi)^T \( upright(bold(J)) upright(bold(J))^T \)^(- 1) bold(xi) lt.eq 1 arrow.r.double.long sum_(i = 1)^6 frac(\( upright(bold(u))_i^T bold(xi) \)^2, sigma_i^2) lt.eq 1 $

The principal axes of the ellipsoid align with the eigenvectors $upright(bold(u))_i$ of $upright(bold(J)) upright(bold(J))^T$, with semi-axis lengths exactly equal to the singular values $sigma_i$.

We define two fundamental metrics for real-time safety gating:

+ #strong[Yoshikawa Manipulability Index ($w \( upright(bold(q)) \)$):] Measures the total volume of the manipulability ellipsoid:

$ w \( upright(bold(q)) \) = sqrt(det (upright(bold(J)) \( upright(bold(q)) \) upright(bold(J))^T \( upright(bold(q)) \))) = product_(i = 1)^6 sigma_i \( upright(bold(J)) \) $

When $w \( upright(bold(q)) \) arrow.r 0$, the ellipsoid collapses along at least one axis, indicating a loss of instantaneous mobility along that direction.

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 2)
+ #strong[Jacobian Condition Number ($kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \)$):] Quantifies the ratio between maximum and minimum dexterity axes:
]

$ kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \) = frac(sigma_(upright("max")) \( upright(bold(J)) \), sigma_(upright("min")) \( upright(bold(J)) \)) = sigma_1 / sigma_6 in \[ 1 \, oo \) $

- When $kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \) = 1.0$, the configuration is #strong[isotropic] (the manipulability ellipsoid is a perfect sphere, providing uniform velocity generation in all directions).
- When $kappa \( upright(bold(J)) \) gt.double 50.0$, the configuration is severely #strong[anisotropic and ill-conditioned], meaning small Cartesian velocity commands along the minor axis demand massive joint velocities.

