#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[AXI Bus Quality-of-Service Priority Arbitration]
<axi-bus-quality-of-service-priority-arbitration>
In Unified Memory Architecture (UMA) Systems-on-Chip, heterogeneous masters (CPU cores, Neural Processing Units, and direct-memory-access camera peripherals) issue concurrent read and write bursts across a shared AXI interconnect crossbar.

Let $cal(M) = { 1 \, dots.h \, M }$ denote the set of bus masters contending for memory crossbar bandwidth $B_(upright("total"))$ ($upright("GB/s")$). Each transaction from master $m in cal(M)$ carries a 4-bit Quality-of-Service priority value $q_m in { 0 \, 1 \, dots.h \, 15 }$, where $q = 15$ designates the highest real-time priority.

The effective service latency $L_m$ for a memory read request of size $S_m$ bytes from master $m$ under weighted priority arbitration is modeled by:

$ L_m = tau_(upright("arb")) + S_m / B_(upright("crossbar")) + sum_(k in cal(M) \, q_k > q_m) S_k / B_(upright("crossbar")) + bb(I) \( q_m < 15 \) dot.op tau_(upright("burst_in_flight")) $

where: \* $tau_(upright("arb"))$: Crossbar arbitration switching latency (typically $4 upright("–") 12 upright(" ns")$). \* $tau_(upright("burst_in_flight"))$: Latency penalty incurred when waiting for an ongoing atomic non-preemptible burst from a lower-priority master to conclude ($S_(upright("burst")) \/ B_(upright("crossbar"))$).

To guarantee that real-time microcontroller reads ($1000 upright(" Hz")$ safety loops) never miss control deadlines, the silicon arbiter enforces hard preemption for $q_(upright("MCU")) = 15$:

$ L_(upright("MCU")) lt.eq tau_(upright("arb")) + S_(upright("payload")) / B_(upright("crossbar")) + S_(upright("burst_max")) / B_(upright("crossbar")) lt.eq 50.0 upright(" ns") $

For background camera sensor DMA streams ($q_(upright("cam")) = 4$), transactions are admitted only during bus idle cycles when higher-priority queues ($q gt.eq 10$) are empty.

