#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[12. Authority Dynamics and Bumpless Transfer]
<authority-dynamics-and-bumpless-transfer>
In collaborative and supervisory robotics, physical control authority transfers dynamically between autonomous neural policies and human safety operators. This section formalizes the authority state space, derives $cal(C)^2$ quintic smoothstep blending polynomials, and establishes statistical bounds on policy drift under human intervention.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[The 4-State Authority Machine and Preemption Invariant]
<the-4-state-authority-machine-and-preemption-invariant>
Let $cal(S)_(upright("auth")) = { upright("MANUAL") \, upright("SHARED") \, upright("AUTONOMOUS") \, upright("ESTOP") }$ denote the set of discrete authority states. State transitions are governed by input event vector $upright(bold(e)) = \[ e_(upright("estop")) \, e_(upright("human")) \, e_(upright("lease")) \, e_(upright("cbf")) \]^T in { 0 \, 1 }^4$:

$ delta : cal(S)_(upright("auth")) times { 0 \, 1 }^4 arrow.r cal(S)_(upright("auth")) $

The state transition logic is defined by the strict priority hierarchy:

$ delta \( s \, upright(bold(e)) \) = cases(delim: "{", upright("ESTOP") & upright("if ") e_(upright("estop")) = 1, upright("MANUAL") & upright("if ") e_(upright("estop")) = 0 and e_(upright("human")) = 1, upright("SHARED") & upright("if ") e_(upright("estop")) = 0 and e_(upright("human")) = 0 and e_(upright("shared_req")) = 1, upright("AUTONOMOUS") & upright("if ") e_(upright("estop")) = 0 and e_(upright("human")) = 0 and e_(upright("lease")) = 1 and e_(upright("cbf")) = 1, upright("ESTOP") & upright("if ") s = upright("AUTONOMOUS") and \( e_(upright("lease")) = 0 or e_(upright("cbf")) = 0 \)) $

Hardware Safe Torque Off lines enforce the preemption invariant: if $e_(upright("estop")) = 1$, gate driver power is physically unlatched within $< 5.0 thin mu upright("s")$, regardless of processor state.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Derivation of the $cal(C)^2$ Quintic Smoothstep Polynomial]
<derivation-of-the-mathcalc2-quintic-smoothstep-polynomial>
When transitioning between autonomous policy command $upright(bold(u))_(upright("policy")) \( t \)$ and human operator input $upright(bold(u))_(upright("human")) \( t \)$ over transition window $T_(upright("blend")) in \[ 100 \, 200 \] upright(" ms")$, the blended command is:

$ upright(bold(u))_(upright("cmd")) \( t \) = \( 1 - S \( alpha \( t \) \) \) dot.op upright(bold(u))_(upright("policy")) \( t \) + S \( alpha \( t \) \) dot.op upright(bold(u))_(upright("human")) \( t \) $

$ alpha \( t \) = frac(t - t_(upright("takeover")), T_(upright("blend"))) \, quad alpha in \[ 0 \, 1 \] $

To eliminate torque derivative shocks ($dot(upright(bold(u))) arrow.r oo$) and mechanical jerk impulses ($accent(upright(bold(q)), ⃛) arrow.r oo$), the blending polynomial $S \( alpha \) = sum_(k = 0)^5 c_k alpha^k$ must satisfy six physical boundary conditions:

$ S \( 0 \) & = 0 \, quad & S \( 1 \) & = 1 quad &  & \( upright("Zero and Unity Amplitudes") \)\
dot(S) \( 0 \) & = 0 \, quad & dot(S) \( 1 \) & = 0 quad &  & \( upright("Zero Initial and Final Velocities") \)\
dot.double(S) \( 0 \) & = 0 \, quad & dot.double(S) \( 1 \) & = 0 quad &  & \( upright("Zero Initial and Final Accelerations") \) $

Setting $S \( 0 \) = dot(S) \( 0 \) = dot.double(S) \( 0 \) = 0$ directly forces $c_0 = c_1 = c_2 = 0$. The polynomial simplifies to:

$ S \( alpha \) = c_3 alpha^3 + c_4 alpha^4 + c_5 alpha^5 $

Applying the remaining three boundary conditions at $alpha = 1$:

$ S \( 1 \) & = c_3 + c_4 + c_5 = 1\
dot(S) \( 1 \) & = 3 c_3 + 4 c_4 + 5 c_5 = 0\
dot.double(S) \( 1 \) & = 6 c_3 + 12 c_4 + 20 c_5 = 0 $

Solving this $3 times 3$ linear system:

$ mat(delim: "[", 1, 1, 1; 3, 4, 5; 6, 12, 20) mat(delim: "[", c_3; c_4; c_5) = mat(delim: "[", 1; 0; 0) arrow.r.double.long mat(delim: "[", c_3; c_4; c_5) = mat(delim: "[", 10; - 15; 6) $

The unique $cal(C)^2$ quintic smoothstep polynomial is:

$ S \( alpha \) = 6 alpha^5 - 15 alpha^4 + 10 alpha^3 $

The peak derivative occurs at the midpoint $alpha = 0.5$:

$ dot(S)_(upright("max")) = \( 30 alpha^4 - 60 alpha^3 + 30 alpha^2 \)\|_(alpha = 0.5) = 30 \( 0.0625 \) - 60 \( 0.125 \) + 30 \( 0.25 \) = 1.875 $

The maximum commanded torque rate of change during human takeover is strictly bounded:

$ parallel dot(upright(bold(u)))_(upright("cmd")) parallel lt.eq 1.875 / T_(upright("blend")) parallel upright(bold(u))_(upright("human")) - upright(bold(u))_(upright("policy")) parallel + parallel dot(upright(bold(u)))_(upright("policy")) parallel $

For $T_(upright("blend")) = 150.0 upright(" ms")$ and a maximum torque discrepancy of $40.0 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m")$, the peak torque rate is bounded to $lt.eq 500.0 upright(" N") dot.op upright("m/s")$, well within the continuous capability of brushless motor gearboxes.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Compounding Covariate Shift Bounds Under Behavioral Cloning]
<compounding-covariate-shift-bounds-under-behavioral-cloning>
When training a policy $pi_theta$ via supervised behavioral cloning on logged human teleoperation data $cal(D) = { \( s_t \, a_t^(*) \) }$, let $epsilon.alt$ denote the expected single-step error rate under the training distribution:

$ bb(E)_(s tilde.op d_(pi^(*))) [ell \( pi_theta \( s \) \, pi^(*) \( s \) \)] lt.eq epsilon.alt $

Under autonomous closed-loop execution, small errors shift the state distribution away from the demonstration support. Following Ross, Gordon, and Bagnell (#cite(<ross2011reduction>, form: "year")), the compounding total variation distance between the expert state distribution $d_(pi^(*))$ and the learned policy distribution $d_(pi_theta)$ over horizon $T$ is bounded by:

$ D_(upright("TV")) \( d_(pi^(*)) \, d_(pi_theta) \) lt.eq 2 \( 1 - \( 1 - epsilon.alt \)^T \) lt.eq 2 epsilon.alt T $

The cumulative task loss over horizon $T$ compounds quadratically:

$ J \( pi_theta \) lt.eq J \( pi^(*) \) + epsilon.alt dot.op T^2 $

- #strong[DAgger Policy Gradient Formulation:] To prevent quadratic error compounding, dataset aggregation (DAgger) samples states from the running policy's own induced distribution $d_(pi_theta)$ and queries an expert oracle for corrections $a_t^(*) = pi^(*) \( s_t \)$. Truncating the pre-takeover drift phase prevents the policy from learning spurious associations between dangerous drift and recovery maneuvers.

