#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[13. STPA Hazard Analysis and Reliability Proofs]
<stpa-hazard-analysis-and-reliability-proofs>
In whole-system verification and safety assurance, formal safety cases require connecting top-level system claims to rigorous control-theoretic hazard analyses and statistical extreme-value reliability proofs. This section establishes the mathematical foundations of STPA hazard networks and derives tail probability bounds using the Generalized Pareto Distribution.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Formal Mathematical Formulation of STPA Hierarchical Control Structures]
<formal-mathematical-formulation-of-stpa-hierarchical-control-structures>
System-Theoretic Process Analysis (STPA) models safety as a hierarchical dynamic control problem @leveson2011engineering.

Let $cal(L) = { L_1 \, L_2 \, dots.h \, L_p }$ denote the set of unacceptable #strong[System Losses] (such as human injury, structural equipment destruction, or environmental damage).

A #strong[System Hazard] $H_k in cal(H)$ is defined as a system state or set of conditions that, together with a worst-case set of environmental conditions, leads to a system loss:

$ H_k = chevron.l cal(S)_k \, cal(C)_k chevron.r arrow.r.double.long union.big_i L_i $

where $cal(S)_k subset cal(X)$ is the unsafe physical state space and $cal(C)_k$ is the active environmental context.

The multi-rate, heterogeneous Physical AI architecture is modeled as a directed hierarchical control graph $cal(G) = \( cal(V) \, cal(E) \)$: \* Vertices $cal(V) = { upright("Cloud") \, upright("Host MPU") \, upright("Edge NPU") \, upright("Bare-Metal MCU") \, upright("Actuators") \, upright("Physical Plant") }$ represent control entities and physical substrates. \* Directed edges $cal(E) = cal(U)_(upright("ctrl")) union cal(Y)_(upright("fb"))$ represent control actions $upright(bold(u)) in cal(U)_(upright("ctrl"))$ descending the hierarchy and feedback measurements $upright(bold(y)) in cal(Y)_(upright("fb"))$ ascending the hierarchy.

Every control action $upright(bold(u))_j$ issued by controller $V_i$ is evaluated across the four canonical classes of #strong[Unsafe Control Actions (UCAs)]:

$ upright("UCA")_(j \, 1) \( upright(bold(x)) \, t \) & = \( upright(bold(u))_j \( t \) = nothing \) and cal(P)_(upright("req")) \( upright(bold(x)) \( t \) \) quad &  & \( upright("Control Action Not Provided") \)\
upright("UCA")_(j \, 2) \( upright(bold(x)) \, upright(bold(u)) \, t \) & = \( upright(bold(u))_j \( t \) = upright(bold(u))_(upright("unsafe")) \) and cal(P)_(upright("hazard")) \( upright(bold(x)) \( t \) \, upright(bold(u))_j \) quad &  & \( upright("Unsafe Action Provided") \)\
upright("UCA")_(j \, 3) \( upright(bold(x)) \, upright(bold(u)) \, t \) & = \( upright(bold(u))_j \( t \) eq.not nothing \) and \( t < t_(upright("early")) or t > t_(upright("late")) \) quad &  & \( upright("Action Too Early or Too Late") \)\
upright("UCA")_(j \, 4) \( upright(bold(x)) \, upright(bold(u)) \, Delta t \) & = \( Delta t < Delta t_(upright("min")) or Delta t > Delta t_(upright("max")) \) quad &  & \( upright("Action Stopped Too Soon or Too Long") \) $

where $cal(P) \( upright(bold(x)) \)$ denotes a Boolean predicate over continuous physical state space $cal(X)$.

The STPA safety verification contract dictates that for every identified $upright("UCA")_(j \, k)$, the system must incorporate a deterministic, hardware-isolated invariant enforcer:

$ forall upright("UCA")_(j \, k) \, quad exists cal(C)_(upright("barrier")) : quad upright("Condition") \( upright("UCA")_(j \, k) \) arrow.r.double.long upright("Trigger") \( upright("Fallback Safety Interlock") \) $

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Extreme Value Theory (EVT) and Peak-Over-Threshold Reliability Proofs]
<extreme-value-theory-evt-and-peak-over-threshold-reliability-proofs-1>
In safety-critical physical systems, nominal statistical distributions (such as Gaussian or Student-$t$ models) systematically underestimate tail risk. Catastrophic collisions occur at the extreme tail of the latency and tracking error distributions ($P_99.999$).

To rigorously prove that the probability of catastrophic hazard occurrence satisfies the top-level safety claim:

$ P \( upright("Hazard") \) < 10^(- 6) upright(" per operating hour") $

Physical AI applies #strong[Extreme Value Theory (EVT)] using the #strong[Peak-Over-Threshold (POT)] methodology @coles2001introduction.

Let $X_1 \, X_2 \, dots.h \, X_N$ be a sequence of independent and identically distributed physical error measurements (such as sense-to-actuation latency $tau_(upright("delay"))$ or spatial clearance violation $Delta d$) with underlying continuous distribution function $F \( x \)$.

Given a high threshold $u in bb(R)$, define the threshold exceedance variable $Y = X - u$ for $X > u$. The conditional excess distribution function $F_u \( y \)$ is:

$ F_u \( y \) = P \( X - u lt.eq y divides X > u \) = frac(F \( u + y \) - F \( u \), 1 - F \( u \)) $

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[The Pickands-Balkema-de Haan Theorem]
<the-pickands-balkema-de-haan-theorem-1>
The fundamental theorem of Extreme Value Theory states that for a broad class of underlying distributions $F \( x \)$, as threshold $u$ approaches the upper endpoint $x_F$, the conditional excess distribution $F_u \( y \)$ converges uniformly to the #strong[Generalized Pareto Distribution (GPD)]:

$ lim_(u arrow.r x_F) sup_(0 lt.eq y < x_F - u) lr(|F_u \( y \) - G \( y \; sigma \, xi \)|) = 0 $

where the Generalized Pareto cumulative distribution function $G \( y \; sigma \, xi \)$ is defined by:

$ G \( y \; sigma \, xi \) = cases(delim: "{", 1 - (1 + frac(xi y, sigma))^(- 1 \/ xi) & upright("for ") xi eq.not 0, 1 - exp (- y / sigma) & upright("for ") xi = 0) $

parameterized by scale parameter $sigma > 0$ and shape parameter (tail index) $xi in bb(R)$, defined on support $y gt.eq 0$ for $xi gt.eq 0$, and $0 lt.eq y lt.eq - sigma \/ xi$ for $xi < 0$.

- #strong[$xi > 0$ (Fréchet / Heavy-Tailed):] Models non-deterministic systems with operating system scheduling stalls and memory bus contention where tail latencies decay polynomially.
- #strong[$xi = 0$ (Gumbel / Exponential):] Models memoryless random processes.
- #strong[$xi < 0$ (Weibull / Bounded):] Models hard real-time systems with deterministic physical upper limits.

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Derivation of the Tail Return Level for Safety Assurance]
<derivation-of-the-tail-return-level-for-safety-assurance>
Let $N_u = sum_(i = 1)^N bb(I) \( X_i > u \)$ denote the number of recorded exceedances above threshold $u$ in a sample of size $N$. The empirical probability of exceeding the threshold is $hat(zeta)_u = N_u \/ N$.

Combining the empirical threshold probability with the Generalized Pareto model yields the cumulative tail distribution for $x > u$:

$ P \( X > x \) = P \( X > u \) dot.op P \( X - u > x - u divides X > u \) = N_u / N (1 + frac(xi \( x - u \), sigma))^(- 1 \/ xi) $

Inverting this equation yields the #strong[High-Quantile Safety Return Level] $x_p$ corresponding to extreme target failure probability $p$:

$ x_p = u + sigma / xi [(N / N_u p)^(- xi) - 1] $

For an embodied robot operating at control cadence $f_s = 1000 upright(" Hz")$, one operating hour contains $N_(upright("hour")) = 3.6 times 10^6$ execution cycles. A target hazard rate of $10^(- 6) upright(" / hour")$ corresponds to a per-cycle failure probability of:

$ p_(upright("cycle")) = frac(10^(- 6), 3.6 times 10^6) = 2.78 times 10^(- 13) $

Evaluating $x_(p_(upright("cycle")))$ on empirical Hardware-in-the-Loop fault injection datasets provides the exact mathematical upper bound on worst-case latency or stopping distance required to substantiate the Claims-Arguments-Evidence safety case.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Runtime STPA Monitor Architecture and Tail Estimation Bounds]
<runtime-stpa-monitor-architecture-and-tail-estimation-bounds>
In whole-system assurance architectures, runtime STPA monitors execute directly on the safety microcontroller in static DTCM SRAM (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");). The monitor evaluates the four Unsafe Control Action (UCA) conditions every $1000 upright(" Hz")$ tick: 1. #strong[Dynamic Clearance Violation (UCA-01):] Verified via $d_(upright("clearance")) gt.eq d_(upright("stop")) \( v \, hat(mu) \, tau_(upright("lat")) \)$. 2. #strong[Heartbeat Loss (UCA-02):] Verified via $Delta t_(upright("heartbeat")) lt.eq 35.0 upright(" ms")$. 3. #strong[Tail Latency Derate (UCA-03):] Verified via online Peak-Over-Threshold (POT) Generalized Pareto return-level tracking. 4. #strong[Trajectory Jitter (UCA-04):] Verified via continuous torque derivative bounds $parallel dot(bold(tau)) parallel lt.eq dot(tau)_(upright("max"))$.

Evaluating all four invariants executes in $< 1.2 thin mu upright("s")$, guaranteeing immediate Category 1 stopping engagement upon any safety invariant violation.

