#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[14. Decentralized Barrier Certificates]
<decentralized-barrier-certificates>
This section provides formal proofs and embedded implementations for distributed multi-agent safety certificates and geometric symmetry constraints in physical foundation models.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Multi-Agent Decentralized Control Barrier Functions (D-CBFs)]
<multi-agent-decentralized-control-barrier-functions-d-cbfs>
Consider a fleet of $N$ autonomous physical agents operating in a shared physical workspace. Each agent $i in { 1 \, dots.h \, N }$ possesses continuous control-affine dynamics:

$ dot(upright(bold(x)))_i = f_i \( upright(bold(x))_i \) + g_i \( upright(bold(x))_i \) upright(bold(u))_i $

where $upright(bold(x))_i = \[ upright(bold(p))_i^T \, upright(bold(v))_i^T \]^T in bb(R)^(n_i)$ is the agent state (position $upright(bold(p))_i in bb(R)^3$ and velocity $upright(bold(v))_i in bb(R)^3$) and $upright(bold(u))_i in cal(U)_i subset bb(R)^(m_i)$ is the control input (acceleration or motor force).

For any pair of agents $\( i \, j \)$ with $i eq.not j$, let $r_i$ and $r_j$ denote their respective physical bounding radii, and let $d_(upright("margin"))$ be a safety buffer. The minimum required physical separation is $R_(i j) = r_i + r_j + d_(upright("margin"))$. The pairwise spatial barrier certificate function $h_(i j) \( upright(bold(x))_i \, upright(bold(x))_j \) : bb(R)^(n_i) times bb(R)^(n_j) arrow.r bb(R)$ is defined as:

$ h_(i j) \( upright(bold(x))_i \, upright(bold(x))_j \) = parallel upright(bold(p))_i - upright(bold(p))_j parallel_2^2 - R_(i j)^2 $

The pairwise collision-free safe set is $cal(C)_(i j) = { \( upright(bold(x))_i \, upright(bold(x))_j \) divides h_(i j) \( upright(bold(x))_i \, upright(bold(x))_j \) gt.eq 0 }$. The collective fleet safe operating set $cal(C)_(upright("fleet"))$ is the intersection across all pairs:

$ cal(C)_(upright("fleet")) = sect.big_(i = 1)^N sect.big_(j > i)^N cal(C)_(i j) $

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Reciprocating Collision Avoidance and Decentralized Safety Guarantees]
<reciprocating-collision-avoidance-and-decentralized-safety-guarantees>
Differentiating $h_(i j) \( upright(bold(x))_i \, upright(bold(x))_j \)$ along the joint trajectories yields:

$ dot(h)_(i j) \( upright(bold(x))_i \, upright(bold(x))_j \, upright(bold(u))_i \, upright(bold(u))_j \) = 2 \( upright(bold(p))_i - upright(bold(p))_j \)^T \( upright(bold(v))_i - upright(bold(v))_j \) = nabla_(upright(bold(x))_i) h_(i j) (f_i \( upright(bold(x))_i \) + g_i \( upright(bold(x))_i \) upright(bold(u))_i) + nabla_(upright(bold(x))_j) h_(i j) (f_j \( upright(bold(x))_j \) + g_j \( upright(bold(x))_j \) upright(bold(u))_j) $

Using Lie derivative notation:

$ dot(h)_(i j) = L_(f_i) h_(i j) + L_(g_i) h_(i j) upright(bold(u))_i + L_(f_j) h_(i j) + L_(g_j) h_(i j) upright(bold(u))_j $

To maintain forward invariance of $cal(C)_(i j)$, the joint control input $\( upright(bold(u))_i \, upright(bold(u))_j \)$ must satisfy:

$ L_(f_i) h_(i j) + L_(g_i) h_(i j) upright(bold(u))_i + L_(f_j) h_(i j) + L_(g_j) h_(i j) upright(bold(u))_j + alpha \( h_(i j) \) gt.eq 0 $

where $alpha \( dot.op \)$ is an extended class-$cal(K)$ function (chosen as linear $alpha \( h \) = gamma h$ with $gamma > 0$).

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Reciprocating Responsibility Decomposition]
<reciprocating-responsibility-decomposition>
In a decentralized fleet lacking a central coordinator, agent $i$ cannot command agent $j$'s control input $upright(bold(u))_j$. Under the #strong[Reciprocating Collision Avoidance Principle], each agent assumes equal responsibility for preserving the pairwise safety barrier:

$ alpha_(i j) \( h_(i j) \) = 1 / 2 alpha \( h_(i j) \) $

Agent $i$ enforces its local decentralized barrier constraint:

$ L_(f_i) h_(i j) \( upright(bold(x))_i \, hat(upright(bold(x)))_j \) + L_(g_i) h_(i j) \( upright(bold(x))_i \, hat(upright(bold(x)))_j \) upright(bold(u))_i + 1 / 2 gamma h_(i j) \( upright(bold(x))_i \, hat(upright(bold(x)))_j \) gt.eq 0 $

Simultaneously, agent $j$ enforces its reciprocal constraint:

$ L_(f_j) h_(i j) \( hat(upright(bold(x)))_i \, upright(bold(x))_j \) + L_(g_j) h_(i j) \( hat(upright(bold(x)))_i \, upright(bold(x))_j \) upright(bold(u))_j + 1 / 2 gamma h_(i j) \( hat(upright(bold(x)))_i \, upright(bold(x))_j \) gt.eq 0 $

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Theorem: Collective Forward Set Invariance under Reciprocating D-CBFs]
<theorem-collective-forward-set-invariance-under-reciprocating-d-cbfs>
If every agent $i in { 1 \, dots.h \, N }$ selects its local control action $upright(bold(u))_i$ from its admissible set:

$ cal(U)_i \( upright(bold(x))_i \) = {upright(bold(u))_i in bb(R)^(m_i) #h(0em) mid(bar.v) #h(0em) L_(f_i) h_(i j) + L_(g_i) h_(i j) upright(bold(u))_i + 1 / 2 gamma h_(i j) gt.eq 0 \, quad forall j eq.not i} $

then the joint system trajectory satisfies:

$ dot(h)_(i j) + gamma h_(i j) = (L_(f_i) h_(i j) + L_(g_i) h_(i j) upright(bold(u))_i + 1 / 2 gamma h_(i j)) + (L_(f_j) h_(i j) + L_(g_j) h_(i j) upright(bold(u))_j + 1 / 2 gamma h_(i j)) gt.eq 0 + 0 = 0 $

Applying the Comparison Lemma, $h_(i j) \( t \) gt.eq h_(i j) \( 0 \) e^(- gamma t) gt.eq 0$ for all $t gt.eq 0$. The collective safe operating set $cal(C)_(upright("fleet"))$ is strictly forward invariant, guaranteeing zero inter-agent collisions across the entire fleet without centralized arbitration.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Asynchronous State Estimation and Network Latency Margin Bounds]
<asynchronous-state-estimation-and-network-latency-margin-bounds>
In realistic radio-frequency networks, agent $i$ receives position broadcasts from neighbor $j$ with transport delay $tau_(i j) in \[ 0 \, tau_(upright("max")) \]$ and bounded packet drop probability.

Let $hat(upright(bold(p)))_j \( t \) = upright(bold(p))_j \( t - tau_(i j) \) + upright(bold(v))_j \( t - tau_(i j) \) tau_(i j)$ be the dead-reckoned position estimate. Given a known maximum physical acceleration $a_(upright("max"))$, the state estimation error is bounded:

$ parallel upright(bold(p))_j \( t \) - hat(upright(bold(p)))_j \( t \) parallel_2 lt.eq 1 / 2 a_(upright("max")) tau_(i j)^2 lt.eq 1 / 2 a_(upright("max")) tau_(upright("max"))^2 = epsilon.alt_(upright("pos")) \( tau_(upright("max")) \) $

To guarantee forward invariance under network latency, agent $i$ inflates the effective pairwise collision radius:

$ R_(i j)^(upright("net")) \( tau_(upright("max")) \) = r_i + r_j + d_(upright("margin")) + 2 epsilon.alt_(upright("pos")) \( tau_(upright("max")) \) = r_i + r_j + d_(upright("margin")) + a_(upright("max")) tau_(upright("max"))^2 $

Evaluating the D-CBF against the inflated radius $R_(i j)^(upright("net"))$ mathematically guarantees collision avoidance despite asynchronous communications.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[$S E \( 3 \)$ Lie Group Equivariance in Neural Physical Policies]
<se3-lie-group-equivariance-in-neural-physical-policies>
In 3D physical manipulation and locomotion, the underlying laws of Newtonian mechanics are invariant under rigid coordinate transformations. A policy that learns to grasp an object at pose $upright(bold(T))_1 in S E \( 3 \)$ should generalize directly when that same object is rotated and translated to pose $upright(bold(T))_2 = g dot.op upright(bold(T))_1$, where $g in S E \( 3 \)$.

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Mathematical Group Action on State and Action Spaces]
<mathematical-group-action-on-state-and-action-spaces>
Let $G = S E \( 3 \) = S O \( 3 \) times.l bb(R)^3$ denote the Special Euclidean group. An element $g = \( upright(bold(R)) \, upright(bold(t)) \) in G$ consists of a 3D rotation matrix $upright(bold(R)) in S O \( 3 \)$ and a translation vector $upright(bold(t)) in bb(R)^3$.

Let $cal(X)$ denote the sensory observation space (point clouds, 3D feature embeddings, or depth tokens) and let $cal(A)$ denote the physical action space (Cartesian end-effector waypoints or spatial wrench vectors). We define the group representations $rho_(cal(X)) \( g \) : cal(X) arrow.r cal(X)$ and $rho_(cal(A)) \( g \) : cal(A) arrow.r cal(A)$:

+ #strong[On Observation Points $upright(bold(P)) in bb(R)^(N times 3)$:] $rho_(cal(X)) \( g \) upright(bold(P)) = upright(bold(P)) upright(bold(R))^T + upright(bold(1))_(N times 1) upright(bold(t))^T$.
+ #strong[On Commanded Trajectory Waypoints $upright(bold(p))_k in bb(R)^3$ and Orientations $upright(bold(R))_k in S O \( 3 \)$:] $ rho_(cal(A)) \( g \) \( upright(bold(p))_k \, upright(bold(R))_k \) = \( upright(bold(R)) upright(bold(p))_k + upright(bold(t)) \, upright(bold(R)) upright(bold(R))_k \) $

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Formal Definition of $S E \( 3 \)$-Equivariance]
<formal-definition-of-se3-equivariance>
A neural policy $pi_theta : cal(X) arrow.r cal(A)$ is strictly #strong[$S E \( 3 \)$-equivariant] if the mapping commutes with the group action:

$ pi_theta \( rho_(cal(X)) \( g \) dot.op upright(bold(O)) \) = rho_(cal(A)) \( g \) dot.op pi_theta \( upright(bold(O)) \) \, quad forall g in S E \( 3 \) \, quad forall upright(bold(O)) in cal(X) $

