When an unconstrained transformer or MLP processes physical observations, it must learn the effect of rotations empirically by consuming thousands of data augmentations. An $S E \( 3 \)$-equivariant architecture enforces this commutation structurally in its layer weights, guaranteeing that rotating the input scene rotates the predicted action trajectory identically by construction.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Steerable Tensor Representations and Clebsch-Gordan Decompositions]
<steerable-tensor-representations-and-clebsch-gordan-decompositions>
To build neural layers that respect $S O \( 3 \)$ rotational symmetries, feature vectors at each 3D point are decomposed into direct sums of irreducible representations (irreps) of $S O \( 3 \)$:

$ V = xor.big_(l = 0)^(l_(upright("max"))) bb(R)^(2 l + 1) $

where $l$ is the rotation order: \* #strong[$l = 0$ (Scalars, dimension 1):] Invariant under rotation (temperatures, masses, distances, grasp probabilities). $rho_0 \( upright(bold(R)) \) = 1$. \* #strong[$l = 1$ (Vectors, dimension 3):] Standard 3D vectors (positions, velocities, forces). $rho_1 \( upright(bold(R)) \) = upright(bold(R))$. \* #strong[$l = 2$ (Tensors, dimension 5):] Symmetric traceless $3 times 3$ matrices (stress tensors, quadric surfaces, inertia moments). $rho_2 \( upright(bold(R)) \) = upright(bold(D))^2 \( upright(bold(R)) \)$, where $upright(bold(D))^l \( upright(bold(R)) \)$ is the $\( 2 l + 1 \) times \( 2 l + 1 \)$ Wigner D-matrix.

When combining geometric features $upright(bold(v))_1 in V_(l_1)$ and $upright(bold(v))_2 in V_(l_2)$, the tensor product is projected back into irreducible channels using #strong[Clebsch-Gordan coefficients] $C_(\( l_1 \, m_1 \) \( l_2 \, m_2 \))^(\( l \, m \))$:

$ \( upright(bold(v))_1 times.circle_(upright("CG")) upright(bold(v))_2 \)_m^l = sum_(m_1 = - l_1)^(l_1) sum_(m_2 = - l_2)^(l_2) C_(\( l_1 \, m_1 \) \( l_2 \, m_2 \))^(\( l \, m \)) v_(1 \, m_1)^(l_1) v_(2 \, m_2)^(l_2) $

for all target orders $\| l_1 - l_2 \| lt.eq l lt.eq l_1 + l_2$.

By constructing neural network layers strictly from Clebsch-Gordan tensor products and radial scalar non-linearities, the entire network satisfies exact $S E \( 3 \)$-equivariance at every hidden layer.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Sample Complexity Scaling Proof in Physical Space]
<sample-complexity-scaling-proof-in-physical-space>
Let $cal(F)$ be the class of $L$-Lipschitz continuous robotic manipulation policies mapping a bounded workspace $Omega subset bb(R)^3$ to bounded action trajectories in $\[ - 1 \, 1 \]^d$.

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Non-Equivariant Hypothesis Space Complexity]
<non-equivariant-hypothesis-space-complexity>
For a standard unconstrained neural network (such as a Vision Transformer or MLP), the $epsilon.alt$-covering number of the policy hypothesis space $cal(H)_(upright("standard"))$ under the infinity norm satisfies:

$ log cal(N) (epsilon.alt \, cal(H)_(upright("standard")) \, parallel dot.op parallel_oo) = cal(O) ((L / epsilon.alt)^(d_(upright("in")))) $

where $d_(upright("in")) = 3 K$ represents the dimensionality of $K$ spatial input points. The number of physical demonstrations $M_(upright("standard"))$ required to achieve generalization error $lt.eq epsilon.alt$ scales as:

$ M_(upright("standard")) = Omega (1 / epsilon.alt^2 (L / epsilon.alt)^(3 K)) $

#heading(level: 4, numbering: none)[Equivariant Hypothesis Space Complexity]
<equivariant-hypothesis-space-complexity>
When the policy space is constrained to the $S E \( 3 \)$-equivariant subspace $cal(H)_(upright("equiv")) subset cal(H)_(upright("standard"))$, any function $f in cal(H)_(upright("equiv"))$ is uniquely determined by its values on the orbit quotient space $Omega \/ S E \( 3 \)$.

Because the group $S E \( 3 \)$ has dimension $dim \( S E \( 3 \) \) = 6$ (3 translational and 3 rotational degrees of freedom), the effective input manifold dimensionality is reduced from $3 K$ to $3 K - 6$ for $K gt.eq 3$. Furthermore, the volume of the search space is divided by the Haar volume of the compact rotation group $\| S O \( 3 \) \| = 8 pi^2$:

$ log cal(N) (epsilon.alt \, cal(H)_(upright("equiv")) \, parallel dot.op parallel_oo) = cal(O) (frac(1, \| S O \( 3 \) \|) (L / epsilon.alt)^(3 K - 6)) $

For 3D robotic grasping where the target object can appear in arbitrary $S E \( 3 \)$ poses, the sample complexity ratio evaluates to:

$ M_(upright("equiv")) / M_(upright("standard")) approx frac(1, \| S O \( 3 \) \| dot.op \( upright("discretization steps") \)^3) approx 1 / 100 upright(" to ") 1 / 1000 $

This mathematical bound explains why $S E \( 3 \)$-equivariant foundation policies (such as Equivariant Diffusion Policy and Perceiver-Actor) achieve $95 %$ pick-and-place success rates with only $20 upright("–") 50$ physical demonstrations, whereas non-equivariant models require $2 \, 000 upright("–") 5 \, 000$ trials to learn spatial rotational invariance.

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none)[Distributed Safety Filter Determinism and Complexity Bounds]
<distributed-safety-filter-determinism-and-complexity-bounds>
In multi-agent fleets, each agent's bare-metal microcontroller evaluates reciprocating Decentralized Control Barrier Functions (D-CBFs) across all neighboring peers within its RF sensing radius (e.g.~up to $N_(upright("nbr")) = 8$ neighbors) at $1000 upright(" Hz")$.

Because pairwise half-space projection calculations are decoupled ($L_f h_(i j) + L_g h_(i j) upright(bold(u))_i + 1 / 2 gamma h_(i j) gt.eq 0$), filtering candidate 3D acceleration vectors $upright(bold(u))_(upright("cand")) in bb(R)^3$ requires only $O \( N_(upright("nbr")) \)$ dot products in static memory (#NormalTok("malloc = 0");). On an ARM Cortex-M7 at $480 upright(" MHz")$, evaluating 8 neighbor barrier certificates and projecting onto the safe velocity half-space executes in under $2.8 thin mu upright("s")$ ($upright("WCET") lt.eq 5.0 thin mu upright("s")$), providing distributed forward set invariance with minimal compute overhead.

